mmary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Erdős on Mahler (1989)</span><span class="result-impact-tag">521² = 111101₁₂</span><span class="result-news-label">Editorial selection</span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Mahler&#x27;s 0/1-digit square was not unique</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A counterexample to the suggested uniqueness of a nontrivial coprime 0/1-digit square, as recorded by Erdős; not Mahler’s fixed-base finiteness conjecture.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="discovery-quote"><p class="quote-label">Source excerpt</p><blockquote><p>“In fact, the only nontrivial solution he found was 7^3 + 7^2 + 7 + 1 = 20^2, and perhaps there are no others”</p><cite>P. Erdős, recalling K. Mahler</cite></blockquote></div><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Erdős&#x27;s 1989 reminiscence, page 2 (verbatim):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source presents the base-7 identity as Mahler&#x27;s only known nontrivial example and immediately suggests that no others may exist.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The finite certificate first proves Nat.Coprime 12 521 and 12^5 + 12^4 + 12^3 + 12^2 + 1 = 271441 = 521^2.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A counterexample to the suggested uniqueness of a nontrivial coprime 0/1-digit square, as recorded by Erdős; not Mahler’s fixed-base finiteness conjecture.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>This refutation makes no claim about Mahler&#x27;s finiteness conjecture for any fixed k &gt; 4 and no claim about infinitude for k = 2, 3, 4.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="erdos-1989-mahler-binary-digit-square-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Refutation of Erdős&#x27;s report of Mahler&#x27;s binary-digit square uniqueness</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Erdős's 1989 reminiscence, page 2 (verbatim):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He told me about his last paper. Let e_i = 0 or 1. Then Σ_{i=1}^{n} e_i k^i = x^2 has infinitely many solutions for k = 2, 3 and 4. He conjectured that it has only a finite number of solutions for k &gt; 4. In fact, the only nontrivial solution he found was 7^3 + 7^2 + 7 + 1 = 20^2, and perhaps there are no others (1 + (k^2 − 1) = k^2 counts as trivial).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The literal refuted statement is the proposition <code>claim</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">∀ (k x : ℕ) (S : Finset ℕ),
  5 ≤ k → 1 &lt; x → BinaryBaseSquare k x S →
    (k = 7 ∧ x = 20) ∨ k = x ^ 2 - 1
</code></pre>
<p>Here <code>BinaryBaseSquare k x S</code> means
<code>Nat.Coprime k x ∧ ∑ i ∈ S, k ^ i = x ^ 2</code>. The exponent set includes zero
because the printed example contains <code>k^0 = 1</code>. &quot;Trivial&quot; means the two-digit
family <code>x^2 = k + 1</code>, equivalently <code>k = x^2 − 1</code>, matching Erdős's
parenthetical after renaming the base variable. The coprimality condition
<code>gcd(k, x) = 1</code> comes from Mahler's setting and excludes digit-shift scalings
<code>x ↦ k·x</code>. The counterexamples <code>(12, 521)</code> and <code>(8, 11677)</code> are in no trivial
family under either reading.</p>
<p>This refutation makes no claim about Mahler's finiteness conjecture for any
fixed <code>k &gt; 4</code> and no claim about infinitude for <code>k = 2, 3, 4</code>.</p>
