s have?</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source knew the spectral region for decomposable witnesses. The full symmetric case left a gap.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Spectral transport and determinant bounds establish necessity; explicit witnesses establish sufficiency.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Two sharp inequalities characterize the three ordered eigenvalues of symmetric two-qubit entanglement witnesses.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The characterization concerns symmetric two-qubit entanglement witnesses.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="champagne-johnston-macdonald-pipes-2022-symmetric-witness-spectrum"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The eigenvalues of symmetric two-qubit entanglement witnesses satisfy µ3 ≥ −√(µ1µ2)</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>G. Champagne, N. Johnston, M. MacDonald and L. Pipes, <em>Spectral properties of symmetric quantum states and symmetric entanglement witnesses</em>, Linear Algebra Appl. 649, 273–300 (2022), arXiv:2108.10405v1, study the eigenvalues of symmetric entanglement witnesses.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Witnesses.</strong> A symmetric entanglement witness is a Hermitian W on C^d ∨ C^d with (v ⊗ v)*W(v ⊗ v) ≥ 0 for all v (§2.3); positive semidefinite W are included (footnote 1).</li>
<li><strong>Theorem 3.</strong> For d = 2 and real µ1 ≥ µ2 ≥ µ3, the inequalities (a) µ2 ≥ 0 and (b) µ3 ≥ −√(µ1µ2) are sufficient for the existence of a symmetric witness with these eigenvalues, and necessary for decomposable ones. Every symmetric witness satisfies (a) and a weaker inequality (c).</li>
<li><strong>The conjecture.</strong> After Theorem 3 and in §6(c) (page 20): &quot;We conjecture that inequalities (a) and (b) from that theorem provide the correct characterization.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/champagnejohnstonmacdonaldpipes2022spectral.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/17536">#17536</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<ul>
<li>C²∨C² is identified with C³ through the orthonormal Dicke basis e₀₀, (e₀₁ + e₁₀)/√2, e₁₁, in which v ⊗ v has coordinates (v₀², √2 v₀v₁, v₁²).</li>
<li>&quot;Eigenvalues µ1 ≥ µ2 ≥ µ3&quot; means that the characteristic polynomial is (X − µ1)(X − µ2)(X − µ3).</li>
<li>The conjecture: for all real µ1 ≥ µ2 ≥ µ3, a symmetric witness with these eigenvalues exists if and only if µ2 ≥ 0 and µ3 ≥ −√(µ1µ2).</li>
</ul>
<p>The conjecture is proved in this reading.</p>
