t k-sets of real finite-dimensional sup-norm spaces question resolved analysis &amp; dynamics the k-number and extreme-point k-sets of real finite-dimensional sup-norm spaces paul, on local preservation of orthogonality and its application to isometries, arxiv:2407.08900v1, linear algebra and its applications (2024), states: local preservation of birkhoff–james orthogonality can force a global scalar isometry. for real sup-norm spaces of dimension n ≥ 2, an extreme-point set is a k-set exactly when it spans, and the k-number is n. a nonzero norm-scaling linear endomorphism is a scalar multiple of a linear isometry. the classification of all such minimal k-sets remains open. released-dossier daba0b8ce7ed3e01f80c25233ee998266bb213201f88d3f420c9f885d5871e73 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Analysis &amp; dynamics</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2024</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The K-number and extreme-point K-sets of real finite-dimensional sup-norm spaces</strong><span class="result-excerpt">For real sup-norm spaces of dimension n ≥ 2, an extreme-point set is a K-set exactly when it spans, and the K-number is n.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Paul, On local preservation of orthogonality and its application to isometries, arXiv:2407.08900v1, Linear Algebra and its Applications (2024), states:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Local preservation of Birkhoff–James orthogonality can force a global scalar isometry.</p><p><span>The idea</span>A nonzero norm-scaling linear endomorphism is a scalar multiple of a linear isometry.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For real sup-norm spaces of dimension n ≥ 2, an extreme-point set is a K-set exactly when it spans, and the K-number is n.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The classification of all such minimal K-sets remains open.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="sain-manna-paul-2024-linf-k-number"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>The K-number and extreme-point K-sets of real finite-dimensional sup-norm spaces</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. Sain, J. Manna, K. Paul, <em>On local preservation of orthogonality and its application to isometries</em>, arXiv:2407.08900v1, <em>Linear Algebra and its Applications</em> (2024), states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Definition 1. Let X be a Banach space. A set A ⊂ S_X is said to be a Koldobsky-set, for the sake of brevity, a K-set, if any operator T ∈ L(X) that preserves Birkhoff-James orthogonality at each point of A, is necessarily a scalar multiple of an isometry.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Definition 3. Let X be a Banach space having a finite K-set. Then the K-number of X is defined by min{|A| : A is a K-set of X}. We denote the K-number of X by κ(X).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Open Question at the end of §2 is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Open Question: Characterize the minimal K-sets in ℓ^n_∞ and hence find κ(ℓ^n_∞).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The spaces are real, and minimality is under inclusion. The result determines the K-number for $n\ge2$ and characterizes the minimal K-sets consisting of extreme points; it leaves the classification involving non-extreme points open.</p>
