<p>An open question at this point is whether our sufficient condition is also necessary, and whether our necessary condition is also sufficient.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source is <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2407.17103v3">arXiv v3</a>, with publication
identifier <a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0231812">DOI 10.1063/5.0231812</a>.
Definition 1 and Lemmas 2–3 specify the elementary channel and inverse;
Proposition 1 and Theorem 1 specify the necessary and sufficient conditions.
This dossier anchors the combined classification of the two reversals,
including their fixed-pair and existential-pair readings.</p>
<p>Let the complex dimension be any finite n ≥ 2, let Φ be an actual CPTP map,
and let (Kⱼ) be any finite Kraus representation of Φ. For an ordered pair of
orthogonal unit vectors (x,y), E(x,y) means that Φ composed on the right
with the inverse of the elementary channel Ψₜ(x,y) is completely positive
for some real 0 &lt; t &lt; 1. N(x,y) means
⟨y,Kⱼ<em>Gx⟩ = 0 for every index j and every complex matrix G in the
Hilbert–Schmidt orthogonal complement of the whole complex Kraus span.
S(x,y) means N(x,y) together with the additional diagonal tests
⟨x,Kⱼ</em>Gx⟩ = 0 over the same indices and complement.</p>
<p>The classification established by the designated result has four cells:</p>
<p>| Reading | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Fixed ordered pair: N(x,y) iff E(x,y) | Proved for every map and representation in the stated scope. |
| Existential ordered pair: some N pair iff some E pair | Proved for the same map and representation. |
| Fixed ordered pair: E(x,y) implies S(x,y) | Refuted in every dimension n ≥ 2. |
| Existential ordered pair: some E pair implies some S pair | Refuted in every dimension n ≥ 2; the counterexample has no S pair in any finite Kraus representation. |</p>
<p>The Scribe claim's <code>Proved</code> kind refers to this combined classification,
whose statement includes the counterexample. It does not label either
E ⇒ S assertion as proved.</p>
