rd</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The fixed CDK image of improper partition matrices</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Shane Chern and Shishuo Fu, <em>Signed counting of partition matrices</em>,
arXiv:2508.21318v2, Section 5, Question 5.5, page 26:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Find a characterization (possibly in terms of pattern avoidance) of the inversion sequences produced by improper partition matrices under the action of the Claesson–Dukes–Kubitzke bijection.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For each integer $n\geq1$, write $[n]={1,\ldots,n}$ and let
$\mathbf I_n={e:[n]\to\mathbb N:0\leq e_j\leq j-1\text{ for all }j\in[n]}$.
A native partition matrix on $[n]$ is an upper triangular square matrix $P$ of
sets, of any permitted positive dimension $k$, whose nonempty cells partition
$[n]$, every row and column is nonempty, and
$\operatorname{col}_P(a)\leq\operatorname{col}_P(b)$ whenever $a&lt;b$.
The row and column of a label are those of its unique cell.</p>
<p>An adjacent pair $i,i+1$, with $1\leq i&lt;n$, is an ascent or descent only when
its labels share a column and have respectively increasing or decreasing row
indices. Such a pair is proper precisely when $i$ has the parity of the least
label of that column. An improper matrix has no proper ascent or descent;
equal-row pairs are neither. Write $\operatorname{IPPM}_n$ for this class.</p>
<p>The fixed source map is</p>
<p>$$
\Pi_n(P)<em>j=\sum</em>{d=1}^{\operatorname{row}<em>P(j)-1} n_d,
\qquad n_d=\sum</em>{r=1}^{k}|P_{r,d}|.
$$</p>
<p>Thus the summands are column cardinalities, and the sum is zero in the first
row. The requested image uses this map and all permitted dimensions.</p>
