itical-point moments</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Q. Tang and T. Zhang, <em>Sharp Schoenberg type inequalities and the de Bruin–Sharma problem</em>, arXiv:2508.10341v3, §1.3, Conjecture 1.13. The source is <a href="../Library/Zeros/tang2025schoenberg.md">tang2025schoenberg</a>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 1.13. Let P(z) = z ∏_{j=1}^n (z − z_j), z_j ∈ C \ {0}, and let w_1, …, w_n be the zeros of P′(z). Then for every real number p ≥ 1 we have Σ_{k=1}^n 1/|w_k|^p ≤ ((n+1)^p + n − 1)/n · Σ_{j=1}^n 1/|z_j|^p. Moreover, equality holds if and only if z_1 = ⋯ = z_n.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For every natural $n\ge1$ and every family $z_1,\ldots,z_n\in\mathbb C\setminus{0}$, put</p>
<p>$$
P(Z)=Z\prod_{j=1}^n(Z-z_j),\qquad
c(n,p)=\frac{(n+1)^p+n-1}{n}.
$$</p>
<p>Let $w_1,\ldots,w_n$ be all roots of $P'$, counted with algebraic multiplicity. No distinctness, simplicity, reality or common argument hypothesis is imposed on the nonzero roots. The result proves the following three clauses:</p>
