tabilizer-commutation-rank"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The prime-dimensional stabilizer commutation-rank bound</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>O. Makuta, B. Kuzaka and R. Augusiak, <em>Fully non-positive-partial-transpose genuinely entangled subspaces</em>, arXiv:2203.16902v2; Quantum 7, 915 (2023), Section 3, Conjecture 1 (<code>final_5.tex</code>, lines 458–463):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Given a stabilizer $\mathbb{S}=\langle G_{1},\dots,G_{k}\rangle$, if the local dimension $d$ is a prime number and the corresponding stabilizer subspace $V$ is genuinely entangled, then</p>
<p>$\dim K(\mathbb{S}) \geqslant \left\lceil \frac{N-1}{d-1} \right\rceil.$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source defines $K(\mathbb{S})$ using the local commutation exponents (lines 375–391). In the Lean coordinates, write the prime local dimension as $p$. A Pauli word is a phase residue together with $x,z:\mathrm{Fin},N\to\mathbb F_p$, acting as $\omega^{\mathrm{phase}}\bigotimes_n X^{x(n)}Z^{z(n)}$, with $\omega=\exp(2\pi i/p)$. For words $a,b$, the commutation vector has coordinates $z_a(n)x_b(n)-x_a(n)z_b(n)$; <code>commutationSpace G</code> is the $\mathbb F_p$-span of all generator-pair vectors.</p>
<p><code>IsStabilizer G</code> requires pairwise commuting generator matrices and excludes every scalar matrix other than the identity from their generated matrix submonoid. <code>stabilizerSubspace G</code> is the intersection of the generators' fixed subspaces. <code>genuinelyEntangled G</code> says that every nonzero vector in this intersection fails to factor across every bipartition with two nonempty sides. Factorization means an amplitude of the form $u(x|<em>Q)v(x|</em>{Q^c})$; normalization is not required.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20105">#20105</a> fixes the statement for every $N\ge1$, prime $p$, and finite generator family. <code>claim</code> uses natural-number division:</p>
<p>$(N-1+(p-2))/(p-1)\le\operatorname{finrank}_{\mathbb F_p}(\operatorname{commutationSpace}G)$.</p>
<p>For $N\ge1$ and prime $p$, the left side is $\lceil(N-1)/(p-1)\rceil$. The conjecture is proved.</p>
