"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Quantum cube-root scalar curvature is strictly Schur-increasing</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Gibilisco and Isola, <em>On the monotonicity of scalar curvature in classical and quantum information geometry</em>, arXiv:math-ph/0407007v2, Conjecture 4.2, printed page 11:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Suppose $n\geq2$. For the function $\mathrm{Scal}_p(\cdot):\mathcal D^1_n\to\mathbb R$ one has the following properties: if $p\in(1,2)$ then $\mathrm{Scal}_p$ is strictly Schur-decreasing; if $p\in(2,+\infty]$ then $\mathrm{Scal}_p$ is strictly Schur-increasing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This settlement proves the quantum $p=3$ slice, equivalently $\alpha=1/3$, in every dimension $n\ge2$. The faithful density states are positive definite complex Hermitian matrices of trace one. The metric is the Euclidean pull-back of $A\mapsto3A^{1/3}$, with real trace inner product. Scalar curvature is defined by coordinate Christoffel symbols and Ricci contraction, rather than by the spectral formula proved below. The source's order calls $x$ more mixed than $y$ when the decreasingly sorted prefix sums of $x$ are at most those of $y$, with equal total sums.</p>
