LI-matrix of graphs</em>, Electronic Journal of Linear Algebra 38 (2022), 680–696,
Conjecture 5.11 (page 694), states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Among all connected graphs with n and m edges n ≤ m ≤ 2n−3, the Gₘ,ₙ is the unique graph with maximal value of ‖LI(G)‖F₂ and ‖LI(G)‖F₃.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $n$ is the number of vertices, $m$ the number of unordered edges,
$L(G)=D(G)-A(G)$, $LI(G)=L(G)-(2m/n)I_n$, and the Ky Fan $k$-norm is the sum
of the $k$ largest singular values, with multiplicity. The assertion is read as a
separate maximum and equality characterization for each of $k=2$ and $k=3$.
The interpretation involving a unique simultaneous maximizer is outside the claim.</p>
