n>Our contribution</span>The ordinary ari coproduct preserves every right-factor level filtration</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="burmester-kuhn-2025-conjecture-4-29-ari-coproduct-filtration"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The original ari coproduct preserves every right-factor level filtration</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Annika Burmester and Ulf Kühn, <em>On post-Lie structures for free Lie
algebras</em>, arXiv:2504.19661v1, Conjecture 4.29, state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For k ≥ 0 we define the k-level of a word v_{k_1}⋯v_{k_d} by
d − #{j | k_j=k}, and extend this to an increasing filtration on
Q⟨V⟩. For w∈Q⟨V⟩, write as usual Δ_a(w)=w_(1)⊗w_(2). Then,
we always have k-level(w_(2)) ≤ k-level(w).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Let $H=\mathbb Q\langle v_0,v_1,\ldots\rangle$ be the algebra of finite
ordinary rational word polynomials, including its unit. For a word $w$,
let $\lambda_k(w)$ count letters different from $v_k$ and put</p>
<p>$$
F_r^k=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb Q}
{w:\lambda_k(w)\le r}.
$$</p>
<p>The precise filtration assertion is, for every $k,r\in\mathbb N$ and
every $P\in F_r^k$,</p>
<p>$$
\Delta_a(P)\in\operatorname{range}
\bigl(\operatorname{id}<em>H\otimes\iota_r^k:
H\otimes</em>{\mathbb Q}F_r^k\longrightarrow H\otimes_{\mathbb Q}H\bigr),
$$</p>
<p>where $\iota_r^k$ is the natural submodule inclusion. All tensor products
are ordinary algebraic tensor products. A Sweedler expression is read
through this subspace assertion, rather than through a chosen tensor
decomposition. The resolution is <strong>Proved</strong> by
<code>D5/S1/Words/PostLie/AriCoproductFiltration.result</code>, with
<code>claim : Prop</code> and <code>result : claim</code>.</p>
<p>| Source clause | Formal counterpart |
