ess survives encoding. A nonempty right-special rotor factor cannot contain the unique zero and must end in 2: every other symbol has forced successor 2.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For every irrational slope greater than one, the unique right-special rotor factor of each positive length is the reversed prefix after the initial zero.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="krishnaswami-rameshan-2026-rotor-right-special-word"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The right-special rotor word is the reversed prefix after the initial zero</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Govind S. Krishnaswami and Anirudh Rameshan, <em>Symbol sequences from three-rotor coincidences and their word-complexity</em>, arXiv:2606.25766v1 [nlin.CD] (2026), §5.3.1, “Conjecture on right-special word”, state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>we conjecture that the $(n-1)$-word that branches (in two ways) coincides with the unique $n$-word starting with 0 read backwards (excluding the 0).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a verbatim excerpt from line 701 of the downloaded v1 TeX <code>3rotor-symb-dyn.tex</code>, md5 <code>5d78bd4b19257909d542bbe5b1469202</code>. The surrounding scope is UHE quasiperiodic trajectories starting at the origin with irrational slope $\sigma&gt;1$ (line 663). Discussion question (1), line 917, explicitly asks for a settlement. The source specifies its encoding in §5.3.4, line 835:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Given a Sturmian sequence $s_1 s_2 s_3 \cdots$, the corresponding rotor sequence is $02 s_1 2 s_2 2 s_3 2 \cdots$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20821">#20821</a> fixes the formal reading. For $m\ge1$, define $s_m=3$ exactly when $m=\lfloor k\sigma\rfloor_{\mathbb N}+k$ for some natural $k\ge1$, and $s_m=1$ otherwise. Here $\lfloor\cdot\rfloor_{\mathbb N}$ is the natural-valued floor used by <code>cutting</code>. Define $R_0=0$, $R_{2j-1}=2$ and $R_{2j}=s_j$ for $j\ge1$, as in <code>rotor</code>.</p>
<p>A finite list $w$ of natural symbols is a factor when $w_t=R_{i+t}$ for some natural starting position $i$ and every $t&lt;|w|$. It is right-special when $wa$ and $wb$ are factors for two distinct natural symbols $a\ne b$; the occurrences may start at different positions. These are <code>IsFactor</code> and <code>IsRightSpecial</code>.</p>
<p>The claim is: for every irrational real $\sigma&gt;1$, every natural $n\ge2$, and every finite list $w$ of length $n-1$,</p>
<p>$w\text{ is right-special in }R\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad w=\operatorname{reverse}(R_1R_2\cdots R_{n-1}).$</p>
