neous symmetric functions of finite path spectra. the path determinant satisfies the constant-weight continuant recurrence.  released-dossier e94936c83ef70b06f8a49d092769e898ddf13b84be6ec20357ab06ee37d7db55 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2023</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Complete homogeneous symmetric norms of paths</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Complete homogeneous symmetric functions of finite path spectra.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>Why it matters</span>The two expressions of the later Bouthat–Chávez–Sheng Remark 4.3 are settled separately in 18566; its one-term simplification is refuted in 18567.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The path determinant satisfies the constant-weight continuant recurrence.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Complete homogeneous symmetric functions of finite path spectra.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="chavez-2023-path-chs-norm-closed-form"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Complete homogeneous symmetric norms of paths</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Chávez et al., <em>Extremal polynomial norms of graphs</em>, arXiv:2311.04689v3, Section 5, Problem 1: “Compute ‖P_n‖_d for d ≥ 4.” The surrounding norm definition requires even $d$.</p>
<p>For $d=2m$, set $L=2m+n-1$ and interpret $\binom Lr$ as zero outside $0\le r\le L$. The answer is</p>
<p>$$h_{2m}(\lambda(P_n))=\sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor m/(n+1)\rfloor}\left[\binom L{m-k(n+1)}-2\binom L{m-k(n+1)-1}+\binom L{m-k(n+1)-2}\right].$$</p>
<p>The norm is the real $2m$-th root of this expression, for every $n\ge1,m\ge2$.</p>
