subcategories for uniformly oriented A_n quivers</em>, arXiv:2610.07999v2,
Section 5.7, conjecture:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It seems reasonable to conjecture that $r(n,n+1-i)$ is a polynomial in $n$ of
degree $2i$ and leading coefficient $\frac{1}{i!}$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The preceding sentence specifies “any fixed number of missing points.” This dossier
anchors the whole conjecture: for every natural $i$, one rational polynomial $P_i$
has exact degree $2i$, leading coefficient $1/i!$, and satisfies
$P_i(n)=r(n,n+1-i)$ for every natural $n$ with $i\le n+1$.</p>
<p>Section 3, before Section 3.1, defines $T(n)={(a,b):0\le a\le b\le n}$ and its
subsets satisfying Condition (star). Three selected points $(a,b),(c,d),(e,f)$,
allowing repetitions, with $a\le c,e\le b\le d,f$ force
$(\max(c,e),\max(d,f))$ and, if $b&lt;\min(d,f)$, also $(b+1,\min(d,f))$.
Section 5.1 defines $r(n,k)$ as the number of these subsets with exactly $k$
selected first-column points. Empty subsets and rows are permitted.</p>
