<p>In A392131, $a(n)$ is the signed determinant; in A392132, $a(n)$ is the permanent. These are two records of one assertion. The source is OEIS-only, submitted in 2025 and published in 2026, with no source DOI or arXiv identifier. Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21886">#21886</a> fixes the conventions and the settlement criterion. The source quotations and locators are in <a href="../Library/Asymptotics/spezia2026pellcirculant.md">the Library note</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>Let $P_0=0$, $P_1=1$ and $P_{k+2}=2P_{k+1}+P_k$. With indices in $\operatorname{Fin}(n)$, the matrix is $C_n(i,j)=P_{n-1-\operatorname{val}(i-j)}$, with natural subtraction truncated at zero and modular subtraction in $i-j$. Its first row is $(P_{n-1},P_0,\ldots,P_{n-2})$, the initial right rotation specified by the source COMMENT and four-by-four EXAMPLE. The formalization reuses the existing Pell sequence <code>D5.S1.Recurrence.PellCompanionGcd.P</code> and Mathlib's <code>Matrix.circulant</code>, <code>Matrix.det</code> and <code>Matrix.permanent</code>.</p>
<p>| Source clause | Formal counterpart |
| --- | --- |
| A392131 COMMENT: first column reversed Pell vector, first row initially rotated right | <code>C</code>, with the literal binding vector and Mathlib's modular circulant indexing |
| A392131 determinant, including the source empty value | <code>A392131 n = Matrix.det (C n)</code>; the determinant retains its sign |
| A392132 permanent of the same matrix | <code>A392132 n = Matrix.permanent (C n)</code> |
| The ratio tends to one | <code>claim</code>: every real $\varepsilon&gt;0$ admits $N\ge2$ such that every $n\ge N$ has $|A392132(n)/A392131(n)-1|&lt;\varepsilon$ |
| No additional matrix-family hypothesis | <code>result : claim</code> has no hypotheses |</p>
