rce record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Exact quantum bound of the Bernards–Gühne nonlocality-depth family</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>F. Bernards and O. Gühne, <em>Bell inequalities for nonlocality depth</em>,
arXiv:2205.04250v2, Phys. Rev. A 107, 022412, Section III.F, Eqs. (28)–(29),
define the permutation-symmetric Bell functional</p>
<p>$$F_n=\sum_{\ell=1}^n(-1)^{1+\lceil\ell/2\rceil}\ell
(1\ldots1,2\ldots2).$$</p>
<p>The bracket sums distinct party permutations, with $n-\ell$ settings 1
and $\ell$ settings 2. The source states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the following, we discuss numerical findings concerning the Bell inequalities F_n for n ≤ 8 parties. We find, that the quantum bound of the inequalities is F_n = √2 n 2^{n−2} (29) up to numerical precision, which is achieved by choosing A₁ = B₁ = … = σ_x and A₂ = B₂ = … = σ_z</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For every integer $n\ge3$, the maximum over all finite local dimensions,
binary observables and density matrices is exactly
$Q_n=\sqrt2,n2^{n-2}$. This is a uniform extension and proof of the numerical
finding; the source does not state a general-$n$ conjecture.
The preregistration is <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16626">#16626</a>.</p>
