<p>where $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n$ are the real adjacency eigenvalues. Among connected regular simple graphs of order $n\ge3$, the cycle $C_n$ attains the minimum and the complete graph $K_n$ attains the maximum:</p>
<p>$$|C_n|<em>{2m}\le|G|</em>{2m}\le|K_n|_{2m}.$$</p>
<p>Here connected means that every two vertices are joined by a walk. Section 2 of the source uses the word connected for graphs without isolated vertices; under that reading the minimum changes for even orders, since a perfect matching is regular without isolated vertices and has smaller coefficients, for example $h_2(2K_2)=2&lt;4=h_2(C_4)$. The formal statement uses ordinary connectedness. In fact the lower coefficient bound $h_{2m}(C_n)\le h_{2m}(G)$ holds for every $r$-regular simple graph of order $n\ge3$ with $r\ge2$, without connectedness. This dossier concerns the regular family. The unicyclic branch is not addressed; the complete multipartite branch is settled separately.</p>
