d in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Generic logarithmic Lie-length of su(n)</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Yifan Jia and Angela Capel, <em>A generic quantum Wielandt's inequality</em>, arXiv:2301.08241v3, Quantum 8 (2024) 1331, Conjecture 6, p. 19:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let $S$ be a random Lie-generating set of $\mathfrak{su}(n)$, then $\mathtt{Lie}\ell(S)=\Theta(\log n)$ almost surely.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Definition 3, p. 17, counts generator letters in right-nested brackets. It takes $U_1=\mathrm{span}(S)$, $U_{k+1}=\mathrm{span}[S,U_k]$, and the least positive $L$ for which the cumulative layers span the Lie algebra. The number $g\ge2$ of generators is fixed independently of $n$. The random law is Lebesgue measure in real linear coordinates on $\mathfrak{su}(n)^g$; the almost-everywhere assertion also applies to probability laws absolutely continuous with respect to this measure (by the definition of absolute continuity).</p>
