rresponding seed-content scaling for first and second powers and asks whether it persists for every higher power. integer seed content scales the gcd of all shifted power windows by its exponent. the set-gcd universal property, cancellation by a positive scalar and nonnegative divisibility antisymmetry give scaling of the whole-family gcd.  released-dossier 7578fa84732bd3d9d6713a9d16d6bd67954684acd1bb5925addaa0fe93090003 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2022</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Gibonacci power-window gcd normalization</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Integer seed content scales the gcd of all shifted power windows by its exponent.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Section 6.1, Question 6.2, PDF p. 14 of arXiv:2204.12626v2 asks:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper gives the corresponding seed-content scaling for first and second powers and asks whether it persists for every higher power.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The set-gcd universal property, cancellation by a positive scalar and nonnegative divisibility antisymmetry give scaling of the whole-family gcd.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Integer seed content scales the gcd of all shifted power windows by its exponent.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="mbirika-spilker-gibonacci-power-gcd-normalization"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Gibonacci power-window gcd normalization</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Section 6.1, Question 6.2, PDF p. 14 of arXiv:2204.12626v2 asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Fix $G_0, G_1 \in \mathbb{Z}$ and set $d := \gcd(G_0,G_1)$. Consider the two Gibonacci sequences $(G_n)<em>{n\geq 0}$ and $(G_n^\prime)</em>{n\geq 0}$, where $(G_n^\prime)_{n=0}^\infty$ is generated by the relatively prime initial conditions $G_0^\prime = \frac{G_0}{d}$ and $G_1^\prime = \frac{G_1}{d}$. Then the following identities holds:</p>
<p>$\mathcal G_{G_0,G_1}(k)=d\cdot\mathcal G_{G'<em>0,G'<em>1}(k)$; $\mathcal G^2</em>{G_0,G_1}(k)=d^2\cdot\mathcal G^2</em>{G'_0,G'_1}(k)$.</p>
<p>The first formula holds from Guyer-Mbirika [4, Theorem 19], while the second formula holds from Theorem 3.6 in this current paper. Will it be the case that $\mathcal{G}<em>{G_0, G_1}^n!(k) = d^n \cdot \mathcal{G}</em>{G_0^\prime, G_1^\prime}^n!(k)$ for all $n \geq 3$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For every signed integer seed pair $(a,b)≠(0,0)$, every natural
$k\geq1$ and every natural $e\geq3$, let $G(0)=a$, $G(1)=b$ and
$G(n+2)=G(n+1)+G(n)$. Define the nonnegative gcd of the entire shift family by</p>
<p>$$C_e(a,b;k)=\gcd_{t\in\mathbb N}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}G(t+i)^e\right).$$</p>
<p>With $d=\gcd(a,b)&gt;0$, the target is</p>
<p>$$C_e(a,b;k)=d^e C_e(a/d,b/d;k).$$</p>
<p>The quotients are exact integers. Both gcds satisfy their divisibility iff
for every integer divisor, including zero and negative divisors. Individual
zero seeds, zero windows and odd exponents remain in scope.</p>
