 an arbitrary integer sequence satisfying u{n+2} = un + u{n+1} and reads off the two conjectures as the fibonacci and lucas instances. the alternating factorial-stirling weighted sum of the even-index terms of a fibonacci recurrence equals the signed weighted sum of the preceding stirling row. this is the classical fubini polynomial, also called the ordered bell polynomial; qn(1) is the number of ordered partitions of an n-element set.  released-dossier 459e4bab17b8b7eea67ea73e4805c820b560ef4566f8c66fc57ccf6726c5aea5 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Recurrence proved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>Source</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2024</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The Alternating Stirling Weighted Sum of Even-Index Fibonacci and Lucas Numbers</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The alternating factorial-Stirling weighted sum of the even-index terms of a Fibonacci recurrence equals the signed weighted sum of the preceding Stirling row.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Parisse, Integers 24 (2024), Article A70, Section 4, states two conjectures.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Neither identity depends on the initial values of the sequence, so the recorded proof establishes the common statement for an arbitrary integer sequence satisfying u{n+2} = un + u{n+1} and reads off the two conjectures as the Fibonacci and Lucas instances.</p><p><span>The idea</span>This is the classical Fubini polynomial, also called the ordered Bell polynomial; Qn(1) is the number of ordered partitions of an n-element set.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The alternating factorial-Stirling weighted sum of the even-index terms of a Fibonacci recurrence equals the signed weighted sum of the preceding Stirling row.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="parisse-stirling-fibonacci-alternating-sum"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The Alternating Stirling Weighted Sum of Even-Index Fibonacci and Lucas Numbers</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Parisse, <em>Integers</em> <strong>24</strong> (2024), Article A70, Section 4, states two conjectures.
The first, after Equation (4.7):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 1. For all ℓ ∈ N_0, we have</p>
<pre><code>∑_{m=0}^{ℓ} (-1)^m m! S(ℓ+1, m+1) F_{2(m+1)} = (-1)^ℓ ∑_{m=0}^{ℓ} m! S(ℓ, m) F_{m+2} .
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>The second, after Equation (4.9):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 2. For all ℓ ∈ N_0, we have</p>
<pre><code>∑_{m=0}^{ℓ} (-1)^m m! S(ℓ+1, m+1) L_{2(m+1)} = (-1)^ℓ ∑_{m=0}^{ℓ} m! S(ℓ, m) L_{m+2} .
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>Here <code>S(j, m)</code> is the Stirling number of the second kind, <code>F</code> the Fibonacci
numbers with <code>F_1 = F_2 = 1</code>, and <code>L</code> the Lucas numbers with <code>L_0 = 2</code>, <code>L_1 = 1</code>.
Both are the constant term of the article's own formula for the weighted sums
<code>∑_{k=0}^{n} k^ℓ F_k</code> and <code>∑_{k=0}^{n} k^ℓ L_k</code>, rewritten through Equation (3.14),
<code>c_{ℓ,m}(0) = (-1)^m m! S(ℓ+1, m+1)</code>.</p>
<p>The article's remarks fix the reading of the stacked bracket symbols. The
right-hand side of Conjecture 1 is named there as A000557, whose recorded formula
is <code>a(n) = ∑_{k=0..n} k! Stirling2(n, k) Fibonacci(k+2)</code>; the right-hand side of
Conjecture 2, times <code>-1</code>, is named as A263968, whose recorded formula is
<code>a(n) = (-1)^{n+1} ∑_{k=0..n} k! Lucas(k+2) Stirling2(n, k)</code>. Both readings are
therefore Stirling numbers of the second kind, not binomial coefficients.</p>
