bhoria-eyyunni-santra-2026-overpartition-threshold-identity"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The overpartition threshold identity</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Bhoria, Eyyunni and Santra, <em>On the number of missing integers in partitions</em>, arXiv:2604.12557v1, Section 4, Question 3, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is there an overpartition analogue of Theorem 2.9, namely, a concise identity for the number of integers less than or equal to the largest part that appear less than k times in all overpartitions of n.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Let $\mathcal O_n$ contain every partition of $n$ together with an arbitrary subset of its distinct positive part sizes. A chosen size has its first occurrence overlined. Write $m_r(o)$ for its total multiplicity, $S(o)$ for the chosen subset, and $L(o)$ for its largest part, with $L(o)=0$ for the empty partition. Define</p>
<p>$$
U_k(n)=\sum_{o\in\mathcal O_n}#{r:1\le r\le L(o),\ m_r(o)&lt;k},
\qquad
T(n)=\sum_{o\in\mathcal O_n}#\operatorname{parts}(o),
$$</p>
<p>$$
N_j(n)=\sum_{o\in\mathcal O_n}\bigl(m_j(o)-\mathbf1_{j\in S(o)}\bigr).
$$</p>
<p>Thus $U_k(n)$ includes absent sizes, $T(n)$ includes every occurrence, and $N_j(n)$ counts actual nonoverlined occurrences. The answer is the finite identity</p>
<p>$$
\boxed{U_k(n)=T(n)-2\sum_{j=k}^{n}(-1)^{j-k}N_j(n)}
\qquad(k&gt;0,\ n\ge0).
$$</p>
<p>The equality is in the integers. Every legal marking pattern is retained, including marking every distinct size. For $k&gt;n$ the sum is empty. The case $n=0$ is a harmless extension supplied here, not a quotation from the paper. The source asks for an analogue; it does not propose the displayed formula as a conjecture.</p>
