to infinite dimensions remain open here. - Both branches are needed.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="fullwood-ma-wu-2026-spatiotemporal-born-dichotomy"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The spatiotemporal Born rule for the LvN distribution has exactly two cases</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. Fullwood, Z. Ma and Z. Wu, <em>The spatiotemporal Born rule is quasiprobabilistic</em>, arXiv:2507.16919v2; published as <em>Quasiprobabilistic nature of the spatiotemporal Born rule</em>, Phys. Rev. A 113, 032204 (2026). Section VI, final paragraph (<code>PRA_rev.tex</code>, line 577), asks the following. The source macro <code>\E</code> denotes $\mathcal E$.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is then natural to ask whether $\rho$ being maximally mixed or $\E$ being a discard-and-prepare channel is also a necessary condition for the existence of the spatiotemporal Born rule for the LvN distribution. While it is straightforward to show that this is indeed the case when $A$ consists of a single qubit, for systems of arbitrary dimension we leave it as an open question.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21996">#21996</a> fixes the statement for all positive finite input and output dimensions. For every $d,e\ge1$, every density matrix $\rho\in M_d(\mathbb C)$ and every CPTP complex linear map $\mathcal E:M_d(\mathbb C)\to M_e(\mathbb C)$,</p>
<p>$$
\begin{aligned}
&amp;\exists W\in M_{de}(\mathbb C);\forall P,Q\text{ orthogonal projections},\quad
\operatorname{tr}[W(P\otimes Q)]
=\operatorname{tr}[\mathcal E(P\rho P)Q] \
&amp;\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\rho=I_d/d\quad\text{or}\quad
\exists\sigma\in M_e(\mathbb C),\quad
\sigma\succeq0,\quad\operatorname{tr}\sigma=1,\quad
\forall X\in M_d(\mathbb C),\quad\mathcal E(X)=\operatorname{tr}(X)\sigma.
\end{aligned}
$$</p>
<p>Here $P$ acts on the input and $Q$ on the output; an orthogonal projection is a self-adjoint idempotent. A density matrix is positive semidefinite with trace one. Complete positivity means positivity of every finite amplification, and trace preservation is required on every matrix. The same $W$ must work for all projections, without a positivity, Hermiticity or normalization assumption on $W$. This equivalence is proved.</p>
<p>The source defines the LvN distribution by $\mathbf P(i,j)=\operatorname{tr}[\mathcal E(P_i\rho P_i)Q_j]$ in Section II (<code>PRA_rev.tex</code>, lines 247–250), and its universal Born representation in Section VI, lines 534–537. The <a href="../Library/Observer/fullwoodmawu2026spatiotemporalborn.md">literature note</a> supplies the corresponding source locators.</p>
