arXiv:2610.14373v1, Section 4.1, page 14, Conjecture 1(iii), states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For a tree T, a word of length 2|E(T)| in the edges of T using every edge has product 1 if and only if every edge occurs exactly twice and, for every vertex x of T, the subword formed by the edges incident to x is a non-crossing double occurrence word.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Each edge acts by the transposition of its endpoints. Multiplication uses
$(\sigma\rho)(v)=\sigma(\rho(v))$. Alternating occurrences of distinct letters
are crossings; nested pairs are allowed. The one-vertex tree and empty word
are included.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Fidelity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Every finite tree | <code>n : ℕ</code>, <code>T : SimpleGraph (Fin n)</code>, <code>T.IsTree</code> in <code>claim</code> | Relabeling of $[n]$; no bound on $n$ |
| A word in the edges | <code>w : List (Sym2 (Fin n))</code>; every member belongs to <code>T.edgeFinset</code> | No nonedge letters |
| Length twice the edge count | <code>w.length = 2 * T.edgeFinset.card</code> | Exact equality |
| Uses every edge | Every member of <code>T.edgeFinset</code> belongs to <code>w</code> | Onto edge support |
| Product is identity | <code>(w.map edgeSwap).prod = 1</code> | Existing endpoint transposition; literal list product |
| Every edge occurs twice | <code>w.count a = 2</code> for each tree edge | Exact multiplicity |
| Every vertex's incident subword is noncrossing | <code>NoncrossingDoubleOccurrence</code> of the order-preserving membership filter | Existing <code>Crossing</code> predicate on the position pairs |
| If and only if | Both directions of <code>result : claim</code> | No dropped clause |</p>
<p><code>NoncrossingDoubleOccurrence</code> displays the source convention literally:
every occurring letter has two copies and distinct letters have no crossing
position pairs. <code>claim</code> retains the preregistered body. The proofs reuse
<code>SupportAndEdgeWordReflection.edgeSwap</code> and
<code>NoncrossingNonnestingGraphRecurrence.Crossing</code>.</p>
