209723-edge-differences"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Hardin–Barker five-column clockwise-edge-difference formulas</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>R. H. Hardin's OEIS A209723, revision 11, NAME states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>1/4 the number of (n+1) X 5 0..2 arrays with every 2 X 2 subblock having distinct clockwise edge differences.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The FORMULA field states the empirical recurrence and Colin Barker's conjectures:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Empirical: a(n) = a(n-1) + 2*a(n-2) - 2*a(n-3).</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>G.f.: x*(6 + x - 11<em>x^2) / ((1 - x)</em>(1 - 2*x^2)).</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>a(n) = 3*2^(n/2 - 1) + 4 for n even.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>a(n) = 2^((n + 1)/2) + 4 for n odd.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The offset is $1,1$. Entries are labelled; no symmetry quotient is taken. For a square with top row $a,b$ and bottom row $c,d$, the differences are the signed integers $b-a,d-b,c-d,a-c$. Let $a(n)$ be one quarter of the number of valid $(n+1)$-by-$5$ arrays in the rationals.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Domain and fidelity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Distinct clockwise differences | <code>DistinctClockwise</code> | Four integer differences, pairwise unequal; entries have type <code>Fin 3</code>. |
| Every consecutive square | <code>IsSourceArray</code> | Every <code>Fin n</code> row gap and <code>Fin 4</code> column gap is tested. |
| One quarter of the array count | <code>a</code> | <code>Nat.card</code> of the entire labelled-array subtype, coerced to rationals and divided by four. |
| Positive even formula | First conjunct of <code>claim</code> | $a(2(m+1))=3\cdot2^m+4$ for every natural $m$. |
| Positive odd formula | Second conjunct of <code>claim</code> | $a(2m+1)=2^{m+1}+4$ for every natural $m$. |
| Recurrence | Third conjunct of <code>claim</code> | $a(m+4)=a(m+3)+2a(m+2)-2a(m+1)$ for every natural $m$. |
