ctured g.f.: x(4 - 19x + 33x^2 - 40x^3 + 8x^4)/(1 - 10x + 29x^2 - 32x^3 + 8x^4). this is a named whole-family enumeration problem. the full original generating identity for all labelled mutual-visibility subsets of the andrasfai graphs. original-host shortest paths reduce to common-neighbor obstructions.  released-dossier ff428468bc57d85b7ea2461086b37c14c8a9634158af843c97594c5bb20cb859 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Howroyd&#x27;s full Andrasfai mutual-visibility generating function</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The full original generating identity for all labelled mutual-visibility subsets of the Andrasfai graphs.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Conjectured g.f.: x(4 - 19x + 33x^2 - 40x^3 + 8x^4)/(1 - 10x + 29x^2 - 32x^3 + 8x^4).</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>This is a named whole-family enumeration problem.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Original-host shortest paths reduce to common-neighbor obstructions.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The full original generating identity for all labelled mutual-visibility subsets of the Andrasfai graphs.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a391632-andrasfai-mutual-visibility"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Howroyd&#x27;s full Andrasfai mutual-visibility generating function</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A391632, revision 27, attributes this assertion to Andrew Howroyd:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjectured g.f.: x*(4 - 19<em>x + 33</em>x^2 - 40<em>x^3 + 8</em>x^4)/(1 - 10<em>x + 29</em>x^2 - 32<em>x^3 + 8</em>x^4).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The entry's name is &quot;Number of mutual-visibility sets in the n-Andrásfai
graph&quot; and its offset is 1,1. For every integer $n\ge1$, let $G_n$ be the
simple undirected Cayley graph on the labelled vertices
$\mathbb Z/(3n-1)\mathbb Z$, with connection set
${1,4,\ldots,3n-2}$. A subset $S$ is mutual-visible when every distinct
pair $u,v\in S$ admits a shortest $u$-$v$ path in the original host $G_n$
whose internal vertices avoid $S$. Different pairs may choose different
paths. Let $a(n)$ count every such labelled subset, including the empty set
and singletons. The entire question is the identity in $\mathbb Q[[X]]$</p>
<p>$$
\sum_{n\ge1}a(n)X^n
=\frac{X(4-19X+33X^2-40X^3+8X^4)}{1-10X+29X^2-32X^3+8X^4}.
$$</p>
<p>The series has constant coefficient zero; no graph of index zero is
introduced. There is no quotient by graph symmetries, maximality,
independence or induced-host restriction. The quotient denotes
multiplication by the formal inverse of a series with constant coefficient
one, without an analytic convergence premise.</p>
<h3>Statement correspondence</h3>
<p>Put $m=n-1$ and $N=3m+2=3n-1$. <code>Andrasfai.host m</code> uses <code>ZMod N</code>, with
adjacency determined by the canonical representative of $v-u$ being one
modulo three. <code>Andrasfai.count m</code> is the cardinality of the original
<code>Configuration (host m)</code> subtype of actual finite vertex subsets.
<code>AndrasfaiActual.generatingSeries</code> is defined directly as
$X\operatorname{mk}(m\mapsto(\operatorname{count}(m):\mathbb Q))$.
<code>AndrasfaiActual.result</code> has no hypotheses and states the displayed
identity for that series.</p>
