k one.  released-dossier 74e7f4b604078de126869351075f5fc5a8ebb7a44e46b91f5dd068e20497ba34 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Geometry &amp; algebra</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2004</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Logarithmic scalar curvature is strictly Schur-increasing</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Strict Schur increase of logarithmic simplex scalar curvature.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Gibilisco and Isola, On the monotonicity of scalar curvature in classical and quantum information geometry, arXiv:math-ph/0407007v2, Conjecture 4.1, printed page 11:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The endpoint gives an intrinsic curvature quantity that increases under mixing for every positive normalized vector of dimension at least three.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The coordinate logarithmic metric is diagonal plus rank one.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Strict Schur increase of logarithmic simplex scalar curvature.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="gibilisco-isola-2004-log-geometry-scalar-curvature"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Logarithmic scalar curvature is strictly Schur-increasing</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Gibilisco and Isola, <em>On the monotonicity of scalar curvature in classical and quantum information geometry</em>, arXiv:math-ph/0407007v2, Conjecture 4.1, printed page 11:</p>
