<p>Suppose $n&gt;2$. For the function $\mathrm{Scal}_p(\cdot):\mathcal P^1_n\to\mathbb R$ one has the following properties: if $p\in(1,2)$ then $\mathrm{Scal}_p$ is strictly Schur-decreasing; if $p\in(2,+\infty]$ then $\mathrm{Scal}_p$ is strictly Schur-increasing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This settlement proves the $p=\infty$ endpoint only. The positive simplex is $\mathcal P^1_n={x\in\mathbb R^n: x_i&gt;0,\ \sum_i x_i=1}$. The source says $x$ is more mixed than $y$ when the decreasingly sorted initial sums of $x$ are at most those of $y$, with equal total sums. Strict Schur increase means weak curvature increase for all such pairs and strict increase unless $x$ is a permutation of $y$.</p>
