esult-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2013</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The alternating vortex polygon has exactly $N/2-2$ distinct real eigenvalue pairs</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The alternating unit polygon has exactly m minus two distinct pairs of nonzero real eigenvalues.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Kevrekidis, Few-particle vortex cluster equilibria in Bose–Einstein condensates: existence and stability, J.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The conjecture specifies how many distinct real eigenvalue pairs occur as the alternating vortex polygon grows.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The alternating unit polygon has exactly m minus two distinct pairs of nonzero real eigenvalues.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="barry-kevrekidis-2013-alternating-vortex-polygon-spectrum"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The alternating vortex polygon has exactly $N/2-2$ distinct real eigenvalue pairs</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. M. Barry and P. G. Kevrekidis, <em>Few-particle vortex cluster equilibria in Bose–Einstein condensates: existence and stability</em>, J. Phys. A: Math. Theor. 46, 445001 (2013), arXiv:1306.1545v2, Section 4.1, Conjecture 4.4 (<code>BEC_VFP6.tex</code>, lines 630–631):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For $N\geq 6$, the $N$-gon has $\frac{N}{2}-2$ distinct pairs of real eigenvalues.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The setting is the precessing point-vortex system of Section 2, Eqs. (1)–(2), with $b=2$:</p>
<p>$$\dot x_j=-S_j\Omega y_j-\sum_{k\ne j}S_k\frac{y_j-y_k}{(x_j-x_k)^2+(y_j-y_k)^2},\qquad
\dot y_j=S_j\Omega x_j+\sum_{k\ne j}S_k\frac{x_j-x_k}{(x_j-x_k)^2+(y_j-y_k)^2}.$$</p>
<p>Section 4.1 specifies the charges:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We will assume hereafter that the vortices are of alternating charge, namely $S_j=(-1)^{j+1}$, $j=1,2, \dots,N$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is <code>BEC_VFP6.tex</code>, lines 506–507. The polygon has vertices $z_j=e^{2\pi i j/N}$, $1\le j\le N$ (line 515); the even polygon is an equilibrium at $\Omega=1/2$ (Lemma 4.2, lines 524–532). Thus the conjecture concerns even $N\ge6$ in this equilibrium family.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26188">#26188</a> fixes the reading. For every natural $m\ge3$, let $J_{2m}$ be the real Cartesian Jacobian of this field at the unit regular $2m$-gon. Every real root of its characteristic polynomial is accompanied by its negative, and the set of nonzero real roots has exactly $2(m-2)$ elements. Pairs count distinct nonzero values, without algebraic multiplicity. This statement is proved.</p>
<p>The source and encoding are described in <a href="../Library/FluidDynamics/barrykevrekidis2013vortex.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
