his question.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Gudder (2019), arXiv:1912.05110v1, Section 5, line 433, asks about pairs of individually incomplete observables:</p><p><span>The idea</span>No probability hypothesis is needed for this lemma.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Two finite real random variables determine every probability weight exactly when their joint value map is injective and their bipartite fiber graph is a forest.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Characterizations in those settings and quantitative recovery from approximate distributions remain open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="gudder-2019-informationally-complete-pairs"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Informationally complete pairs are exactly complementary pairs with forest fiber graphs</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Gudder, <em>Finite-Dimensional Convex Effect Algebras</em> (2019), arXiv:1912.05110v1, Section 5, ends with this question. The following is the verbatim TeX text at line 588 of <code>finitedimensionalCEA.tex</code> (md5 <code>9dfe03bd484881c9db3b8b737095b005</code>):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We conclude from Example~5 that complementarity is a necessary but not a sufficient condition for IC. It is an open problem to find a simple characterization of IC for a pair $\brac{f,g}\subseteq\rscript (X)$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source macros denote ${f,g}$ and $\mathcal R(X)$, respectively. Here $X={1,\ldots,n}$ with $n\ge2$, $\mathcal R(X)$ consists of all maps $X\to\mathbb R$, and a probability weight $\mu$ has $\mu(x)\ge0$ and $\sum_x\mu(x)=1$. Its distribution under $f$ is $\Phi_{f,\mu}(\Delta)=\sum_{x:,f(x)\in\Delta}\mu(x)$ for every $\Delta\subseteq\mathbb R$ (TeX lines 435–439). The pair is informationally complete (IC) when, for every two probability weights $\mu,\nu$, equality of their distributions under both $f$ and $g$ implies $\mu=\nu$ (line 474).</p>
<p>Let $\Gamma(f,g)$ have vertices in two tagged copies of $\mathbb R$, with a left value $a$ adjacent to a right value $b$ exactly when some $x\in X$ satisfies $f(x)=a$ and $g(x)=b$. Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23501">#23501</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<p>${f,g}$ is IC if and only if $x\mapsto(f(x),g(x))$ is injective and $\Gamma(f,g)$ is acyclic.</p>
<p>Injectivity is precisely the source's complementarity condition: every nonempty intersection of an $f$-level set and a $g$-level set is a singleton (lines 507–508). The characterization is proved, for every finite domain, without the restriction $n\ge2$. The definitions and source locators are also recorded in <a href="../Library/Observer/gudder2019finiteconvexeffect.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
