s MIC channel forces SIC existence</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. B. DeBrota and B. C. Stacey, <em>Lüders channels and the existence of symmetric-informationally-complete measurements</em>, Phys. Rev. A 100, 062327 (2019), arXiv:1907.10999v2, Section III, “Entropic Optimality”, Conjecture 1 (<code>lmc.tex</code>, lines 417–420):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Equality is achievable in the statement of Theorem
\ref{avgentropybnd} only if a SIC exists in dimension $d$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The reference is to Theorem 2 (lines 385–390): every Lüders MIC channel has Haar-averaged pure-input output entropy at most
$B_d=\log(d+1)-\frac{2}{d+1}\log2$, with equality achievable if a SIC exists in dimension $d$.</p>
<p>A MIC is a family $E=(E_i)_{i=1}^{d^2}$ of positive semidefinite complex $d\times d$ matrices, linearly independent over $\mathbb C$, with $\sum_iE_i=I$. Its Lüders channel is
$\mathcal E_E(X)=\sum_i\sqrt{E_i},X\sqrt{E_i}$, using the positive square roots. A SIC is a rank-one equiangular MIC. These are the definitions in the source, lines 80–90 and 104–114, also recorded in <a href="../Library/Entropy/debrotastacey2019luders.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25617">#25617</a> fixes the statement for every positive natural dimension $d$: if a MIC $E$ has Haar-averaged pure-input output entropy $B_d$, then there exists a SIC in dimension $d$. This is the proposition <code>claim</code>, proved by <code>result</code>.</p>
