</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The distinct upper degrees of deterministic simplicial complexes shift by dimension</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The distinct upper degrees in positive dimension are zero together with the vertex degrees at the dimension-shifted generation.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Krapivsky, Deterministic simplicial complexes, arXiv:2507.07402v1; J.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source studies local structure through upper degrees, alongside simplex counts and Hodge Laplacian spectra.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Upper degree is the number of singleton members of the link.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The distinct upper degrees in positive dimension are zero together with the vertex degrees at the dimension-shifted generation.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="dorogovtsev-krapivsky-2025-deterministic-simplicial-degree-shift"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The distinct upper degrees of deterministic simplicial complexes shift by dimension</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. N. Dorogovtsev and P. L. Krapivsky, <em>Deterministic simplicial complexes</em>, arXiv:2507.07402v1; J. Stat. Mech. (2025) 123404, Section II.3, equation (34). The source TeX <code>DSC-7.tex</code>, lines 505–510, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Another useful general relation</p>
<p>$K^{(d)}(n) = 0 \cup K^{(0)}(n-d)$</p>
<p>is conjecturally valid for all $n \geq d + 1$ and $d \geq 1$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $K^{(d)}(n)$ lists the distinct upper degrees in ascending order, without multiplicities (TeX lines 415–425); the $0$ on the right denotes adjoining the value zero. The growth rule and upper degrees are also described in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/dorogovtsevkrapivsky2025deterministic.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20715">#20715</a> fixes the precise reading. Let $V$ be a type with decidable equality, let $v_0\in V$, and let $v:\mathbb N\to\operatorname{Finset}(V)\to V$ be injective as a function of the pair $(s,\sigma)$, with $v(s,\sigma)\ne v_0$ for every $s,\sigma$. Define the finite families of nonempty faces by</p>
<p>$C_0={{v_0}}$,</p>
<p>$C_{s+1}=C_s\cup\bigcup_{\sigma\in C_s}{{v(s+1,\sigma)}\cup\beta:\beta\subseteq\sigma}$.</p>
<p>Every existing nonempty face receives its own fresh vertex, all subsets $\beta$ including the empty subset are used, and earlier faces persist. The labeling hypotheses ensure that a new vertex differs from every earlier vertex and from the other vertices added in the same step.</p>
<p>For a finite family $C$ and a finite vertex set $\tau$, define</p>
<p>$\operatorname{upperDegree}(C,\tau)=#{\rho\in C:\tau\subseteq\rho,\ |\rho|=|\tau|+1}$,</p>
<p>$\operatorname{degreeSet}(C,d)={k\in\mathbb N:\exists\tau\in C,\ |\tau|=d+1,\ \operatorname{upperDegree}(C,\tau)=k}$.</p>
<p>Write $K^d(n)=\operatorname{degreeSet}(C_n,d)$. The claim is that, for every such $V,v_0,v$ and all natural numbers $d,n$ with $d\geq1$ and $n\geq d+1$,</p>
<p>$K^d(n)={0}\cup K^0(n-d)$.</p>
<p>Subtraction is natural subtraction; the hypotheses imply $n-d\geq1$. Equality of these sets expresses equality of the source's lists of distinct values. This claim is proved.</p>
