<span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Perfect homogeneous Wronskian extensions exist exactly at order one</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A perfect homogeneous one-generator polynomial extension exists exactly at differential order one.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>The following is the verbatim TeX paragraph at line 1259 of FinDim220526v2.tex (md5 777135f176c9db701f21e5554216fb33):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source&#x27;s research problem concerns perfect finite-dimensional polynomial algebras with the complete Wronskian as bracket (TeX lines 525–527).</p><p><span>The idea</span>Repetitions give equal columns and zero determinant; injective selections differ from one omission bracket by a column-permutation sign.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A perfect homogeneous one-generator polynomial extension exists exactly at differential order one.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="kenins-kiselev-2026-wronskian-perfect-lonely-algebra"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Perfect homogeneous Wronskian extensions exist exactly at order one</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Markuss G. Ķēniņš and Arthemy V. Kiselev, <em>Explicit class of finite-dimensional polynomial algebras with Wronskians over $\mathbb R^d$ as $N$-ary Lie brackets: beyond $\mathfrak{sl}(2)$</em>, arXiv:2605.27305v2 (2026), subsection <code>(Im)perfect N-ary SH-Lie polynomial algebras</code>, Open problem <code>open:perfect-algebras</code>. The following is the verbatim TeX paragraph at line 1259 of <code>FinDim220526v2.tex</code> (md5 <code>777135f176c9db701f21e5554216fb33</code>):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let the base dimension $d\geqslant 2$ and differential order $k\geqslant 1$ be arbitrary. Put the complete generalised Wronskian $W_d^k=\Bone\wedge \partial_\bx\wedges \partial^k_{\bx...\bx}$ as the $N$\nobreakdash-ary bracket, $N={d+k\choose d}$, on the $N$\nobreakdash-ary SH-Lie polynomial algebra $\Bbbk[x^1,...,x^d]$. Does there exist an homogeneous polynomial $p(\bx)\in \Bbbk[\bx]$ of degree $k+1$ such that the $N$\nobreakdash-ary SH-Lie polynomial subalgebra $\EuA=\Bbbk_k[\bx]\oplus\langle p(\bx)\rangle$ is \emph{perfect} w.r.t\ the Wronskian, i.e.\ $\Span_\Bbbk W_d^k(\EuA,...,\EuA)=\EuA$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source macros are retained in the quotation: <code>\Bone</code> is the identity operator, <code>\bx</code> denotes $\boldsymbol x$, <code>\wedges</code> denotes $\wedge\cdots\wedge$, <code>\EuA</code> denotes $\mathcal A$, and <code>\Span</code> denotes linear span. The source takes $\Bbbk=\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$ and writes $\Bbbk_k[\boldsymbol x]$ for polynomials of degree at most $k$ (TeX lines 538–542 and 613–623).</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22115">#22115</a> fixes the statement over a characteristic-zero field $K$. Put</p>
<p>$I={\alpha\in\mathbb N^d:|\alpha|\le k},\qquad W(f)=\det(\partial^\alpha f_\beta)_{\alpha,\beta\in I},$</p>
<p>$A_p=K_{\le k}[\boldsymbol x]+Kp,\qquad D_p=\operatorname{span}_K{W(f):f:I\to A_p}.$</p>
<p>Here $\partial^\alpha$ is ordinary iterated partial differentiation, without divided-power normalization. The settled statement is: for every characteristic-zero field $K$, every $d\ge2$ and every $k\ge1$,</p>
<p>$\bigl(\exists p\in K[\boldsymbol x],\ p\ne0,\ p\text{ homogeneous of degree }k+1,\ D_p=A_p\bigr)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ k=1.$</p>
<p>This classification is proved.</p>
