span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The simple evolution-algebra automorphism maximum</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The full automorphism maximum for simple complex evolution algebras.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Kaygorodov, Martín González and Viruel, Evolution algebras: a survey of results and a list of open problems, arXiv:2610.22248v1, Conjecture 4, section 2.3, page 28:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Simplicity adds strong connectivity to the invertible structure map.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The full automorphism maximum for simple complex evolution algebras.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="kmvc4-simpleaut"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The simple evolution-algebra automorphism maximum</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Kaygorodov, Martín González and Viruel, <em>Evolution algebras: a survey of
results and a list of open problems</em>, arXiv:2610.22248v1, Conjecture 4,
section 2.3, page 28:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Over ℂ, the maximal order of the automorphism group of a simple
n-dimensional evolution algebra is max{n!, n(2ⁿ−1)}.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The dimension is positive. The source's natural-basis condition is zero
mixed products. The product is complex bilinear and commutative, without
associativity or a multiplicative unit. Automorphisms are the entire group
of complex linear multiplication-preserving equivalences.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart |
| --- | --- |
| Every positive dimension | <code>claim</code> quantifies over $n\in\mathbb N$ with $1\le n$ |
| Every complex vector space of dimension $n$ | Arbitrary carrier, complex module, finite-dimensional instance and finrank equality |
| Evolution product and natural basis | Bilinear product, arbitrary finite basis, zero mixed products and commutativity |
| Simple algebra | <code>IsSimple</code>: $A^2\ne0$ and every complex-linear two-sided ideal is zero or the whole space |
| Full finite automorphism group and upper bound | <code>Finite (Aut μ)</code> together with the bound on <code>Nat.card</code> |
| Maximum is attained | A product on $\mathbb C^n$ with the dimension, commutativity, natural basis, simplicity, finiteness and exact cardinality |</p>
<p>The simplicity definition is literal: “We recall that an algebra A is
simple if A² ≠ 0 and 0 is the only proper ideal.” This sentence is from
the survey's cited arXiv:1601.01832v2, section 4, page 18. All two-sided
complex-linear ideals are quantified, including those not spanned by
natural-basis vectors. The coordinate representative in the attainment
clause does not restrict the universal clause. <code>Finite</code> prevents the
natural cardinality of an infinite group from satisfying a bound through
