classes of subgroups of a finite group</em>, arXiv:2508.14267v2, Section 4,
Problem 4.1:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let a,b be integers such that $1\leq a&lt;b$. In Section 2 we proved that
any ratio of the form $\frac{a}{a+1}$ can be achieved by computing $d'$
for specific non-nilpotent supersolvable groups having one conjugacy
class of non-normal subgroups. We also showed that each ratio of the
form $\frac{a}{b}$ can be achieved as the limit of $(d'(G_n))<em>{n\geq1}$
where $(G_n)</em>{n\geq1}$ is a sequence of nilpotent groups formed as
direct products of some modular p-groups. Hence, we pose the following
question: Is it true that there is a group G such that
$d'(G)=\frac{a}{b}$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section 1 specifies that all groups are finite, defines $L(G)$ as the
subgroup lattice and $k'(G)$ as the number of conjugacy classes of
subgroups, and sets $d'(G)=k'(G)/|L(G)|$. Bottom and top belong to $L(G)$.
Conjugacy means inner conjugation by elements of $G$.</p>
<p>The exact settling statement is</p>
<pre><code class="language-lean">∀ a b : ℤ, 1 ≤ a → a &lt; b → ∃ W : FiniteGroup,
  b * (classCount W.Carrier : ℤ) = a * (latticeCount W.Carrier : ℤ)
</code></pre>
