A004123 totient-period conjecture</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Weighted Stirling transforms have totient periods, proving A064618 and A004123.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Number of generalized weak orders on n points.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>This is a first-tier OEIS conjecture recorded in 2022.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Its live proof path has three steps. Twindow collapses the sum to the fixed range k &lt; m: terms with k ≥ m vanish because m ∣ k!, and terms with k &gt; n vanish because S(n,k) = 0. Texponentialsum rewrites that window by inclusion-exclusion as a fixed integer combination of jⁿ. powaddtotient supplies the prime-power split: for each prime power q = p^e dividing m, a unit base uses Euler&#x27;s theorem together with φ(q) ∣ φ(m); a non-unit base has both powers vanishing once the exponent reaches e.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Weighted Stirling transforms have totient periods, proving A064618 and A004123.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a004123-generalized-weak-orders-totient-period"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>A004123 totient-period conjecture</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A004123 NAME:</p>
