ult-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Conjecture proved</span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>A064618 totient-period conjecture</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Weighted Stirling transforms have totient periods, proving A064618 and A004123.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Conjecture: for fixed k = 1,2,..., the sequence a(n) (mod k) is eventually periodic with the exact period dividing phi(k), where phi(k) is the Euler totient function A000010.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>This is a first-tier OEIS conjecture recorded in 2018.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Its live proof path has three steps. Twindow collapses the sum to the fixed range k &lt; m: terms with k ≥ m vanish because m ∣ k!, and terms with k &gt; n vanish because S(n,k) = 0. Texponentialsum rewrites that window by inclusion-exclusion as a fixed integer combination of jⁿ. powaddtotient supplies the prime-power split: for each prime power q = p^e dividing m, a unit base uses Euler&#x27;s theorem together with φ(q) ∣ φ(m); a non-unit base has both powers vanishing once the exponent reaches e.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Weighted Stirling transforms have totient periods, proving A064618 and A004123.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a064618-stirling-transform-totient-period"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>A064618 totient-period conjecture</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A064618 NAME:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Stirling transform of (n!)^2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>OEIS A064618 COMMENT (Peter Bala, Jan 15 2018):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture: for fixed k = 1,2,..., the sequence a(n) (mod k) is eventually periodic with the exact period dividing phi(k), where phi(k) is the Euler totient function A000010.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The finite formula for this entry is <code>a(n) = T (fun k =&gt; (k.factorial : ℤ)) n</code>; its proved onset is <code>n ≥ m</code>
for every positive modulus <code>m</code>. The entry has offset 0.</p>
<p>The companion entry is recorded separately with its own Library note and dossier:</p>
