">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Orders k of Hermite polynomials whose maximal coefficient in absolute value appears twice.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The original forecasts and clock remain those immutable source records.</p><p><span>The idea</span>HermiteAbsoluteTieOrders.result supplies positivity, strict increase and exhaustive unique positive indexing for the original predicate, with</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The fifth-lag recurrence for the complete increasing enumeration of physicists Hermite absolute-tie orders.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a381737-hermite-tie-recurrence"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>The complete A381737 Conjecture 2 recurrence</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A381737 revision 28, by Mike Sheppard (2025), names the sequence:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Orders k of Hermite polynomials whose maximal coefficient in absolute value appears twice.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Its Formula field states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 2: a(n) = a(n-1) + 2*a(n-2) - 2*a(n-3) - a(n-4) + a(n-5).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The offset is 1,1. Let $H_k$ be the physicists Hermite polynomial represented
by <code>HermiteSignedMode.physicistsHermite</code> over the rationals. For every
natural order $k$ and exponent $j$, put</p>
<p>$$
M(k,j)\iff j\le k\ \land\ \forall i\le k,\quad
|[x^i]H_k|\le |[x^j]H_k|.
$$</p>
<p>Let $S$ consist of exactly the natural orders $k$ for which there are
natural exponents $u&lt;v$ such that, for every natural $j$, $M(k,j)$ holds
if and only if $j=u$ or $j=v$. Thus exactly two distinct exponents attain
the absolute maximum of the entire row $0,\ldots,k$, including parity-zero
coefficients.</p>
<p>The complete assertion is that there exists $g:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb N$
satisfying all of the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>For every integer $n\ge1$, $g(n)&gt;0$.</li>
<li>For all integers $1\le n&lt;m$, $g(n)&lt;g(m)$.</li>
<li>For every natural order $k$, including zero, $k\in S$ if and only if
there is exactly one integer $n\ge1$ with $k=g(n)$.</li>
<li>For every integer $n\ge6$,</li>
</ul>
<p>$$
(g(n):\mathbb Z)=(g(n-1):\mathbb Z)+2(g(n-2):\mathbb Z)
-2(g(n-3):\mathbb Z)-(g(n-4):\mathbb Z)+(g(n-5):\mathbb Z).
$$</p>
<p>Every sequence value is cast separately to the integers before subtraction.
The positive-index restriction of $g$ is the actual increasing exhaustive
one-based enumeration of $S$. Its values at nonpositive indices impose no
restriction on this event. There is no finite cutoff, eventual qualification,
selected subfamily, signed maximum, probabilists normalization or
recurrence-defined replacement sequence.</p>
