pan></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Optimality of the Ordered Zeckendorf Long Game Strategy</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Complete ordered Long Game Strategy optimality with every permitted switch choice.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>A state is an ordered list of Fibonacci numbers.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>- Erasing order maps a game state to RawDigits.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The weighted split-only subproblem is now proved in SplitStabilization.splitprefixpromotion, splitphasepromotion, and greedysplitoptimality.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Complete ordered Long Game Strategy optimality with every permitted switch choice.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="ordered-zeckendorf-long-game-strategy"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Optimality of the Ordered Zeckendorf Long Game Strategy</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A state is an ordered list of Fibonacci numbers. The game starts with <code>n</code> copies
of <code>F_1</code>. Legal adjacent moves are <code>(F_i, F_{i+1}) -&gt; F_{i+2}</code>;
<code>(F_1, F_1) -&gt; F_2</code>; <code>(F_i, F_i) -&gt; (F_{i-2}, F_{i+1})</code> for <code>i &gt; 2</code>;
