when <code>i &gt; j</code>.</p>
<p>The Long Game Strategy uses the priority: all switch moves in any order, combine
adjacent ones from the left, split from the right, then merge from the left.</p>
<p>Conjecture 1.7, quoted from arXiv:2508.20222v2:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“The LGS has the longest game length.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Status statement, quoted from the same version:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“The longest-game strategy described in Conjecture 1.7 is supported by
empirical simulations. However, a rigorous proof establishing its optimality
remains an open problem.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Formal statement, with the five moves represented exactly:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">∀ n &gt; 0, ∃ g, CompleteLGSRun n g
∀ n &gt; 0, ∀ g, CompleteLGSRun n g -&gt;
  ∀ h, LegalTerminalRun n h -&gt; h.length ≤ g.length
</code></pre>
<p>Every permitted switch ordering is retained in the proved universal comparison.
Priorities restart after every move, including a switch.</p>
<p>The paper says the conjecture is backed by exhaustive simulations rather than a
proof. In its broader exact game-tree analysis it also states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“Due to the combinatorial explosion in the number of game states and legal
move sequences, a full resolution of the winner for <span class="math-inline">n&gt;25</span> was
computationally infeasible.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That second quote concerns winner computation, not directly Conjecture 1.7, but
it identifies the same state-space obstacle. For length, the paper proves only
upper and lower asymptotics and a structural lemma about repetitions under LGS.</p>
