<p>The all-CPTP clause is refuted. The unitary clause and the source's two-qutrit unitary conjecture remain open. The statement and source scope are fixed in <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/17047">#17047</a>; the source quotations are in the <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/campbell2010optimalentangling.md">Campbell note</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Every bipartite CPTP map | <code>claim</code> quantifies local dimensions and finite Kraus families with completeness | Finite-dimensional square input/output spaces; the Choi–Kraus representation supplies this interpretation. |
| Free local ancillary systems | <code>extend</code>, and the suprema in <code>capSep</code> and <code>capAssisted</code> | Every pair of natural-number ancillary dimensions; the cut is $AA':BB'$. |
| Eq. (1), separable inputs | <code>capSep</code> | Density matrices that are finite convex mixtures of products of density matrices. |
| Eq. (2), increase of entanglement | <code>capAssisted</code> | All density inputs, subtracting the input's own log-negativity. |
| Eqs. (4)–(5), logarithmic rescaling | <code>Real.logb 2</code> of <code>traceNorm (partialTranspose ...)</code> | Base two; the partial transpose acts on $BB'$. |
| Resource independence | Equality of the two capacities in <code>claim</code> | Suprema are in <code>EReal</code>, preserving unbounded suprema. |
| A negative answer to the all-CPTP clause | <code>result</code> | A fixed two-qutrit channel with 18 Kraus operators and $t=1$. |</p>
<p>The generalized <code>IptPptSquaredEquivalence.IsSeparable</code> retains the finite-convex-mixture condition. Its local factors are positive semidefinite with trace one, the body of the reused <code>IsDensity</code> predicate. The auxiliary density condition in <code>capSep</code> is consistent with those mixtures; it does not exclude a source separable state.</p>
