>The question</span>Matsushita, Optimized Synthesis of Circuits for Diagonal Unitary Matrices with Reflection Symmetry, J.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Huang, Kosugi, Nishi and Matsushita (2024, arXiv:2310.06676v2) use reflection symmetry to reduce circuit resources for diagonal unitaries occurring in first-quantized Hamiltonian simulation.</p><p><span>The idea</span>2. Circuit normal form and return. Wire labels start at the identity rows, and a CNOT replaces its target label by target plus control.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every positive even number of wires admits a reflection-symmetric diagonal unitary forcing the exponential CNOT lower bound.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The optimal depth question in the source&#x27;s Conclusion (lines 847–853) remains open here: a gate-count lower bound does not determine how many disjoint gates can run simultaneously.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="huang-kosugi-nishi-matsushita-2024-reflection-diagonal-cnot-lower-bound"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The worst-case CNOT count for reflection-symmetric diagonal unitaries</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>X. Huang, T. Kosugi, H. Nishi and Y.-i. Matsushita, <em>Optimized Synthesis of Circuits for Diagonal Unitary Matrices with Reflection Symmetry</em>, J. Phys. Soc. Jpn. 93, 054002 (2024), arXiv:2310.06676v2, state in Sect. 6, Conclusion (<code>main_arXiv2.tex</code>, line 847):</p>
