tion of the problem, the completeness of polynomial invariants, is answered in the source by the Onishchik–Vinberg theorem.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>An affine family of qubit–qutrit density operators excludes a single polynomial inequality characterizing separability, even when the polynomial may depend on the local dimensions.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="kruger-werner-2005-single-polynomial-separability-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>No single polynomial inequality characterizes separability in every dimension</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>O. Krüger and R. F. Werner, <em>Some Open Problems in Quantum Information Theory</em>, arXiv:quant-ph/0504166v1, Problem 3, &quot;Polynomial entanglement invariants&quot;, the third closely related problem:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Decide whether the set of separable states can be described in terms of a polynomial invariant $f$, such that $f(\rho)\ge 0$ is equivalent to separability. There are many weaker versions of this statement, which may be of interest. For example, we might merely ask for a sufficient or a necessary separability criterion, and we might allow $f$ to depend on the dimensions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here a polynomial invariant is a real function on bipartite density operators that is constant on local-unitary orbits $\rho\mapsto(U_A\otimes U_B)\rho(U_A\otimes U_B)^*$ and is computed by a polynomial in the matrix elements of $\rho$. The definitions are quoted in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/krugerwerner2005openproblems.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20112">#20112</a> fixes the reading: for all local dimensions $d_A,d_B\ge2$ there is a real polynomial $f$ in the real and imaginary parts of the entries, invariant under local unitaries on density operators, with $f(\rho)\ge0$ iff $\rho$ is separable for every density operator $\rho$. This dimension-dependent version, the weakest the source names for a characterization, is refuted.</p>
