pan>Why it matters</span>The paper proves the bound only for canonical states, whose Bob marginal is maximally mixed (Theorem 1). §V explains why that does not extend: CHSH nonlocality does not transform simply under the local filtering that makes a state canonical.</p><p><span>The idea</span>1. CHSH to a plane. For unit b, b′ write b ± b′ = r e₁, s e₂ with e₁ ⊥ e₂ and r² + s² = 4.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every two-qubit density matrix with steering-ellipsoid centre of length c has CHSH nonlocality at most max(2 sqrt(2(1 - c)), 2).</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="milne-jennings-jevtic-rudolph-2014-chsh-centre-bound"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>CHSH nonlocality is tightly bounded by the steering-ellipsoid centre</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Milne, D. Jennings, S. Jevtic and T. Rudolph, <em>Quantum correlations of two-qubit states with one maximally mixed marginal</em>, Phys. Rev. A <strong>90</strong>, 024302 (2014), arXiv:1404.3951v2, define the CHSH nonlocality in §II as β(ρ) = max_B |tr(ρB)|, with B = α·σ ⊗ (β + β′)·σ + α′·σ ⊗ (β − β′)·σ and the maximum over all directions α, α′, β, β′. §V, Conjecture 1:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let ρ be a general two-qubit state with E centred at c. The CHSH nonlocality is tightly bounded as β(ρ) ≤ max[2√(2(1 − c)), 2].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here E is Alice's steering ellipsoid and c = |c⃗| is the length of its centre. The verbatim statements and the centre convention are in <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/milne2014fullyentangledfraction.md">the literature note</a>. The repository settled Conjecture 2 of the same paper in <a href="milne-jennings-jevtic-rudolph-2014-fully-entangled-fraction.md">the fully-entangled-fraction dossier</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16558">#16558</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<ul>
<li>β is the supremum of |tr(ρB)| over four unit vectors of ℝ³;</li>
<li>the centre is the companion module's <code>centre</code>: (a − Tb)/(1 − |b|²) for |b| ≠ 1 and a for |b| = 1.</li>
