<blockquote>
<p>Let $f$ be a non-negative function that is operator monotone on $[0, \infty)$. Then $E_{f}(c) \leq Cf(c)$ holds for all $c &gt; 0$ when $C = 1$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source defines</p>
<p>$$
E_f(c)=\inf_{g'\in\widehat{\mathcal M}<em>+}
\left(\sup</em>{x\ge0}(f-g)(x)-\inf_{x\ge0}(f-g)(x)+cg'(0)\right),
$$</p>
<p>where $\widehat{\mathcal M}_+$ consists of Fourier transforms of finite positive Borel measures on $\mathbb R$. Section 5, equation (28), requires $g\in C^1(\mathbb R)$. The conjecture is refuted by $f(x)=x/(1+x)$ and $c=1$. Preregistration: <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/15279">#15279</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| “$g$ is $C^1(\mathbb R)$ and $g'$ is the Fourier transform of a finite positive Borel measure” | <code>IsAdmissible</code> | <code>ContDiff</code> and a finite positive measure, with the complex exponential sign convention. |
| The displayed oscillation plus $cg'(0)$ | <code>approxCost</code> | Supremum and infimum on the subtype of nonnegative reals. |
| Infimum over admissible $g$ | <code>approxError</code> | Real infimum over finite oscillation costs. |
| “a non-negative function” | The nonnegativity premise of <code>claim</code> | Every nonnegative real argument. |
| “operator monotone on $[0,\infty)$” | <code>OperatorMonotoneOn</code> | Loewner order for positive semidefinite complex matrices at every finite dimension. |
| “for all $c&gt;0$ when $C=1$” | The positive-$c$ conclusion of <code>claim</code> | The constant is exactly one. |
| Failure of the conjecture | <code>result : ¬ claim</code> | The witness satisfies all premises; the lower bound exceeds $f(1)$. |</p>
<p><code>approxError</code> is a real infimum; Lean's real empty-set infimum convention is zero. The refutation instance has a nonempty finite cost set, witnessed by $g=0$, and a uniform finite lower bound. Discarding infinite-cost candidates preserves the source infimum in this domain. No extended-real equivalence is claimed for targets without a finite-cost approximant. Matrix monotonicity at all finite dimensions is the classical Löwner formulation used here; no separate infinite-dimensional transport theorem is claimed.</p>
