umber theory a self-dual irreducible extreme thermal process can factor on proper subspaces horodecki, decomposability and convex structure of thermal processes, new j. the problem concerns whether detailed balance obstructs building an extreme thermal process from extreme operations that each leave some energy level untouched. a five-level self-dual irreducible extreme thermal process factors into two extreme processes acting on fewer levels, refuting the non-self-duality conjecture. 2. extremality. nonnegativity forces both terms of any strict convex decomposition to vanish wherever the decomposed matrix vanishes.  released-dossier 0e3005123a2a3665da9c1eb130d8d7ec49474b263b06221ca31bd7e04f4d5ae6 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Number theory</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2017</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>A self-dual irreducible extreme thermal process can factor on proper subspaces</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A five-level self-dual irreducible extreme thermal process factors into two extreme processes acting on fewer levels, refuting the non-self-duality conjecture.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Horodecki, Decomposability and convex structure of thermal processes, New J.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The problem concerns whether detailed balance obstructs building an extreme thermal process from extreme operations that each leave some energy level untouched.</p><p><span>The idea</span>2. Extremality. Nonnegativity forces both terms of any strict convex decomposition to vanish wherever the decomposed matrix vanishes.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A five-level self-dual irreducible extreme thermal process factors into two extreme processes acting on fewer levels, refuting the non-self-duality conjecture.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="mazurek-horodecki-2018-thermal-process-self-dual-factorization-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A self-dual irreducible extreme thermal process can factor on proper subspaces</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>P. Mazurek and M. Horodecki, <em>Decomposability and convex structure of thermal processes</em>, New J. Phys. 20, 053040 (2018), arXiv:1707.06869v2, Section IV.B, “Detailed balance symmetry”, Conjecture 1:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If an extremal TP $C$ for $d$ dimensional space is decomposable into a sequence of extremal TPs $A$, $B$, each acting non-trivially on at most $d-1$ dimensional space: $C=AB$, and $C$ is not a direct sum of extremal TPs from lower dimensional subspaces, then $C$ is not self-dual with respect to the conjugation associated with the operator scalar product.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This quotation is from <code>manuscript.tex</code>, lines 425–427, read from the <a href="https://arxiv.org/src/1707.06869v2">arXiv v2 source</a>, md5 <code>69e7001dd049721e95968ea93ad550c2</code>. The source's matrix characterization of thermal processes is at line 159, its discussion of proper-subspace factors at lines 208–211, and its conjugation at lines 415–420. These passages are also located in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/mazurekhorodecki2018decomposability.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20157">#20157</a> fixes the following precise reading. For every $d\ge2$ and every positive, pairwise distinct weight vector $g\in\mathbb R^d$ with $g_i\le g_0$ for every $i$, let</p>
<p>$\mathrm{TP}(g)={T\in\mathbb R^{d\times d}:T_{ij}\ge0\text{ for all }i,j,\ \sum_iT_{ij}=1\text{ for all }j,\ Tg=g}$.</p>
<p>For all extreme points $A,B,C$ of this set, suppose $C=AB$, each of $A$ and $B$ has a diagonal entry equal to one, and there is no nonempty proper set of levels whose two cross-blocks in $C$ are zero. The claim concludes that $C$ is not self-dual: $C_{ij}g_j\ne C_{ji}g_i$ for some $i,j$. The module calls these predicates <code>thermalProcesses</code>, <code>actsOnFewerLevels</code>, <code>irreducible</code> and <code>selfDual</code>, and calls the universal implication <code>claim</code>.</p>
