 fractals 93, 71–79 (2016), arxiv:1610.05919v1, §2.2, state the following conjecture (snlmatrixrr.tex, lines 449–452; the displayed formula is rendered inline): the inverse coefficients supply the increasing-path weights in the paper&#x27;s critical-iteration representation immediately following the conjecture (lines 456–466). the inverse of each normalized critical carleman jordan chain is an increasing-path sum. the proof uses induction.  released-dossier 06a92f20cf17905391dc9cc271c9ded34f5dc596832ace1247568cc58e378e04 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Analysis &amp; dynamics</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2016</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The inverse of a critical Carleman Jordan chain is an increasing-path sum</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The inverse of each normalized critical Carleman Jordan chain is an increasing-path sum.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Huillet, Some combinatorial aspects of discrete non-linear population dynamics, Chaos, Solitons &amp; Fractals 93, 71–79 (2016), arXiv:1610.05919v1, §2.2, state the following conjecture (snlmatrixRR.tex, lines 449–452; the displayed formula is rendered inline):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The inverse coefficients supply the increasing-path weights in the paper&#x27;s critical-iteration representation immediately following the conjecture (lines 456–466).</p><p><span>The idea</span>The proof uses induction.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The inverse of each normalized critical Carleman Jordan chain is an increasing-path sum.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="grosjean-huillet-2016-carleman-jordan-chain-inverse"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The inverse of a critical Carleman Jordan chain is an increasing-path sum</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>N. Grosjean and T. Huillet, <em>Some combinatorial aspects of discrete non-linear population dynamics</em>, Chaos, Solitons &amp; Fractals 93, 71–79 (2016), arXiv:1610.05919v1, §2.2, state the following conjecture (<code>snlmatrixRR.tex</code>, lines 449–452; the displayed formula is rendered inline):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Then, we conjecture that $W^{-1}(k,k^{\prime })=\sigma (k-1,k^{\prime })$, $k^{\prime }\geq k\geq 1$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The paper defines $\phi(x)=\sum_{r\geq1}\alpha_rx^r$ and $P(k,k')=[x^{k'}]\phi(x)^k$ in Eq. (2) (lines 126–141). In the critical case $\alpha_1=1$, its normalized columns satisfy $P\mathbf w_1=\mathbf w_1$, $P\mathbf w_k=\mathbf w_{k-1}+\mathbf w_k$ for $k&gt;1$, $\mathbf w_1=e_1$, $\mathbf w_k(1)=0$ for $k&gt;1$, and $\mathbf w_k(r)=0$ for $r&gt;k$ (lines 403–428). Its recursion divides by $P(r,r+1)=r\alpha_2$ (lines 420–436), so this construction requires $\alpha_2\ne0$.</p>
<p>For $m\geq1$, $\sigma(m,j)$ is the sum of $\prod_{\ell=1}^mP(i_\ell,i_{\ell+1})$ over $1=i_1&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{m+1}=j$; it is zero when $j\leq m$, and $\sigma(0,j)=\delta_{1,j}$ (lines 442–447). The definitions and quotation are also recorded in <a href="../Library/Eigenstructure/grosjeanhuillet2016carleman.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24105">#24105</a> fixes the finite reading: for every real coefficient sequence with $\alpha_0=0$, $\alpha_1=1$, $\alpha_2\ne0$, and every size $N\geq1$, a normalized upper triangular Jordan-chain matrix exists for the principal truncation, and every such matrix is invertible with the stated inverse entries for $1\leq k\leq k'\leq N$. This is proved.</p>
