inverses of one another. Their Remark 4.12 records that it holds in many cases where the entry
point <code>e_V(m)</code> does not exist at all, and their Theorem 4.11 proves it for <code>q = 1</code> and order
statistic <code>omega_V(m) = 4</code>, where the axis passes through the two zeros. Question 5.5 of
arXiv:2408.14632v2 asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Can we find necessary and sufficient conditions for the &quot;vertical slice result&quot; to hold for
(V_n(p,q)) modulo m with q = 1 and omega_V != 4?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The sentence quoted from Remark 4.12 is looser than the property the paper actually tracks.
Modulo 28 with <code>p = 7</code> and <code>q = 1</code> the period is <code>2, 7, 19, 14, 23, 7, 26, 7, 23, 14, 19, 7</code>, and
the chord joining positions 0 and 6 does join two additive inverses, <code>2 + 26 = 28</code>; the paper
