ails</summary><article class="news-result" id="kag329479-binarynull"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Kagey&#x27;s order-eight recurrence for binary null polynomials modulo three</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Peter Kagey's OEIS A329479, revision 25, final FORMULA line, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjectured recurrence: a(n) = 2a(n-1) + 2a(n-2) - 5a(n-3) - 2a(n-4) + 10a(n-5) - 4a(n-6) - 4a(n-7) + 8a(n-8).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source NAME is: “Number of degree n polynomials f with all nonzero coefficients equal to 1 such that f(k) is divisible by 3 for all integers k.” Thus $a(n)$ counts distinct integer polynomials of degree exactly $n$, with leading coefficient one, every coefficient in ${0,1}$, and $3\mid f(k)$ for every integer $k$. The source offset is one. The conjecture is interpreted for every natural $n\ge9$, using integer arithmetic for the counts and natural subtraction for the indices.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Formal counterpart | Fidelity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Degree $n$ integer polynomials | <code>a</code>: subtype of <code>Polynomial Int</code> with <code>natDegree = n</code> | Exact degree; distinct polynomials remain distinct |
| All nonzero coefficients equal one | <code>a</code>: every coefficient is zero or one, with <code>Monic</code> | For the positive degrees used here, monicness expresses the forced leading one |
| Divisible by three for all integers | <code>a</code>: divisibility of every integer evaluation | Full universal condition |
| Eight-term recurrence | <code>claim</code>; <code>result</code> proves it | All eight coefficients and signs retained |
| Offset one | Guard $9\le n$ | Every preceding index is at least one |</p>
<p>The source COMMENT describes strings of length $n$, while the NAME and six degree-seven examples use polynomial degree $n$. The formal count follows the NAME and examples, so the coefficient string has length $n+1$. No recurrence-defined sequence or quotient by polynomial functions replaces this count.</p>
