/span>If C is a singleton board, then perhaps counting the number of Ferrers boards B with l(B) = C would be a good start to this problem, but this too remains open.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="barrese-prescribed-ordered-level-enumeration"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Ferrers Boards with Prescribed Ordered Level Numbers</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Kenneth Barrese, <em>Weighted File Placements on Singleton Boards</em>,
<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.10855v1">arXiv:2312.10855v1</a>, Section 2.2, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This suggests the open question, given an ordered set of level numbers:
(l_1, l_2, . . . , l_n), how many unique Ferrers boards have those specific level numbers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section 5 repeats this question. For every positive m and n, count every weakly increasing
n-tuple of column heights between zero and mn in the fixed n-column, mn-row rectangle,
with a prescribed top-down vector l of n nonnegative level totals. A level has m rows;
its total counts actual occupied cells. Leading zero columns and empty top levels are retained.
The input board need not be singleton, and no permutation of l is allowed.</p>
<p>The same prescribed-level counting problem has an earlier all-width formulation in Kenneth
Barrese, Nicholas Loehr, Jeffrey Remmel and Bruce E. Sagan, <em>Bijections on m-level Rook Placements</em>,
<a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1508.05944v1">arXiv:1508.05944v1</a>, Section 6.2:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If C is a singleton board, then perhaps counting the number of Ferrers boards B
with l(B) = C would be a good start to this problem, but this too remains open.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For every positive m and every nonempty positive singleton target C, count all normalized
Ferrers boards B of arbitrary width with l(B)=C. Definition 4 reverses the nonempty
bottom-up level totals. Here normalization uses positive weakly increasing column-height lists:
Section 4.1 aligns lower-right cells and treats leading zero padding as another notation
for the same board. Arbitrary horizontal translations as distinct unbounded absolute-grid
