x27;s shadow Markoff positivity region</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Nathan Bonin, <em>Conditions of positivity on a shadow Markoff tree</em>, J. Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.8.3, arXiv:2403.07668v2, Conjecture 1, asks for the quadrilateral of projective initial shadows that give an integer positive shadow Markoff tree. The source and its literal quotations are recorded in <a href="../Library/Factorization/bonin2024shadowmarkoff.md">the Library note</a> and preregistration <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/17060">#17060</a>.</p>
<p>The precise assertion uses the rooted tree of Sections 2 and 5: start at $(1,\alpha,1,\beta,1,\gamma)$, include the root, apply the mandatory right and left mutations, then every finite word in the two moves. For $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\in\mathbb N^3\setminus{(0,0,0)}$, every node has all six entries in $\mathbb N$ if and only if</p>
<p>$$\alpha+\beta\leq 2\gamma,\qquad 2\alpha\leq\beta+\gamma.$$</p>
<p>Equivalently, $\gamma&gt;0$ and $(\alpha/\gamma,\beta/\gamma)$ belongs to the rational convex hull of $(0,0),(1/2,0),(1,1),(0,2)$. Natural numbers include zero, as in Equation (8) and the boundary vertices. The result does not quantify over independent root permutations.</p>
