<p>The formal statement uses the source's three-letter Arnoux–Rauzy blocks,
languages, standard words, conjugacy, Burrows–Wheeler transform and clustering
definitions. It records the second alternative of the conjecture as <code>claim</code>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>The alphabet is $\mathrm{Fin},3$, with $a=0$, $b=1$, and $c=2$; words
are lists. The three AR rules are the pointwise update that fixes the selected
block and appends it to each other block. <code>arBlocks</code> uses <code>Nat.rec</code> and
<code>Function.update</code> for that recurrence. <code>InfOften</code> is the universal occurrence
condition expressed using <code>Filter.Frequently</code> at <code>Filter.atTop</code>, equivalent to
$\forall x,N,\ \exists k\ge N,\ d(k)=x$. The boundary $\mu$ is
<code>Nat.findGreatest</code> of the positions $j$ with $d(j-1)\ne d(\mu_y-1)$,
bounded by $\mu_y$; the value is $0$ when no such positive position exists.
Stage blocks are enumerated by <code>List.ofFn</code> and sorted by length. For BWT,
the non-strict permutation-rank order is the negation of reverse <code>List.lex</code>;
<code>List.insertionSort</code> sorts the rotations and <code>List.getLast?</code> extracts their
last letters. The quantified conditions in <code>claim</code> are exactly the weaker
standard-conjugacy alternative of Conjecture 1.</p>
