determined by an arithmetic function</em>, arXiv:2303.13017v1, Question 3
(Question 3.3 in Utilitas Mathematica 124, DOI 10.61091/um124-06), ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We obtain in Theorem 2 that Out(s_b, n) contains every multiple of s_b(n). Does Out(s_b, n) contain an infinite number of positive integers that are not divisible by s_b(n)? When d = 1 and s_b(n) ≥ 2, the answer is yes. Can we determine a simple necessary and sufficient condition for the infinitely many elements in A ∩ Out(s_b, n) where A is the set of positive integers that are not divisible by s_b(n)? In that case, does Out(s_b, n) has a natural density?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For $b\ge2$ and $n\ge1$, write $s=s_b(n)$ and $d=\gcd(b-1,n)$.
The answer is that the difference set is infinite exactly when $d&lt;s$, and
$\operatorname{Out}(s_b,n)$ has natural density $1/d$. It contains every
sufficiently large positive multiple of $d$.
Preregistration: <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/19415">#19415</a>.</p>
