te mixtures with shared classical randomness.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Tavakoli, Scalable entanglement certification via quantum communication, PRX Quantum 5, 020319 (2024), arXiv:2401.00796v2, certify entanglement through a symmetric prepare-and-measure task. - The task. For an odd prime d, Alice and Bob receive x, y ∈ {0, …, d − 1}² and send d-dimensional messages; Charlie receives z ∈ {0, …, d}, may measure the two messages jointly, and outputs c.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Roch i Carceller and Tavakoli (arXiv:2410.15388, Phys.</p><p><span>The idea</span>1. Character expansion. With wz = az(x) + bz(y), the indicator of c = wz is d⁻¹ Σk χ(k(wz − c)).</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For every odd prime dimension, unentangled quantum messages have sharp average success probability 2/(d + 1), including finite mixtures with shared classical randomness.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="bakhshinezhad-mehboudi-roch-i-carceller-tavakoli-2024-odd-prime-unentangled-bound"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Without shared entanglement the odd-prime communication task succeeds with probability at most 2/(d + 1)</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>P. Bakhshinezhad, M. Mehboudi, C. Roch i Carceller and A. Tavakoli, <em>Scalable entanglement certification via quantum communication</em>, PRX Quantum 5, 020319 (2024), arXiv:2401.00796v2, certify entanglement through a symmetric prepare-and-measure task.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The task.</strong> For an odd prime d, Alice and Bob receive x, y ∈ {0, …, d − 1}² and send d-dimensional messages; Charlie receives z ∈ {0, …, d}, may measure the two messages jointly, and outputs c. He wins when c = w_z, with w_z = x₁ + y₁ − 2z(x₀ − y₀) for z ≠ d and w_d = x₀ − y₀ mod d (Eq. (27)), and R_d is the average success probability (Eq. (28)).</li>
<li><strong>Without shared entanglement.</strong> p(c | x, y, z) = tr(α_x ⊗ β_y M_c|z) (Eq. (19)); sending x₀ and y₀ achieves R_d = 2/(d + 1).</li>
<li><strong>The conjecture.</strong> After see-saw searches at d = 3 and d = 5: &quot;Conjecture 1. For every odd prime d, the largest average success probability achievable in a quantum model without shared entanglement is R_d = 2/(d + 1). (29)&quot; With shared entanglement and product measurements R_d = 1, so the bound would certify every state with a non-trivial maximally entangled fraction.</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/bakhshinezhad2024scalable.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16770">#16770</a> fixes the reading: messages are d × d density matrices, Charlie's measurement for each z is any d-outcome POVM on the joint d²-dimensional space, and shared classical randomness is a finite mixture of strategies with one common index. The conjecture is proved for every odd prime d.</p>
