22-classical-markov-monogamy"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Classical Markov monogamy for every finite alphabet and every permutation</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M. Capela, L. C. Céleri, R. Chaves and K. Modi, <em>Quantum Markov monogamy inequalities</em>, Phys. Rev. A 106, 022218 (2022), arXiv:2108.11533v4, Section II, Conjecture 1 (<code>PRA-v1.tex</code>, lines 153–159):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Consider the Markovian process $X_n \rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow X_1 \rightarrow Y_1 \rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow Y_n$. The variables $X_1$ and $Y_1$ are to be interpreted as input and output of a given channel, respectively. The variables  $X_i$ and $Y_i$, with $i=2,\dots,n$, are interpreted as pre-processed and post-processed variables, respectively. Then for any bijective function $f \colon {1,\dots,n} \rightarrow {1,\dots,n}$, it holds that</p>
<p>$\sum_{i=1}^{n} I(X_i:Y_i) \geq \sum_{i=1}^{n} I(X_i:Y_{f(i)}),$</p>
<p>where $I(X:Y)$ denotes the mutual information of random variables $X$ and $Y$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source reports at line 161:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The conjecture above has been checked for $n$ up to 4, that is, Markov chains with 8 random variables</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $I(X:Y)=H(X)+H(Y)-H(X,Y)$, with Shannon entropy. The definitions and source locators are in <a href="../Library/Entropy/capelacelerichavesmodi2022markov.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22399">#22399</a> fixes the finite-alphabet reading. The module proves it for every $n\in\mathbb N$, including $n=0$ with empty sums: choose finite alphabets $\alpha_t$, a nonnegative initial mass $p:\alpha_0\to\mathbb R$ with $\sum_xp(x)=1$, and matrices $k_t:\alpha_t\to\alpha_{t+1}\to\mathbb R$ satisfying $k_t(x,y)\ge0$ and $\sum_y k_t(x,y)=1$ whenever $t+1&lt;2n$. For every permutation $f$ of $\mathrm{Fin},n$, the sum paired by $f$ is at most the diagonal sum.</p>
<p>Precisely, <code>composedTransition</code> is the identity at equal positions and the product of successive matrices for ordered positions $s\le t$; write it as $K_{s,t}$. The definitions <code>mixture</code> and <code>mixtureJoint</code> give $p_s(x)=\sum_zp(z)K_{0,s}(z,x)$ and $q_{s,t}(x,y)=p_s(x)K_{s,t}(x,y)$. For $i\in\mathrm{Fin},n$, position $n-1-i.val$ represents $X_{i.val+1}$ and position $n+i.val$ represents $Y_{i.val+1}$. Each summand in <code>claim</code> is <code>mutualInformation</code> of the corresponding $q_{s,t}$, defined as its KL divergence from the product of its marginals, in nats.</p>
