\operatorname{IsUnit}(P)\ \land<br />
(\forall k\in\operatorname{Fin}(C),\ \operatorname{Toeplitz}(T(k)))\ \land<br />
M=P,\operatorname{List.prod}(\operatorname{List.ofFn}(T)).
$$</p>
<p>For every <code>i,j : Fin n</code> and proofs <code>hi : i.val + 1 &lt; n</code>, <code>hj : j.val + 1 &lt; n</code>, the Toeplitz condition is
<code>T i j = T ⟨i.val + 1, hi⟩ ⟨j.val + 1, hj⟩</code>.
This also defines the empty-size predicate; F uses positive sizes only. <code>IsUnit P</code> is actual matrix invertibility. The multiplier is on the left. <code>List.ofFn</code> orders the factors by their finite indices, and <code>List.prod</code> uses <code>prod([])=1</code>, <code>prod(A::L)=A*prod(L)</code>, with ordinary finite-sum matrix multiplication. The factor count is independent of both n and M. Exact C and at most C are equivalent by padding with the ordinary Toeplitz identity.</p>
<p>The result negates this entire statement. The separately printed numerical assertion J, <code>Toep_n ≤ Toep_n^Inv + C</code>, remains unsettled. No converse J ⇒ F, negation of J, or unbounded global numerical gap is established. Conjectures 5.2 and 5.3 are separate and excluded.</p>
