resentation set.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Changing exact trace or the saturated coordinates removes hypotheses used by this proof; the outcome for those changed problems remains open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="carli-silva-2026-theta-representation-convexity-clause"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>The theta representation set: Question (iv), first convexity clause</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Marcel K Carli Silva, Gabriel Coutinho, Thiago Oliveira and Levent Tunçel,
<em>On some k-fold generalizations of Lovász theta and their sandwich theorems</em>,
arXiv:2609.06312v1, §7, Open questions, item (iv), ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is the orthonormal representation set $\texttt{M}_k(G)$ convex? If not, can one characterize its closed convex lower hull, i.e., the convex corner whose support function is $\varphi_k(G,\cdot)$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This dossier identifies <strong>only the first convexity clause</strong> of that version
and item. The closed convex lower hull characterization is a separate,
unresolved question. The preregistration is
<a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/15881">issue #15881</a>.</p>
<p>In §5.3, the proof of Theorem 16 defines, for a finite ordinary simple graph
$G=(V,E)$ and $n=|V|$,</p>
<p>$$
\texttt{M}<em>k(G)=\left{x\in\mathbb R</em>+^V:\begin{array}{l}
\exists M\in\mathbb S_+^V,\quad I-M\in\mathbb S_+^V,\quad \operatorname{tr}(M)=k,\
\exists u:V\to\mathbb R^V,\quad \forall i\in V,\ \langle u_i,u_i\rangle=1,\
\forall i,j\in V,\ ij\in E\Rightarrow\langle u_i,u_j\rangle=0,\
\forall i\in V,\ x_i=u_i^{\mathsf T}Mu_i
\end{array}\right}.
$$</p>
<p>Here $\mathbb S_+^V$ consists of real symmetric positive semidefinite
$V\times V$ matrices. The source's order $M\preccurlyeq I$ means
$I-M\succeq0$, and its matrix inner product is $\langle A,B\rangle
=\operatorname{tr}(AB)$, so $\langle I,M\rangle=k$ is exact trace equality.
One common matrix and one common vector family realize every coordinate.
There is no rank or projector restriction. The ambient real dimension is
exactly $|V|$. In the source's convention, $u$ is an orthonormal
representation of $\overline G$: orthogonality is imposed on edges of $G$,
with no condition on its nonedges. Vectors at nonadjacent vertices may
coincide; the family need not be a mutually orthogonal basis.</p>
