3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Saeid Alikhani and Max Griswold, <em>On the Integer Domination Root Conjecture</em>,
arXiv:2608.00109v1, Section 4 item 3 asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does there exist an infinite family of graphs G satisfying D(G, -4) = 0?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source uses finite simple undirected connected graphs. For ordinary
domination, a subset S dominates when each vertex belongs to S or has a
neighbor in S. The polynomial counts each such subset exactly once:</p>
<p>$$D(G,x)=\sum_{S\text{ dominates }G}x^{|S|}.$$</p>
<p>The exact result is: for every natural N there exist n&gt;N and a simple graph G
on Fin n such that G is connected and D(G,-4)=0. Unbounded orders ensure
infinitely many isomorphism classes. Connectedness applies to G; the
dominating subsets need not themselves be connected. There is no additional
degree, planarity, cyclomatic-number, or root-multiplicity hypothesis.</p>
<p>The declaration is
<code>D5/S3/Combinatorics/Graph/Domination/MinusFourReassembly.result</code>.
Its ordinary dominating subsets are exactly <code>DominatingSetAverage.domSets</code>;
the summands are integer powers of the integer minus four. Both existential
witnesses follow the universal N, and the inequality is strict.</p>
