iv:2302.12361v1, Section 5.2 (Open Problems), Theme B:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Similarly, it is considered that AQ measurement might be non-simulable because AQ measurement also has an extraordinary performance. This is an open problem.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>DOVM:</strong> a two-outcome measurement ${M_1,M_2}$ on $\mathcal H_A\otimes\mathcal H_B$ whose effects are block positive (nonnegative on product vectors) and sum to $I$.</li>
<li><strong>AQ:</strong> one effect satisfies $\lambda_1(M_i)&lt;0$ and $1+\lambda_1(M_i)&lt;\lambda_d(M_i)&lt;1$.</li>
<li><strong>Domain:</strong> the states $\rho$ with $\mathrm{tr},\rho M_i\ge0$ for both $i$.</li>
<li><strong>Quantum $n$-simulable:</strong> some POVM ${N_i}$ on $(\mathcal H_A\otimes\mathcal H_B)^{\otimes n}$ has $\mathrm{tr},\rho^{\otimes n}N_i=\mathrm{tr},\rho M_i$ on the domain.</li>
</ul>
<p>The definitions are quoted in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/arai2023diversity.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20035">#20035</a> fixes the reading: no AQ DOVM is quantum $n$-simulable for any $n$, with the simulation identity required only on trace-one domain states, which makes the statement stronger than the literal domain. This is proved.</p>
