<p>Maxim R. Burke, Maleeha Haris and Madhavendra, <em>Repeated integrals of increasing
functions</em>, arXiv:2512.02151v1, Conjecture 1.2, p. 2:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>$(P_n)$ is true for all nonnegative integers $n$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For each $n\geq 0$, let $W_n\subseteq\mathbb R^{n+1}$ consist of vectors
$b=(b_0,\ldots,b_n)$ for which some $f\in C^n[0,1]$ has nondecreasing,
nonconstant $D^n f$ and satisfies $D^j f(0)=0$ and $D^j f(1)=b_j$ for every
$0\leq j\leq n$. Here $D^0f=f$ and endpoint derivatives are one-sided.
Nondecreasing does not mean strictly increasing, and absolute continuity is
not an input hypothesis.</p>
<p>The assertion $(P_n)$ requires both openness of $W_n$ and, for every $b\in W_n$,
one function $F\in C^\infty[0,1]$ satisfying all of the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>$D^jF(0)=0$ and $D^jF(1)=b_j$ for every $0\leq j\leq n$;</li>
<li>$D^{n+1}F(x)&gt;0$ for every $0&lt;x&lt;1$;</li>
<li>$D^{n+1}F(0)=D^{n+1}F(1)=1$;</li>
<li>$D^jF(0)=D^jF(1)=0$ for every $j&gt;n+1$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sole problem anchor is this original Conjecture 1.2, including $n=0$.
The follow-up arXiv:2512.23949v1 calls the same interval property $Q_n$ and
retains its all-order assertion as Conjecture 2.5.</p>
