t gives exactly one nonzero entry in every column tail before the last column.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="leskovjanova-mista-2025-reverse-level-order-tree-labeling"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Reverse level order labeling gives one nonzero entry in every nonfinal column tail</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>O. Leskovjanová and L. Mišta Jr., <em>Minimal criteria for continuous-variable genuine multipartite entanglement</em>, arXiv:2401.04376v3; Quantum 9, 1837 (2025), Section 4.1.3, “Generic tree”, state the conjecture in <code>Quantum-accepted.tex</code>, lines 837–839:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We checked by a direct calculation that for all configurations of up to $N=10$ the established reverse level order labeling leads to an adjacency matrix, where each vector $A_{i+1:N,i}$ contains only
one non-zero component and therefore the respective quadrature combinations (\ref{uxpi}) contain at most two non-zero terms. This leads us to a conjecture
that this statement holds generally for an arbitrary $N$-vertex tree.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source construction, lines 828–835, roots the tree at its center vertex or at an endpoint of its central edge, assigns label $N$ to the root, and assigns decreasing labels level by level, from left to right within each level. The paper assumes $N\ge3$ for its entanglement application (lines 429–431). The source quotations and bibliographic entry are also in <a href="../Library/GraphInvariants/leskovjanovamista2025gaussiangme.md">the literature note</a>. The checked TeX member has MD5 <code>b1e8a86c3fefec6858c4ac36ca46f9fd</code>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21778">#21778</a> fixes the graph statement with an arbitrary root and arbitrary order within each level. In the module, for every $n\in\mathbb N$, every simple graph $g$ on $\operatorname{Fin} n$ with decidable adjacency and $g.\mathrm{IsTree}$, every root $r\in\operatorname{Fin} n$, and every bijection $\ell:\operatorname{Fin} n\simeq\operatorname{Fin} n$ satisfying</p>
<p>$$
\operatorname{dist}_g(r,u)&lt;\operatorname{dist}_g(r,v)
\ \Longrightarrow\ \ell(v)&lt;\ell(u),
$$</p>
<p>define $A^\ell_{ji}$ to be the natural-valued adjacency entry of $g$ at $\ell^{-1}(j),\ell^{-1}(i)$: it is $1$ for adjacent vertices and $0$ otherwise. Then <code>claim</code> states</p>
<p>$$
\forall i\in\operatorname{Fin} n,\quad
i.\mathrm{val}+1&lt;n\ \Longrightarrow
\exists! j\in\operatorname{Fin} n,\quad i&lt;j\ \land\ A^\ell_{ji}\ne0.
$$</p>
<p>The domain preorder in <code>StrictAnti</code> is lifted along $\operatorname{dist}_g(r,\cdot)$; its codomain has the usual label order. Thus equal levels impose no label comparison. Lean's labels $0,\ldots,n-1$ correspond to the source's $1,\ldots,N$. The graph statement is proved by <code>result : claim</code>; the Cholesky and quadrature implications are outside that declaration.</p>
