p>G. L. Spitzer and I. Nechita, <em>Quantum Graph Theory by Example</em>, arXiv:2603.23651v3 [math.OA, cross-listed math-ph, quant-ph] (Quantum 10, 2228, 2026), leave the component-count question in the conclusion. The source text states in <code>content/conclusion.tex</code>, line 8:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It would be interesting to determine the exact number of connected components of $X_{A, \ccdot, C}$ in terms of $\mathfrak{G}(A, C)$, and in particular to understand the role played by isolated strange edges with phase $\pi$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The paper defines the strange graph $\mathfrak{G}(A,C)$ from the nonzero two-vertex blocks of a quantum graph of the form $X_{A,B,C}$. The formal module fixes the $B=\operatorname{diag}A$ reading through the following declarations. For $A,C : \operatorname{Mat} n$ and $d : \operatorname{Fin} n \to \mathbb C$, <code>realignedProjector A C d</code> is the matrix with diagonal-coordinate entries $d$ and off-diagonal blocks determined by $A$ and $C$, and <code>adjacency A C d</code> is the linear map obtained from that realigned matrix. <code>HasComponents G k</code> means that there are $k$ nonzero star projections summing to the identity whose cross terms $P_s G(P_t Z)$ vanish for every $s \ne t$ and every $Z$. <code>underlyingGraph A C</code> has an edge exactly when the corresponding off-diagonal entry of $A$ or $C$ in either direction is nonzero. <code>IsLoopFreeStrangeComponent A C d Q</code> says that a connected component $Q$ has support ${i,j}$ for distinct vertices, is adjacent, has $A_{ij}=1/2$, and has $d_i=d_j=0$. The two counts are <code>graphComponentCount A C</code> and <code>loopFreeStrangeComponentCount A C d</code>, the latter counting such components.</p>
<p>The Lean <code>claim</code> quantifies over every natural $n$, every $A,C : \operatorname{Mat} n$, and every $d : \operatorname{Fin} n \to \mathbb C$. Under $A_{ii}=d_i$, $C_{ii}=d_i$, Hermiticity and idempotence of <code>realignedProjector A C d</code>, and Hermiticity of <code>offDiag A</code>, it asserts that the set of $k$ for which <code>HasComponents (adjacency A C d) k</code> holds has greatest element <code>graphComponentCount A C + loopFreeStrangeComponentCount A C d</code>.</p>
