div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Non-isotropic mixed-unitary qubit channels do not commute with marginal pinching</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Z.-W. Liu, R. Takagi and S. Lloyd, <em>Diagonal quantum discord</em>, J. Phys. A: Math. Theor. 52, 135301 (2019), arXiv:1708.09076v2, Section 3.2, “$\pi$-commuting local operations and monotonicity”, conjecture following the probabilistic-Hadamard example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We conjecture (which is not important for our current purpose) that ${\bf ISO} = X_A(\pi_A)$. That is, qubit mixed-unitary channels that are not isotropic all fail to satisfy the condition.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This quotation is line 463 of the source archive's <code>revised_jphysa_clean.tex</code> (md5 <code>3ae0def434187ebe1875d87961e23048</code>). The relevant definitions are at lines 261, 325–329 and 347; line 339 assumes the input marginal nondegenerate. See also <a href="../Library/Entropy/liutakagilloyd2019diagonaldiscord.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>The formal definitions use the following conventions:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>IsMixedUnitary</code> means a finite mixture $\Phi(a)=\sum_j p_j U_j a U_j^\dagger$ on every complex $2\times2$ matrix, with real $p_j\ge0$, $\sum_jp_j=1$, and unitary $U_j$.</li>
<li><code>IsIsotropic</code> means that, for some fixed $\gamma\in[0,1]$ and unitary $U$, either $\Phi(a)=(1-\gamma)UaU^\dagger+\gamma\operatorname{tr}(a)I/2$ for every $a$, or $\Phi(a)=(1-\gamma)Ua^{\mathsf T}U^\dagger+\gamma\operatorname{tr}(a)I/2$ for every $a$. Transpose gives the complex linear extension of the antiunitary action on Hermitian states.</li>
<li><code>localMap</code> applies $\Phi$ to each first-qubit block; for these linear maps it is $\Phi\otimes\mathrm{id}_B$. The marginal $\rho_A=\operatorname{tr}_B\rho$ is <code>partialTraceRight</code>.</li>
<li><code>IsNondegenerate</code> means Hermitian with two distinct eigenvalues. For a Hermitian marginal, <code>pinchA</code> is $\pi_A(\rho)=\sum_i(P_i\otimes I)\rho(P_i\otimes I)$, where $P_i$ are its rank-one spectral projectors; its spectral basis is recomputed on the argument to which pinching is applied.</li>
</ul>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22787">#22787</a> fixes the reading to the quotation's second sentence: for every mixed-unitary qubit map $\Phi$ that is not isotropic, there is a positive semidefinite two-qubit matrix $\rho$ of trace one such that $\rho_A$ and $\Phi(\rho_A)$ are nondegenerate and $\pi_A((\Phi\otimes\mathrm{id}_B)(\rho))\ne(\Phi\otimes\mathrm{id}_B)(\pi_A(\rho))$. This is proved.</p>
