ss="result-statement prose"><p>Ricardorr (MathOverflow user 49534; real-name attribution unverified) asks in
<a href="https://mathoverflow.net/questions/512655">question 512655</a>, revision 1,
GUID <code>E06A6743-2C15-41CD-9390-04D1862ABA02</code>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For every odd $n$ and every nonzero $b$, does the affine hull of
$\mathrm{Supp}(b)$ have dimension at least $n-1$? Equivalently: is
$\mathrm{Supp}(b)$ contained in no affine subspace of codimension $\ge 2$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The <a href="https://api.stackexchange.com/2.3/revisions/E06A6743-2C15-41CD-9390-04D1862ABA02?site=mathoverflow&amp;filter=withbody">original revision</a>
is attributed under CC BY-SA 4.0. The title's hyperplane wording is a locator;
the target is the dimension lower bound in the body.</p>
<p>Precisely, let $n$ be any positive odd integer, $b\in\mathbb F_2^n$ nonzero,
and $a\in\mathbb F_2^n$. All indices are modulo $n$. Put
$g_k=a_{k-1}$ and $h_k=a_k+a_{k-2}+b_k$ in $\mathbb F_2$, and define the
integer matrix</p>
<p>$$
A_k(a)=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\(-1)^{h_k}&amp;(-1)^{h_k+g_k}\end{pmatrix}.
$$</p>
<p>Equivalently, its entry indexed by $s,t\in{0,1}$ is
$(-1)^{h_ks+g_kst}$. With the literal ordered integer product, set</p>
<p>$$
W_b(a)=\operatorname{Tr}(A_0(a)A_1(a)\cdots A_{n-1}(a)),\qquad
\mathrm{Supp}(b)={a\in\mathbb F_2^n:W_b(a)\ne0}.
$$</p>
<p>The assertion is
$\dim_{\mathbb F_2}\operatorname{aff}(\mathrm{Supp}(b))\ge n-1$.
Length one, coincident cyclic indices, every nonzero mask, and every tested
word including zero and all-one are included. There is no length or weight
cutoff, additional nonempty-support premise, or equality assertion.</p>
