details</summary><article class="news-result" id="tavakoli-abbott-renou-gisin-brunner-2018-multipartite-partial-tightness"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Multipartite partial tightness of the entangled-measurement bound</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Tavakoli, A. A. Abbott, M.-O. Renou, N. Gisin and N. Brunner, <em>Semi-device-independent characterisation of multipartite entangled states and measurements</em>, arXiv:1805.00377v1 (Phys. Rev. A 98, 052333, 2018), Appendix C, <code>SDI_manuscript.tex</code>, line 497:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We leave it as an open question whether a similar partial tightness result holds in the more general $n$ partite case.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source text at lines 474–496 concerns attainment of Eq. (10) for two parties with exactly $k=md$ fully separable measurement operators, for $m=0,\ldots,d$. Result 2, lines 265–272, bounds the average winning probability by $\mathcal A_{n,d}\le(d^n-k+k/d)/d^n$ whenever at least $k$ of the $d^n$ measurement operators are fully separable, for any shared state and local transformations in the source's scenario.</p>
<p>The game has uniformly distributed inputs $\mathbf x,\mathbf y\in(\mathbb Z_d)^n$ and winning output $C_1=\sum_jx_j$, $C_j=y_j-y_1$ for $j\ge2$, all modulo $d$; $\mathcal A_{n,d}=d^{-2n}\sum_{\mathbf x,\mathbf y}P(C(\mathbf x,\mathbf y)\mid\mathbf x,\mathbf y)$ (lines 137–159). The definitions and source locator are also in <a href="../Library/Observer/tavakoliabbottrenougisinbrunner2018sdi.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23843">#23843</a> fixes the affirmative reading: for every natural $n,d\ge2$ and $0\le m\le d^{n-1}$, there exist a density matrix on $(\mathbb C^d)^{\otimes n}$, local unitary encodings and a projective measurement with exactly $dm$ fully separable outcome operators and score $(d^n-dm+m)/d^n$. This is proved.</p>
