<p>Statement echo: $F_0=a$, $F_1=b$, $F_{n+2}=F_{n+1}+F_n$; $\pi_p(a,b)$ is the least positive scalar period, valid at every index including zero. For $j\in{-1,0,1}$, $N_j$ counts indices $0\le n&lt;\pi_p(a,b)$ with Legendre symbol $j$, with multiplicity. Set $K=N_1-N_{-1}-N_0$ in the integers, $\chi(a,b)={K(p,a,b):p\text{ odd prime}}$, and $\vartheta_{(a,b)}(k)={p:p\text{ odd prime},K(p,a,b)=k}$. The Lean <code>claim</code> asserts that every such attained fiber is infinite, equivalently countably infinite because it is a subset of the natural numbers. Seeds are signed and may be nonprimitive; primes dividing both are included.</p>
