-loop-path"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Even double paths with a nonempty loop set allow nSSP</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Koljančić–Oblak Question 5.11 asks whether every positive even order n and every nonempty loop set L on the double path admit a real nSSP matrix with exactly that pattern. Both orientations of every consecutive path edge occur, and only the loops at vertices in L are added. The final witness has no sign, symmetry, trace, spectrum or genericity restriction. The real test matrix X is unrestricted; the defining equations are entrywise A times X equal to zero and A times transpose X minus transpose X times A equal to zero.</p>
<p>The exact Lean proposition is <code>D5.S3.Combinatorics.SpectralGraph.KoljancicOblakEvenPath.claim</code>. Unit #25129, preregistration comment 6554378006, owns this event. The companion <code>D5/S3/Combinatorics/SpectralGraph/KoljancicOblakEvenPathNSSP.result</code> has this exact proposition as its type. Both mathematical hosts are frozen. The result proves the full event stated here.</p>
<p>The source is Sara Koljančić and Polona Oblak, <em>Combinatorial aspects of the non-symmetric Strong Spectral Property for graphs</em>, arXiv:2604.21552v1, Question 5.11, with Definitions 1.1, 1.3 and 1.5 fixing nSSP, allowing a pattern and double-path loops. The later Electronic Journal of Linear Algebra publication, volume 42, pages 755–779, DOI 10.13001/ela.2026.10523, states Question 5.11 on PDF page 22, printed page 776. The linked Library note owns the source and license correspondence.</p>
<p>For every natural n with 0 &lt; n and Even n, and every nonempty finite set L of vertices in Fin n, there exists a real matrix A indexed by Fin n whose nonzero entries are exactly the two orientations of consecutive path edges and the diagonal entries selected by L. For every real matrix X on the same indices, A ∘ X = 0 together with A * Xᵀ − Xᵀ * A = 0 implies X = 0. Here ∘ is the entrywise product. This includes n = 2 and singleton endpoint loops; no restriction on X or extra premise on A is added to the event.</p>
