lass="result-statement prose"><p>Q. Tang, T. Zhang, &quot;Sharp Schoenberg type inequalities and the de Bruin–Sharma problem&quot;, arXiv:2508.10341v3 (5 Dec 2025), §6, p. 31.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The identity (5.2) leads us to the following conjectural refinements of Theorem 6.1 in the case of order −2.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 6.3. Let P(z) = z ∏_{j=1}^n (z − z_j), z_j ∈ C \ {0}, and let w_1, …, w_n denote the zeros of P′(z). Then Σ_{k=1}^n 1/|w_k|² ≤ 3 Σ_{j=1}^n 1/|z_j|² + |Σ_{j=1}^n 1/z_j|². (6.9) Moreover, equality in (6.9) holds whenever z_1, …, z_n all lie on the same line passing through the origin.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>In the case n = 2 we can verify Conjecture 6.3 by an explicit computation. (Proposition 6.4.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Theorem 6.1 there proves the weaker right side (n+3) Σ 1/|z_j|².
The exact public definitions and result are:</p>
<pre><code class="language-lean">noncomputable def P {n : ℕ} (z : Fin n → ℂ) : Polynomial ℂ :=
  Polynomial.X * ∏ j, (Polynomial.X - Polynomial.C (z j))

def claim : Prop := ∀ (n : ℕ) (hn : 1 ≤ n) (z : Fin n → ℂ), (∀ j, z j ≠ 0) →
    (((Polynomial.derivative (P z)).roots.map (fun w =&gt; ‖w‖⁻¹ ^ 2)).sum ≤
      3 * ∑ j, ‖z j‖⁻¹ ^ 2 + ‖∑ j, (z j)⁻¹‖ ^ 2) ∧
    ((((Polynomial.derivative (P z)).roots.map (fun w =&gt; ‖w‖⁻¹ ^ 2)).sum =
      3 * ∑ j, ‖z j‖⁻¹ ^ 2 + ‖∑ j, (z j)⁻¹‖ ^ 2) ↔
        ∀ j, ∃ r : ℝ, z j = (r : ℂ) * z ⟨0, hn⟩)

theorem result : claim
</code></pre>
<p>Here n≥1, all z_j are nonzero complex numbers, and roots of the derivative
are counted with multiplicity. No simplicity, distinctness, reality or
common argument hypothesis is imposed on the nonzero roots. Since
P′(0)=∏_j(−z_j)≠0, no critical point is zero. The final equivalence says
that all z_j are real multiples of the nonzero first root, which is exactly
collinearity on a line through the origin.</p>
