ed in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>Global-coalition graph universality</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Anholcer, Besharati, Mojdeh and Samadzadeh, <em>Global coalitions in graphs</em>, arXiv:2509.15386v4, Section 7 Problem 58, PDF p. 29, asks whether every graph is realisable as a global coalition graph. The earlier alias is Besharati, Mojdeh and Samadzadeh, <em>Global coalition sets in graphs</em>, arXiv:2509.15386v1, Section 6 Problem 4, PDF p. 19. Both aliases identify one target.</p>
<h3>Original question and definitions</h3>
<p>The following is the complete question and its conventions as recorded in preregistration <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23084">#23084</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The current complete question asks whether every finite simple undirected graph H of order at least two is isomorphic to the global coalition graph of some finite simple undirected host G of order at least two and some actual gc-partition of G. A gc-partition need not maximize the number of blocks. There is no connectedness, isolate-free, positive-edge-count or independent-block restriction.</p>
<p>For the same vertex set, S dominates G precisely when every vertex is in S or adjacent to a member of S. Global domination means domination of both G and its genuine loopless complement. Two actual subsets A,B form a global coalition precisely when they are disjoint, neither is individually globally dominating, and their union globally dominates. The partition consists of nonempty pairwise-disjoint actual subsets covering all host vertices; every block has another distinct block in that same partition forming such a coalition. There is no singleton-dominating exception. The global coalition graph has these actual blocks as vertices and adjacency exactly the full global-coalition relation. Realization means genuine graph isomorphism.</p>
<p>Current v4 places the order&gt;=2 convention after Definition 2. V1 has no corresponding global lower-order restriction; its unrestricted full question is preserved separately, not silently narrowed. The proposed two-vertex witness obeys both versions and they count as one target. V3 withdrew v2 because it had been uploaded without consulting all authors and contained incorrect results; current v4 is restored and retains the question.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The formal claim is the literal closed assertion</p>
<pre><code class="language-lean">def claim : Prop :=
  ∀ (V : Type) [Finite V], 2 ≤ Nat.card V → ∀ H : SimpleGraph V,
    ∃ (W : Type) (_ : Finite W) (G : SimpleGraph W), 2 ≤ Nat.card W ∧
      ∃ π : GCPartition G, Nonempty (globalCoalitionGraph G π ≃g H)
</code></pre>
<p>The model retains actual finite Type-valued target and host types, native simple graphs and native loopless complements, genuine <code>Setoid.IsPartition</code> nonempty/disjoint-cover structure, and a distinct actual coalition partner for every actual block. The v1 unrestricted-order alias is a source boundary, not a second theorem or credit.</p>
