tonians h^(k) with explicit universal coefficients</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. Avan, L. Frappat and E. Ragoucy, <em>Quadratic Poisson brackets for the Camassa–Holm peakons</em>, arXiv:2512.11066v3, J. Phys. A <strong>59</strong>, 245202 (2026), study the two-parameter deformation of the Camassa–Holm peakon Lax matrix.</p>
<ul>
<li>Its matrices are A, T and S of Eqs. (2.1)–(2.3), and its Lax matrix is L(z) = S + Λ(z)T + ξ(z)A with ξ² = Λ² + γΛ + 1 (5.18).</li>
<li>The transfer matrices are τ_m(z) = tr L(z)^m.</li>
<li>Proposition 5.1 gives their degree-one term in Λ, h^(m) = m tr((T + γA/2)(S + A)^{m−1}) (5.17).</li>
</ul>
<p>Conjecture 5.2 asserts that τ_m(z) = Σ_j Λ^j Σ a_j(k_1, …, k_j) h^(k_1)⋯h^(k_j), summed over partitions k_1 ≥ ⋯ ≥ k_j of m (5.20), with a_1(m) = 1 and the values a_m(1, …, 1) = 1 and a_{j+1}(m − j, 1, …, 1) = m/(m − j) for 0 ≤ j &lt; m − 1 (5.21). It leaves &quot;the other coefficients … to be determined&quot;, and the conclusion calls for a proof. The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/FluidDynamics/avan2026peakonquadratic.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16323">#16323</a> fixes the reading: for every N and m ≥ 1, there are coefficients independent of N and of all parameters with these values such that (5.20) holds. It also preregisters the explicit coefficients a_j(k) = m (j − 1)! / (∏_i k_i · ∏_r e_r!), where e_r counts the parts equal to r.</p>
