<summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Structural characterization</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Half letter frequency characterizes balanced borders</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Half letter frequency characterizes balanced borders of nonempty binary words.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The idea</span>The first-hit decomposition and overlap bijection prove the cleared-denominator counting identity (ForbiddenWordCounting.forbiddenwordcountingidentity).</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Half letter frequency characterizes balanced borders of nonempty binary words.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="bona-maga-richey-2026-half-frequency-balanced-borders"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>Half letter frequency characterizes balanced borders</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Miklós Bóna, Balázs Maga and Jacob Richey, <em>Letter frequency in shifts of finite type with one forbidden word</em>, arXiv:2606.06655v2, Section 6, Conjecture 6.1: “A word $w$ has $\rho^w = 1/2$ if and only if $w$ has balanced borders. The same holds with $\rho^w$ replaced by $q^w$.”</p>
<p>Only the first sentence is settled. For every nonempty binary word $w$, let $\Omega_m^w$ be the length-$m$ binary words without $w$ as a factor and let</p>
<p>$$
\rho_m^w=\frac{\sum_{u\in\Omega_m^w}|u|_1}{m|\Omega_m^w|}.
$$</p>
<p>A border is a nonempty prefix that is also a suffix, including $w$ itself. Balanced borders means $2|b|_1=|b|$ for every border $b$. Since the source defines $\rho^w$ as the limit if it exists, the literal settlement is $\rho_m^w\to1/2$ if and only if every border is balanced; no independent convergence assumption is added.</p>
