t-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Geometry &amp; algebra</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>A null direct summand in a tensor power of a singular semigroup module</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A tensor power of every singular finite-semigroup representation contains a one-dimensional null summand; every indecomposable-summand count is asymptotic to the tensor dimension.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>David He and Daniel Tubbenhauer, Tensor powers of representations of (diagram) monoids, arXiv:2508.04054v1, Conjecture 4.2, page 17:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>It proves the entire claim, including count-sequence existence and the limit for every internal indecomposable decomposition.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Tensor ranks are bounded by individual-rank powers.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A tensor power of every singular finite-semigroup representation contains a one-dimensional null summand; every indecomposable-summand count is asymptotic to the tensor dimension.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="ht2508"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A null direct summand in a tensor power of a singular semigroup module</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>David He and Daniel Tubbenhauer, <em>Tensor powers of representations of (diagram) monoids</em>, arXiv:2508.04054v1, Conjecture 4.2, page 17:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let V be a kS-module on which no element acts invertibly, then there is some m ≥ 1 such that Z is a summand of V^{⊗m}, and consequently a(n)=(dim V)ⁿ.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The field is algebraically closed, the semigroup is nonempty and finite with no two-sided identity, and the representation is finite dimensional and nonunital. The one-dimensional null representation has zero action. The source defines $a(n)$ as an asymptotic equivalent of the indecomposable-summand count $b(n)$, with multiplicity. Thus the final clause means $b(n)/(\dim V)^n\to1$, rather than exact equality at finite exponents. Preregistration: <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22531">#22531</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart |
