<blockquote>
<p>Orders k of Hermite polynomials whose maximal coefficient in absolute value appears twice.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Its Formula field states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 3: G.f.: (8<em>x + 5</em>x^2 + 5*x^3 - x^4 - x^5) / ((1 - x)^3 * (1 + x)^2).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The offset is 1,1. Let $H_k$ denote the physicists Hermite polynomial over
$\mathbb Q$, represented by <code>HermiteSignedMode.physicistsHermite</code>. For natural
orders $k$ and exponents $j$, define</p>
<p>$$
M(k,j)\iff j\le k\ \land\ \forall i\le k,\quad
|[x^i]H_k|\le |[x^j]H_k|.
$$</p>
<p>Write $T(k)$ when there exist natural exponents $u&lt;v$ such that, for every
natural $j$, $M(k,j)$ holds if and only if $j=u$ or $j=v$. This is precisely
<code>HermiteAbsoluteTieOrders.HasExactlyTwo k</code>: exactly two distinct positions
maximize the absolute coefficient over the full row $0,\ldots,k$, including
parity-zero coefficients.</p>
<p>The entire assertion is that there exists a total $a:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$
satisfying all of the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>$a(n)&gt;0$ for every natural $n\ge1$.</li>
<li>$a(n)&lt;a(m)$ whenever $1\le n&lt;m$.</li>
<li>For every natural order $k$, including zero,
$T(k)\iff\exists! n\in\mathbb N,\ 1\le n\land a(n)=k$.</li>
<li>There exists $S\in\mathbb Q[[X]]$ with $[X^0]S=0$ and
$[X^n]S=(a(n):\mathbb Q)$ for every $n\ge1$, such that</li>
</ul>
<p>$$
P=8X+5X^2+5X^3-X^4-X^5,\qquad
D=(1-X)^3(1+X)^2,\qquad [X^0]D=1,\qquad S=PD^{-1}.
$$</p>
<p>The inverse is the formal power-series unit inverse: the constant
coefficient of $D$ is one. The zero coefficient of $S$ is padding, not an
assertion about $a(0)$. This is a formal series identity at every positive
coefficient, with no analytic convergence assertion, finite prefix,
selected subfamily or recurrence-defined substitute.</p>
