Conjecture 5.4:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The numerical data suggest the following sharper parity-dependent statement.
(i) If n is even, then D(B_n,x) has exactly four real roots counting multiplicity:
0 with multiplicity 2, one simple root in (-infinity,-2), and one simple root in (-1/2,0).
(ii) If n is odd, then D(B_n,x) has exactly four real roots counting multiplicity:
0 with multiplicity 2 and two simple roots in (-1/2,0).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section 2, printed page 3, defines the domain and graph:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For n &gt;= 1, the book graph B_n is formed by identifying a common edge in n copies of C_4, see Figure 1(b).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source uses finite simple undirected graphs and ordinary domination:
$S$ dominates $G$ when every vertex is in $S$ or has a neighbor in $S$,
equivalently $N[S]=V(G)$. Its polynomial counts each dominating subset once:</p>
<p>$$D(G,X)=\sum_{S\text{ dominates }G}X^{|S|}.$$</p>
<p>The complete closed claim quantifies over every natural $n\geq1$. For even
$n$ there are distinct nonzero $a,b\in\mathbb R$ with $a&lt;-2$ and
$-1/2&lt;b&lt;0$; for odd $n$ there are distinct nonzero $a,b\in(-1/2,0)$.
In each branch, $0,a,b$ are actual roots, their exact multiplicities are
$2,1,1$, and for every real $r$,</p>
<p>$$D(B_n,r)=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad r=0\ \lor\ r=a\ \lor\ r=b.$$</p>
<p>The delivered conclusion is the literal negation of this entire universal
claim. It retains both parity branches and all intervals, multiplicities
and exhaustiveness conditions. The legal odd value $n=1$ is the witness;
restricting the source to $n\geq2$ gives a different, unsettled assertion.
Proposition 5.3 has a local $n\geq2$ hypothesis, which is not a hypothesis
of Conjecture 5.4. Printed page 11 explicitly identifies $B_1=C_4$.</p>
