<p>Mills-ratio definition, printed p. 2:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Mills ratio [3] is defined by</p>
<p>$$R\equiv R(x)=\frac{1-\Phi(x)}{\phi(x)}
=e^{x^2/2}\int_x^\infty e^{-t^2/2},dt,\quad x\ge0,$$</p>
<p>which satisfies the differential equation of first order:</p>
<p>$$R'=xR-1.\qquad(1.1)$$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $\phi(x)=e^{-x^2/2}/\sqrt{2\pi}$ and
$\Phi(x)=\int_{-\infty}^x\phi(t),dt$ are the standard Gaussian density and
cumulative distribution function used in the preceding source sentences.</p>
<p>The bound family in (1.5), printed p. 2, is</p>
<p>$$\varphi_{p,q,r}(x)=\frac{p}{\sqrt{x^2+q}+rx},
\qquad p,q&gt;0,\quad r\ge0.$$</p>
<p>Equation (1.8), printed p. 3, with its complete surrounding statement:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Note that all bounds of the form $\varphi_{p,q,r}(x)$ can be derived from the inequality</p>
<p>$$Y_{a,b}(x):=(x^2+a)R^2(x)+bxR(x)&lt;(&gt;)c\qquad(1.8)$$</p>
<p>for all $x&gt;0$, $a,b\in\mathbb R$, where</p>
<p>$$c=\lim_{x\to0}Y_{a,b}(x)=\frac{a\pi}{2}
\quad\text{or}\quad c=\lim_{x\to\infty}Y_{a,b}(x)=b+1.$$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Equation (3.7), printed p. 6, gives the coefficients following the asymptotic
expansion in Theorem 3.1:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>where</p>
<p>$$u_n\equiv u_n(a,b)=(b+1)\frac{2^n\Gamma(n+1/2)}{\Gamma(1/2)}
+(n-a)(n-1)!\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}
\frac{2^k\Gamma(k+1/2)}{\Gamma(1/2)k!}.\qquad(3.7)$$</p>
<p>In particular, $u_1=b+2-a$, $u_2=3b+7-2a$, $u_3=15b+36-7a$, and $u_4=105b-36a+249$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 7.1, printed p. 27, complete statement:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Moreover, inspired by Theorem 4.2, we propose the following conjecture.</p>
<p>Conjecture 7.1 Let</p>
<p>$$D_n(x,a)=Y_{a,a-2}(x)-\left(a-1+\sum_{k=1}^{n}
\frac{(-1)^ku_k}{x^{2k}}\right),$$</p>
<p>where $u_k$ is defined by (3.7). Then $x\mapsto(-1)^nD_n(x,-1)$ is CM on $(0,\infty)$.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Formal reading</h3>
<p>The namespace is <code>D5.S3.Analytic.XuYangMillsRemainder.Result</code>.
The definitions <code>millsRatio</code>, <code>Y</code>, <code>u</code> and <code>D</code> retain the displayed
Gaussian integral, polynomial expression, Gamma coefficients and finite
remainder. Here $\Gamma$ is Euler's Gamma function, $k!$ is the factorial of the
nonnegative integer $k$, and all integrals use Lebesgue measure.
<code>CompletelyMonotoneOn f</code> means $f$ is $C^\infty$ on $(0,\infty)$
and every signed derivative $(-1)^m f^{(m)}(x)$ is nonnegative there.
<code>claim</code> quantifies over every integer $n\ge2$; <code>result</code> states <code>claim</code>.
The source does not display a lower bound for $n$ in Conjecture 7.1.
The restriction $n\ge2$ is necessary: $n=1$ fails, as explained in Triage.</p>
