 Zhang and Bing Xie, <em>A Survey of Bargmann Invariants: Geometric Foundations and Applications</em>, arXiv:2601.01858v2, p. 26, ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The quantity τ_n predicts the upper bound for the imaginary part of any nth-order Bargmann invariant. This immediately raises the question: what is the complete set of n-tuples Ψ = (|ψ_1⟩, …, |ψ_n⟩) in ℂ² that achieve Im Tr(ψ_1 … ψ_n) = τ_n? We leave this characterization, along with the operational interpretation of the condition z ∈ 𝓑_{n+1} ∖ 𝓑_n, as interesting open problems for future work.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The first question is <strong>Proved</strong> with the exact conventions in <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/19224">preregistration #19224</a>. For every $n\ge3$, a tuple of unit vectors in $\mathbb C^2$ attains</p>
<p>$$\tau_n=\frac{\cos^n(\pi/n)}{\cos^{n-1}(\pi/[2(n-1)])}$$</p>
<p>if and only if $\psi_j=\eta_jUv_j$ for one common $2\times2$ unitary $U$, independent phases $|\eta_j|=1$, and</p>
<p>$$v_j=(\sqrt{1-t_n},\sqrt{t_n},e^{2\pi ij/n}),\qquad t_n=\frac{1-\tan(\pi/[2(n-1)])/\tan(\pi/n)}2.$$</p>
<p>The <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/zhangxie2026bargmann.md">Library note</a> gives Definition 3.1, Corollary 5.5, Eq. (5.15), and the source question.</p>
