ss="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Analysis &amp; dynamics</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2007</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Refutation of the sum-condition Luke upper bound</strong><span class="result-excerpt">An alternating lower bracket contradicts the sum-condition Luke upper bound.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Sitnik, Inequalities and monotonicity of ratios for generalized hypergeometric function, J.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The sum condition permits an individual denominator parameter smaller than its numerator partner, destroying the first-order comparison required by the upper expression.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Geometric comparison gives summability and monotonicity.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>An alternating lower bracket contradicts the sum-condition Luke upper bound.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="karp-sitnik-2009-luke-upper-bound"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Refutation of the sum-condition Luke upper bound</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. Karp and S. M. Sitnik, <em>Inequalities and monotonicity of ratios for
generalized hypergeometric function</em>, J. Approx. Theory 161 (2009),
337–352, DOI 10.1016/j.jat.2008.10.002, arXiv:math/0703084v2,
Conjecture 2, printed p. 14, equation (38):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For 0 &lt; σ ≤ min(a₁, a₂, . . . , a_q), ∑ᵢ₌₁ᵠ (bᵢ − aᵢ) &gt; 0 and x &gt; 0:</p>
<p>$\displaystyle {}<em>{q+1}F_q(\sigma,(a_q);(b_q);-x)&lt;\frac{1}{\left[1+x\prod</em>{i=1}^q\frac{\Gamma(a_i)\Gamma(b_i-\sigma)}{\Gamma(b_i)\Gamma(a_i-\sigma)}\right]^\sigma}.\qquad (38)$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The preceding sentence reads:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The asymptotic formula (16) and numerical experiments suggest the following</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Formula (16), printed p. 5, is preceded by:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It follows that for σ &lt; min(a₁, a₂, . . . , a_q) we will have</p>
</blockquote>
<p>$$ {}_{q+1}F_q(\sigma,(a_q);(b_q);-z)
=\frac{\prod_i\Gamma(a_i-\sigma)\Gamma(b_i)}
{\prod_i\Gamma(a_i)\Gamma(b_i-\sigma)}z^{-\sigma}
+o(z^{-\sigma}),\qquad z\to\infty. $$</p>
<p>The quotation transcribes the extracted display, which places a $\sigma$
next to the Gamma product. Write
$K=\prod_{i=1}^q\Gamma(a_i)\Gamma(b_i-\sigma)/(\Gamma(b_i)\Gamma(a_i-\sigma))$.
Separately, the asymptotically precise reading via (16) is
$1/(1+xK^{1/\sigma})^\sigma$: the inner exponent $1/\sigma$
makes its leading asymptotic coefficient agree with (16).
The counterexample has $\sigma=1$, where both readings coincide.</p>
<h3>Formal reading</h3>
<p>Lean names below have prefix <code>D5.S3.Analytic.KarpSitnikLuke.UpperResult</code>,
