/div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>The complete A381737 Conjecture 1 enumeration</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A381737, revision 28, by Mike Sheppard (2025), names the sequence:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Orders k of Hermite polynomials whose maximal coefficient in absolute value appears twice.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Its Formula field states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 1: a(n) = 2*n*(n + 2) + (n + 1)*(-1)^(n+1).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The offset is 1,1. Let $H_k$ be the physicists Hermite polynomial, with the
original coefficients represented by <code>HermiteSignedMode.physicistsHermite</code>
over the rationals. For natural $k$ and $j$, write</p>
<p>$$
M(k,j)\iff j\le k\ \land\ \forall i\le k,\quad
|[x^i]H_k|\le |[x^j]H_k|.
$$</p>
<p>Exactly two maxima means that there exist $u&lt;v$ such that, for every natural
$j$, $M(k,j)$ holds if and only if $j=u$ or $j=v$. The entire row from zero
through $k$ is compared, including zero coefficients of the other parity.
For every positive natural index, define the integer value</p>
<p>$$
a(n)=2n(n+2)+(n+1)(-1)^{n+1}.
$$</p>
<p>The complete assertion is that $a(n)&gt;0$ for every $n\ge1$, that
$a(n)&lt;a(m)$ whenever $1\le n&lt;m$, and that for every natural order $k$,
including zero, exactly two maxima occur if and only if there is exactly one
$n\ge1$ with $(k:\mathbb Z)=a(n)$. The additions and products in the formula
are integer operations; $n+1$ in the exponent is natural. There is no finite
cutoff or asymptotic qualification.</p>
