pan class="result-impact-year">2020</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Second-family singlet-like data exist in every dimension</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Cyclic translations supply the orthogonal matrices, difference labels, and sum-indexed shield vectors required for the second family in every dimension.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Vértesi, Bound entangled singlet-like states for quantum metrology, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Supplying the data in every dimension closes the second family&#x27;s construction gap.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The proof uses factorization.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Cyclic translations supply the orthogonal matrices, difference labels, and sum-indexed shield vectors required for the second family in every dimension.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Conjecture 1, the optimality assertion at TeX line 221, still requires an upper bound over all PPT states and remains open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="pal-toth-bene-vertesi-2021-second-family-all-dimensions"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Second-family singlet-like data exist in every dimension</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>K. F. Pál, G. Tóth, E. Bene and T. Vértesi, <em>Bound entangled singlet-like states for quantum metrology</em>, Phys. Rev. Research 3, 023101 (2021), arXiv:2002.12409v4, section “Second family of states and its quantum Fisher information,” state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For this family of states we have constructed analytical examples only for $d=3$ and $2^n.$ However, we believe that such states exist for all $d$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This quotation is from line 674 of the v4 TeX source <code>metroboundent53.tex</code>. Definition 2 and its accompanying requirements are at lines 556–640; the source's analytic constructions are in the appendix “Special properties of orthogonal matrices,” lines 1196–1308. See <a href="../Library/Estimation/paltothbenevertesi2021singlet.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25511">#25511</a> fixes the question as existence of the data required by Definition 2 for every natural $d\ge2$. In the module, <code>claim</code> asserts that there exist real $d\times d$ matrices $Q^k$, real vectors $\bar q_m\in\mathbb R^d$, labels $\mu_{ij}\in\mathrm{Fin},d$, real coefficients $a_{ij}$ and complex vectors $s_m\in\mathbb C^{d\times d}$ satisfying all six conditions below. All indices range over $\mathrm{Fin},d$; the coefficient $a_{ij}$ is the source's $S_{ij}$.</p>
<p>Write $N_m=#{(i,j):a_{ij}\ne0,\ \mu_{ij}=m}$, the module's <code>multiplicity</code>, and define the module's <code>tVec</code> by $t_m(i,j)=a_{ij}/\sqrt d$ when $a_{ij}\ne0$ and $\mu_{ij}=m$, and $t_m(i,j)=0$ otherwise. The conditions in <code>IsSecondFamilyData</code> are:</p>
<ol>
<li>$(Q^k)^{\mathsf T}Q^k=I_d$ for every $k$.</li>
<li>$\sum_k\bar q_m(k)\bar q_n(k)=\delta_{mn}$ for every $m,n$.</li>
<li>$Q^k_{ij}=a_{ij}\bar q_{\mu_{ij}}(k)$ for every $i,j,k$.</li>
<li>$a_{ij}\ne0$ implies $a_{ij}^2=d/N_{\mu_{ij}}$ for every $i,j$.</li>
<li>$\sum_{i,j}\overline{s_m(i,j)}s_n(i,j)=\delta_{mn}$ for every $m,n$.</li>
<li>$(\sum_m s_ms_m^\dagger)^\Gamma=\sum_m t_mt_m^\dagger$, where $M^\Gamma_{(i,j),(i',j')}=M_{(i,j'),(i',j)}$.</li>
</ol>
