multiplicity of graph Laplacians</em>, arXiv:2610.26875v1, Problem 30, p. 27, ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Do there exist infinite families of connected twin-free graphs satisfying exactly one of the two equalities mm_L(G)=dim(G), mm_Q(G)=dim(G)? The computational census shows that finite twin-free examples occur in both directions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The result answers this existence question affirmatively. Here $\dim(G)$ is
the minimum size of a resolving set for actual shortest-path distances,
$\operatorname{mm}_L$ and $\operatorname{mm}_Q$ maximize real eigenspace
dimension over every real eigenvalue, and twin-freeness excludes both true
and false twins. The signless Laplacian is $D(G)+A(G)$.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Formal counterpart |
| --- | --- |
| Finite simple graphs | <code>SimpleGraph (Fin (orders i))</code> in <code>claim</code> |
| Connected | the connectivity conjunct for every natural index |
| Twin-free, both kinds | <code>TwinFree</code>, defined using neighborhoods with the other vertex removed |
| Metric dimension | minimum cardinality of an injective distance representation |
| Maximum multiplicity | <code>maximumMultiplicity</code>, using actual real eigenspaces and all real eigenvalues |
| Exactly one equality | disjunction of one equality and the other inequality |
| Infinite family | all-natural indexing and pairwise nonisomorphism |</p>
<p>The concrete witness has $G_i=\Theta_{3,3,6(i+1)+4}$ and order
$6(i+1)+9$. The source permits either equality direction; the construction
uniformly supplies the Laplacian direction. No source clause is dropped.
The fixed finite carrier only labels the graph; pairwise nonisomorphism
expresses infinitude independently of labels.</p>
