<a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2611.01243v1">arXiv:2611.01243v1</a>, Definition 1 and
section 6, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This raises the question of whether a(n) is 2-regular: does there exist a finite family of integer sequences containing a(n) such that, for each sequence b(n) in the family, both b(2n) and b(2n+1) are integer linear combinations of sequences in the family [16]?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This dossier anchors exactly that existence question. The negative answer is
<code>D5/S1/Recurrence/Partitions/HalvingPartitionNonregularity.result</code>.
The resolution kind <code>Refuted</code> refers to the proposed existence of such a family.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>For every natural total n, the source carrier consists of every finite ordered
list of positive integers summing to n. With virtual predecessor p0=n, each
successive part belongs to the value set consisting of the lower and upper
integer halves of its predecessor. Equal halves give one value. Repeated parts,
arbitrary finite lengths and repeated terminal ones are retained. At zero the
empty list is the sole object.</p>
<p>The count a(n) is the cardinality of that independently defined carrier. Its
integer cast admits no positive finite dimension d, vector sequence F and fixed
integer matrices M0,M1 satisfying, for every n including zero,</p>
<p>$$
F(n)_0=a(n),\qquad F(2n)=M_0F(n),\qquad F(2n+1)=M_1F(n).
$$</p>
<p>The same theorem proves both directions of the matrix equivalence with finite
indexed families for every integer sequence, including arbitrary finite index
types with a distinguished member. Duplicate and dependent constituent sequences
are allowed. The distinguished member makes the family nonempty; no dimension
bound, basis assumption or invertibility condition enters the statement. The
result also identifies the actual-list count with the filtered Composition
cardinality for every n and proves a(0)=1.</p>
