span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Yero, The weak k-metric dimension of the direct product of complete graphs, arXiv:2605.22307v1, Section 3, paragraph S3.p17.1:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source gives exact formulas for the other weak parameters and leaves the weak-four case open.</p><p><span>The idea</span>View the landmarks as edges of a bipartite incidence graph on n row labels and n column labels.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Exact weak-four metric dimension of complete graph direct squares</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="farhan-kuziak-yero-2026-direct-square-weak-four"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Weak-four metric dimension of complete graph direct squares</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Mohammad Farhan, Dorota Kuziak and Ismael G. Yero, <em>The weak k-metric dimension
of the direct product of complete graphs</em>, arXiv:2605.22307v1,
Section 3, paragraph <code>S3.p17.1</code>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The result above clearly leads to a question concerning the exact value of
wdim4(Kn × Kn) when n ≥ 4.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section 6, first bullet, asks for the remaining exact formula at k = 4 and
whether the upper bound in Theorem 3.3 is the exact value.
This is one question over all integer orders at least four.</p>
<h3>Exact value and semantics</h3>
<p>For every integer $n\ge4$,</p>
<p>$$
\operatorname{wdim}<em>4(K_n\times K_n)
=2n+\left\lceil\frac{2n}{11}\right\rceil+\mathbf 1</em>{n=5}.
$$</p>
<p>The vertex set is $[n]\times[n]$. Two vertices are adjacent exactly when
both coordinates differ. This is the direct product in the source.
For $n\ge3$ the actual shortest-walk distance is zero at equal vertices,
one when both coordinates differ, and two otherwise. In the last case,
a third row and a third column give a common neighbor. The graph is connected.</p>
<p>A finite landmark set $S$ is weak four-resolving if</p>
<p>$$
\sum_{w\in S}|d(x,w)-d(y,w)|\ge4
\quad\text{for every distinct }x,y.
$$</p>
<p>The introductory source definition omits the word “distinct.” Proposition 2.2
explicitly restricts to distinct pairs and supplies the coordinate identities
used by the paper. Self-pairs have separation zero and cannot be intended
when the paper discusses positive k. The formal predicate retains this
explicit distinct-pair intent.</p>
<p>The dimension is the least cardinality of such a set. The formal construction
proves that its feasible family is nonempty for every order in the question;
the universal lower bound and an equality-size set prove attainment. Natural
number division represents the ceiling as $(2n+10)/11$.</p>
<p>At order nine the answer is twenty. The source upper bound is twenty-one
there, so Theorem 3.3 is not always sharp. The answer covers every order;
this strict inequality alone is not the whole result.</p>
