ue modulo three.   released-dossier 0caf4b02132d5a1fd6e5787c6bc6e3ba9c67498d8782e6f334a9fccf63d2c0a1 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The binary kite construction works exactly when $n$ is not one modulo three</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Binary kite forms are doubly perfect exactly off one residue modulo three.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Goedicke, Thirty-six officers, artisanally entangled (2025), arXiv:2504.15401v2, subsection “Construction of doubly perfect functions”, Remarks after Theorem 5 (artisanal.tex, line 2316):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Their Theorem 2 turns doubly perfect functions into complex Hadamard matrices proportional to two-unitaries (statement at lines 505–526; proof at lines 2144–2202).</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Binary kite forms are doubly perfect exactly off one residue modulo three.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="gross-goedicke-2025-kite-shaped-quadratic-forms"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The binary kite construction works exactly when $n$ is not one modulo three</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. Gross and P. Goedicke, <em>Thirty-six officers, artisanally entangled</em> (2025), arXiv:2504.15401v2, subsection “Construction of doubly perfect functions”, Remarks after Theorem 5 (<code>artisanal.tex</code>, line 2316):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As a concrete example, computer experiments indicate that the matrices with ``kite-shaped support'', $A_{ij}=B_{ij}=1$ iff $i+j\leq n+1$, give a solution for all $n$ not congruent to $1$ modulo $3$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The quotation is from the downloaded v2 TeX source <code>artisanal.tex</code>, MD5 <code>29b677f0287fbbb335cd79eb4f5f33d2</code>. Its surrounding paragraph (lines 2304–2316) takes $A,B$ to be nonsingular symmetric binary matrices with $AB-I$ also nonsingular, and uses $N=\operatorname{diag}(A,B)$ as the coefficient matrix of a quadratic exponential. See <a href="../Library/QuadraticForms/grossgoedicke2025artisanal.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>For $n\in\mathbb N$, let $K_n\in M_n(\mathbb F_2)$ have entry $1$ exactly when $i+j\le n+1$, with indices $1,\ldots,n$, and entry $0$ otherwise; at $n=0$ this is the empty matrix. Put $N_n=\operatorname{diag}(K_n,K_n)$ and $J_n=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_n\-I_n&amp;0\end{pmatrix}$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20969">#20969</a> fixes the following reading, including the converse to the source's observation: for every $n\ge1$,</p>
<ul>
<li>$K_n$ is symmetric and has trivial right kernel;</li>
<li>$N_n$ has trivial right kernel;</li>
<li>$K_n^2-I_n$ has trivial right kernel if and only if $n\not\equiv1\pmod3$;</li>
<li>$N_n+J_n$ has trivial right kernel if and only if $n\not\equiv1\pmod3$.</li>
