$T(n+m,m)=(n+m+1)P(n+1,n+1,m)$ for all $n,m\in\mathbb N$.
The preregistration is <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21639">#21639</a>.
The source is OEIS-only, with no claim-source DOI or arXiv identifier identified.</p>
<p>The source NAME defines a lower triangular matrix by $T=D+\operatorname{SHIFT_LEFT}(T^2)$ with diagonal $1,2,3,\ldots$.
Thus $T(a,b)=0$ for $a&lt;b$, $T(a,a)=a+1$, and
$T(a,b)=\sum_{j=b+1}^{a}T(a,j)T(j,b+1)$ for $b&lt;a$.
Kurkov's three-argument table satisfies $P(r,1,m)=1$ for $r\ge1$,
$P(r,k,m)=P(r-1,k,m)+(r+m)P(r,k-1,m)$ for $1&lt;k&lt;r$, and
$P(r,r,m)=(r+m)P(r,r-1,m)$ for $r\ge2$.
Values outside $r\ge k\ge1$ are unrestricted.
This table is distinct from the source's earlier two-argument conjugating matrix also called $P$.</p>
