act-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Finite generation of one-parameter polynomial Vassiliev algebras</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Every family of rational univariate polynomials has a finite subfamily generating the same algebra.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Sleptsov, Algebraic structures of Vassiliev invariants for knot families, arXiv:2508.02385v2, Nuclear Physics B 1022 (2026), 117268, conjecture in the abstract (main.tex, line 165):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source&#x27;s primary invariants generate the descended algebra: every restricted invariant is a polynomial in them.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Natural degree zero includes the zero polynomial.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every family of rational univariate polynomials has a finite subfamily generating the same algebra.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="lanina-sleptsov-2026-vassiliev-one-parameter-finite-generation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Finite generation of one-parameter polynomial Vassiliev algebras</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>E. Lanina and A. Sleptsov, <em>Algebraic structures of Vassiliev invariants for knot families</em>, arXiv:2508.02385v2, Nuclear Physics B 1022 (2026), 117268, conjecture in the abstract (<code>main.tex</code>, line 165):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We conjecture that such 1-parametric algebra of Vassiliev invariants is always finitely generated, while in the case of more parameters, we provide example of the knot family with infinite number of generators.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section 4.1, “Generic one-parametric family” (<code>main.tex</code>, line 1166), asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Can this force such a 1-parametric algebra of Vassiliev invariants to be infinitely generated? Examples and reasonings provide the intuition that a 1-parametric algebra of polynomial Vassiliev invariants always has only a finite number of generators. Still, we currently lack strict mathematical proof.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source restricts attention to rational-valued invariants (<code>main.tex</code>, line 350), defines primary invariants as generators of the algebra restricted to a family (line 356), and represents the one-parameter restrictions by polynomials with rational coefficients (lines 1114–1117). These passages are recorded in <a href="../Library/Topology/laninasleptsov2026vassilievfamilies.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22008">#22008</a> fixes the algebraic reading: for every index type $\iota:\mathrm{Type}$ and every family $p:\iota\to\mathbb Q[X]$, there is a finite set $s$ of indices such that</p>
<p>$\mathbb Q[p_i\mid i\in s]=\mathbb Q[p_i\mid i:\iota]$.</p>
<p>Here $\mathbb Q[S]$ denotes the smallest rational subalgebra containing $S$. Thus the generators can be chosen from the original family of restricted invariants. This is the statement of <code>claim</code>, proved by <code>result</code>.</p>
