put.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Independent local measurements attain the global unambiguous optimum for sequences of distinct equal-overlap states.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="murshid-gupta-russo-bandyopadhyay-2026-no-repetition-unambiguous-discrimination"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Optimal unambiguous discrimination without repetition for equal-overlap states</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Murshid, T. Gupta, V. Russo and S. Bandyopadhyay, <em>Quantum nonlocality without entanglement and state discrimination measures</em>, arXiv:2506.20560v3; Quantum 10, 2174 (2026), consider the uniform ensemble $\mathcal E_{N,k}$ of ordered products of $k$ distinct members of $N$ normalized states $\psi_i\in\mathbb C^N$ with $\langle\psi_i,\psi_j\rangle=s\in(0,1)$ for $i\ne j$.</p>
<p>Their conjecture appears in the TeX source <code>revised_02.tex</code>, lines 970–984, subsection “Conjecture for multipartite systems”. The quotations retain the source macro <code>\E</code>, meaning $\mathcal E$, and its equation reference; the displays are written inline.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For the family of ensembles of multipartite pure product states $\E_{N,k}$ given by Equation \eqref{eq:gensemble} the following holds for all $N \geq 3$ and $2 \leq k \leq N$</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>$p_{<em>{L}}^{me}\left(\E</em>{N,k}\right) &lt;p_{<em>{G}}^{me}\left(\E</em>{N,k}\right),$</p>
<p>$p_{<em>{L}}^{ud}\left(\E</em>{N,k}\right) =p_{<em>{G}}^{ud}\left(\E</em>{N,k}\right).$</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Moreover, the optimal unambiguous discrimination probability of $\E_{N,k}$ is given by</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>$p_{<em>{G}}^{ud}\left(\E</em>{N,k}\right) = \begin{cases} (1-s)^k, &amp; \text{ when } k &lt; N \ (1-s)^{N-1}(1+\left(N-1\right)s), &amp; \text{ when } k=N. \end{cases}$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>T. Gupta, S. Murshid and S. Bandyopadhyay, <em>Unambiguous discrimination of sequences of quantum states</em>, arXiv:2402.06365v2; Phys. Rev. A 109, 052222 (2024), pose the earlier no-repetition question in <code>GMBjournal-revision-02.tex</code>, lines 883–903:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In our scenario, as well as in more general ones (which we could not solve), repetitions of states are allowed in a sequence. The third problem considers the situation where it is not. Restrict $k&lt;N$ and by $S'_{N,k}$ denote the set of sequences of length $k$, where no element is repeated.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Now assume that the inner products of the states $\left|\psi_{i}\right\rangle$ are equal and positive, say, $s&gt;0$. Then $S_{N,k}^{\prime}\subset S_{N,k}$, where $S_{N,k}$ is the set of sequences considered in this paper. Under these restricted conditions, numerical experiments ($N=3$, $k=3$) still suggest that the optimal probability for distinguishing between the elements of $S_{N,k}^{\prime}$ unambiguously once again obeys $(1-s)^{k}$. However, for arbitrary values of both $N$ and $k$, whether one could benefit from collective measurements in this scenario is an interesting problem to consider in the future.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source definitions and locators are also recorded in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/murshidguptarussobandyopadhyay2026nonlocality.md">the 2026 literature note</a> and <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/guptamurshidbandyopadhyay2024sequences.md">the 2024 literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21233">#21233</a> fixes the settlement as the unambiguous clauses of the conjecture, together with the 2024 question for $2\le k&lt;N$ under these hypotheses. These are proved. The minimum-error clause of the conjecture is not settled by this result.</p>
