<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>Over $\mathbb Q[[X]]$, set
$H(X)=\sum_{m\ge1}X^{m^2}/m^m$ and $A(X)=\exp(H(X))$.
For every $n\in\mathbb N$, including zero, there exists $k\in\mathbb Z$ with
$(n!:\mathbb Q)[X^n]A=1+6(k:\mathbb Q)$.
There is no integrality assumption or finite cutoff. The denominator is $m^m$,
not $m!$, and the exponent is $m^2$, not a triangular number.</p>
<p>The public <code>H</code> is the coefficientwise guarded <code>finsum</code> over natural indices;
private <code>coeff_H</code> proves its degree-n support lies in <code>range(n+1)</code>.
The public <code>A</code> substitutes <code>H</code> into the rational formal exponential.
The public <code>innerIntegral(n)</code> sums the natural quotient $n!/m^m$ over
$0\le m\le n$ with $0&lt;m$ and $m^2=n$, zero otherwise.
The private <code>factorial_ratio</code> and <code>eCoeff_H</code> identify its rational cast with
$n![X^n]H$. The sole public theorem <code>result</code> is the exact all-index assertion.</p>
