</span>— Jaroslav Krizek, OEIS A265714, revision 23.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The two predicates ask respectively for a first floor level and a first threshold crossing of the same exact divisor sum.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Prime divisor terms give a lower bound by sums of 1/(p+1).</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Reciprocal sigma sums and the first crossing of every positive integer threshold.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a265714-positive-threshold-common-minimum"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Common positive threshold minima for reciprocal-sigma sums</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><blockquote>
<p>a(n) = the smallest number k such that floor(Sum_{d|k} 1/sigma(d)) = n.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture: a(n) is also the smallest number k such that Sum_{d|k} 1/sigma(d) &gt;= n.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>— Jaroslav Krizek, OEIS A265714, revision 23.</p>
<p>This dossier anchors exactly the common-minimum conjecture. For every
natural r&gt;0, let g(k) be the exact rational sum of 1/sigma(d) over all
positive divisors d of the positive natural k, where sigma(d) is the
sum of all positive divisors of d. The sets
{k&gt;0 : floor(g(k))=r} and {k&gt;0 : g(k)&gt;=r} must both be nonempty and have
the SAME least element. The floor is ordinary floor, r=1 is included,
and neither r nor k has a finite upper bound. There is no additional
existence or integrality premise. The source's separate integrality
question and proposed numerical minima are outside this single assertion.</p>
