ing row sums of the Fibonacci harmonic triangle have the conjectured closed form.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Conjecture: Alt. row sums of the harmonic triangle are Fibonacci(n-2) / Fibonacci(n+1), where Fibonacci(-1) = 1.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>This is a first-tier recent OEIS conjecture selected in the implementation brief.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Moving the diagonal replaces its old denominator and adds the next diagonal term, giving, for n &gt;= 2, S(n+1) - S(n) = 2(-1)^n / (F(n+1)F(n+2)).</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Schulte&#x27;s alternating row sums of the Fibonacci harmonic triangle have the conjectured closed form.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a378277-harmonic-triangle-alternating-row-sums"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Alternating row sums of the Fibonacci harmonic triangle</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A378277 records the following conjecture:</p>
