esult-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Spectral bound for conditionally 2-positive maps</strong><span class="result-excerpt">ConditionalTwoPositiveSpectralBound for the spectral bound of conditionally two-positive maps.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Muratore-Ginanneschi, Universal Bound on the Eigenvalues of 2-Positive Trace-Preserving Maps, arXiv:2506.02145v1, §3 “Outlook”, page 11, question (10), asks:</p><p><span>The idea</span>Its Hilbert–Schmidt adjoint is 2-positive and trace preserving, so the formalized Theorem 1 applies.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>ConditionalTwoPositiveSpectralBound for the spectral bound of conditionally two-positive maps.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="vom-ende-chruscinski-kimura-muratore-ginanneschi-2025-conditional-two-positive-bound"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Spectral bound for conditionally 2-positive maps</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>F. vom Ende, D. Chruściński, G. Kimura and P. Muratore-Ginanneschi,
<em>Universal Bound on the Eigenvalues of 2-Positive Trace-Preserving Maps</em>,
arXiv:2506.02145v1, §3 “Outlook”, page 11, question (10), asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hence one may wonder whether all (generators of) completely positive maps, resp. 2-positive maps satisfy</p>
</blockquote>
<p>$$
\mathrm{tr}(L)\le d\min\Re\sigma(L)+(d^2-d)\max\Re\sigma(L).
$$</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A map $L$ is called conditionally 2-positive if $e^{tL}$ is 2-positive for all $t\ge0$.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>With this, (10) becomes really a conjecture about the spectrum of arbitrary conditionally 2-positive maps.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>VCKM-10 quantifies every integer $d\ge1$ and every complex-linear
endomorphism $L$ of $\mathbb C^{d\times d}$ satisfying this definition.
There is no trace-preservation assumption. The trace is the superoperator
trace; the spectrum is the set of roots of the characteristic polynomial.
The Lean claim also asserts that the superoperator trace is real.</p>
