Costa and C. Branciard, <em>Genuinely Multipartite Noncausality</em>, arXiv:1708.07663v2 (Quantum 1, 39, 2017), Section III.C, ask in <code>genuine_multi_noncausal.tex</code>, line 343:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is interesting to ask if this inequality is tight (i.e., facet inducing) for all $N$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Their conjecture is stated at line 344:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By explicitly enumerating the vertices of the 2-causal polytope for $N=4$ (of which there are $136,818,592$) we were able to verify that $J_1(4)\ge 0$ is indeed also a facet, and we conjecture that this is true for all $N$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Let $U={0,\ldots,N-1}$ and represent binary inputs and outputs by their supports $T,A\subseteq U$. A deterministic lazy two-causal strategy consists of a nonempty proper first group $K\subsetneq U$ and a function $f$ on input supports such that $f(T)\subseteq T$ for every $T$, and
$T\cap K=S\cap K$ implies $f(T)\cap K=f(S)\cap K$ for every $T,S$. The complementary group's outputs may depend on the entire input. Its deterministic table is $P_f(T,A)=1$ when $f(T)=A$ and $0$ otherwise. Let $C_N$ be the real convex hull of these tables.</p>
<p>In these coordinates, Eq. (10) is
$J_1(p)=\sum_{\varnothing\ne K\subsetneq U}p(K,K)-p(U,U)\ge0$.
Laziness makes $p(K,K)$ exactly the marginal event in the source's formula. Definition 2, the deterministic characterization, and the lazy convention occur at TeX lines 146–151, 203–205, and 213–214; Eq. (10) is at lines 333–337. See <a href="../Library/Observer/abbottwechscostabranciard2017gmnc.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23904">#23904</a> fixes the reading: for every natural $N\ge2$, $J_1$ is nonnegative on $C_N$, and $F_N=C_N\cap{p\mid J_1(p)=0}$ has affine dimension one less than $C_N$. This is proved. Precisely, the module uses $d(S)=\dim_{\mathbb R}\operatorname{span}{p-q\mid p,q\in S}$ and asserts $d(F_N)+1=d(C_N)$.</p>
