Question 8, printed p. 13:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Question 8. Is it true that the gamma-gamma distribution satisfies the NBU property?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The preceding definition, printed p. 13, reads:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By definition a life distribution satisfies the new-is-better-than-used (NBU) property if u ↦ log F̄(u) is sub-additive, i.e.</p>
<p>$$\overline F(u_1+u_2)\le\overline F(u_1)\overline F(u_2)$$</p>
<p>for all u₁, u₂ &gt; 0.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The density, §4, printed p. 10, is introduced as follows:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The probability density function fₐ,ᵦ,α : (0, ∞) → (0, ∞) of the three parameter gamma-gamma random variable is defined by (see [21])</p>
<p>$$f_{a,b,\alpha}(u)=\frac{2(ab)^{(a+b)/2}u^{(a+b)/2-1}}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)\alpha^{(a+b)/2}}K_{a-b}!\left(2\sqrt{\frac{ab}{\alpha}u}\right),$$</p>
<p>where a, b &gt; 0 are the distribution shaping parameters, Kν stands for the modified Bessel function of the second kind, and α &gt; 0 is the mean of the gamma-gamma random variable.</p>
</blockquote>
