div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Kryvonos (ed.), Open problems on polynomials, their zero distribution and related questions: 2023, arXiv:2312.07754v1, section “Snake polynomials with positive Chebyshev expansions”, states:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Nonnegative Chebyshev expansion is the hypothesis under which Nikolov–Shadrin, arXiv:1210.7795, prove extremality of the snake polynomial in Markov–Duffin–Schaeffer inequalities with a majorant.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The maximum of the constant convex function and the positively scaled absolute-value function with an added constant is convex.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>An even convex majorant has a degree-six snake with a negative Chebyshev coefficient.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>UP24 Problem 7.1, asking for classes with nonnegative or sign-alternating expansions, remains open.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="nikolov-2023-snake-polynomial-chebyshev-expansion"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>Nonnegative Chebyshev expansions of snake polynomials</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>L. Kryvonos (ed.), <em>Open problems on polynomials, their zero distribution and related questions: 2023</em>, arXiv:2312.07754v1, section “Snake polynomials with positive Chebyshev expansions”, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Consider a majorant µ ∈ C[−1, 1], µ(x) ≥ 0, x ∈ [−1, 1]. If there exists P ∈ Pn, P ≠ 0, such that −µ(x) ≤ P(x) ≤ µ(x), x ∈ [−1, 1], then there exists a unique (up to orientation) polynomial ωµ ∈ Pn called snake polynomial associated with µ, which oscillates most between ±µ.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The n-th snake polynomial ωµ is uniquely determined by the following properties: a) |ωµ(x)| ≤ µ(x) x ∈ [−1, 1]; b) There exists a set δ* = (τi*)_{i=0}^n, 1 ≥ τ0* &gt; · · · &gt; τn* ≥ −1, such that ωµ(τi*) = (−1)^i µ(τi*), i = 0, . . . , n</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture (G.N.) If µ ≥ 0 is a continuous even convex function in [−1, 1], then the associated with µ snake polynomials have non-negative expansion in the Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The assertion is repeated as Conjecture 7.1 (Geno Nikolov) in <em>Open problems UP24</em>, arXiv:2504.04845, §7. Here $\mathcal P_n$ denotes polynomials of degree at most $n$, and $T_k$ denotes the first-kind Chebyshev polynomial.</p>
