rjee, characterizing qubit channels in the context of quantum teleportation, phys. ccritical is the least entanglement of the initial pure state for which some qubit channel on bob&#x27;s side can still deliver a state that teleports every input state with fidelity above the classical 2/3. necessity holds for every finite kraus family, and a non-unital amplitude-damping channel attains sufficiency. put r = 2a − 1 = √(1 − c²), the length of alice&#x27;s bloch vector, which every channel on bob and every unitary on alice preserves.  released-dossier aed2b3dcef4353f8eab0e1b15c72507a8bbfc44a77fd703b3c4d3e8cc0529647 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2021</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The exact critical concurrence for universal teleportation through qubit channels</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Necessity holds for every finite Kraus family, and a non-unital amplitude-damping channel attains sufficiency.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Banerjee, Characterizing qubit channels in the context of quantum teleportation, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>CCritical is the least entanglement of the initial pure state for which some qubit channel on Bob&#x27;s side can still deliver a state that teleports every input state with fidelity above the classical 2/3.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Put r = 2a − 1 = √(1 − C²), the length of Alice&#x27;s Bloch vector, which every channel on Bob and every unitary on Alice preserves.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Necessity holds for every finite Kraus family, and a non-unital amplitude-damping channel attains sufficiency.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="ghosal-das-banerjee-2021-critical-concurrence-universal-teleportation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The exact critical concurrence for universal teleportation through qubit channels</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Ghosal, D. Das and S. Banerjee, <em>Characterizing qubit channels in the context of quantum teleportation</em>, Phys. Rev. A 103, 052422 (2021), arXiv:2102.02054v2, send one half of |Ψa⟩ = √a|00⟩ + √(1 − a)|11⟩ (1/2 &lt; a &lt; 1, concurrence C = 2√(a(1 − a))) through a qubit channel Λ and ask when the final state (id ⊗ Λ)(|Ψa⟩⟨Ψa|) is useful for universal quantum teleportation (UQT).</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The criterion.</strong> A two-qubit state is useful for UQT if and only if, in its canonical form under product unitaries, |t₁₁| = |t₂₂| = |t₃₃| &gt; 1/3 (§II.B–§II.C).</li>
<li><strong>Unital and non-unital channels.</strong> Unital channels need C &gt; 1/2. For the two-Kraus non-unital family of Eq. (50), the final state is useful for UQT exactly when C &gt; √((2/3)(4 + √3)(1 − (4 + √3)/6)) ≈ 0.41.</li>
<li><strong>The question.</strong> Conjecture 1 states that the final state is useful for UQT for some non-unital channels if and only if C_Critical &lt; C &lt; 1, &quot;where 0 &lt; C_Critical ≤ 0.41. We could not determine the precise value of C_Critical; further investigations are needed.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumChannels/ghosal2021qubitchannelsteleportation.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16624">#16624</a> fixes the reading: &quot;useful for UQT&quot; means that some product of unitaries brings the state to correlation matrix −tI₃ with t &gt; 1/3, and channels are finite Kraus families. The question settled is the precise value of C_Critical: C_Critical = C_* = √(5 − 2√3)/3 = 0.4131045583…, the exact value of the source's radical. The source's printed bound &quot;C_Critical ≤ 0.41&quot; is the rounding of that radical; taken literally as the rational 41/100 it would be false, so the settlement answers the precise-value question and does not assert the printed inequality. Conjecture 1's further clause that only a strict subset of the non-unital channels succeeds is not part of the formal statement.</p>
