ng sigma and radical formulas.  released-dossier f23725e5d530c20fa7139ca1a4b2c99f82d53db16a32b79d4ad638620cdbaabf "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Number theory</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>Posed in</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2017</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Yanev&#x27;s general sigma-radical identity</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Yanev&#x27;s identity expresses every positive divisor-power sum through ordinary divisor sums and the radical.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>a(n) = sigman(n): sum of n-th powers of divisors of n.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The identity expresses a divisor sum of any positive exponent using only ordinary divisor sums and the distinct prime factors of the input.</p><p><span>The idea</span>On a prime power, apply the existing sigma and radical formulas.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Yanev&#x27;s identity expresses every positive divisor-power sum through ordinary divisor sums and the radical.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a023887-yanev-sigma-radical-identity"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Yanev&#x27;s general sigma-radical identity</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A023887, NAME (<code>%N</code>, verbatim):</p>
