<blockquote>
<p>Triangle (0 &lt;= k &lt;= n) read by rows: T(n, k) is the number of Schröder paths from (0,0) to (2n,0) having k peaks.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>COMMENT:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>T(n,k) is the number of Schroeder paths (i.e., consisting of steps U=(1,1), D=(1,-1), H=(2,0) and never going below the x-axis) from (0,0) to (2n,0), having k peaks. Example: T(2,1)=3 because we have UU*DD, U*DH and HU*D, the peaks being shown by *.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>FORMULA:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture: Sum_{k=0..n} (-1)^k*T(n,k)*(n+1-k)^2 = 1+n+n^2. - <em>Werner Schulte</em>, Jan 11 2017</p>
</blockquote>
