nds into a count of unstable spectral directions, independently of chain size and break placement. for a clamped steady lennard-jones chain with positive pre-stretch, positive bond lengths, at least one free particle and k ≥ 1 broken bonds, the positive force-jacobian eigenvalue count lies between k − 1 and k. for a hermitian matrix and a subspace with this positivity property, rhlinalg.finrankleposindexofposdefon bounds the subspace dimension by the positive eigenvalue count.  released-dossier b4ff44a35e0557ef27c0871608d5013397b6b731df640298989782a65a52565b "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Analysis &amp; dynamics</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2018</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The instability count of a clamped Lennard–Jones chain</strong><span class="result-excerpt">For a clamped steady Lennard-Jones chain with positive pre-stretch, positive bond lengths, at least one free particle and k ≥ 1 broken bonds, the positive force-Jacobian eigenvalue count lies between k − 1 and k.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Dobson, N-break states in a chain of nonlinear oscillators, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The conjecture turns a count of broken bonds into a count of unstable spectral directions, independently of chain size and break placement.</p><p><span>The idea</span>For a Hermitian matrix and a subspace with this positivity property, RHLinalg.finrankleposIndexofposDefOn bounds the subspace dimension by the positive eigenvalue count.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For a clamped steady Lennard-Jones chain with positive pre-stretch, positive bond lengths, at least one free particle and k ≥ 1 broken bonds, the positive force-Jacobian eigenvalue count lies between k − 1 and k.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="rodrigues-kevrekidis-dobson-2019-n-break-instability-count"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The instability count of a clamped Lennard–Jones chain</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. S. Rodrigues, P. G. Kevrekidis and M. Dobson, <em>N-break states in a chain of nonlinear oscillators</em>, Phys. Rev. E 99, 022201 (2019), arXiv:1802.03477v2, state in the abstract:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Each of these higher ``excited state'' solutions possesses an additional unstable pair of eigenvalues. We thus conjecture that $k$ break solutions will possess at least $k-1$ (and at most $k$) pairs of unstable eigenvalues.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This quotation is from <code>oscillatorsNN_resubmit.tex</code>, lines 55–59, in the v2 source, whose MD5 is <code>f0e3a2d64045c090b53b217d75e86a05</code>. The model and terminology are documented in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/rodrigueskevrekidisdobson2019nbreak.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>The module uses $m$ free particles, displacements $u_0,\ldots,u_{m+1}$, and zero-based bond indices $j=0,\ldots,m$. Its definitions are</p>
<p>$\phi(r)=r^{-12}-2r^{-6}$, $r_i=(13/7)^{1/6}$, and $r_j=d+u_{j+1}-u_j$.</p>
<p>The force at free particle $n+1$, indexed by $n=0,\ldots,m-1$, is $F_n=\phi'(r_{n+1})-\phi'(r_n)$. With $c_j=\phi''(r_j)$, the defined matrix $J$ has entries $J_{nn}=-c_n-c_{n+1}$, $J_{n,n+1}=J_{n+1,n}=c_{n+1}$, and zero elsewhere.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23865">#23865</a> fixes the reading: for every $m\ge1$, $d&gt;0$, and displacement vector with $u_0=u_{m+1}=0$, $F_n=0$ at every free particle, and all $r_j&gt;0$, if exactly $k\ge1$ bonds satisfy $r_j&gt;r_i$, then, for every Hermitian witness $h$ for $J$, the number $U=#{i\in\mathrm{Fin},m:0&lt;h.\mathrm{eigenvalues}(i)}$ satisfies $k-1\le U\le k$. The eigenvalues are counted with multiplicity. This is proved as <code>result : claim</code>.</p>
