f colin barker. a coordination sequence measures the growth of graph-distance spheres in a periodic net. the alternating prism graph has coordination numbers (13n squared + 4)/2 at positive even radii and (13n squared + 3)/2 at odd radii. the proof treats the specified alternating graph, without quantifying over arbitrary slab-orientation sequences.  released-dossier 9acdc546e3523d1ff57b4ec06d8aa6fe4ad4eb8f3add34a164a91ffa7d2da3fa "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Analysis &amp; dynamics</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>Posed in</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2018</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Barker’s coordination formulas for the tsi alternating prism graph</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The alternating prism graph has coordination numbers (13n squared + 4)/2 at positive even radii and (13n squared + 3)/2 at odd radii.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Sloane, Coordination sequence for &quot;tsi&quot; 3D uniform tiling, OEIS A299289 (2018), records the following conjectures of Colin Barker.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>A coordination sequence measures the growth of graph-distance spheres in a periodic net.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The proof treats the specified alternating graph, without quantifying over arbitrary slab-orientation sequences.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The alternating prism graph has coordination numbers (13n squared + 4)/2 at positive even radii and (13n squared + 3)/2 at odd radii.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="barker-2018-oeis-a299289-tsi-coordination-sequence"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Barker’s coordination formulas for the tsi alternating prism graph</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>N. J. A. Sloane, <em>Coordination sequence for &quot;tsi&quot; 3D uniform tiling</em>, OEIS A299289 (2018), records the following conjectures of Colin Barker. The source is the OEIS entry itself. Its plain-text response at <code>https://oeis.org/search?q=id:A299289&amp;fmt=text</code>, entry revision 16, has this verbatim <code>%F</code> block at lines 14–18:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjectures from <em>Colin Barker</em>, Feb 11 2018: (Start)</p>
<p>G.f.: (1 + 6<em>x + 12</em>x^2 + 6<em>x^3 + x^4) / ((1 - x)^3</em>(1 + x)).</p>
<p>a(n) = (13*n^2 + 4) / 2 for n&gt;0 and even.</p>
<p>a(n) = (13*n^2 + 3) / 2 for n odd.</p>
<p>a(n) = 2*a(n-1) - 2*a(n-3) + a(n-4) for n&gt;4. (End)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The next <code>%F</code> line, line 19, records a further conjecture:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjectured e.g.f.: ((4 + 13<em>x + 13</em>x^2)<em>cosh(x) + (3 + 13</em>x + 13*x^2)*sinh(x) - 2)/2. - <em>Stefano Spezia</em>, Jun 08 2024</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The graph <code>tsiGraph</code> has vertices $(i,j,k)\in\mathbb Z^3$. Within a layer, edges change exactly one horizontal coordinate by $1$ in either direction. Between layers $k$ and $k+1$, every $(i,j,k)$ is adjacent to $(i,j,k+1)$ and also to $(i+1,j,k+1)$ for even $k$, or to $(i,j+1,k+1)$ for odd $k$; all these edges are undirected. Define $a(n)=\operatorname{shell}(n)$ as the natural cardinality of the vertices at graph distance exactly $n$ from $(0,0,0)$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26425">#26425</a> fixes the reading: for every positive even natural $n$, $2a(n)=13n^2+4$, and for every odd natural $n$, $2a(n)=13n^2+3$. This is the proposition <code>claim</code>, proved by <code>result</code>. The generating functions, recurrence and partial sums are not conjuncts of <code>claim</code>.</p>
<p>The source and its encoding are described in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/barker2018a299289.md">the literature note</a>. The OEIS entry cites B. Grünbaum, <em>Uniform tilings of 3-space</em>, Geombinatorics 4 (1994), 49–56, tiling 12, and links the RCSR <code>tsi</code> net; the geometric identification is external to the Lean statement.</p>
