<ul>
<li>$\Pi_\alpha$ runs over the $d^2$ projectors onto $|j\rangle$, $(|j\rangle+|k\rangle)/\sqrt2$ and
$(|j\rangle+i|k\rangle)/\sqrt2$ ($j&lt;k$);</li>
<li>$\Omega=\sum_\alpha\Pi_\alpha$;</li>
<li>$E_\alpha=\Omega^{-1/2}\Pi_\alpha\Omega^{-1/2}$;</li>
<li>the Gram matrix is $G_{\alpha\beta}=\operatorname{tr}E_\alpha E_\beta$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The paper states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The entries in $G$ for orthocross MICs can become arbitrarily small with increasing $d$, but no two
elements of an orthocross MIC can be exactly orthogonal.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumStates/debrota2020varieties.md">the literature note</a>.
Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/14213">#14213</a> reads the conjecture
as two clauses, both for every $d$ and every orthonormal basis:</p>
<ul>
<li>(A) every off-diagonal Gram entry is a strictly positive real number;</li>
<li>(B) every Gram entry tends to zero uniformly as $d\to\infty$.</li>
</ul>
