<article class="news-result" id="jacq-lardizabal-2017-oqw-gambler-mean-hitting-time-form"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The Jacq–Lardizabal mean-hitting-time form is false</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>T. S. Jacq and C. F. Lardizabal, <em>Open Quantum Random Walks on the Half-Line: The Karlin–McGregor Formula, Path Counting and Foster's Theorem</em> (J. Stat. Phys. 169, 547–594, 2017; arXiv:1701.08400v3), state the following in Remark 5.3 of the source TeX <code>JL_rev2.tex</code>, lines 1291–1293:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Numerical experiments allows us to recognize a behavior that such OQW has in common with Example 5.1, namely, that the mean hitting time has the form $E_{k,\rho}(\tau)=1+h(M,M-k)\rho_{11}+h(M,k)\rho_{22}+j(M,k)Re(\rho_{12}),$ where $h, j$ are functions of $M$ and $k=1,\dots, M-1$, with $j$ being a function satisfying $j(M,k)=-j(M,M-k)$. We conjecture that this holds for the gambler's ruin problem associated to *every* pair or matrices $L$, $R$ with $L^*L+R^*R=I$ inducing an OQW on vertices ${0,\dots, M}$. A proof of this statement is, up to our knowledge, unknown.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The formal <code>claim</code> fixes two-dimensional complex matrices $l,r$ with $l^*l+r^*r=1$, every natural $m$ with $3\le m$, and functions $h,j:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N\to\mathbb R$. It requires the stated antisymmetry for every $k$ with $1\le k\le m-1$ and, for every such $k$ and every <code>IsDensity</code> matrix $\rho$, requires <code>meanHittingTime</code> to be different from $\top$ and its <code>toReal</code> value to equal $1+h(m,m-k)(\rho\ 0\ 0).re+h(m,k)(\rho\ 1\ 1).re+j(m,k)(\rho\ 0\ 1).re$. Here <code>IsDensity</code> is positive semidefiniteness together with trace one, and <code>meanHittingTime</code> has codomain <code>ℝ≥0∞</code>, the extended nonnegative reals.</p>
