<p>C. Mukhopadhyay, S. Sazim and A. K. Pati, <em>Coherence makes quantum systems 'magical'</em>, J. Phys. A 51, 414006 (2018), arXiv:1801.04807v3, Eq. (18), stated from numerical simulation:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The following condition on quantum coherence, quantified via the $l_1$-norm, and 'magic', quantified by the sum negativity, holds for qutrit pure states $M_{SN}[|\psi\rangle] \geq \frac{C_{l_{1}}[|\psi\rangle]}{2}\sqrt{1- \frac{C_{l_{1}}[|\psi\rangle]}{2}}$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $M_{SN} = \sum_u |W_u| - 1$ for the discrete Wigner function $W$ of the paper's Eq. (12), and $C_{l_1}$ is the sum of the moduli of the off-diagonal entries. The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumChannels/mukhopadhyay2018coherencemagic.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18729">#18729</a> fixes the reading: the bound for every normalized x ∈ ℂ³ with ρ = x x†. This is proved.</p>
