<p><strong>Conjecture.</strong> For any $N$ and $m$, there exists an $m$-resistant $N$-qudit state in some local dimension $d$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Their Section III definition says that an entangled state remains entangled after tracing out any $m$ subsystems and becomes separable after tracing out any $m+1$ subsystems. This dossier uses finite full separability for the separable predicate and the admissible range $N\geq3$, $m+2\leq N$.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>The Lean <code>claim</code> quantifies all natural $N,m$ with $3\leq N$ and $m+2\leq N$, then supplies a natural local dimension $d$, a pure amplitude function on $(\mathrm{Fin},N)\to\mathrm{Fin},d$, unit norm by the finite sum of <code>Complex.normSq</code>, and <code>IsMResistant m ψ</code>. <code>IsMResistant</code> is the conjunction of initial failure of full separability, failure after every loss set of cardinality $m$, and full separability after every loss set of cardinality $m+1$. The pure-state convention is the frozen <code>rankOneDensity</code> declaration.</p>
