ermutes its columns, giving affineDetalternating d.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Alternating complex polynomials of total degree at most d in d+1 vector variables are scalar affine determinants; with more variables they vanish.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="rouleux-2019-affine-determinant-antisymmetric-forms"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Rouleux&#x27;s affine-determinant conjecture for alternating polynomials</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M. Rouleux, <em>Multimode entanglement for fermions</em>, arXiv:1910.04790v1 (2019), §3, &quot;Affine antisymmetric forms in higher dimensions or of lower degree&quot;, states in <code>3fermiProc.tex</code>, lines 266–269:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We conjecture that: (1) the affine determinant is
the only non degenerate antisymmetric tensor $\omega$ on ${\bf C}^d$ of degree $d$,
depending on $d+1$ variables, and: (2)
there is no non trivial antisymmetric tensor on ${\bf C}^d$ of degree $d$, depending on more than $d+1$ variables.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The preceding discussion specifies &quot;degree being understood as degree of homogeneity&quot; (line 247) and uniqueness &quot;up to a factor&quot; (lines 264–265). Definition 3, lines 251–253, gives the determinant of the differences from the first point. The source and its conventions are described in <a href="../Library/Eigenstructure/rouleux2019multimodeentanglement.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23528">#23528</a> fixes the polynomial reading. For natural numbers $d,r$, write $X_{i,j}$ for coordinate $j$ of vector variable $i$, with $0\le i&lt;r$ and $0\le j&lt;d$, and let $f$ be a complex polynomial in these $rd$ coordinates. <code>IsAlternating f</code> means that renaming the vector indices by any permutation $\sigma$, while fixing coordinate indices, multiplies $f$ by $\operatorname{sign}(\sigma)$. No multilinearity is assumed. Write $D_d=\texttt{affineDet}\ d$ for the polynomial whose evaluation is $\det(x_1-x_0,\ldots,x_d-x_0)$.</p>
<p>The four conjuncts of <code>result</code> establish:</p>
<ol>
<li>For every $d,r$ with $r\ge d+2$, every alternating $f$ of total degree at most $d$ is zero.</li>
<li>For every $d$, $D_d$ is alternating and has total degree at most $d$.</li>
<li>For every $d$, every alternating $f$ in $d+1$ vector variables of total degree at most $d$ is $cD_d$ for some $c\in\mathbb C$.</li>
<li>For every $d\ge2$, the evaluated $D_d$ is non-degenerate: for every family $x_1,\ldots,x_d\in\mathbb C^d$, if $D_d(x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_d)=0$ for every $x_0\in\mathbb C^d$, then the direction of the affine span of $x_1,\ldots,x_d$ has complex dimension at most $d-2$.</li>
</ol>
<p>This proves the preregistered polynomial reading of the two clauses. The total-degree hypothesis includes homogeneous degree-$d$ polynomials; the non-degeneracy statement has the explicit restriction $d\ge2$.</p>
