d in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Coefficients of the two-block hypergeometric Turanian</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. I. Kalmykov and D. B. Karp, <em>Log-concavity and Turán-type inequalities
for the generalized hypergeometric function</em>, arXiv:1611.06724v1, Section 3,
page 6:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 2. Suppose p1 ≤ q1 , p2 ≤ q2 and conditions (13) hold for a1 , b1 and a2 , b2 . Then the function µ → f (µ; x) defined in (3) is log-concave on [1, ∞) and the corresponding generalized Turánian ∆f (α, β; x) has nonnegative coefficients at all powers of x.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The standing assumption on page 2 is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We will always assume that a2 is not empty (i.e. p2 ≥ 1), while all other subvectors are allowed to be empty.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For positive parameter vectors in two blocks, equation (3) defines</p>
<p>$$
f(\mu;x)=\frac{\Gamma(\mathbf a_2+\mu)}{\Gamma(\mathbf b_2+\mu)}
,{}_pF_q(\mathbf a_1,\mathbf a_2+\mu;
\mathbf b_1,\mathbf b_2+\mu;x).
$$</p>
<p>Here a Gamma value at a vector means the product over its entries. Writing
$(a)_n$ for the rising factorial and $c(t)$ for the shifted Gamma product
ratio, the Taylor coefficient is</p>
<p>$$
F_n(\mu)=
\frac{\prod_i(a_{1,i})<em>n}{\prod_j(b</em>{1,j})<em>n}
\frac{c(\mu+n)}{n!},\qquad
c(t)=\frac{\prod_i\Gamma(a</em>{2,i}+t)}{\prod_j\Gamma(b_{2,j}+t)}.
$$</p>
<p>Equation (2) defines</p>
<p>$$
\Delta_f(\alpha,\beta;x)=
f(\mu+\alpha;x)f(\mu+\beta;x)
-f(\mu;x)f(\mu+\alpha+\beta;x),
$$</p>
<p>whose degree-$m$ coefficient is</p>
<p>$$
\delta_m=\sum_{k=0}^m
\bigl[F_k(\mu+\alpha)F_{m-k}(\mu+\beta)
-F_k(\mu)F_{m-k}(\mu+\alpha+\beta)\bigr].
$$</p>
<p>For each block separately, condition (13) is</p>
<p>$$
\frac{e_{q-j}(\mathbf b)}{e_{p-j}(\mathbf a)}
\le\frac{e_{q-j-1}(\mathbf b)}{e_{p-j-1}(\mathbf a)}
\qquad(0\le j&lt;p),
$$</p>
<p>with $e_0=1$. The coefficient clause quantifies over $p_1\le q_1$,
$1\le p_2\le q_2$, all four positive vectors satisfying their respective
chains, every $\mu\ge1$, all $\alpha,\beta\ge0$, and every natural degree $m$.</p>
