with a common normalization state have canonical povm robustness question resolved quantum information ancilla-free testers with a common normalization state have canonical povm robustness ziman, incompatible measurements on quantum causal networks, phys. for a common normalization state, the question isolates incompatibility arising from the measurements. ancilla-free testers with a common normalization state have the robustness of their canonical povms. all matrix families below have finite coordinate and outcome types; the output type has decidable equality. thus it does not settle the mutually unbiased basis conjectures in section vi.b or appendix g. released-dossier c668db6a27e41d6e25c4fea6e1df748d3443791cf95a09a357d526dc9c09540a "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2015</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Ancilla-free testers with a common normalization state have canonical POVM robustness</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Ancilla-free testers with a common normalization state have the robustness of their canonical POVMs.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Ziman, Incompatible measurements on quantum causal networks, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>For a common normalization state, the question isolates incompatibility arising from the measurements.</p><p><span>The idea</span>All matrix families below have finite coordinate and outcome types; the output type has decidable equality.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Ancilla-free testers with a common normalization state have the robustness of their canonical POVMs.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Thus it does not settle the mutually unbiased basis conjectures in Section VI.B or Appendix G.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="sedlak-reitzner-chiribella-ziman-2016-ancilla-free-tester-robustness"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Ancilla-free testers with a common normalization state have canonical POVM robustness</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M. Sedlák, D. Reitzner, G. Chiribella and M. Ziman, <em>Incompatible measurements on quantum causal networks</em>, Phys. Rev. A 93, 052323 (2016), arXiv:1511.00976v2, Section VI.C, ask (verbatim from <code>ti_pra_07.tex</code>, line 807, retaining the source's TeX macros):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is an open question whether  the equality $\Rt({\st A},  {\st B})  =  \Rm(  \st P,  \st Q )$ holds for all ancilla-free testers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $R_{\rm t}$ and $R_{\rm m}$ denote tester and measurement robustness. Section VI restricts attention to testers with the same normalization state (<code>ti_pra_07.tex</code>, lines 758 and 772); that hypothesis is retained. Proposition 3 characterizes ancilla-free testers as $T_a=M_a\otimes\rho$ for an output POVM $M$ and an input density matrix $\rho$ (lines 333–338). Definitions and locators are in <a href="../Library/Observer/sedlakreitznerchiribellaziman2016testers.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23653">#23653</a> fixes the following finite-dimensional reading. Let $y,x,j,k$ be finite types with decidable equality, indexing output coordinates, input coordinates and the two outcome sets. Let $\rho$ be a complex matrix on $x$ with $\rho\succeq0$ and $\operatorname{Tr}\rho=1$. Let $M=(M_a)<em>{a\in j}$ and $N=(N_b)</em>{b\in k}$ be complex output POVMs: each effect is positive semidefinite and each family sums to $I_y$. With $r=\operatorname{rank}\rho$, the statement is</p>
<p>$$
R_{\rm t}\bigl((M_a\otimes\rho)_a,(N_b\otimes\rho)<em>b\bigr)
=R</em>{\rm m}\bigl((M_a\otimes I_r)_a,(N_b\otimes I_r)_b\bigr).
$$</p>
<p>In the module, <code>IsTester</code> means positive semidefinite effects summing to $I_y\otimes\sigma$ for some positive semidefinite $\sigma$ of trace one. <code>TesterCompatibleAt</code> at a real $\lambda$ requires noise testers $E,F$ and a joint tester $C$ with</p>
<p>$$
\sum_b C_{ab}=(1-\lambda)A_a+\lambda E_a,
\qquad
\sum_a C_{ab}=(1-\lambda)B_b+\lambda F_b.
$$</p>
<p>The noises have separate existential witnesses; their normalization states are not prescribed to equal $\rho$, and their effects need not be products. <code>POVMCompatibleAt</code> uses the same marginal equations with noise POVMs and a joint POVM, each positive semidefinite and summing to the identity. <code>testerRobustness</code> and <code>povmRobustness</code> are the real infima of their respective feasible $\lambda\in[0,1]$. The source uses minima; attainment is not part of <code>claim</code> or <code>result</code>. The displayed equality, with these infimum definitions and $I_r$ indexed by <code>Fin ρ.rank</code>, is proved.</p>
