<blockquote>
<p>For all $d \geq 2$, the following trade-off between error and disturbance holds: $\varepsilon(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{A}') + \eta(\mathcal{A}',\mathcal{B}) \geq f(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B})$ and $\Delta(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{A}',\mathcal{B}) \geq f(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B})$, where $f(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}) = \min { \varepsilon(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}),\eta(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}) }$. Moreover, the equalities are both attained either when for all $i$, $\ket{a'_i} = \ket{a_i}$, or when for all $i$, $\ket{a'_i} = \ket{b_i}$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source uses rank-one projective measurements associated with orthonormal bases of $\mathbb C^d$. Section II defines the outcome-distance by $D(x,y)=\max_i|x_i-y_i|$ and compares the $\mathcal B$ statistics before and after the nonselective $\mathcal A'$ measurement. Its overall quantity is explicitly</p>
<blockquote>
<p>$\Delta(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{A}', \mathcal{B}) = \max_\rho \left( \varepsilon_{\rho}(  \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{A}') + \eta_{\rho}(  \mathcal{A}', \mathcal{B})\right).$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is line 221 of the source. Both terms use one common density matrix. Immediately before the conjecture, lines 616–620 relax the endpoint error by</p>
<blockquote>
<p>$\varepsilon(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B}) = \min_{\tilde{\mathcal B}} \varepsilon(\mathcal{A},\tilde{\mathcal{B}})$,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>with the explanation</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This relaxed definition of $\varepsilon$ means that we only distinguish between the classical probability distributions resulting from measurements $\mathcal{A}$ and $\mathcal{B}$ with no regard to the labeling of different measurement outcomes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The definitions and source locator are collected in <a href="../Library/Estimation/zhouwuchau2016errordisturbance.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23208">#23208</a> fixes the statement: for every natural $d\ge2$ and all three unitary measurement bases $A,A',B$, the minimum of the endpoint error, minimized over outcome permutations, and the disturbance caused by $A$ is at most $\Delta(A,A',B)$. This second inequality is refuted. The first inequality, concerning the sum of two separately maximized quantities, is not settled here.</p>
