<p>The Appendix, <code>main.tex</code> line 804, specifies the skewness minimum:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The minimum value $\min (\mathcal{S})$ is found for $a=1/(N-1)$; it is given by Eq.~\eqref{skewunidet}.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The reading fixed in issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22468">#22468</a> concerns only the minimum skewness for unidirectional cycles. For every integer $N\ge2$ and positive clockwise rates $k_i$, put</p>
<p>$R_j=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}k_i^{-j},\qquad \mathcal{S}(k)=3R_2^2/R_1^4-2R_3/R_1^3,\qquad g(N)=-\frac{8-8N+N^2}{16(N-1)^2}.$</p>
<p>The claim is that $g(N)$ is the least value of $\mathcal{S}(k)$ over all such positive rate vectors, including attainment. The source's candidate rates are $(1/(N-1),1,\ldots,1)$, up to an overall positive scale. This minimum claim is proved.</p>
<p>The source defines $\mathcal{S}$ as the third scaled current cumulant divided by the first, rather than the standardized third central moment. Its definition is at <code>main.tex</code> line 53; the Appendix's current and waiting-time cumulant formulas at lines 772–789 give the expression above. The finite-$N$ candidate is displayed in Section III E 1, lines 417–423. See <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/ptaszynski2022skewness.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
