reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>This is quoted from GGcremonav1clean.tex, lines 3209–3211, Remark ex:celledoni; the same remark says that a complete proof is left for further research.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source paper identifies the Euler top as the diagonal special case of these Nambu systems and proves in its Lemma 2.6 that the Euler-top KHK map is a composition of two projectivities and the standard cubic Cremona map.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The rank condition is therefore used exactly to obtain pairwise distinct diagonal coefficients.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The homogeneous KHK map of a positive definite quadratic Nambu pair with a simple pencil factors through the cubic Cremona map.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The source’s assertion that its invariant net has eight distinct base points, and the implication from that geometric condition to the rank hypothesis, are not formalized here, so the relationship between those two genericity descriptions remains open.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="graffeo-gubbiotti-2024-nambu-kahan-cremona-decomposition"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The generic quadratic Nambu KHK map has a Cremona decomposition</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M. Graffeo and G. Gubbiotti study the Kahan–Hirota–Kimura (KHK) discretisation of the quadratic three-dimensional Nambu system
$ \dot{x}=\nabla H_1(x)\mathbin{\times}\nabla H_2(x),\qquad H_i(x)=x^{\mathsf T}A_i x. $
In Remark 7.7 of <em>Growth and Integrability of Some Birational Maps in Dimension Three</em>, they write:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“We conjecture that, up to conjugation by a projectivity, the map $\Phi_{h}^{(N)}$ is of type (A)-(i), i.e. it exists an analogue of Lemma 2.6 for the map $\Phi_{h}^{(N)}$.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is quoted from <code>GG_cremona_v1_clean.tex</code>, lines 3209–3211, Remark <code>ex:celledoni</code>; the same remark says that a complete proof is left for further research.</p>
<p>The Lean module defines, for $X:\mathrm{Fin},4\to\mathbb C$, the polarised matrix <code>nambuLinearPart</code>, the homogeneous KHK matrix
$ K_{A,B,h}(X)=X(3)I_3-2h,\mathrm{nambuLinearPart}(A,B)(X\mathbin{\circ}\mathrm{Fin.castSucc}), $
the homogeneous lift
$ \mathrm{kahan}(A,B,h,X)  =\mathrm{Fin.snoc}\bigl(\operatorname{adj}(K_{A,B,h}(X)),\mathrm{mulVec},    (X\mathbin{\circ}\mathrm{Fin.castSucc}),\det K_{A,B,h}(X)\bigr), $
and
$ \mathrm{cremona}(X)= \bigl(X(1)X(2)X(3),,X(0)X(2)X(3),,X(0)X(1)X(3),,X(0)X(1)X(2)\bigr). $
Its <code>claim</code> states that for every real $3\times3$ matrices $A,B$ with <code>A.PosDef</code> and <code>B.PosDef</code>, with
$ \forall t:\mathbb R,\quad 2\le \operatorname{rank}(B-tA), $
and every $h:\mathbb R$ with $0&lt;h$, there are complex matrices $l_1,l_2:\mathrm{Matrix}(\mathrm{Fin},4)(\mathrm{Fin},4)\mathbb C$ such that <code>IsUnit l₁.det</code>, <code>IsUnit l₂.det</code>, and, for every $X:\mathrm{Fin},4\to\mathbb C$,
$ \mathrm{kahan}(A,B,h,X)  =l_2,\mathrm{mulVec},    \mathrm{cremona}(l_1,\mathrm{mulVec},X). $
The declaration <code>result</code> has type <code>claim</code>.</p>
