/h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Mohsen Aliabadi, <em>A nonabelian twist on differences of bijections</em>,
arXiv:2605.16478v2, Section 7, Problem 7.6, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does every finite nonabelian group admit a multiset A of cardinality |G|
satisfying the abelianization obstruction but not quotient-realizable?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The following paragraph says that a positive answer would characterize finite
abelian groups as precisely those finite groups for which the abelianization
obstruction is the only obstruction. The official journal article, DOI
<a href="https://doi.org/10.61091/um128-18">10.61091/um128-18</a>, has the same question,
global conventions and relevant definitions. The source note is
<a href="../Library/FiniteGroups/aliabadi2026nonabeliantwist.md">aliabadi2026nonabeliantwist</a>.</p>
<p>For every finite group G with multiplication not commutative, let n=|G| and
let pi:G-&gt;G/[G,G] be the canonical abelianization map. There exists a
multiset A of elements of G, counted with multiplicity, such that |A|=n
and the multiplicity-counted product of pi(a) over A is 1, but there are
no bijections b,c:{1,...,n}-&gt;G for which A is the multiset of
b(i)*c(i)^(-1). Repeated identity entries are allowed. No
product-one ordering in G, identity-free condition, group-family,
solvability or parity restriction is imposed.</p>
<p>Definition 2.1 supplies exactly this two-bijection meaning of quotient
realizability. Proposition 2.3 supplies the necessary abelianization
condition: the product is taken after projection, with multiplicities,
in the abelian group G/[G,G]. It does not assert an unordered product in G.</p>
