ormation instead of choosing a scalar measure.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The following helper names refer to the canonical module.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every majorization upper bound of the coherence set has first component times largest eigenvalue at least one over the dimension.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The conjectural premise of this consequence is discharged; the tensor-product majorization statement and lattice infimum are not formalized by result. - Neighbouring questions.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="alvarez-losada-portesi-bosyk-2023-coherence-spectrum-product-bound"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The coherence vector and spectrum satisfy the product bound in every positive dimension</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Y. Alvarez, M. Losada, M. Portesi and G. M. Bosyk, <em>Complementarity between quantum coherence and mixedness: a majorization approach</em>, Communications in Theoretical Physics 75(5), 055102 (2023), DOI 10.1088/1572-9494/acc92f, Section 3.4, journal PDF p. 7:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Finally, we state a conjecture for the greatest component of $\nu(\rho)\otimes\lambda(\rho)$ that allows us to obtain a nonoptimal but nontrivial bound for the tensor-product majorization relation, which depends only on the dimension of the Hilbert space.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 5. Let $\rho\in\mathcal S(\mathcal H_d)$. Then $\nu_1(\rho)\lambda_1(\rho)\ge\frac1d$. (20)</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>For $d=2$ the conjecture is clearly valid, whereas for $d=3$ we have numerical evidence of its validity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $\mathcal S(\mathcal H_d)$ is the set of density operators on the $d$-dimensional Hilbert space, with a fixed incoherent basis. Section 2.2, Eqs. (9)–(12), PDF p. 3, defines the pure-state squared-amplitude vector $\mu(|\psi\rangle\langle\psi|)$, its decreasing rearrangement $\mu^\downarrow$, and</p>
<p>$\mathcal U^{\rm psd}(\rho)={\sum_jp_j\mu^\downarrow(|\psi_j\rangle\langle\psi_j|):\rho=\sum_jp_j|\psi_j\rangle\langle\psi_j|}$,</p>
<p>where the ensembles are finite, their weights are nonnegative and sum to one, and their vectors have norm one. The source defines $\nu(\rho)=\bigvee\mathcal U^{\rm psd}(\rho)$ in the majorization lattice; Section 2.3, PDF p. 4, defines $\lambda(\rho)$ as the decreasingly ordered spectrum. Thus the subscript $1$ denotes the greatest component in each vector. The definitions are also recorded in <a href="../Library/Entropy/alvarezlosadaportesibosyk2023complementarity.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23165">#23165</a> fixes the formal reading: for every $d\ge1$, every positive semidefinite complex $d\times d$ matrix $\rho$ of trace one, and every decreasing, nonnegative vector $\nu$ of sum one that majorizes every member of $\mathcal U^{\rm psd}(\rho)$, one has $\nu_1\lambda_1(\rho)\ge1/d$. This is proved. Applying it to the source's supremum uses that supremum's upper-bound property; the module does not construct the majorization lattice.</p>
