</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Question 1. Is it always the case that the minimum size of both the sumsets hA
and h(≤r) A are the same in any abelian group G, where the minimum runs over the
subsets of G of size (say) k?</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Formal reading</h3>
<p>Names have prefix <code>D5.S3.FiniteGroups.BhanjaRestrictedSumset.Result</code>.
<code>hSumset h A</code> is Mathlib's pointwise $h\mathbin{\bullet}A$, with exactly
$h$ summands. <code>restrictedSumset h r A</code> is the image of functions
$f:\operatorname{Fin}h\to G$ taking values in $A$ whose image has at most
$r$ elements, under $f\mapsto\sum_i f(i)$.
<code>minSize G k h</code> and <code>minRestrictedSize G k h r</code> are infima in the natural
numbers of the corresponding cardinalities over $k$-element finsets.
The claim requires a $k$-element subset to exist, so these infima are
actual minima. It quantifies over all abelian groups, $h\ge1$, $r\ge2$,
and all attainable cardinalities $k$. Allowing $k=0$ adds only a case in
which both sumsets are empty for $h\ge1$.</p>
