="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Foucart–Liao&#x27;s Chebyshev-center regularization question</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Foucart and C. Liao, <em>S-Procedure Relaxation: a Case of Exactness
Involving Chebyshev Centers</em>, arXiv:2310.09677v1, in <em>Explorations in
the Mathematics of Data Science</em> (Springer, 2024),
DOI 10.1007/978-3-031-66497-7_1, §5 “Some Open Questions”, Question 2:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Is the Chebyshev center always obtained via regularization?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The authors' gloss is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>From [10], we know that the answer is affirmative under the specific conditions mentioned above, since the Chebyshev center equals the regularizer $f_{\tau_\sharp}$ with parameter $\tau_\sharp\in(0,1)$ satisfying the implicit equation (9). Without the specific conditions, we also know from [8] that the answer is affirmative if one considered the complex setting rather than the real setting.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The section takes $Q=\mathrm{Id}_H$ and, in (25),
$\mathcal K={f\in H:|Pf|_H\leq\varepsilon}$ and
$\mathcal E={e\in\mathbb R^m:|e|_2\leq\varepsilon}$.
The “specific conditions” are that $P$ is an orthogonal projector
and $\Lambda\Lambda^*=\mathrm{Id}_m$.
Equation (8) defines</p>
<p>$$
f_\tau=\operatorname{argmin}_f
\bigl((1-\tau)|Pf|^2+\tau|y-\Lambda f|^2\bigr).
$$</p>
<p>The Lean <code>claim</code> quantifies over finite-dimensional real Euclidean
spaces, linear $P$ and $\Lambda$, data $y$, and positive bounds
$\varepsilon,\eta$. For every nonempty bounded feasible set
$C={f:|Pf|\leq\varepsilon,\ |y-\Lambda f|\leq\eta}$,
it asks whether every Chebyshev center minimizes this objective for
