-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Ruskai, Extreme Points and Factorizability for New Classes of Unital Quantum Channels, arXiv:2006.03414v4 (Annales Henri Poincaré 22, 3455–3496, 2021), §2.3.1, “Non-extreme cases”, HMR2021J.tex, lines 387–390:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper distinguishes extremality in the intersection UCPT from extremality in either of the larger UCP and CPT sets.</p><p><span>The idea</span>These normalizations force the coefficient matrix to be the identity in the respective convex-decomposition arguments.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every equal-modulus Haagerup–Musat–Ruskai qutrit channel is a mixture of a unitary conjugation and a rank-three channel extreme in both the unital and trace-preserving completely positive maps.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The argument supplies neither a classification of the possible unitaries and remainder maps nor an extension to unequal moduli or higher dimensions; those questions remain open here. - Effect on the source.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="haagerup-musat-ruskai-2021-qutrit-channel-unitary-extreme-decomposition"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Every equal-modulus HMR qutrit channel has a unitary and doubly extreme decomposition</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>U. Haagerup, M. Musat and M. B. Ruskai, <em>Extreme Points and Factorizability for New Classes of Unital Quantum Channels</em>, arXiv:2006.03414v4 (Annales Henri Poincaré 22, 3455–3496, 2021), §2.3.1, “Non-extreme cases”, <code>HMR2021J.tex</code>, lines 387–390:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It seems reasonable to conjecture that,
whenever  $|\alpha | = |\beta| = \1rt2$, the channel $\Phi_{\alpha,\beta} $ is a convex combination of a
unitary conjugation and a map with Choi-rank $3$ which is extreme in both the UCP and CPT maps.
However, one would need a different unitary transformation relating ${ A_k } $ to ${ X_k } $.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source macro <code>\1rt2</code> means $1/\sqrt2$ (line 56). For the standard basis of $\mathbb C^3$, the four operators of Theorem <code>thm:UCPT</code> (lines 280–290) are</p>
<p>$A_1=\alpha|e_1\rangle\langle e_1|+|e_2\rangle\langle e_3|$, $A_2=\beta|e_1\rangle\langle e_3|+|e_3\rangle\langle e_2|$,</p>
<p>$A_3=-|e_1\rangle\langle e_2|-\overline\beta|e_3\rangle\langle e_1|$, $A_4=|e_2\rangle\langle e_1|+\overline\alpha|e_3\rangle\langle e_3|$,</p>
<p>and $\Phi_{\alpha,\beta}(\rho)=\tfrac12\sum_{k=1}^4 A_k^*\rho A_k$. Here UCP means unital completely positive, CPT means completely positive and trace preserving, and UCPT means both. The definitions and source locators are in <a href="../Library/Eigenstructure/haagerupmusatruskai2021extreme.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22743">#22743</a> fixes the statement: for every $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb C$ with $|\alpha|=|\beta|=1/\sqrt2$, there exist a unitary $U\in M_3(\mathbb C)$, a real $\lambda\in[0,1]$, and a complex linear map $\Psi:M_3(\mathbb C)\to M_3(\mathbb C)$ such that $\Psi$ is completely positive, unital and trace preserving, its Choi matrix has rank $3$, it is separately extreme in the UCP and CPT sets under real convex combinations, and</p>
<p>$\Phi_{\alpha,\beta}(\rho)=\lambda U^*\rho U+(1-\lambda)\Psi(\rho)$</p>
<p>for every complex matrix $\rho\in M_3(\mathbb C)$. This is proved.</p>
