">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Coefficientwise log-concavity of the triple rising-factorial series</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Dmitrii Karp and Yi Zhang, <em>Log-concavity and log-convexity of series containing
multiple Pochhammer symbols</em>, arXiv:2305.09029v3, page 8, Conjecture 3.8:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Assume that ${f_n}_{n=0}^{\infty}$ is log-concave and independent of $\mu$. Then the function</p>
<p>$$
\mu\to f(\mu;x)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} f_n\frac{(\mu)_{3n}}{(3n-1)!}x^n
$$</p>
<p>is coefficient-wise log-concave.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Definition 1.1, page 2, uses nonnegative, nontrivial real sequences,
$f_k^2\ge f_{k-1}f_{k+1}$ for positive natural $k$, and interval support.
In particular, internal zeros are excluded, but zero endpoints and finite
supports are allowed. Define</p>
<p>$$
a_0(t)=0,\qquad a_n(t)=\frac{(t)_{3n}}{(3n-1)!}\quad(n\ge1),
$$</p>
<p>and, for every natural $m$ and nonnegative real $\mu,\alpha,\beta$,</p>
<p>$$
\delta_m=\sum_{k=0}^{m} f_kf_{m-k}
\left[a_k(\mu+\alpha)a_{m-k}(\mu+\beta)
-a_k(\mu)a_{m-k}(\mu+\alpha+\beta)\right].
$$</p>
<p>The assertion is $\delta_m\ge0$ for every such sequence, parameter triple
and degree. The endpoint summands vanish. These are finite formal Cauchy
coefficients, without convergence or growth assumptions.</p>
<p>The coefficient-wise convention on printed page 1 reads:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>if the coefficients $\delta_k$ at all powers of $x$ in (2) are non-negative (non-positive) for all $\alpha,\beta\ge0$ we will say that $\mu\to f(\mu;x)$ is coefficient-wise log-concave (log-convex ).</p>
</blockquote>
