span>The idea</span>Local complementation uses lcsource and the cut-rank invariance lceq; input permutations reindex the cut, and input-edge removal changes no entry of an output cut.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For graphs with only input–output edges, equivalence generated by local complementation, the two vertex permutations, binary input-row addition and input-edge removal implies reachability by the two permutations and row additions alone.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Existence of bipartite representatives for other classes, selection of a unique representative, an efficient test, and the Definition 6.2/Conjecture 6.3 bridge remain open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="khesin-li-2024-bipartite-encoder-equivalence"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Bipartite encoder forms in an operation-generated class differ only by permutations and row operations</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. B. Khesin and A. M. Li, <em>Equivalence Classes of Quantum Error-Correcting Codes</em> (2024), arXiv:2406.12083v1, Section 8, Conjecture 8.3 (<code>main.tex</code>, lines 1768–1771):</p>
