<p>In an equivalence class with bipartite forms, all bipartite forms in the class can be transformed from one to another using only output permutations, input permutations, and row operations on the input-output adjacency matrix.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source introduces these forms as graphs with “no edges among output nodes” (<code>main.tex</code>, line 1631). Its Section 7 class construction uses the operations of Conjecture 6.3 (<code>main.tex</code>, lines 1557 and 1582–1584). The operations and their locators are recorded in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/khesinli2024equivalence.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26139">#26139</a> fixes the operation-generated reading. A graph has vertex set $\mathrm{Fin}(k)\sqcup\mathrm{Fin}(n)$, with inputs on the left and outputs on the right. A bipartite form has only input–output edges: neither input–input nor output–output edges. Equivalence is <code>Relation.EqvGen Step</code>, generated by local complementation, output permutations, input permutations, binary additions of distinct input rows, and input-edge removal. Local Clifford decorations are absent.</p>
<p>For all natural $k,n$ and all such bipartite forms $G,H$, <code>claim</code> says that operation-generated equivalence implies <code>Relation.ReflTransGen BipartiteStep G H</code>: a finite, possibly empty, sequence of output permutations, input permutations and binary additions of distinct input rows. This is proved. There is no positivity, connectedness, full-rank or normalization hypothesis.</p>
