ue spaces and the hornich–hlawka inequality niculescu, a survey of the hornich-hlawka inequality, arxiv:2407.03278v1, §3, the paragraph introducing problem 3 and problem 3 itself, state verbatim: variable exponents permit different power laws on different parts of the measure space. a two-valued variable exponent gives a luxemburg norm violating the hornich–hlawka inequality. the zero vector has norm zero by the defining infimum; that case is mathematical reasoning rather than a separate theorem in the module.  released-dossier 1404c8f433c94b20d944700ee8bcd29534d85b7bf499592b3d52049ca1f810a7 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Geometry &amp; algebra</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2024</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Variable-exponent Lebesgue spaces and the Hornich–Hlawka inequality</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A two-valued variable exponent gives a Luxemburg norm violating the Hornich–Hlawka inequality.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Niculescu, A survey of the Hornich-Hlawka inequality, arXiv:2407.03278v1, §3, the paragraph introducing Problem 3 and Problem 3 itself, state verbatim:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Variable exponents permit different power laws on different parts of the measure space.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The zero vector has norm zero by the defining infimum; that case is mathematical reasoning rather than a separate theorem in the module.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A two-valued variable exponent gives a Luxemburg norm violating the Hornich–Hlawka inequality.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="marinescu-niculescu-2024-variable-exponent-hornich-hlawka"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>Variable-exponent Lebesgue spaces and the Hornich–Hlawka inequality</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D.-Ş. Marinescu and C. P. Niculescu, <em>A survey of the Hornich-Hlawka inequality</em>, arXiv:2407.03278v1, §3, the paragraph introducing Problem 3 and Problem 3 itself, state verbatim:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A natural generalization of Lp spaces is provided by the class of Lp(·) spaces with variable exponent. They differ from the classical Lp spaces in that the exponent p is not constant but a function from the measure space under attention to [1, ∞]. See the monograph of L. Diening, P. Harjulehto, P. Hästö and M. Ruzicka [6] for a thorough presentation. Problem 3. Is every Lp(·)(0, 1) space with a variable exponent p(·) : (0, 1) → [1, 2] a Hornich-Hlawka space?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A Hornich–Hlawka space satisfies</p>
<p>$$
|f+g|+|g+h|+|h+f|
\le |f|+|g|+|h|+|f+g+h|
$$</p>
