<h3>Diophantine exponents of aperiodic episturmian words</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. Peltomäki, <em>Initial nonrepetitive complexity of regular episturmian words and their Diophantine exponents</em>, European Journal of Combinatorics 118 (2024), 103942, DOI 10.1016/j.ejc.2024.103942, arXiv:2103.08351v3, Section 8, Conjecture 8.4:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let $\mathbf t$ be an aperiodic episturmian word. Then $\mathrm{dio}(\mathbf t)&lt;\infty$ if and only if $\mathbf t$ has bounded partial quotients.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source proves the regular case in Theorem 6.6. The unrestricted statement is the target of <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/15858">#15858</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Formal counterpart |
| --- | --- |
| A finite alphabet | Every natural $d$, with letters in <code>Fin d</code> |
| A directive word $\Delta$ | Every stream $\mathbb N\to\mathrm{Fin},d$ |
| An episturmian word with directive $\Delta$ | <code>Lang t = Lang (cDelta Delta)</code> |
| Aperiodic | The literal negation of an eventually periodic natural tail, expanded in <code>claim</code> |
| Bounded partial quotients | <code>BoundedPQ Delta</code>, a uniform bound on the distance to the next different directive letter |
| Finite Diophantine exponent | <code>dio t &lt; ⊤</code> |
| If and only if | Both implications of <code>DiophantineExponentCriterion.result</code> |</p>
<p>Finite factors use the frozen operator <code>AbelianBorderQuestionDefs.factor</code> at start zero. Its value is the literal list of the first $n$ letters. Directive prepending uses Mathlib's <code>Stream'.cons</code>, and finite powers use <code>Powers.wordPower</code>. No alphabet size, regularity, intercept or recurrence hypothesis is added.</p>
<p>The source's run convention and <code>BoundedPQ</code> agree: if every maximal run has length at most $K$, the next different letter occurs within $K+1$ positions; conversely a witness $B$ bounds each run by $B-1$. An eventually constant directive has neither property. This is a finite run-length argument, proved in prose.</p>
