actorization has perfect two-wire factors wanless, tensor network decompositions for absolutely maximally entangled states, quantum 8 (2024) 1339, arxiv:2308.07042v3, ask whether the linear factorization result extends to nonlinear classical maps. for a classical map, the source&#x27;s array has rows consisting of the input triple followed by its output triple. every three-gate factorization of a classical six-leg perfect tensor has perfect two-wire factors. every column selector used in the proof has its injectivity discharged explicitly. the source&#x27;s quantum, nonclassical, and existence questions are outside this result. - effect on the source. released-dossier ad9a2e295b00cb09d88d79efe42a1d7bfc1d3ccce5aa2c30b8069af522ac0fd4 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2023</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Every classical six-leg perfect tensor factorization has perfect two-wire factors</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Every three-gate factorization of a classical six-leg perfect tensor has perfect two-wire factors.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Wanless, Tensor network decompositions for absolutely maximally entangled states, Quantum 8 (2024) 1339, arXiv:2308.07042v3, ask whether the linear factorization result extends to nonlinear classical maps.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>For a classical map, the source&#x27;s array has rows consisting of the input triple followed by its output triple.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Every column selector used in the proof has its injectivity discharged explicitly.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every three-gate factorization of a classical six-leg perfect tensor has perfect two-wire factors.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The source&#x27;s quantum, nonclassical, and existence questions are outside this result. - Effect on the source.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="pozsgay-wanless-2024-nonlinear-perfect-tensor-factors"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Every classical six-leg perfect tensor factorization has perfect two-wire factors</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>B. Pozsgay and I. M. Wanless, <em>Tensor network decompositions for absolutely maximally entangled states</em>, Quantum 8 (2024) 1339, arXiv:2308.07042v3, ask whether the linear factorization result extends to nonlinear classical maps. In <code>factoringtriunit.tex</code>, the source defines orthogonal columns and orthogonal arrays in lines 891–902, associates a map from three input symbols to three output symbols with an array in lines 929–937, and gives the six-leg factorization
$u^{(123)}=\mathcal C^{(23)}\mathcal B^{(13)}\mathcal A^{(12)}$
with classical two-wire functions in lines 945–964. After the linear calculation, lines 1196–1200 state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In other words, if $G$ represents a linear perfect tensor with 6 legs, and it has a $\triangleright$-decomposition into linear tensors with 4 legs, then those factors will themselves be perfect. It is an open question whether this statement is true in the nonlinear case for $k=6$.</p>
