anded"><p><span>The question</span>Srikanth, On the eternal non-Markovianity of non-unital quantum channels, arXiv:2203.10625v2, International Journal of Quantum Information 22(1), 2350039 (2024), DOI 10.1142/S0219749923500399, Section IV, conjecture:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>It identifies a rotating replacement state as a mechanism by which a divisible isotropic contraction coexists with a generator that fails conditional complete positivity at every positive time.</p><p><span>The idea</span>3. inversepair, generatorcomposition, rationalderiv and phigenerator give the exact inverse and entrywise generator.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A rotating replacement state gives a completely positive trace-preserving qubit dynamics whose unital part is divisible, while its generator fails conditional complete positivity at every positive time.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Although we have only studied the qubit case, we conjecture that it is true for non-unital channels in arbitrary finite dimensions.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="utagi-banerjee-srikanth-2024-purely-nonunital-eternal-nonmarkovianity"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A rotating replacement state refutes purely non-unital eternal non-Markovianity impossibility</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Utagi, S. Banerjee and R. Srikanth, <em>On the eternal non-Markovianity of non-unital quantum channels</em>, arXiv:2203.10625v2, International Journal of Quantum Information 22(1), 2350039 (2024), DOI 10.1142/S0219749923500399, Section IV, conjecture:</p>
