onjecture 4.1: the target is the complete original iff under preregistration 25489. the original four-clause classification holds for every positive k and s. the formal endpoint is d5.s3.finitegroups.bajnokbeattyallregimes.result.  released-dossier 0e6cb7f7c2630263f6d2958de32b9979eb621e862224e1c69596150be2b4a65f "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Geometry &amp; algebra</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Conjecture proved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2024</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The original Bajnok–Beatty Conjecture 4.1</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The original four-clause classification holds for every positive k and s.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Beatty, On the Diameter of Undirected Cayley Graphs of Finite Abelian Groups, arXiv:2406.04045v1, Section 4, Conjecture 4.1:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The target is the complete original iff under preregistration 25489.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The formal endpoint is D5.S3.FiniteGroups.BajnokBeattyAllRegimes.result.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The original four-clause classification holds for every positive k and s.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="bajnok-beatty-original-conjecture-4-1"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>The original Bajnok–Beatty Conjecture 4.1</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>B. Bajnok and W. K. Beatty, <em>On the Diameter of Undirected Cayley Graphs of Finite Abelian Groups</em>, arXiv:2406.04045v1, Section 4, Conjecture 4.1:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let k and s be positive integers. The group Z2 × Z2k has an s-spanning set of size 2 if, and only if, one of the following holds:</p>
<ul>
<li>s is odd and k ≤ (s² − 1)/2;</li>
<li>s is even and k ≤ (s² − s)/2;</li>
<li>s ≡ 0 mod 4, k is even, and (s² − s)/2 &lt; k ≤ s²/2; or</li>
<li>s ≡ 2 mod 4, k ≡ 2 mod 4, and (s² − s)/2 &lt; k ≤ s²/2.</li>
</ul>
