<blockquote>
<p>Are there any noncyclic groups that have a perfect restricted s-basis of size s + 1?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The paper works with finite abelian groups and positive integers $s$. Its definition is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A is a perfect restricted s-basis of G if each element of G can be written uniquely as the sum of at most s distinct elements of A.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Uniqueness disregards the order of the summands. The empty subset contributes the empty sum $0$.</p>
