t" id="alsing-birrittella-2024-log-negativity-sandwich"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The Alsing–Birrittella logarithmic-negativity sandwich</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>P. M. Alsing and R. J. Birrittella, <em>Numerical Evidence for a Bipartite Pure State Entanglement Witness from Approximate Analytical Diagonalization</em>, arXiv:2404.13725v1, Section IV, p. 11, Equation (22), state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>we have numerically found that 𝓛𝓝_a^avg(ρ_ab) ≤ LN_e(ρ_ab) ≤ LN_a(ρ_ab) when the mixed state is written as a pure state decomposition (PSD) ρ_ab = Σ_i p_i |ψ_i⟩⟨ψ_i|, and in addition the quantum amplitude matrix C_i of each pure state component |ψ_i⟩ = Σ_nm C_i,nm |n,m⟩ is uniformly generated (over the Haar measure) as a positive Hermitian matrix, C_i = C_i† ≥ 0.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Further, we find that the second inequality is surprisingly saturated, i.e. LN_e(ρ_ab) = LN_a(ρ_ab) in Eq.(22), if the uniformly randomly generated C_i matrices above are real, i.e. C_i = C_i^T.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The settling theorem proves both inequalities and the real-amplitude equality for every finite ensemble of positive semidefinite complex amplitude matrices, with nonnegative weights summing to one and $\operatorname{tr}(C_iC_i^\dagger)=1$. Different amplitudes need not commute. Zero weights and singular amplitudes are allowed.</p>
