<p>The quotation preserves the source's words and mathematical content, with inline TeX math delimiters rendered as dollar delimiters and the source line break joined. The source read is <code>main.tex</code> from the v1 source archive, md5 <code>c47930e35162d18bc08d65fb1201100d</code>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21125">#21125</a> fixes the reading: for every integer $n\ge2$, maximize over $\gamma\in[0,1]$ and binary probability laws $p_i,q_i$ the merit of</p>
<p>$r(a)=\gamma\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}p_i(a_i)+(1-\gamma)\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}q_i(a_i).$</p>
<p>Each local law is nonnegative and sums to one. This is the mathematical mixture in the source's equation <code>pncl</code>, lines 248–253. Define $E={a:\sum_i a_i\equiv0\pmod2}$ and $O={a:\sum_i a_i\equiv1\pmod2}$. The reading of equation <code>cn</code>, lines 263–271, is</p>
<p>$C_n(r)=\frac12\left|\sum_{\substack{a,b\in E\a\ne b}}r(a)r(b)-\sum_{\substack{a,b\in O\a\ne b}}r(a)r(b)\right|.$</p>
<p>The pairs are ordered, and both strings in each pair have the stated parity. The factor $1/2$ counts each unordered pair once. The conclusion is an attained maximum of $1/4$, as expressed by <code>IsGreatest</code>, rather than an unevidenced supremum. The restriction $n\ge2$ is explicit: at $n=1$, each parity class has only one string and the merit is zero.</p>
