process</em>, arXiv:2310.03343v2, Section 4.5, page 21:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We focus on when t ≪ 1 and t ≫ 1. From the discussion in Sections 4.2
and 4.3, we know that the most likely number of particles is either k or k + 1.
The question we want to address is which one is most likely.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The question is AM-1 of <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18570">#18570</a>.
Write $L=2r\ge4$, $K\ge1$, $0\le k&lt;K$, and $n=(2k+1)r$.
There is an $\varepsilon&gt;0$ such that, whenever $t&gt;0$ and either
$t&lt;\varepsilon$ or $t&gt;1/\varepsilon$, the unique modal occupation is
$k$ if $2k+1&lt;K$, and $k+1$ if $K&lt;2k+1$. If $K=2k+1$, occupations
$k$ and $k+1$ tie and strictly exceed every other occupation in these regimes.</p>
