<p>Write $E_i=\operatorname{Fin}(C.\mathrm{dim}(i))\sqcup\operatorname{Fin}(D.\mathrm{dim}(i))$ and $q_i:E_i\to\mathrm{PBasis}(\varphi,\psi,i)$ for the quotient map. Under both monicity hypotheses, <code>D5.S3.Quantum.Information.CssMonicPushoutLdpc.pushoutD2_representative_independent</code> proves, for every $Y:\mathrm{PBasis}(\varphi,\psi,2)$, $y:E_2$ with $q_2(y)=Y$, and $X:\mathrm{PBasis}(\varphi,\psi,1)$,</p>
<p>$$\mathrm{pushoutD2}(\varphi,\psi)(X,Y)=\sum_{x\in E_1,\ q_1(x)=X}\mathrm{sumD2}(C,D)(x,y).$$</p>
<p><code>D5.S3.Quantum.Information.CssMonicPushoutLdpc.pushoutD1_representative_independent</code> proves the analogous formula for $Y:\mathrm{PBasis}(\varphi,\psi,1)$, $y:E_1$ with $q_1(y)=Y$, and $X:\mathrm{PBasis}(\varphi,\psi,0)$:</p>
<p>$$\mathrm{pushoutD1}(\varphi,\psi)(X,Y)=\sum_{x\in E_0,\ q_0(x)=X}\mathrm{sumD1}(C,D)(x,y).$$</p>
<p>Thus the two matrices represent the induced maps $[y]\mapsto[\partial y]$, independently of the selected representative. The degree-two and degree-one formulas use <code>BPChainMap.comm2</code> and <code>BPChainMap.comm1</code>, respectively. The identity $\partial^Q_1\partial^Q_2=0$ is checked by the pinned experiment below; it is not delivered as a Lean theorem.</p>
<p>The categorical universal property is proved in prose here, rather than by a Lean categorical theorem. In degree $i$, take the free binary vector space on $\mathrm{PBasis}(\varphi,\psi,i)$. Its maps from the two feet send a basis vector $x$ to $[x]$ and agree on every apex basis vector. Any two linear maps from the feet agreeing on the apex assign equal values to each generating glued pair, so extend uniquely to a linear map on the quotient basis. The representative formulas above identify its differentials with $[y]\mapsto[\partial y]$. If the two maps from the feet are chain maps, that extension commutes with the differential on every quotient generator, hence everywhere by linearity. Uniqueness follows because those generators span. This identifies the construction with the basis-preserving pushout of Lemma 5.4.</p>
