class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="kamin-shchukin-schmidt-van-loock-2023-swap-earliest-dephasing"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Swapping an earliest adjacent pair minimizes dephasing for every number of segments</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>L. Kamin, E. Shchukin, F. Schmidt and P. van Loock, <em>Exact rate analysis for quantum repeaters with imperfect memories and entanglement swapping as soon as possible</em>, arXiv:2203.10318v1 (Phys. Rev. Research 5, 023086, 2023), subsection “Optimal swapping scheme”, state in the TeX source <code>skr.tex</code>, lines 1082–1084:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Our conjecture is that the statement is valid for all $n \geqslant 2$, but in this work we consider repeaters with up to eight segments only, and for such $n$ we have verified this statement directly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The statement is the displayed equality at line 1080:</p>
<p>$\tilde{D}_n(N_1, \ldots, N_n) = D^\star_n(N_1, \ldots, N_n)$.</p>
<p>Here $N_i$ is the positive integer ready time of segment $i$. Swapping adjacent segments adds $|N_i-N_{i+1}|$ and replaces their ready times by $\max(N_i,N_{i+1})$. The source defines $\tilde D_n$ by repeatedly choosing an adjacent pair with least maximum, allowing arbitrary ties (<code>skr.tex</code>, lines 1033–1044). It defines $D^\star_n$ by minimizing the swap cost plus the recursively optimal cost of the shortened list over every adjacent first pair, with $D^\star_2(N_1,N_2)=|N_1-N_2|$ (lines 1049–1065).</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25201">#25201</a> fixes the reading: for every list of $n\ge2$ positive integers and every complete greedy history, its accumulated cost equals $D^\star_n$. This is proved. The formal statement also includes zero ready times and terminal lists.</p>
