ence of a complete commuting product encoding for every three-qubit pure state. released-dossier 1242c83d3464acdb5bb6c2e742fcf465bf486cd1c8da8e15066633f081f1cd71 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Commuting product encoders define exactly the commuting-reflection qubit state class</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Commuting product unitaries and commuting local reflections encode exactly the same qubit states.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Gühne, The Entanglement Content of Quantum Measurement Bases, arXiv:2608.21185v1 (2026), Section VI, asks:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper organizes locally encoded measurement bases by the allowed encoder structure.</p><p><span>The idea</span>1. Local signs. Commutation of the nonzero tensor products makes the corresponding local products proportional.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Commuting product unitaries and commuting local reflections encode exactly the same qubit states.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>This remains open here; result only makes it equivalent to existence of a complete commuting product encoding for every three-qubit pure state.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="pauwels-guhne-2026-commuting-encoding-reflection-class"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Commuting product encoders define exactly the commuting-reflection qubit state class</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. Pauwels and O. Gühne, <em>The Entanglement Content of Quantum Measurement Bases</em>, arXiv:2608.21185v1 (2026), Section VI, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Which other encoding classes define natural state classes, and what do these sets look like?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>One of the proposed classes is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>commuting families whose local factors are not Hermitian, the smallest step beyond our theorem</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The paragraph states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For none of these is a characterization known, although $M_4$ now proves that the last class is strictly larger than commuting reflections.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These quotations are from the downloaded source <code>main.tex</code>, lines 1539–1547, md5 <code>217f1dae7e17966b2c9016fa75ef756b</code>. In the last quotation, “the last class” means tables of local Clifford unitaries. The source's local-encoding definition is at lines 328–340, and its encoder-structures definition is at lines 357–368; see also <a href="../Library/QuantumStates/pauwelsguhne2026measurementbases.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20897">#20897</a> fixes the following reading. For every $n\ge1$, put $N=2^n$ and let $\psi:{0,1}^n\to\mathbb C$ satisfy $\sum_x|\psi(x)|^2=1$. A commuting product encoding is a family of exactly $N$ operators $U_a=\bigotimes_{j=1}^n U_{a,j}$, with every $U_{a,j}$ a unitary $2\times2$ matrix, such that $U_aU_b=U_bU_a$ and $\langle U_a\psi,U_b\psi\rangle=\delta_{ab}$ for every pair of labels. A commuting-reflection encoding additionally requires $U_{a,j}^\dagger=U_{a,j}$ and $U_{a,j}^2=I$ for every label and site.</p>
<p>The claim is that $\psi$ admits a commuting product encoding if and only if it admits a commuting-reflection encoding. This equality of state classes is proved.</p>
