source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The Ma–Weigert derivative-sign regions form a descending chain</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Rourou Ma and Julian Weigert, <em>Complete monotonicity of log-functions</em>, arXiv:2505.04225v2 (Analysis and Mathematical Physics 15, 141, 2025), §4, Conjecture <code>conj:shrinking</code>, <code>infiniteOrderDeriv.tex</code>, lines 154–160 (TeX macros expanded and display mathematics set inline):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For any $n\in \mathbb N$ the semialgebraic sets $D_k=\left{f\in \mathcal{F}<em>{1,n}\middle|\forall x\in \mathbb R:\left(\frac{-\mathrm d}{\mathrm d x}\right)^kf(x)\geq 0\right}$ form a descending chain under inclusion, i.e. $D_k\supseteq D</em>{k+1}$, which converges to the region $\mathrm{CM}<em>{1,n}=\bigcap</em>{k=1}^\infty D_k$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source defines $\mathcal F_{1,n}$ as the smooth functions $f(x)=p(\log x)/x$ on $x&gt;0$, where $p$ is a real polynomial of degree at most $n$ (<code>Intro.tex</code>, lines 20–23). Although the conjecture's display writes $x\in\mathbb R$, the functions' domain and the preceding example's sign condition are $x&gt;0$ (<code>infiniteOrderDeriv.tex</code>, line 150). Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21992">#21992</a> fixes this positive-domain reading.</p>
<p>Write $f_p(x)=p(\log x)/x$ and $D_{n,k}={p\in\mathbb R[y]:\operatorname{natDegree}(p)\le n,\ \forall x&gt;0,\ (-1)^k f_p^{(k)}(x)\ge0}$. The module represents regions by their polynomial parameters. Its <code>claim</code> states, for every $n\in\mathbb N_0$:</p>
<ul>
<li>$D_{n,k+1}\subseteq D_{n,k}$ for every natural $k\ge1$;</li>
<li>for each real polynomial $p$ with $\operatorname{natDegree}(p)\le n$, membership in every $D_{n,k}$ with $k\ge1$ is equivalent to $(-1)^j f_p^{(j)}(x)\ge0$ for every natural $j\ge0$ and every $x&gt;0$.</li>
