uently the row and column degree sums must be at least two.  released-dossier 0a4793a45657d8212d9b31283a0a1c0f4f19fb0552bafd5a5b3d1d5a64b078c5 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Kumar&#x27;s remaining rectangular weak two-metric dimensions</strong><span class="result-excerpt">For 4 &lt;= n &lt; m &lt; 2n - 2, the weak two-metric dimension of the rectangular Hamming graph is min(ceil(2(n+m)/3), 2n - 2).</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>The rectangular k = 2 values below the threshold m = 2n − 2 remain to be determined.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Consequently the row and column degree sums must be at least two.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For 4 &lt;= n &lt; m &lt; 2n - 2, the weak two-metric dimension of the rectangular Hamming graph is min(ceil(2(n+m)/3), 2n - 2).</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="kumar-2026-rectangular-weak-two-metric-dimension"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Kumar&#x27;s remaining rectangular weak two-metric dimensions</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Aryan Kumar, arXiv:2609.32161v1, §7:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The rectangular k = 2 values below the threshold m = 2n − 2 remain to be determined.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The vertices of $K_n\square K_m$ are pairs in $[n]\times[m]$, with
$d((i,j),(a,b))=[i\ne a]+[j\ne b]$. For a landmark set $S$, put
$\Delta_S(x,y)=\sum_{w\in S}|d(x,w)-d(y,w)|$.
A weak $k$-resolving set has $\Delta_S(x,y)\ge k$ for every distinct pair;
$\operatorname{wdim}_k$ is its least cardinality.</p>
<p>The exact rectangular answer is</p>
<p>$$
\operatorname{wdim}_2(K_n\square K_m)
=\min\left{\left\lceil\frac{2(n+m)}3\right\rceil,,2n-2\right}
\qquad(4\le n&lt;m&lt;2n-2).
$$</p>
<p>For natural-number division, the ceiling is $(2(n+m)+2)/3$.
The Lean <code>claim</code> retains all three size conditions and both upper-bound terms.</p>
