ack to midpoint convexity. a signed quadratic norm gives an all-input counterexample to the unrestricted rank-only jensen converse. the rational nonsquare theorem supplies its field structure.  released-dossier 25d897dd9cf41b99d1f0585d6f5e9ed2fe4d10a0592ffa1c7fa72b070dbb0e7c "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Geometry &amp; algebra</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The unrestricted rank-only Jensen converse is false</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A signed quadratic norm gives an all-input counterexample to the unrestricted rank-only Jensen converse.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Mátyás Barczy and Zsolt Páles, Jensen convex functions and doubly stochastic matrices, arXiv:2510.03715v1, Remark 2.2(i), ask:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The question asks whether the dimension of the image of a stochastic matrix alone can force a functional inequality back to midpoint convexity.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The rational nonsquare theorem supplies its field structure.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A signed quadratic norm gives an all-input counterexample to the unrestricted rank-only Jensen converse.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="barczy-2025-unrestricted-jensen-converse"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>The unrestricted rank-only Jensen converse is false</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Mátyás Barczy and Zsolt Páles, <em>Jensen convex functions and doubly stochastic matrices</em>, arXiv:2510.03715v1, Remark 2.2(i), ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, we do not know whether the assertion in part (i) of Theorem 2.1 holds for every doubly stochastic matrix having rank at least 2. We leave this question as an open problem.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The referenced assertion is Theorem 2.1(i), equation (2.1), with Jensen convexity defined in Definition 1.1. The complete target is the following closed universal assertion:</p>
<p>For every positive integer $n$, every nonempty open real interval $I$ (including unbounded intervals and $I=\mathbb R$), every arbitrary function $f:I\to\mathbb R$, and every real $n\times n$ matrix $P=(p_{ij})$ satisfying</p>
<p>$$
p_{ij}\geq0,\qquad \sum_jp_{ij}=1,\qquad
\sum_ip_{ij}=1,\qquad \operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb R}P\geq2,
$$</p>
<p>does</p>
<p>$$
f!\left(\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\right)
\leq\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n f!\left(\sum_{j=1}^np_{ij}x_j\right)
\qquad\text{for every }(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\in I^n
$$</p>
<p>imply</p>
<p>$$
f!\left(\frac{u+v}{2}\right)\leq\frac{f(u)+f(v)}2
\qquad\text{for every }u,v\in I?
$$</p>
<p>The rank condition replaces the original special column-span premise; it is not conjoined with it. There is no continuity, measurability, boundedness, rational-entry or rational-input assumption. The function is defined on the original interval itself, with no ambient-extension datum. Convexity of the interval puts each mean and each matrix row output in its domain.</p>
<p>The answer is negative. The exact result is the negation of this whole universal assertion:</p>
<p>$$
\neg\Bigl[\forall n\in\mathbb N_{&gt;0};\forall I\subseteq\mathbb R
\text{ nonempty open interval};\forall f:I\to\mathbb R;
\forall P\in\mathbb R^{n\times n},\quad
\bigl(P\text{ doubly stochastic}\ \land\ \operatorname{rank}<em>{\mathbb R}P\geq2\bigr)
\Longrightarrow
\Bigl(\bigl[\forall x\in I^n,\ f(\tfrac1n\sum_jx_j)
\leq\tfrac1n\sum_i f(\sum_jp</em>{ij}x_j)\bigr]
\Longrightarrow
\bigl[\forall u,v\in I,\ f(\tfrac{u+v}2)\leq\tfrac{f(u)+f(v)}2\bigr]\Bigr)\Bigr].
