Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Czerwińska–Franco–Merino: radial convexity and matrix monotonicity</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M. M. Czerwińska, J. A. Franco and A. Merino, <em>Towards a Characterization of Matrix Monotonicity via Radial Convexity</em>, arXiv:2610.17393v1, §4, Conjecture 4.10. See <a href="../Library/Eigenstructure/czerwinska2026radial.md">czerwinska2026radial</a>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let f : (0, ∞) → R. Then f is radially convex of order n (with respect to the Frobenius norm) if and only if f is matrix monotone of degree n.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>arXiv:2610.17393v1, §4, directly after Conjecture 4.10, verbatim:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By Proposition 3.9, matrix monotonicity of degree n implies radial convexity of order n. Thus, it remains to prove the converse implication. The next theorem establishes the conjecture for n = 2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The matrices are complex Hermitian positive definite, and the order is Loewner order. Radial convexity requires both endpoint distance inequalities for every ordered triple $A\preceq C\preceq B$. Matrix monotonicity allows either of the two uniform order directions. The universal assertion quantifies over every $n\ge1$ and every real function on the positive half-line.</p>
