k-distance Graphs</em>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25493v1">arXiv:2606.25493v1</a>,
Section 4, Conjecture 1, printed PDF page 11:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 1. Any connected graph G=(V,E) has ρ²(G)≤|V|−3.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The exact target and integer interpretation are registered in
<a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23003">#23003</a>.
The <a href="../Library/Geometry/demo2026periodicity.md">source note</a> records the
source qualification; the
<a href="../Library/Geometry/erickson2024metamour.md">earlier metamour note</a> records
the known fixed-point example.</p>
<h3>Definitions and statement echo</h3>
<p>Let $V$ be a finite vertex set and $G$ a simple undirected graph on $V$.
Define $T(G)$ on the same vertices by
$u\sim_{T(G)}v\iff\operatorname{edist}_G(u,v)=2$.
The extended distance is infinity for unreachable pairs, so $T$ is defined
also on disconnected graphs and every iterate remains in its domain.
Write $T^0(G)=G$. A positive return is an integer iteration count $t&gt;0$
with an ordinary graph isomorphism $T^t(G)\cong G$. Define
$\rho_2(G)=\min{t\in\mathbb N: t&gt;0,\ T^t(G)\cong G}$ when that set is
nonempty, and $\rho_2(G)=0$ otherwise. The source starts at $G_1^2=G$,
so its return index is $f=t+1$ and its period $f-1=t$.</p>
<p>The full closed assertion is</p>
<p>$$\forall V\text{ finite},\ \forall G\text{ simple on }V,\quad
G\text{ connected}\ \Longrightarrow
\operatorname{Int.ofNat}(\rho_2(G))\leq
\operatorname{Int.ofNat}(|V|)-3.$$</p>
<p>Both casts precede integer subtraction. There is no size lower bound,
nontriviality requirement, edge requirement, periodicity hypothesis or
restriction on intermediate graphs. Isomorphism need not preserve labels;
eventual periodicity is a different assertion.</p>
