PDF p. 4, Question 10 and matrix (5), asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Suppose f∈P⁺₂, and write f = ∑ f_i(x)y^i. Form the infinite matrix</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Show that the resulting matrix is totally stable. That is, every minor is a stable polynomial.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The matrix has $T_f(r,c)=f_{c-r}$ for $r\le c$ and zero otherwise.
The definitions on PDF p. 1 require upper-half-plane nonvanishing and
positive coefficients on the support, with Hurwitz stability meaning all
roots have nonpositive real part. The related <em>totally stable matrix
conjecture</em> is Conjecture 21.84, Section 21.10, printed p. 648 of
arXiv:math/0612833v2. Its input class is defined through real substitution,
homogeneous positivity and coefficient positivity; no equality of the two
source classes is assumed.</p>
