ernary extension-counterexample length counterexample found quantum information five is not the minimal quaternary extension-counterexample length mahmoud, minimal counterexamples of the macwilliams extension theorem for stabilizer codes, arxiv:2607.26214v1 (2026), §6.2, open problem 2, states: distinguishing these requirements is necessary to identify the actual obstruction and its length. the quaternary quantum extension property already fails at lengths one and three, so length five is not minimal. both parameterizations are additive and injective.  released-dossier 0d0c03ff5b5435dcd06304229278eacd9918e79cebcce65c2e958d548716c223 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Five is not the minimal quaternary extension-counterexample length</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The quaternary quantum extension property already fails at lengths one and three, so length five is not minimal.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Mahmoud, Minimal Counterexamples of the MacWilliams Extension Theorem for Stabilizer Codes, arXiv:2607.26214v1 (2026), §6.2, open problem 2, states:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Distinguishing these requirements is necessary to identify the actual obstruction and its length.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Both parameterizations are additive and injective.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The quaternary quantum extension property already fails at lengths one and three, so length five is not minimal.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="mahmoud-2026-quaternary-extension-minimal-length-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Five is not the minimal quaternary extension-counterexample length</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. A. Mahmoud, <em>Minimal Counterexamples of the MacWilliams Extension Theorem for Stabilizer Codes</em>, arXiv:2607.26214v1 (2026), §6.2, open problem 2, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>\textbf{Minimal length for general $q$.} Determine the minimal counterexample length for general $q$. For $q=2$ it is $3=q+1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:census}); we expect $q+1$ to be minimal in general, in the light of the threshold results for additive isometries in \cite{Dyshko16,DyshkoGeom}, but we claim it only for $q=2$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a verbatim excerpt of <code>MzWi_Ext_Th.tex</code>, line 429, retrieved from the versioned arXiv source; its md5 is <code>ee3d0a4f9ba273634eb55aad8ed7b850</code>. The rest of the item asks separately about full-support minimal lengths. The definitions and source locators are also in <a href="../Library/Quantum/mahmoud2026macwilliams.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>The source's Definition 2.3 (TeX lines 237–239) is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Fix $q$ and $n$. We say the quantum extension property $\mathrm{QEP}(q,n)$ holds if for every pair $C,D\le\A_q^n$ of trace-symplectically self-orthogonal additive codes and every additive bijection $f\colon C\to D$ with $\wt(f(u))=\wt(u)$ for all $u\in C$, the map $f$ extends to a monomial transformation of $\A_q^n$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $A_q=\mathbb F_q^2$, and additive means $\mathbb F_p$-linear, where $p$ is the characteristic (TeX lines 211–217). Definition 2.1 (lines 221–223) specifies a coordinate permutation followed by maps in $\operatorname{Sp}(2,q)$, and explicitly mentions the larger group of all coordinatewise $\mathbb F_p$-linear automorphisms.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20851">#20851</a> fixes both readings at $q=4$. Write $\mathrm{QEP}(4,n)$ for the specified symplectic coordinate group and $\mathrm{QEP}^{\mathrm{add}}(4,n)$ for the larger additive coordinate group. The formal <code>claim</code> is</p>
<p>$\operatorname{IsLeast}{n\in\mathbb N:1\le n\ \land\ \neg\mathrm{QEP}(4,n)},5\ \lor\ \operatorname{IsLeast}{n\in\mathbb N:1\le n\ \land\ \neg\mathrm{QEP}^{\mathrm{add}}(4,n)},5.$</p>
<p><code>IsLeast</code> requires both membership in the failure set and being no larger than any member. This disjunction is refuted: neither reading has least positive failure length $5$.</p>
