d">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Conjecture refuted</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Tian–Dokyeesun–Klavžar Conjecture 3.4 refutation</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Induced vertex deletion can raise the outer general position number by more than the deleted degree.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Conjecture 3.4 in section 3.2 of arXiv:2510.01294v2 states:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The counterexample has 19 vertices, an actual non-cut vertex of degree two, deletion outer number at least eight, and original outer number at most five.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Replace two opposite vertices by independent sets A and B of four vertices, preserving their cycle neighbors.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Induced vertex deletion can raise the outer general position number by more than the deleted degree.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="tian-dokyeesun-klavzar-conjecture34-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Tian–Dokyeesun–Klavžar Conjecture 3.4 refutation</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Conjecture 3.4 in section 3.2 of arXiv:2510.01294v2 states:</p>
