fication details</summary><article class="news-result" id="aliabadi-2025-matchability-subspace-product"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Aliabadi&#x27;s uncapped subspace-product conjecture is false</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Mohsen Aliabadi, <em>Conditions for matchability in groups and field extensions II</em>,
Discussiones Mathematicae General Algebra and Applications 45 (2025), 135–157,
section 5.1, Conjecture 38, printed page 154 / PDF page 20, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let $K\subset L$ be a field extension and $A,B\subset L$ be a $K$-subspace
of $L$ of finite positive dimensions. Assume further that for every
$a,b\in L$ and every nontrivial proper finite intermediate subfield
$K\subset M\subsetneq L$, we have</p>
<p>$$\dim_K(aM\cap A)+\dim_K(bM\cap B)\le [M:K]+1.$$</p>
<p>Then for all $K$-subspaces $S\subset A$ and $T\subset B$, the following holds</p>
<p>$$\dim_K\langle ST\rangle\ge\dim_K S+\dim_K T-1.$$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The same statement is Conjecture 5.1 in arXiv:2309.14664v1, PDF page 14,
and v2/v3, PDF page 15. Section 2.2 defines the brackets as the $K$-linear
span of the actual product set in $L$. The
<a href="../Library/Factorization/aliabadi2025matchabilityii.md">source note</a>
records the primary versions, DOI and locators. The sole target is the
whole original universal assertion. Preregistration:
<a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23409">#23409</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement correspondence</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Formal counterpart and witness |
| --- | --- |
| Arbitrary field extension $K\subset L$ | <code>claim</code> quantifies fields with a <code>K</code>-algebra structure on <code>L</code>. The witness is the actual field <code>AdjoinRoot (X^7-C 2)</code> over $\mathbb Q$, with the canonical injective algebra map and degree seven. |
| Finite positive-dimensional $A,B$ | Both finite-dimensional hypotheses and both positive-finrank hypotheses occur in <code>claim</code>; $A=B=\operatorname{powerSpan}(6)$ has dimension six. |
| Every $a,b\in L$, including zero | Both unrestricted scalar binders occur in <code>intersectionPremise</code>; <code>translate</code> maps the underlying submodule of $M$ by actual multiplication by the scalar. |
| Every finite nontrivial proper intermediate field | <code>IntermediateField K L</code>, <code>⊥ &lt; M</code>, <code>M &lt; ⊤</code>, and <code>FiniteDimensional K M</code>. Neither endpoint is included. Prime degree proves that no witness field has such an $M$. |
| Both intersection dimensions and $[M:K]+1$ | Submodule infima and finranks in <code>intersectionPremise</code> express the exact displayed inequality. No assumed certificate replaces it. |
| Every allowed $S\subset A$, $T\subset B$ | <code>claim</code> quantifies all submodules with <code>S ≤ A</code> and <code>T ≤ B</code>, including the zero subspaces. The counterexample has both containments strict, so it also refutes the strict reading of the printed subset symbols. |
| Actual $\langle ST\rangle$ | <code>Submodule.span K ((S : Set L) * (T : Set L))</code>, using field pointwise multiplication. |
| Uncapped lower bound | <code>finrank K S + finrank K T - 1 ≤ finrank K (span ...)</code>; natural subtraction gives the same inequality truth value as integer subtraction, including zero dimensions. |
| Whole-claim negation | The sole public theorem <code>result : ¬ claim</code> has no extra mathematical premise. |</p>
<p>The matching definitions in the introduction use equal dimensions and
$1\notin B$ when defining matchability. Conjecture 38 states neither
matchability nor these conditions as an added premise. The witness
nonetheless has $A=B$ and $S=T$, with $1$ absent from all four spaces.
It also has $S&lt;A&lt;L$ and $T&lt;B&lt;L$. No assumption that a subspace contain
$1$, that the ambient extension be infinite, or that the dimension sum
be small is inserted. No ambient cap replaces the source conclusion.</p>
