Conjecture 5.6, printed p. 8:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 5.6. Let d be a Bernoulli denominator.
(i) If F (d) = 4a for some integer a, then T (am) = T (a) whenever D₄ₐₘ = D₄ₐ.
(ii) If D₄ₐₘ ≠ D₄ₐ = d, then t(am) ≥ t(a).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Definition 1.1, §1, printed p. 1:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Definition 1.1. Let n be an integer. A Toda prime of n is an odd prime p such that
p − 1 | 4n and gcd(p, 4n/(p−1)) = 1. Denote the set of Toda primes of n by T (n) (see Table 1),
and let t(n) := |T (n)| (see Figure 1).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Notation 5.5, §5.1, printed p. 8:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Notation 5.5. Given an even integer 2m, let P (2m) denote the set of primes such that
p − 1 | 2m, so that D₂ₘ = ∏ₚ∈P(2m) p. Let F (d) = min{2m &gt; 0 : D₂ₘ = d} [PW23].</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Formal reading</h3>
<p>Lean names have prefix <code>D5.S3.Arith.McKeanTodaPrimes.Result</code>.</p>
<p>| Source object | Lean name | Correspondence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $T(n)$ | <code>todaPrimes</code> | Odd primes with predecessor dividing $4n$ and coprime quotient. |
| $t(n)$ | <code>t</code> | Cardinality of the finite Toda-prime set. |
| $D_k$ | <code>bernDen</code> | Denominator of Mathlib's rational <code>bernoulli k</code>. |
| $P(k)$ | <code>denominatorPrimes</code> | Primes whose predecessor divides $k$. |
| Conjecture 5.6(i) | <code>clauseI</code> | Positive $a,m$, first even index $4a$, denominator unchanged. |
| Conjecture 5.6(ii) | <code>clauseII</code> | Positive $a,m$, denominator changed. |
| The full conjecture | <code>claim</code> | Conjunction of the two universal assertions. |
| Both negations | <code>refutedClauses</code> | Each component assertion is false. |
| Refutation | <code>result</code> | Exactly <code>¬ claim</code>. |</p>
<p>The condition $F(D_{4a})=4a$ is expressed equivalently as the absence of a
smaller positive even $k$ with $D_k=D_{4a}$. The equality at $4a$ is automatic.
For positive $n$, the range $p&lt;4n+2$ is exhaustive because
$p-1\mid4n$ implies $p-1\le4n$. At $n=0$, the quotient is zero and
no prime is coprime to zero, so $T(0)$ is empty. All counterexamples have positive indices.
