m):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Number of elements in the Redheffer matrix that contribute to the Moebius function.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>COMMENT conjecture line (verbatim):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>a(11) to a(30) are conjectured to be 11, 23, 13, 18, 19, 25, 17, 29, 19, 30, 26, 26, 23, 45, 27, 30, 32, 39, 29, 48. [From <em>Mats Granvik</em>, Dec 21 2008]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For <code>n≥2</code>, let <code>B_n</code> have rows and columns indexed by <code>1,…,n</code>. Its entry
is 1 exactly when <code>j=1</code> or <code>i</code> divides <code>j</code>, except that <code>B_n(n,n)=0</code>.
A position <code>(i,j)</code> contributes when there is a permutation <code>σ</code> of
<code>{1,…,n}</code> with <code>σ(i)=j</code> and <code>B_n(k,σ(k))=1</code> for every <code>k</code>. Let <code>a(n)</code>
be the number of such positions. Thus the conjecture is the conjunction
of the twenty displayed equalities, starting with <code>a(11)=11</code> and ending
with <code>a(30)=48</code>.</p>
<p>The entry's array for <code>n=8</code> is the transpose of this matrix convention and
contains 13 positions. At <code>n=1</code> the literal modified-matrix definition gives
