t-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The second exterior trace of Spezia’s serpentine matrix</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The second exterior trace of the serpentine matrix has Spezia’s magnitude.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Stefano Spezia, OEIS A322844, COMMENTS revision 68, states:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The two conjectures ask for uniform identities across every matrix order.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The first result therefore proves the absolute-value formula.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The second exterior trace of the serpentine matrix has Spezia’s magnitude.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The result changes their status in the characteristic-coefficient formulation; it makes no claim about unrelated Spezia matrix families or a new general rectangular-product identity.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="sp322844-serpentine"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>The second exterior trace of Spezia’s serpentine matrix</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Stefano Spezia, OEIS A322844, COMMENTS revision 68, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For n &gt; 1, a(n) is the absolute value of the trace of the 2nd exterior power of an n X n square matrix M(n) defined as M[i,j,n] = j + n*(i-1) if i is odd and M[i,j,n] = n*i - j + 1 if i is even (see A317614). Equivalently, a(n) is the absolute value of the coefficient of the term [x^(n-2)] in the characteristic polynomial of the matrix M(n), or the absolute value of the sum of all principal minors of M(n) of size 2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The matrix definition is shared with A317614 revision 194. With one-based indices $1\leq i,j\leq n$, its integer entries are $M_{ij}=j+n(i-1)$ for odd $i$ and $M_{ij}=ni-j+1$ for even $i$. Put $\varepsilon=n\bmod 2$ and</p>
<p>$$
a(n)=\frac{n^2}{12}\bigl(3(1+n^2)-2(2+n^2)\varepsilon\bigr).
$$</p>
