<p>The conjecture resolved here is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For n = 2^m (m natural number), a(n) == 1 (mod n).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Preregistration: <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18644">#18644</a>.
The companion conjecture is recorded in <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18643">#18643</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>The natural binder $m$ ranges over all natural numbers including zero. The conclusion is an integer $z=a(2^m)$ with $z\equiv1\pmod{2^m}$.
The rational formal series have coefficients $1/n!$ in even indices for cosh
and in odd indices for sinh, and zero in their other indices. The coefficient
$a(n)$ is $n!$ times the ordinary coefficient of their formal quotient.
The existential integer equals the rational coefficient after casting;
thus integrality and the congruence are both conclusions. No finite index bound
or extra hypothesis is imposed.</p>
