<p>A permutation class is a downset of actual permutations under subsequence
order-isomorphism. Write <code>C_n</code> for its length-<code>n</code> slice, <code>C_n°</code> for the
fixed-point-free members, and <code>r_C(n) = |C_n°| / |C_n|</code>. Let <code>Attained(l)</code>
mean that some such class has nonempty slices at every length and
<code>r_C(n) -&gt; l</code> as <code>n -&gt; infinity</code>. The affirmative largest-attained-limit
proposition is</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">exists L in R, L &lt; 1 and Attained(L) and
  forall l in R, (l &lt; 1 and Attained(l)) implies l &lt;= L.
</code></pre>
<p>The source imposes no growth or finite-basis condition. Nonempty slices are
provided by the constructed witnesses, so their ratios do not rely on an
empty-denominator convention. Even if a broader convention admits additional
limits, any proposed maximum <code>L &lt; 1</code> is contradicted by the same witness
construction with <code>q = L</code>.</p>
