n class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The unrestricted exact Wang–Tian saturation formula is false</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The unrestricted exact eventual formula for the maximum number of arcs in a saturated strict digraph fails at connectivity three and arbitrarily large odd orders.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>There is no rounding, parity, divisibility restriction or error term.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>An obstruction at arbitrarily large orders defeats the eventual quantifier without computing any extremizer.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The left side is even and the right side odd, a contradiction for every threshold.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The unrestricted exact eventual formula for the maximum number of arcs in a saturated strict digraph fails at connectivity three and arbitrarily large odd orders.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="wang-tian-2026-strong-saturation-exact-formula"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The unrestricted exact Wang–Tian saturation formula is false</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Qinglin Wang and Yingzhi Tian, <em>Extremal Problems for the Family of k-Strongly
Connected Digraphs</em>, arXiv:2605.01269v1, Section 4, Conjecture 1, printed PDF
page 13, state: “For sufficiently large $n$,
$\operatorname{ex}(n,\mathcal D_k)=\frac32(k-\frac43)(n-k+1)+\binom n2$.”
The source is <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2605.01269v1#Thmconj1">Conjecture 1</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>The closed proposition <code>claim</code> quantifies every $k\in\mathbb N$ with
$1\le k$, then a threshold $N\in\mathbb N$ depending on $k$, then every
$n\in\mathbb N$ with $N\le n$. Its equality is</p>
<p>$$
(\operatorname{ex}(n,\mathcal D_k):\mathbb Q)
=\frac32\left((k:\mathbb Q)-\frac43\right)
\left((n:\mathbb Q)-(k:\mathbb Q)+1\right)
+(\operatorname{Nat.choose}(n,2):\mathbb Q).
$$</p>
<p>Every division and subtraction is rational. There is no rounding, parity,
divisibility restriction or error term. The local Section 4 reading $k\ge2$
is also contradicted by $k=3$.</p>
<p>Strict digraphs have ordered distinct-endpoint arcs, permit reciprocal arcs,
and have no parallel arcs. A subdigraph chooses arbitrary vertex and arc
subsets, retaining isolated vertices. Dipaths are vertex-simple directed
paths. A separator leaves a nonstrong or singleton remainder. For nonempty
records the connectivity is the attained minimum separator size. Positive
strong connectivity excludes empty records; the source leaves null
connectivity unspecified. This explicit totalization retains the source's
Lemma 2.2 consequence that a $k$-strong graph has at least $k+1$ vertices.
Saturation means forbidden-free and that every missing ordered arc creates
a $k$-strong arbitrary subdigraph. The extremum is the actual attained
maximum arc count over a proved nonempty finite family of saturated hosts.
The Blueprint explains the invertible relabelling to <code>Fin n</code>; it does not
claim a separate compiled transport theorem.</p>
