i and henning for every clique size at least two. the proof preserves exterior adjacencies and degrees.  released-dossier de676205c28a52a35ccd72c5a5c0fdbdf3e2da432cc85f44543fcb2004c419a4 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>Source</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Strong domination under clique gluing</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The strong domination number of a clique gluing satisfies Inequality (4) of Alikhani, Ghanbari and Henning for every clique size at least two.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>A strong dominating set contains, for every vertex outside it, an adjacent vertex of at least that vertex&#x27;s degree.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The cited Library note records the journal locator and source definitions.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The proof preserves exterior adjacencies and degrees.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The strong domination number of a clique gluing satisfies Inequality (4) of Alikhani, Ghanbari and Henning for every clique size at least two.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="alikhani-ghanbari-henning-clique-gluing-problem-1"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Strong domination under clique gluing</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Saeid Alikhani, Nima Ghanbari and Michael A. Henning, <em>Strong domination number
of graphs from primary subgraphs</em>, Quaestiones Mathematicae 49(2), 195–211,
Problem 1, p. 202, asks whether, for every $r\ge2$,</p>
<p>$$\gamma_{st}(G)\ge\gamma_{st}(G_1)+\gamma_{st}(G_2)-1-\Sigma_{X,Y}.\tag{4}$$</p>
<p>The finite connected graphs $G_1,G_2$ have order at least $r+1$ and contain
enumerated $r$-cliques $A,B$. The graph $G$ identifies corresponding clique
vertices. Here $X_i=N_{G_1}(u_i)\setminus A$,
$Y_i=N_{G_2}(v_i)\setminus B$, and
$\Sigma_{X,Y}=\sum_i|X_i|+\sum_i|Y_i|$. A strong dominating set contains,
for every vertex outside it, an adjacent vertex of at least that vertex's degree.
The strong domination number is its minimum cardinality.</p>
<p>The settlement is <strong>Proved</strong> for Inequality (4), under exactly these hypotheses;
the preregistration is <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/19327">#19327</a>.
The source's request for an optimum using finer parameters is outside this claim.</p>
