<blockquote>
<p>We conclude that, when the point $(s,t)$ attains the second equality in \eqref{eq:QI_comp_neg}, the instrument $\tilde{\ii}^{(t)}$ is actually the unique $W$-covariant joint instrument for $\Qo_s$ and $I_t$. It is an open question whether in this case there exist other (necessarily not $W$-covariant) joint instruments.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The meter:</strong> $\mathsf Q_s(x)=s|\varphi_x\rangle\langle\varphi_x|+(1-s)I/d$.</li>
<li><strong>The channel:</strong> $I_t=t,\mathrm{id}+(1-t),\mathrm{tr}(\cdot),I/d$.</li>
<li><strong>Joint instrument:</strong> completely positive maps $J_x$ with $\sum_xJ_x=I_t$ and $\mathrm{tr},J_x(\rho)=\mathrm{tr}(\rho,\mathsf Q_s(x))$.</li>
<li><strong>The upper boundary:</strong> $d^2s=(d^2-2)(1-t)+2\sqrt{(1-d^2)t^2+(d^2-2)t+1}$, for $t\in[-1/(d^2-1),1]$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The definitions and the instrument $\tilde{\mathcal J}^{(t)}$ are quoted in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/carmeliheinosaaritoigo2025postponing.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20028">#20028</a> fixes the reading: on the upper boundary, every joint instrument equals $\tilde{\mathcal J}^{(t)}$, for every $d\ge2$ and every orthonormal basis. This is proved.</p>
