-input exclusion distribution over all rebit strategies is exactly one twelfth. 1. linear dependence. real symmetric 2 × 2 matrices form a three-dimensional space, so the four states satisfy σx wx ρx = 0 for some real w ≠ 0.  released-dossier 485f2fbc29012ab3714dd9648b4c086c2739a995be7d0acf5b318dd0806c3b5a "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The minimum average trace distance of a rebit strategy for Elliott&#x27;s exclusion task is exactly 1/12</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The minimum average trace distance from the four-input exclusion distribution over all rebit strategies is exactly one twelfth.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Elliott, Strict advantage of complex quantum theory in a communication task, arXiv:2505.16523v2, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The exact minimum turns the qualitative real–complex separation into a quantitative one.</p><p><span>The idea</span>1. Linear dependence. Real symmetric 2 × 2 matrices form a three-dimensional space, so the four states satisfy Σx wx ρx = 0 for some real w ≠ 0.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The minimum average trace distance from the four-input exclusion distribution over all rebit strategies is exactly one twelfth.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="elliott-2025-rebit-minimum-distance"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The minimum average trace distance of a rebit strategy for Elliott&#x27;s exclusion task is exactly 1/12</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>T. J. Elliott, <em>Strict advantage of complex quantum theory in a communication task</em>, arXiv:2505.16523v2, Phys. Rev. A <strong>111</strong>, 062401 (2025), studies a four-input task with target distribution P(y|x) = (1 − δ_xy)/3 on X = Y = {0, 1, 2, 3}. A complex qubit realizes it exactly, while no real qubit (rebit) strategy does. For the robustness of this separation the source measures the error by d = (1/|X|) Σ_x (1/2) Σ_y |P(y|x) − P̃(y|x)|. It shows that d = 1/12 is achievable with a rebit (Appendix D), and writes: &quot;We therefore only weakly conjecture that d = 1/12 the the minimum distance for a rebit strategy.&quot; The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/Quantum/elliott2025complex.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16523">#16523</a> fixes the reading. A rebit strategy is:</p>
<ul>
<li>four real symmetric positive-semidefinite 2 × 2 states of trace 1;</li>
<li>a four-outcome POVM of real symmetric positive-semidefinite effects summing to the identity.</li>
</ul>
<p>Mixed states and POVMs cover private randomness and post-processing on either side. The claim is that 1/12 is the least distance attained by such strategies. It is proved.</p>
