he A193020 plus-one eventual identity</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The endpoint-gap rectangle is exactly the distinct ordinary self-shuffle support.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Layman, states the separate assertion a(2^k+1)=k^2 with its sufficiently-large lower-bound caveat.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Counting the support of the shuffle language makes the distinction between output words and interleaving schedules essential.</p><p><span>The idea</span>For k at least one, the ordinary binary numeral of 2^k+1 is one, k-1 zeros, one.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The endpoint-gap rectangle is exactly the distinct ordinary self-shuffle support.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a193020-plus-one-eventual-identity"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>The A193020 plus-one eventual identity</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A193020, revision 13, with the 2011 entry attribution to John W.
Layman, states the separate assertion <code>a(2^k+1)=k^2</code> with its
sufficiently-large lower-bound caveat. Here a(n), for positive n, counts
DISTINCT output words obtained by shuffling the ordinary binary expansion
of n with itself. Each shuffle preserves the order within both copies;
different schedules producing the same word contribute only once.</p>
<p>The exact selected assertion is</p>
<p>$$\exists K\in\mathbb N,\quad 1\le K\ \land<br />
\forall k\in\mathbb N,\quad K\le k\ \Longrightarrow\ a(2^k+1)=k^2.$$</p>
<p>This dossier anchors only that plus-one question. The other identities in
A193020 are separate questions.</p>
