e2bc04cc "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Operational comparison of qubit incompatibility without equal lengths</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Restricted-state compatibility determines incompatible unbiased qubit pairs.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Imamura, Comparing quantum incompatibility of device sets from an operational perspective, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The operational ordering compares incompatibility by the state sets on which it can be detected.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Equality of all restricted compatibility relations forces the four vectors into one plane.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Restricted-state compatibility determines incompatible unbiased qubit pairs.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="torii-takakura-imamura-2025-proposition-7-generalization"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Operational comparison of qubit incompatibility without equal lengths</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>K. Torii, R. Takakura and R. Imamura, <em>Comparing quantum incompatibility of device sets from an
operational perspective</em>, Phys. Rev. A <strong>112</strong>, 062228 (2025), arXiv:2509.08284v2, §V:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Future work will also address the generalization of Proposition 7, as it remains an open problem whether
the result holds without the constraints of |a₁|=|a₂| and |b₁|=|b₂|.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Two pairs of unbiased qubit observables are operationally equivalent when, for every set $S$ of states, one
pair is $S$-compatible exactly when the other is; $S$-compatibility means that some four-outcome POVM has
both margins with the same Born probabilities as the pair on every state of $S$. Proposition 7 proves that,
for $|a_1|=|a_2|$ and $|b_1|=|b_2|$ in $(1/\sqrt2,1]$, equivalence forces equality up to signs and exchange.
