an></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Cui–Sun Conjecture 2.1: integral Legendre telescopers</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Integral coefficient polynomials provide telescopers for every positive odd-power Legendre sum.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Li-Li Cui and Zhi-Hong Sun, Curious identities involving Legendre polynomials and Apéry-like numbers, arXiv:2607.12330v1, page 10, state:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Equality as polynomials also includes x = 1 and entails every rational specialization without division by 1 − x.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The operator lowers positive degree by one and annihilates constants.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Integral coefficient polynomials provide telescopers for every positive odd-power Legendre sum.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Such consequences still require the source&#x27;s specialization formulas, denominator controls and congruence estimates; their all-m analogues remain open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="cui-sun-2026-legendre-odd-power-telescoper"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Cui–Sun Conjecture 2.1: integral Legendre telescopers</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Li-Li Cui and Zhi-Hong Sun, <em>Curious identities involving Legendre polynomials and Apéry-like numbers</em>, arXiv:2607.12330v1, page 10, state:</p>
