<blockquote>
<p>We also conjecture that for any fixed n and any choice of skew-symmetrizer d₁, …, dₙ, the invariant δ is binary. This is supported by several explicit computations.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For an integer matrix $B$, fix positive integer weights $d_i$ with $d_i b_{ij}=-d_j b_{ji}$. The real symmetric matrix $S(B)$ has diagonal $2$, upper entries $\operatorname{sgn}(b_{ij})\sqrt{|b_{ij}b_{ji}|}$ and lower entries $\operatorname{sgn}(b_{ji})\sqrt{|b_{ij}b_{ji}|}$. The invariant is $\delta(B)=\det S(B)\pmod4$. Binary means at most two residues for fixed size and weights, without asserting that both are attained.</p>
