contains Benedict W. J. Irwin's unnumbered conjecture:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture: a(n) = HookSum(T(n))-HookSum(T(n-1)), where HookSum(A) is the sum of all hooks across a tableau A, and T(n) are the spiraling sequence of tableaux:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The complete displayed diagrams and arithmetic rows are reproduced in
<code>Library/FiniteGeometry/irwin2016a138099.md</code>. The arithmetic row is
$R(k,j)=j+\lfloor k/2\rfloor$, for $k\geq1$ and
$1\leq j\leq\lceil k/2\rceil$. Put
$S(k)=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\lceil i/2\rceil$. A source sequence satisfies
$a(S(k-1)+j)=R(k,j)$ on that domain, with no restriction on $a(0)$.</p>
<p>The literal square spiral begins at $(0,0)$, travels west, south, east,
north, and repeats these directions with leg lengths $1,1,2,2,3,3,\ldots$.
$T_0=\varnothing$ and $T_{n+1}$ inserts walk cell $n$ into $T_n$.
For occupied integer cells, an ordinary hook contains its own cell and the
occupied cells strictly right in its row and strictly below in its column.
$H(D)$ sums these hook lengths; $H(\varnothing)=0$.</p>
