<p>The answer is <strong>six</strong>, including attainment.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>A state is a positive semidefinite trace-one matrix on $\mathbb C^3\otimes\mathbb C^3$. The public definition <code>apStates</code> is exactly the set of such matrices satisfying the frozen <code>QutritPerturbationAttainment.APPT</code>: every global unitary conjugate has positive semidefinite partial transpose. Extremality is <code>Set.extremePoints ℝ apStates</code>; the number of distinct eigenvalues is <code>(spectrum ℂ rho).ncard</code>, without multiplicity. <code>claim</code> is the conjunction of the universal bound $\le 6$ and existence of an extreme state with exactly six eigenvalues. <code>result : claim</code> has no extra hypotheses. The real scalar field is the field of convex coefficients; the complex spectrum belongs to the original complex matrix, rather than a real surrogate. Finite-dimensional Hermitian spectra are finite.</p>
