dence. In the source member <code>FMG_v1.tex</code>, lines 689–690, they write (mathematical macros expanded and the assumption cross-reference rendered):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the <code>MathematicaNotebook1.nb</code> sheet we provide numerical evidence of the validity of Assumption 2.1, and specifically of the fact that for all $m\in[-2,2]\setminus{0}$, $\min_{\vec{k}\in\mathbb{B}}(\lambda_4(\vec{k};m)-\mu)&gt;0$ and $\max_{\vec{k}\in\mathbb{B}}(\lambda_3(\vec{k};m)-\mu)&lt;0$. We expect that, with some additional technical effort, this can be fully proved analytically, but we prefer not to belabor the details of such a proof here.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Their Bloch Hamiltonian is Eq. <code>eqn:Hofst_Hamiltonian</code>, lines 663–671:</p>
<p>$$
H(\vec k;m)=-\begin{pmatrix}
d&amp;1-i e^{2ik_1}&amp;1-i e^{2ik_2}&amp;0\
1+i e^{-2ik_1}&amp;0&amp;0&amp;-i+e^{2ik_2}\
1+i e^{-2ik_2}&amp;0&amp;0&amp;1+i e^{2ik_1}\
0&amp;i+e^{-2ik_2}&amp;1-i e^{-2ik_1}&amp;-d
\end{pmatrix},\qquad d=2+m.
$$</p>
<p>The substitution $d=2+m$ and the Fermi energy are specified at lines 686–687. Write $\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\lambda_3\le\lambda_4$ for its real eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity, $\vec k_F=(\pi/4,\pi/4)$, and $\mu:=\lambda_3(\vec k_F;0)$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22177">#22177</a> fixes the reading: $\lambda_4(\vec k_F;0)=\mu$, and for every real $m\in[-2,2]\setminus{0}$ there is $\delta&gt;0$ such that</p>
<p>$$
\mu+\delta\le\lambda_4(\vec k;m),\qquad
\lambda_3(\vec k;m)\le\mu-\delta
\quad\text{for every }\vec k\in\mathbb R^2.
$$</p>
<p>The same $\delta$ works for all momenta at a fixed mass; it may depend on the mass. This is proved.</p>
