<p>This dossier anchors exactly this question, repeated in Section 7. The source's
standing convention is finite, simple, undirected, connected graphs. Connected
hosts are nonempty; orders one and two are included. The preceding theorem's
order-at-least-three hypothesis does not restrict the question.</p>
<p>For every distinct selected pair, some shortest path in the original host
must avoid the other selected vertices. Different pairs may use different
paths. The configuration graph contains every feasible subset, including empty
and all feasible cardinalities. Distinct configurations $S,T$ are adjacent
when $|S\mathbin{\triangle}T|=1$, or when $S\setminus T={x}$,
$T\setminus S={y}$ and $xy$ is an actual host edge. Thus a slide replaces
an occupied vertex by an unoccupied neighbor.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart |
| --- | --- |
| Every finite nonempty simple undirected host | <code>V</code>, <code>[Fintype V]</code>, <code>[DecidableEq V]</code>, <code>[Nonempty V]</code>, <code>H : SimpleGraph V</code> in <code>FullMaximalConnectivityClaim</code> |
