ning all metallic parameters and higher shifts. every higher shifted metallic hankel sequence has the stated residue polynomial laws from the first term. its ordered indices, sign, common-degree reversals and zero-support guards retain the original moments.  released-dossier e4bf57ee592c0d9dfe53a88f83003188643d9eddbe7b58deb334a9dd65dd394a "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Conjecture proved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Han–Pedon Conjecture C: all higher-shift metallic Hankel residue polynomials</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Every higher shifted metallic Hankel sequence has the stated residue polynomial laws from the first term.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Guo-Niu Han and Emmanuel Pedon, Hankel determinants for the (n+2)-shifted q-metallic numbers, arXiv:2610.36132v1, introduction, Conjecture C.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Conjecture C supplies that structure from the first term while retaining all metallic parameters and higher shifts.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Its ordered indices, sign, common-degree reversals and zero-support guards retain the original moments.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every higher shifted metallic Hankel sequence has the stated residue polynomial laws from the first term.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="han-pedon-metallic-hankel-quasipolynomial"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Han–Pedon Conjecture C: all higher-shift metallic Hankel residue polynomials</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Guo-Niu Han and Emmanuel Pedon, <em>Hankel determinants for the (n+2)-shifted q-metallic numbers</em>, arXiv:2610.36132v1, introduction, Conjecture C. The defining quadratic is equation (2.13); equations (1.4) and (1.5) define the shifted determinant and its empty value. The original preregistration is <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23253">issue #23253</a>. The follow-up is distinct from arXiv:2502.05993v2 and its Conjecture E on bounded-shift periodicity and higher-shift unboundedness.</p>
<p>For every natural $n\ge1$, there exists $\Phi\in\mathbb Z[[q]]$ with constant coefficient one satisfying</p>
<p>$$q\Phi^2+\bigl((1+q^n)(1-q)-q[n]_q\bigr)\Phi=1,
\qquad [n]<em>q=\sum</em>{i=0}^{n-1}q^i.$$</p>
<p>For every series satisfying these conditions and every natural shift $\ell\ge n+3$, define</p>
<p>$$\Delta_j^{(\ell)}=\det\bigl([q^{\ell+a+b}]\Phi\bigr)_{0\le a,b&lt;j},
\qquad \Delta_0^{(\ell)}=1,$$</p>
<p>and put $\rho=2n(n+1)$ for even $n$ and $\rho=4n(n+1)$ for odd $n$. There is a family $Q_r\in\mathbb Q[X]$, indexed by every $0\le r&lt;\rho$, such that</p>
<p>$$\Delta_{r+m\rho}^{(\ell)}=Q_r(m)\quad\text{for every }m\in\mathbb N,$$</p>
<p>and $Q_r(z)\in\mathbb Z$ for every $z\in\mathbb Z$. Here $\mathbb N$ includes zero. There is no eventual threshold, excluded initial residue, target or neighboring-minor invertibility condition, degree bound, or assertion that $\rho$ is minimal. Existence is a conclusion, and every satisfying metallic series is covered.</p>
