<blockquote>
<p>Unlike the upper bound, we note that the lower bound from (\ref{eq.A_bounds}) has not yet shown to be tight.
We believe that the availability at $q=1$ of a policy that always fails purification ($c_k=d_k=0$, $\forall k$) constitutes a tight lower bound for any other purification policy.
We leave this proof as future work.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The system has one long-lived storage memory and $n$ short-lived generation memories per node. Write $p_g$ for the generation probability per short-lived memory, $p_c$ for the consumption probability, $q$ for the probability of attempting purification, $\Gamma$ for the depolarising decay parameter and $F_{\mathrm{new}}$ for the newly generated fidelity.</p>
<p>The module's <code>admissible</code> requires, for each $k\in\mathrm{Fin},n$ and every $f\in[1/4,1]$,
$$
0\le c_k(f-1/4)+d_k\le1,\qquad
0\le a_k(f-1/4)+b_k\le\frac34\bigl(c_k(f-1/4)+d_k\bigr).
$$
Here $k$ represents the source's $k.\mathrm{val}+1$ generated links. These are the probability and unnormalised output-fidelity conditions of Appendix C, <code>main.tex</code> lines 1734–1767.</p>
<p>Write $A((a,b,c,d),q)$ for <code>availability n pg pc q gam fnew a b c d</code>, the real-valued closed formula in the source's Theorem 1 (<code>main.tex</code> lines 378–415). Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21747">#21747</a> fixes the reading: for every natural $n\ge1$, real $p_g,p_c\in(0,1]$, $\Gamma\ge0$, $F_{\mathrm{new}}\in(1/4,1]$, admissible real coefficient families $a,b,c,d:\mathrm{Fin},n\to\mathbb R$, and $q\in[0,1]$,
$$
A((0,0,0,0),1)\le A((a,b,c,d),q).
$$
This is <code>claim</code>, and <code>result : claim</code> proves it. The identification of the closed formula with the limiting fraction of consumption requests finding a link is the source's Theorem 1; that stochastic identification is not formalized in this module. The definitions and source locators are also in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/inestaetal2026entanglementbuffering.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
