span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Jenzer&#x27;s permuted rotor-loop formula without the star condition</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Permuted rotor loops obey the subset formula without the star condition.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Nik Jenzer, Star-Ordered Rotor Loops in Clifford Algebras, arXiv:2610.21593v1, Theorem 5.5 and the following scope remark, TeX Remark 5.7, rendered Remark 5.5, page 19, asks whether the permuted loop formula remains true without the star condition:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The scalar-cyclicity and exterior-word modules supply consumed parts of this proof.</p><p><span>The idea</span>JenzerPermutedRotorLoopAlgebra.NativeBridge.scalarcyclic proves cyclicity of the native scalar coefficient.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Permuted rotor loops obey the subset formula without the star condition.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="jen2610"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Jenzer&#x27;s permuted rotor-loop formula without the star condition</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Nik Jenzer, <em>Star-Ordered Rotor Loops in Clifford Algebras</em>, arXiv:2610.21593v1,
Theorem 5.5 and the following scope remark, TeX Remark 5.7, rendered Remark 5.5,
page 19, asks whether the permuted loop formula remains true without the star condition:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Nevertheless, numerical evidence suggests that the formula also holds without the star condition in that case: tested on the triangle family from Remark 4.4, which violates the star condition, under all six permutations of {1, 2, 3} with random parameter sets; the largest deviation encountered was in the range of double machine precision. A general proof would have to capture the cancellation of the odd off-diagonal terms in coarser groups than pairs; we have not determined such a grouping.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For an anticommuting unit bivector family $B_1,\ldots,B_k$ in a finite-dimensional
nondegenerate real Clifford algebra, $k\geq1$, put
$c_i=\cos(\theta_i/2)$, $s_i=\sin(\theta_i/2)$, $w_i=s_i^2$,
$U_i=c_i+s_iB_i$, and
$\Theta_k(\tau)=(U_1\cdots U_k)(\widetilde U_{\tau(1)}\cdots\widetilde U_{\tau(k)})$.
Then the proved statement is</p>
<p>$$
\langle\Theta_k(\tau)\rangle_0
=\sum_{S\subseteq{1,\ldots,k}}
\eta(|S|)(-1)^{\iota(\tau,S)}
\prod_{i\in S}w_i\prod_{i\notin S}(1-w_i),
\qquad
\eta(m)=(-1)^m(-1)^{m(m+1)/2}.
$$</p>
<p>Here $\iota(\tau,S)$ counts pairs $i&lt;j$ in $S$ with
$\tau^{-1}(i)&gt;\tau^{-1}(j)$. No star condition is assumed.</p>
