IS A398726 states:</p><p><span>The idea</span>The delivery contains only the two necessary source definitions and one result.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The total weight of distinct cycle lengths over all permutations is asymptotic to n times n factorial.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a398726-distinct-cycle-size-limit"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>OEIS A398726: the distinct cycle size limit</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Vaclav Kotesovec's August 14, 2026 FORMULA line in OEIS A398726 states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture: a(n) ~ n * n!.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The sequence, entered by Alois P. Heinz on August 8, 2026, is the sum of the
distinct cycle sizes over all permutations of [n]. The exact source and full
statement, preregistered in issue #8059, are:</p>
<pre><code class="language-lean">noncomputable def DistinctCycleSizes {n : ℕ}
    (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) : Finset ℕ :=
  σ.partition.parts.toFinset

noncomputable def CycleSizeSum (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=
  ∑ σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n), ∑ k ∈ DistinctCycleSizes σ, k

theorem result :
  Filter.Tendsto
    (fun n : ℕ =&gt; (CycleSizeSum n : ℝ) /
      ((n : ℝ) * (Nat.factorial n : ℝ)))
    Filter.atTop (nhds (1 : ℝ))
</code></pre>
<p>Partition parts include fixed points as lengths one; the finite set removes
multiplicities. Each distinct length contributes its size. Both sums range
over their entire finite domains. The empty permutation contributes zero.
Fin n is a relabeling of [n], and the real normalization is positive for n&gt;0.
No sequence, counting identity or asymptotic estimate is an assumed premise.</p>
