 is strictly smaller than 5/8.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="khavinson-pereira-putinar-saff-shimorin-2011-nonunivalence-radius"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Khavinson–Pereira–Putinar–Saff–Shimorin: nonunivalence radius</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. Khavinson, R. Pereira, M. Putinar, E. B. Saff and S. Shimorin, <em>Borcea's variance conjectures on the critical points of polynomials</em>, arXiv:1010.5167v2, §5, Theorem 5.1 and Conjecture 4. See <a href="../Library/Zeros/khavinson2011borcea.md">khavinson2011borcea</a>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Theorem 5.1 (Alexander-Kakeya). If a polynomial F of degree n has no critical points in a closed disk of radius R, then it is univalent in the concentric disk of radius R sin(π/n).</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>If F is an nth degree polynomial with F(0) = 0, then F is not univalent in any closed disk of radius larger than σ_p(F) sin(π/n), p ≥ 1, centered at zero.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Of course, if the conjecture is true for p = 1, then it follows for all p ≥ 1. By the Alexander-Kakeya Theorem, the truth of this conjecture implies the truth of Conjecture 1 for the same value of p. It can be easily seen that the strongest case p = 1 of this conjecture is true for n = 2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For every $n\ge1$, every complex polynomial $F$ of degree $n$ vanishing at zero, every real $p\ge1$ and every real $r&gt;\sigma_p(F)\sin(\pi/n)$, the assertion forbids injectivity on the closed disk centered at zero with radius $r$. Zeros are counted with algebraic multiplicity, and $\sigma_p$ is the infimum over all complex centers of the normalized root moment. The formal definition includes every stated quantifier and imposes no simple-root or geometric restrictions.</p>
