parameters of the family of linear Jaco graphs</em>, arXiv:2507.16500v1,
Section 2.2, Conjecture 2.2, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For the finite linear Jaco graphs J_{n}(x), n=2,3,4,\dots the respective
maximum degrees \Delta(J_{n}(x)) are yielded by the sequence A319433 with
closed formula \Delta(J_{n}(x))=\lfloor\frac{2(n+2)}{1+\sqrt{5}}\rfloor-1,
n=2,3,4,\dots</p>
<p>Furthermore, it implies that sequence A319433 is p-graphical where
p(G)=\Delta(G) and \mathcal{F}={G:G=J_{n}(x),n=1,2,3,\dots}.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The formal graph is the underlying simple undirected graph of the finite
directed linear Jaco graph with <code>f(x)=x</code>. For vertices <code>v_i</code>, <code>1 ≤ i ≤ n</code>,
write <code>d_i</code> for the number of earlier vertices <code>v_k</code> satisfying
<code>2*k-d_k ≥ i</code>; for <code>i &lt; j ≤ n</code>, the directed arc <code>(v_i,v_j)</code> exists exactly
when <code>2*i-d_i ≥ j</code>. <code>J_n(x)</code> forgets orientation and <code>Delta</code> is its ordinary
maximum degree.</p>
<p>The sequence term uses its actual offset-zero index. For <code>n &gt; 0</code>, write the
canonical Zeckendorf representation with weights <code>Fib_2=1</code>, <code>Fib_3=2</code>, and no
consecutive occupied positions. Delete the digit position of weight <code>Fib_2</code>,
whether its digit is one or zero: remove occupied index 2 if present, lower
every surviving occupied index by one, and decode in the same Fibonacci
system. Call the result
<code>a(n)=deleteLeast(n)</code>, with <code>a(0)=a(1)=0</code>.</p>
<p>The complete assertion is</p>
<p>$$
\left(\forall n\in\mathbb N,;2\le n\Rightarrow
\left(\Delta(J_n(x))=\left\lfloor\frac{2(n+2)}{1+\sqrt5}\right\rfloor-1
\land\Delta(J_n(x))=a(n)\right)\right)
\land
\left(\forall n\in\mathbb N,;1\le n\Rightarrow
\Delta(J_n(x))=a(n)\right).
$$</p>
<p>The second conjunct retains the singleton <code>J_1(x)</code>, whose degree and deletion
value are both zero. The first conjunct starts at <code>n=2</code>, exactly as in the
source. The index-zero sequence term is not reassigned to <code>J_1</code>; no <code>J_0</code>,
offset shift, order cutoff, subsequence, changed graph, or extra premise is
included.</p>
