p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>An all-radius formula for other root layers, other slab patterns or other uniform tilings is not supplied by result; those extensions remain open here. - Effect on dependent source statements.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="barker-2018-oeis-a299285-tea-coordination-sequence"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Barker&#x27;s coordination formulas for the tea slab graph</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Colin Barker's 2018 formulas occur in N. J. A. Sloane's OEIS entry A299285, <em>Coordination sequence for &quot;tea&quot; 3D uniform tiling</em>. The source is an OEIS entry, without a DOI or an arXiv version. Its plain-text representation at <a href="https://oeis.org/search?q=id:A299285&amp;fmt=text">the source endpoint</a>, revision 25, has the following verbatim text in its five consecutive <code>%F</code> records, lines 15–19:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>From <em>Colin Barker</em>, Feb 11 2018: (Start)
G.f.: (1 + 8*x + 14*x^2 + 17*x^3 + 14*x^4 + 8*x^5 + x^6) / ((1 - x)^3*(1 + x)*(1 + x^2)).
a(n) = 2*a(n-1) - a(n-2) + a(n-4) - 2*a(n-5) + a(n-6) for n&gt;6. (End)
[I suspect Barker's formulas only conjectures. - <em>N. J. A. Sloane</em>, Jun 12 2024]
If the above formulas are true, then a(n) = (31 - 3*(-1)^n + 126*n^2 + 4*A056594(n))/16 for n &gt; 0. - <em>Stefano Spezia</em>, Jun 08 2024</p>
</blockquote>
<p>OEIS A056594 defines the period-four sequence $1,0,-1,0$. Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26493">#26493</a> fixes the graph reading of Spezia's closed form. The vertices are $(i,j,k)\in\mathbb Z^3$. In each layer, the six horizontal edge differences are $(1,0)$, $(-1,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(0,-1)$, $(1,-1)$ and $(-1,1)$. An edge from layer $k$ to layer $k+1$ has horizontal shift in</p>
<p>$S_k={(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)}$ if $k\equiv0\pmod4$;</p>
<p>$S_k={(0,0),(-1,0),(0,-1)}$ if $k\equiv2\pmod4$;</p>
<p>$S_k={(0,0)}$ if $k$ is odd.</p>
<p>All edges are undirected. Let $a(n)$ be <code>shell n</code>, the natural cardinality <code>Set.ncard</code> of the vertices at graph distance exactly $n$ from $(0,0,0)$ in <code>teaGraph</code>. The proposition <code>claim</code> says that, for every positive natural number $n$,</p>
<p>$8a(n)=63n^2+c_r$, where $r=n\bmod4$ and $(c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3)=(16,17,12,17)$.</p>
<p>The module proves <code>result : claim</code>. The <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/barker2018a299285.md">literature note</a> records the source and its geometric interpretation.</p>
