summary><article class="news-result" id="bergman-question19-question16-field-condition"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Bergman&#x27;s Question 19: the field condition in Question 16</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Bergman, <em>Thoughts on Eggert's Conjecture</em>, arXiv:1206.0326v2,
Contemporary Mathematics 609 (2014), 1–17, Question 16, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let R be a finite-dimensional nilpotent commutative algebra over an
algebraically closed field F, let V be a subspace of R, and let n be a
positive integer such that every nonzero element of V has nonzero n-th
power. Must dim R ≥ n dim V?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Question 19 explicitly asks whether, for Question 16, algebraic closedness
can be dropped or weakened to “F is infinite”. The target is only this
field-condition branch, retaining associativity and commutativity. Convention 2
assumes associativity unless explicitly stated otherwise; an identity is not
required. All dimensions and all linear structures use the same F.</p>
<p>The affirmative infinite-field assertion is quantified over every infinite
field F, every finite-dimensional F-vector space R with an actual F-bilinear
multiplication satisfying associativity and commutativity, every linear
subspace V, and every positive integer n. Common global nilpotence and
nonvanishing n-th powers of every nonzero vector in V are its antecedents.
The conclusion is n dim_F V ≤ dim_F R.</p>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart |
| --- | --- |
| every infinite field | <code>F : Type</code>, <code>[Field F]</code>, <code>[Infinite F]</code> |
| F-vector space | <code>R : Type</code>, <code>[AddCommGroup R]</code>, <code>[Module F R]</code> |
| finite dimension | <code>[FiniteDimensional F R]</code> |
| F-bilinear multiplication | <code>μ : R →ₗ[F] R →ₗ[F] R</code> |
| associativity | <code>∀ x y z, μ (μ x y) z = μ x (μ y z)</code> |
| commutativity | <code>∀ x y, μ x y = μ y x</code> |
| common nilpotence | <code>CommonNilpotent μ</code> |
| actual F-linear subspace | <code>V : Submodule F R</code> |
| positive exponent | <code>n : ℕ</code>, <code>0 &lt; n</code> |
| all nonzero vectors | <code>∀ v ∈ V, v ≠ 0 → … ≠ 0</code> |
| n-th power without identity | <code>(List.replicate (n-1) v).foldr (fun x y =&gt; μ x y) v</code> |
| dimension bound | <code>n * Module.finrank F V ≤ Module.finrank F R</code> |
| exact negation | closed <code>claim : Prop</code>, designated <code>result : ¬ claim</code> |</p>
<p><code>CommonNilpotent μ</code> means that there is k in N such that every list xs of
length k and every terminal z have <code>xs.foldr μ z = 0</code>. The terminal is an
additional factor: the common vanishing length is k+1, always positive.
Under associativity this is global algebra nilpotence, equivalently the
corresponding product-span being zero. It is stronger than pointwise
nilpotence. For positive n the displayed power has exactly n factors;
for n=1 it is v. The closed assertion uses ordinary small carriers, including
arbitrary infinite carriers. The counterexample lies in these universes and
also refutes the unrestricted mathematical assertion.</p>
