<p>In order to prove that our conjecture of $\eta^<em>=4/(m+4)$ for $m_{\mathrm{A}}=m_{\mathrm{B}}=m$ is correct, one would need to provide a extremal $\mathcal{NS}$ distribution $p^{\mathrm{NS}}$, and corresponding Bell inequality $s_{a'b'}^{xy}$, such that $\sum_{a',b',x,y}s_{a'b'}^{xy}p^{\mathrm{NS}}_{\eta}(a'b'|xy) \not \leq k,; \forall \eta &gt; \eta^</em>$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The named conjecture asserts $\eta^*(m)=4/(m+4)$ for every $m\ge2$. Its definitions and the refutation criterion are fixed in <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/15572">#15572</a>. The source definitions and locator are in <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/cope2021binarydetection.md">the Library note</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Fidelity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Two parties, $m$ inputs each, binary outputs | Two <code>Fin m</code> inputs and two <code>Bool</code> outputs in <code>IsNSBox</code> | Equal input counts; failure is an additional output only after loss. |
| Conditional probabilities and no signalling (Section 2) | Four conjuncts of <code>IsNSBox</code>: nonnegativity, normalization, and both input-independent marginals | Every input and output is quantified. |
| Independent equal-efficiency loss, equation (3) | All four branches of <code>lossy</code> | <code>some</code> denotes a detected bit and <code>none</code> denotes $F$; the two single-failure terms use the original marginals. |
| Convex hull of deterministic local responses | <code>IsLocal</code> | A finite weight for every pair of response functions, nonnegative weights, sum one, and pointwise reconstruction. |
| No-signalling threshold, minimum of extremal-box thresholds | <code>etaStar m</code>, the supremum of $\eta\in[0,1]$ making every no-signalling box local after loss | The all-box formulation below is the convention of #15572 and expresses the same threshold. |
| Symmetric conjecture $\eta^<em>=4/(m+4)$ | <code>claim</code> | Quantifies every natural $m\ge2$; division is in $\mathbb R$. |
| Proposed extremal-box Bell-inequality certificate above the conjectured threshold | No additional Lean hypothesis | This is the source's proposed way to establish tightness, not an assumption of its equality conjecture. The local construction above that conjectured value disproves the equality instead. |
| A counterexample to the universal equality | <code>result : ¬ claim</code> | The private <code>lower_bound</code> proves $6/25\le\eta^</em>(13)$, whereas the equality gives $4/17$. |</p>
<p>The threshold formulation follows from convexity. Every no-signalling box is a convex combination of extremal boxes. At a fixed efficiency, loss preserves normalized convex combinations and the local polytope is convex. Thus every box is local after loss if and only if every extremal box is. Further independent erasure preserves locality, so the admissible efficiencies of each extremal box form a downward interval. There are finitely many extremal boxes in this finite polytope; the supremum of efficiencies shared by all of them is the minimum of their thresholds. This is the mathematical interpretation of <code>etaStar</code>, not an additional Lean theorem in this module.</p>
<p>The efficiency interval $[0,1]$ is physical, and $m\ge2$ restricts the conjecture to its nontrivial input domain. Neither restricts the refuting instance $m=13$. Nonnegativity and normalization express a conditional probability distribution and are not extra assumptions about the conjecture.</p>
