ta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The relabelled chained inequality holds for every odd-input binary COD model</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. B. Brask and R. Chaves, <em>Bell scenarios with communication</em>, J. Phys. A: Math. Theor. 50(9), 094001 (2017), arXiv:1607.08182v1, subsection “Generalization to more inputs”, conjecture following Eq. (25). The source member <code>Bell_comm_arXiv_v1.tex</code>, line 325, states verbatim:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For the inequality to hold for the COD model, Alice's outputs must be relabelled for every second input. That is $a=0 \leftrightarrow a=1$ for even $x$. With this relabelling, we conjecture that \eqref{eq.validchained} holds for any odd number of inputs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source label <code>eq.validchained</code> denotes Eq. (25), at TeX lines 318–324:</p>
<p>$$[a_1-b_1]+[b_1-a_2]+[a_2-b_2]+\cdots+[a_n-b_n]+[b_n-a_1-1]\geq1.$$</p>
<p>At line 325, the bracket is defined by $[a_x-b_y]=\sum_{j=0}^1j,p(a-b=j\bmod2\mid x,y)$. For binary outputs, the ordinary brackets are unequal-output probabilities and the closing bracket is an equal-output probability.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24183">#24183</a> fixes the finite hidden-variable reading of the source's causal outcome-dependent (COD) model, Eq. (7), TeX lines 150–155:</p>
<p>$$p(a,b\mid x,y)=\sum_{\lambda\in\Lambda}w_\lambda A_x(a\mid\lambda)B_y(b\mid a,\lambda),$$</p>
<p>where $\Lambda$ is finite, $w_\lambda\geq0$, $\sum_\lambda w_\lambda=1$, and both response functions are nonnegative and normalized over their binary output, including Bob's normalization for each raw Alice output. Let $r_x=1$ for even one-based inputs $x$, and $r_x=0$ otherwise. The inequality reads Alice's relabelled output $c=a\oplus r_x$, while Bob receives the raw $a$. Writing $q(c,b\mid x,y)=p(c\oplus r_x,b\mid x,y)$, the statement is</p>
<p>$$\sum_{i=1}^nq(c\ne b\mid i,i)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}q(c\ne b\mid i+1,i)+q(c=b\mid1,n)\geq1$$</p>
<p>for every odd natural $n$ and every such model. This is proved by <code>result : claim</code>. The definitions and source locator are also in <a href="../Library/Observer/braskchaves2017bellcommunication.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
