 is schulte&#x27;s named expansion of all a008287 quadrinomial coefficients at indices 0 ≤ k ≤ 3n. schulte&#x27;s conjectured a008287 alternating-binomial expansion is proved. apply addpow and regroup powers, with 3(n−j)+j = 3n−2j for j≤n.  released-dossier 6b062710c7b630f1cd1d4da4c489e04aa4ca1a00272a2a1ad83bfd196542c17f "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>Posed in</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2015</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Schulte&#x27;s A008287 alternating-binomial expansion</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Schulte&#x27;s conjectured A008287 alternating-binomial expansion is proved.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Triangle of quadrinomial coefficients, row n is the sequence of coefficients of (1 + x + x^2 + x^3)^n.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The independent question is Schulte&#x27;s named expansion of all A008287 quadrinomial coefficients at indices 0 ≤ k ≤ 3n.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Apply addpow and regroup powers, with 3(n−j)+j = 3n−2j for j≤n.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Schulte&#x27;s conjectured A008287 alternating-binomial expansion is proved.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a008287-schulte-quadrinomial-alternating-binomial"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Schulte&#x27;s A008287 alternating-binomial expansion</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A008287, NAME (<code>%N</code>, verbatim):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Triangle of quadrinomial coefficients, row n is the sequence of coefficients of (1 + x + x^2 + x^3)^n.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The settled FORMULA (<code>%F</code>, verbatim) is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>T(n,k) = Sum_{j=0..k} (-2)^j*binomial(n,j)*binomial(3*n-2*j,k-j) for n &gt;= 0 and 0 &lt;= k &lt;= 3*n (conjectured). - <em>Werner Schulte</em>, Sep 09 2015</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The full-quantifier reading is: for all <code>n k : ℕ</code>, if <code>k ≤ 3 * n</code>,
the integer coefficient <code>T n k</code> of <code>X^k</code> in
<code>(1 + X + X^2 + X^3 : ℤ[X]) ^ n</code> equals
<code>∑ j ∈ Finset.range (k + 1), (-2 : ℤ)^j * (n.choose j : ℤ) * ((3 * n - 2 * j).choose (k - j) : ℤ)</code>. Subtraction in binomial indices
is truncated natural subtraction, and <code>n.choose j = 0</code> for <code>j &gt; n</code>.
The scope wall is only this Schulte 2015 formula. The Shevelev 2010 and
Bala 2013 formulas in the same entry are different and are not settled here.</p>
