w">Open problem</p><h3>The zero higher-order EPR bound is tight at every positive order</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>E. Shchukin and P. van Loock, <em>Higher-order EPR correlations and inseparability conditions for continuous variables</em>, arXiv:1511.08444v1 (Phys. Rev. A 93, 032114, 2016), subsection “Approach two”, state the following conjecture in <code>bip-ent2.tex</code>, lines 961–963:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Our conjecture is that the inequality \eqref{eq:nS:1} cannot be violated by Gaussian states (including all inseparable ones) and the inequality \eqref{eq:nS:2} is tight. We verified this by numerical computations, but we could not find rigorous proofs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here the source's inequalities (nS:1) and (nS:2), at lines 914–924, concern two bosonic modes and every positive integer $n$:</p>
<p>$$
\left\langle(\hat a^{\dagger n}\pm\hat b^n)(\hat a^n\pm\hat b^{\dagger n})\right\rangle
\ge
\begin{cases}
n! &amp; \text{for separable states},\
0 &amp; \text{for all states}.
\end{cases}
$$</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24246">#24246</a> fixes the reading to the tightness clause: for every $n\ge1$, either sign $\sigma\in{1,-1}$ and every $\varepsilon&gt;0$, there is a normalized finitely supported occupation-basis vector $\psi$ with $|(\hat a^n+\sigma\hat b^{\dagger n})\psi|^2&lt;\varepsilon$. This is proved. The Gaussian clause is a separate question.</p>
<p>The source and its bibliographic locator are recorded in <a href="../Library/Observer/shchukinvanloock2016higherorderepr.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
