Pauli channels</em>, Phys. Rev. A 101, 062323 (2020), arXiv:2002.04657v2, states three conjectures in Section 4.2, “$N=d+1$ and $N=d$ mutually unbiased bases”. The v2 TeX source <code>GGPC_volume5.tex</code>, lines 243–246, reads:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and conjecture that the general formula reads
$\frac{V_{CP}(d)}{V_P(d)}=\frac{d}{(d+1)!}.$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Lines 276–279 read:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and conjecture that the general formula reads
$\frac{V_{G}(d)}{V_{CP}(d)}=\frac{d^2-1}{d^2}\left(\frac{d-1}{d}\right)^{d}.$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Lines 302–305 read:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>from which we conjecture that
$\frac{V_{EB}(d)}{V_{G}(d)}=\frac{1}{d+1}.$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For a natural number $d\ge2$, put $m=d+1$ and $s(x)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}x_i$ for $x\in\mathbb R^m$. The module defines precisely these four sets:</p>
<ul>
<li>$P={x:\ -1/(d-1)\le x_i\le1\text{ for every }i}$ (<code>boxRegion</code>).</li>
<li>$C={x:\ -1/(d-1)\le s(x)\text{ and }s(x)\le1+dx_j\text{ for every }j}$ (<code>cpRegion</code>).</li>
<li>$G=C\cap{x:\ x_i\ge0\text{ for every }i}$ (<code>generatorRegion</code>).</li>
<li>$E=G\cap{x:\ s(x)\le1}$ (<code>ebRegion</code>).</li>
</ul>
<p>For these sets write $V_A=\operatorname{channelVolume}(d,A)=(\sqrt{d-1}/d)^m\operatorname{Leb}<em>m(A)$, for $A=P,C,G,E$. In Lean, <code>channelVolume</code> uses <code>(volume A).toReal</code>; the proof obtains finite positive Lebesgue measures for all four sets when $d\ge2$. Thus $V_P,V_C,V_G,V_E$ match the source's $V_P(d),V</em>{CP}(d),V_G(d),V_{EB}(d)$ as coordinate-region volumes. The source locates the box and its weight at lines 210–228, complete positivity at lines 359–364, nonnegative eigenvalues at lines 256–267, and the nonnegative entanglement-breaking region at lines 282–293; see also <a href="../Library/QuadraticForms/siudzinska2020pauligeometry.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22754">#22754</a> fixes the statement as the conjunction of these three identities for every natural $d\ge2$. This is proved for the specified coordinate regions.</p>
