t" id="jokinen-et-al-2024-k-measurement-pseudobroadcasting"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>One generating measurement does not make pseudo-broadcasting equivalent to non-contextuality</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>P. Jokinen, M. Weilenmann, M. Plávala, J.-P. Pellonpää, J. Kiukas and R. Uola, <em>No-Broadcasting Characterizes Operational Contextuality</em>, Phys. Rev. Lett. 133, 240201 (2024), arXiv:2406.07305v1, Appendix D, state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One can also see that $\mathcal S(\mathcal A)$ is a Cartesian product of $k$ simplexes if $\mathcal A$ is generated by $k$ independent measurements. This leads to a conjecture that if $\mathcal A$ is generated by $k$ measurements, then $1\mapsto k$-pseudobroadcasting is equivalent to non-contextuality. This is only a conjecture since even if $\mathcal A$ is generated by $k$ measurements, $\mathcal S(\mathcal A)$ in general is only a subset of a Cartesian product of $k$ simplexes. We leave resolving this issue and proving or disproving the conjecture for future work.</p>
