The <a href="../Library/QuadraticForms/malkoun2023cousins.md">source note</a> gives the identity,
attribution, license and bounded literature comparison.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let $H$ denote an $n$ by $n$ hermitian positive semidefinite matrix. Let $G$
and $K$ be two subgroups of the symmetric group $\Sigma_n$. Define
$f_{G,K}(H)=\sum_{(\sigma,\tau)\in G\times K}\prod_{i=1}^n
h_{\sigma(i),\tau(i)}$. Does there exist a positive constant $c(n,G,K)$
depending on $n$, $G$ and $K$ such that
$\Re(f_{G,K}(H))\geq c(n,G,K)\prod_{i=1}^n h_{ii}$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The complete quantifier order is</p>
<p>$$
\forall n\in\mathbb N\ \forall G,K\leq\operatorname{Sym}(n)\quad
\exists c\in\mathbb R\quad c&gt;0\ \land<br />
\forall H\in M_n(\mathbb C),\quad
(H=H^*\land H\succeq0)\Longrightarrow
c\prod_i\Re(h_{ii})\leq\Re(f_{G,K}(H)).
$$</p>
<p><code>claim</code> has this proposition as its definition and <code>result</code> has type <code>¬ claim</code>.
Native index $i:\mathrm{Fin}(n)$ corresponds to source label $i+1$; every
permutation acts on that same index set. <code>crossSum</code> sums over the actual
subgroup types, without normalization. No Young-subgroup, rank, entry-sign
