"verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="barato-seifert-2015-waiting-time-randomness-skewness-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Skewness at most two does not force randomness at most one in multicyclic enzyme networks</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. C. Barato and U. Seifert, <em>Skewness and Kurtosis in Statistical Kinetics</em>, Phys. Rev. Lett. 115, 188103 (2015), arXiv:1510.03279v1, ask in <code>skewkurtstatkinarxiv.tex</code>, line 358, at the end of the multicyclic discussion:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It remains as an open question whether this property is true for arbitrary networks that do not consist of a single cycle.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The preceding numerical investigation concerns a four-state network with two product-forming routes and its off-pathway specialization. At lines 350–352 the authors report that constraining the skewness to be at most two prevents the randomness parameter from exceeding one. The question asks whether the superior detection of departures from a single cycle by higher moments extends to arbitrary networks.</p>
<p>The source defines cycle completion at an irreversible product-release transition (Eq. <code>fullreaction</code>, lines 110–117). For completion-time moments $t_n$, its definitions at lines 133–149 are
$$
\sigma^2=t_2-t_1^2,\qquad
\mathcal R=\frac{\sigma^2}{t_1^2},\qquad
\mathcal S=\frac{t_3-3t_1\sigma^2-t_1^3}{\sigma^3}.
$$</p>
<p>The source's kurtosis is $\mathcal K=(t_4-4t_3t_1+6\sigma^2t_1^2+3t_1^4)/\sigma^4$ (line 149); it is not used by the settlement.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26046">#26046</a> fixes the implication to be refuted: for every finite network with nonnegative off-diagonal rates, a positive marked rate $p\to r$, every state able to reach $p$, and positive variance, $\mathcal S\le2$ implies $\mathcal R\le1$. Moments use the first-passage matrix definition below. This implication is refuted.</p>
<p>The <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/baratoseifert2015skewness.md">literature note</a> records the source and its scope.</p>
