ta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The Fuchs–Caves fidelity measurement need not give optimal ROC points</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Bodor and M. Koniorczyk, <em>Receiver operation characteristics of quantum state discrimination</em> (J. Russ. Laser Res. 38, 150–163, 2017; arXiv:1603.06801v1), ask whether the cumulative points of the fidelity measurement lie on the optimal receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve. The v1 TeX member <code>Bodor_Koniorczyk_QuantumROC_submit_1.tex</code>, lines 940–944, in the subsection “The quantum Bhattacharyya coefficient”, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This obviously defines $d+1$ points in the feasible ROC domain, where $d$ stands for the dimension of the joint support of the two density operators. (It is an interesting open question whether these points are actually on the optimal ROC curve.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The observable in the preceding equation, labelled <code>eq:fidelitymeasurable</code>, is
$$M=(\sqrt{\rho})^{-1}\sqrt{\sqrt{\rho},\sigma\sqrt{\rho}},(\sqrt{\rho})^{-1},$$
where $\rho=\varrho_N$ and $\sigma=\varrho_P$. For an ordered orthonormal eigenbasis $(a_i)<em>{i=0}^{d-1}$, put $q_i=\operatorname{Re}(a_i^\dagger\rho a_i)$ and $p_i=\operatorname{Re}(a_i^\dagger\sigma a_i)$, ordered by nonincreasing $p_i/q_i$, and define $Q_k=\sum</em>{i&lt;k}q_i$, $P_k=\sum_{i&lt;k}p_i$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24220">#24220</a> fixes the universal reading: for every finite dimension $d$, every pair of positive-definite trace-one complex matrices $\rho,\sigma$, every such ordered eigenbasis, and every $0\le k\le d$, every binary effect $0\le F\le I$ satisfying $\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}(F\rho)=Q_k$ must satisfy $\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}(F\sigma)\le P_k$. This is <code>claim</code>; <code>result</code> refutes it.</p>
