r the convex roof, for every mixed state and every function in the source&#x27;s class.</p><p><span>The idea</span>1. Admissible function and state. For this four-dimensional function, quadraticcoherence proves IsCoherenceFunction f, and quadraticnonzero gives a nonzero simplex value.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A rank-two state in four dimensions has equal coherence roofs, refuting the universal strict-gap claim.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>A characterization of all states or all functions with equal roofs remains open here. - Consequences for dependent assertions.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="zhao-pereira-ma-fei-2021-coherence-assistance-roof-equality"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A mixed four-dimensional state has equal coherence roofs</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M.-J. Zhao, R. Pereira, T. Ma and S.-M. Fei, <em>Coherence of assistance and assisted maximally coherent states</em>, Scientific Reports 11, 5935 (2021), arXiv:2103.08818v1, ask in the paragraph “Relation between the convex roof extension and the least concave majorant extension” (<code>ZhaoMJ-AMC4.tex</code>, line 303):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The strict relation between the coherence of convex roof and the coherence of assistance, that is, whether $C_c(\rho)&lt; C_a(\rho)$ holds for all mixed quantum states is an interesting topic.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source's standing assumption is $f\in\mathfrak F\setminus{0}$ (lines 189–190): $f$ is real, symmetric and concave on the probability simplex and vanishes at its vertices. The pure-state coherence is $C_f(|\psi\rangle)=f(\mu(|\psi\rangle))$, where $\mu(|\psi\rangle)_i=|\psi_i|^2$ (lines 207–211).</p>
<p>In the formal reading, for every natural dimension $n$, <code>IsCoherenceFunction f</code> requires concavity on $\Omega_n={p\in\mathbb R^n:p_i\ge0,\ \sum_i p_i=1}$, invariance under every coordinate permutation on $\Omega_n$, and $f(e_i)=0$ for every vertex. Nonzeroness means $\exists p\in\Omega_n,\ f(p)\ne0$.</p>
<p>A finite pure-state decomposition has nonnegative real weights $p_j$ summing to one, unit vectors $\psi_j\in\mathbb C^n$ for every index, and $\rho=\sum_jp_j|\psi_j\rangle\langle\psi_j|$. Let $D_f(\rho)$ be the set of all averages $\sum_jp_jf(\mu(\psi_j))$ over these decompositions. The module defines $C_c(\rho)=\inf D_f(\rho)$ and $C_a(\rho)=\sup D_f(\rho)$, using real <code>sInf</code> and <code>sSup</code>; the source uses minimum and maximum (lines 212–216 and 232–236). <code>IsMixed ρ</code> means $\rho\succeq0$, $\operatorname{tr}\rho=1$ and $\operatorname{tr}(\rho^2)\ne1$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22770">#22770</a> fixes the claim: for every $n$, every such nonzero $f$, and every mixed $\rho$, $C_c(\rho)&lt;C_a(\rho)$. This universal statement is refuted.</p>
