3, supplementary subsection “cooling maps and thermal operations”, conjecture 1 (lttarxiv.tex, lines 924–927): narasimhachar and gour (2015), arxiv:1409.7740v3, give a state-transition criterion for cooling maps, while thermal operations impose an operational requirement involving one energy-conserving unitary and a thermal ancilla. a four-level cooling-map transition cannot be realized by any finite ancilla in its normalized ground-space state and any energy-conserving unitary. the input is the existing matrix niceerrorbasisnonnormalstabilizer.pm.  released-dossier 9df722774a93f4e9ae767d9fbda7653ef88dddf5172ad88512110fbdf54123f7 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2014</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>A cooling-map transition has no finite ground-space thermal realization</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A four-level cooling-map transition cannot be realized by any finite ancilla in its normalized ground-space state and any energy-conserving unitary.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Gour, Low-temperature thermodynamics with quantum coherence, Nature Communications 6, 7689 (2015), arXiv:1409.7740v3, Supplementary subsection “Cooling maps and thermal operations”, Conjecture 1 (LTTarXiv.tex, lines 924–927):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Narasimhachar and Gour (2015), arXiv:1409.7740v3, give a state-transition criterion for cooling maps, while thermal operations impose an operational requirement involving one energy-conserving unitary and a thermal ancilla.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The input is the existing matrix NiceErrorBasisNonNormalStabilizer.Pm.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A four-level cooling-map transition cannot be realized by any finite ancilla in its normalized ground-space state and any energy-conserving unitary.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="narasimhachar-gour-2015-cooling-map-thermal-operation-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A cooling-map transition has no finite ground-space thermal realization</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>V. Narasimhachar and G. Gour, <em>Low-temperature thermodynamics with quantum coherence</em>, Nature Communications 6, 7689 (2015), arXiv:1409.7740v3, Supplementary subsection “Cooling maps and thermal operations”, Conjecture 1 (<code>LTT_arXiv.tex</code>, lines 924–927):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Cooling maps are equivalent to low-temperature thermal operations, with regard to the feasibility of state transitions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The next sentence distinguishes state-transition feasibility from equality of channel sets:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Note that this could be true even if the set of cooling maps is strictly larger than that of thermal operations---there could still be a thermal operation achieving every state transition that is possible through cooling maps.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26067">#26067</a> fixes the following reading of the cooling-to-thermal direction. For every finite dimension $d$, every strictly increasing real energy spectrum $E$ with distinct positive gaps, and every pair of density matrices $\rho,\sigma$, a cooling-map transition $\rho\mapsto\sigma$ has a low-temperature thermal realization. Here:</p>
<ul>
<li>Density matrices are positive semidefinite complex matrices of trace one (<code>IsDensity</code>).</li>
<li>A cooling map has a finite Kraus family, each member diagonal or of the form $c|j\rangle\langle k|$ with $j&lt;k$, satisfying $\sum_a K_a^\dagger K_a=I$ and $\sum_a K_a\rho K_a^\dagger=\sigma$ (<code>IsCoolingKraus</code>, <code>IsCoolingMap</code>). This is the source's definition in <code>LTT_arXiv.tex</code>, lines 693–700.</li>
<li>A thermal realization uses an arbitrary positive finite ancilla dimension $m$, arbitrary real ancilla energies $e$, and a unitary $U$ commuting with the diagonal total Hamiltonian $H_S\otimes I+I\otimes\operatorname{diag}(e)$ (<code>IsEnergyConserving</code>). If $G={a:e_a\le e_b\text{ for every }b}$ and $g=|G|$, its output is $\operatorname{Tr}_A[U(\rho\otimes P_G/g)U^\dagger]$ (<code>groundSet</code>, <code>groundState</code>, <code>thermalOutput</code>). The full ground eigenspace, including every degeneracy, is retained.</li>
</ul>
<p>The source writes the bath state as a low-temperature approximation to the normalized ground-space projector (lines 137–141 and 651–654). The formal assertion uses that projector exactly, as stipulated in #26067; it makes no assertion about an error tolerance or a positive-temperature Gibbs state. The resulting universal assertion is <code>claim</code>, and the module proves <code>result : ¬ claim</code>.</p>
