or, Post-selection-free preparation of high-quality physical qubits, Quantum 7, 994 (2023), arXiv:2209.05391v3, Section 3.2, PDF page 20 (purification.tex, lines 859–862), ask:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source uses purification circuits to improve preparation quality without post-selection.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Its eight input cases use weightgetwo: for any bitstring and two distinct indices whose bits are both true, its Hamming distance from the all-false string is at least two.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A tight purification circuit has strictly decreasing output error between two preparation error rates.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Which noisy purification circuits satisfy this response condition remains open here. - A surviving special case.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="barber-gillespie-taylor-2023-purification-monotonicity"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Tight purification circuits need not have monotone output error</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>B. Barber, N. I. Gillespie and J. M. Taylor, <em>Post-selection-free preparation of high-quality physical qubits</em>, Quantum 7, 994 (2023), arXiv:2209.05391v3, Section 3.2, PDF page 20 (<code>purification.tex</code>, lines 859–862), ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We conjecture that it should be possible to prove monotonicity under some reasonable set of conditions. For instance, suppose that $\big|\binom n 0 + \cdots + \binom n e\big| = 2^{n-k}$ and $p_0 &lt; 0.5$. Is $p_{\text{out}}(p_0,p_I,p_C,p_T)$ an increasing function of $p_0$, $p_I$, $p_C$ and $p_T$? We leave this as an open question.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Their $(n,k,e)$ purification circuit consists of CNOT and Toffoli gates and sends every input bitstring of Hamming weight at most $e$ to a bitstring zero in $k$ nominated output positions (Section 2, line 203). Each input bit independently starts at one with probability $p_0$. Each idle qubit depolarises independently with probability $p_I$, and each CNOT or Toffoli independently depolarises its entire support with probability $p_C$ or $p_T$. Depolarisation replaces that support by uniformly random bits (Section 2.1, lines 219–243). The quantity $p_{\text{out}}$ is the expected number of erroneous output bits divided by $k$ (lines 711–713).</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22506">#22506</a> fixes the nondecreasing reading: for all natural $n,k,e$ with $1\le k\le n$ and $1\le e\le n$, every gate list executed one gate per round, and every set of exactly $k$ outputs, purification through weight $e$ together with $\sum_{j=0}^{e}\binom nj=2^{n-k}$ implies separate nondecreasing dependence on each noise coordinate. Both parameter vectors must lie in $0\le p_0&lt;1/2$, $0\le p_I,p_C,p_T\le1$, and the other three coordinates are held fixed in each comparison. This is <code>claim</code>. Its refutation already occurs in the preparation coordinate. One gate per round is an allowed subclass of the source's scheduled circuits.</p>
<p>The definitions and source locators are also in <a href="../Library/Hamming/barbergillespietaylor2023purification.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
