ate, and uses this to obtain asymptotically good CSS-T codes.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Let A be the 13 × 13 circulant with (Ax)i = xi + x{i+1} + x{i+2} (indices mod 13).</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A binary cyclic map gives an admissible CSS-T extension with distance at least five, while identity doubling has distance at most four.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Doubled inputs make the parity condition automatic; whether non-doubled admissible maps give further gains remains open.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="berardini-et-al-2024-csst-doubling-map-improvement"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A CSS-T doubling map with better distance than the identity</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>E. Berardini, R. Dastbasteh, J. Etxezarreta Martinez, S. Jain and O. Sanz Larrarte, <em>Asymptotically good CSS-T codes and a new construction of triorthogonal codes</em>, arXiv:2412.08586v2, Remark 1:</p>
