<blockquote>
<p>As discussed in Sec.~\ref{subsec:unmark}, it was numerically conjectured that the minimum and maximum values of $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{K}$, $\mathcal{S}+\mathcal{K}$, $\mathcal{S}-\mathcal{K}$ and $\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{K}$ are always obtained for a specific type of network topology, with all rates $k_{ij}$ equal to $k$ apart from one (here $k_{21}$) equal to $ak$, with the parameter $a$ depending on the optimized quantity and the number of states $N$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section III E 1, <code>main.tex</code> lines 424–428, gives the kurtosis lower endpoint $C=-(1+\sqrt5)/10$ in the limit $N\to\infty$ along rates $(A/N,1,\ldots,1)$, where $A=(3-\sqrt5)/2$. The asymptotic family supplies a candidate endpoint; the conjectured extremal topology leaves a universal bound over arbitrary positive rates to establish.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25526">#25526</a> fixes the reading. For each natural $n\ge2$ and strictly positive real rate vector $k:\mathrm{Fin},n\to\mathbb R$, put</p>
<p>$R_j=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}k_i^{-j},\qquad \mathcal K(k)=\frac{15R_2^2}{R_1^4}-\frac{20R_3}{R_1^3}+\frac{6R_4}{R_1^2R_2},\qquad C=-\frac{1+\sqrt5}{10}.$</p>
<p>The claim is that $\mathcal K(k)&gt;C$ for every such $n$ and $k$, and that $C$ is the greatest lower bound of the set of all these values, allowing $n$ to vary. This is proved. The endpoint is an infimum over state counts, with no attainment by a finite positive cycle of at least two states.</p>
<p>The source defines $\mathcal K$ as the fourth scaled current cumulant divided by the second (<code>main.tex</code> line 54). Substituting its current-cumulant formulas at lines 773–775 and waiting-time cumulants at lines 787–790 gives the displayed expression. This is the quantity encoded by <code>kurtosis</code>, rather than the standardized fourth central moment. See <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/ptaszynski2022skewness.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
