<blockquote>
<p>As discussed in Sec.~\ref{subsec:unmark}, it was numerically conjectured that the minimum and maximum values of $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{K}$, $\mathcal{S}+\mathcal{K}$, $\mathcal{S}-\mathcal{K}$ and $\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{K}$ are always obtained for a specific type of network topology, with all rates $k_{ij}$ equal to $k$ apart from one (here $k_{21}$) equal to $ak$, with the parameter $a$ depending on the optimized quantity and the number of states $N$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section III E 1, <code>main.tex</code> line 424, specifies the limiting interpretation:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The other bounds have been derived in the assymptotic limit $N \rightarrow \infty$ in which the saturating value of $a$ scales as $a=A/N$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The lower endpoint displayed at lines 434–437 is $\min(\mathcal S+\mathcal K)=-8/27$ for $A=1/2$.</p>
<p>The reading fixed in issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25636">#25636</a> concerns this lower endpoint for positive unidirectional cycles. For $n\ge2$ and $k:\mathrm{Fin},n\to\mathbb R$ with every $k_i&gt;0$, put
$R_j=\sum_i k_i^{-j}$ and</p>
<p>$$
\mathcal S(k)=\frac{3R_2^2}{R_1^4}-\frac{2R_3}{R_1^3},\qquad
\mathcal K(k)=\frac{15R_2^2}{R_1^4}-\frac{20R_3}{R_1^3}+\frac{6R_4}{R_1^2R_2}.
$$</p>
<p>The claim is that $\mathcal S(k)+\mathcal K(k)&gt;-8/27$ for every such $n,k$, and that $-8/27$ is the greatest lower bound of the set $V$ of all these values as $n$ and $k$ vary. This is proved.</p>
<p>The source defines $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal K$ as the third-to-first and fourth-to-second scaled current-cumulant ratios (<code>main.tex</code> lines 53–54). Substitution of its winding-number cumulants at lines 772–775 and waiting-time cumulants at lines 787–790 gives the displayed expressions. These are the reused Lean definitions <code>UnicyclicSkewnessMinimum.skewness</code> and <code>UnicyclicKurtosisLowerBound.kurtosis</code>; they are not standardized central moments. See <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/ptaszynski2022skewness.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
