<blockquote>
<p>All conclusions of Theorem 3 are valid for all α &gt; 0, while conditions on other parameters remain intact. In particular, µ → g(µ; x) is log-concave for each fixed 0 &lt; x &lt; Rg.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Theorem 3, printed p. 11:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For a real sequence {gₙ}ₙ≥₀ and fixed 0 &lt; q &lt; 1 define</p>
<p>$g(\mu;x)=\displaystyle\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{g_nx^n}{\Gamma_q(\mu+n)}.$ (16)</p>
<p>Suppose {gₙ}∞ₙ₌₀ is doubly positive. Then (16) has a positive radius of convergence Rg and the generalized Turánian</p>
<p>$\Delta_g(\alpha,\beta;x)=g(\mu+\alpha;x)g(\mu+\beta;x)-g(\mu;x)g(\mu+\alpha+\beta;x)$</p>
<p>is positive for 0 &lt; x &lt; Rg if α ∈ N, β &gt; 0 and µ ≥ max(−β, −1). If, in addition, α ≤ β + 1, then Δg(α, β; x) has positive power series coefficients at all powers of x.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The sequence is nontrivial, nonnegative, log-concave and has no
internal zeros, the definition of doubly positive on printed p. 3.
Equation (6), printed p. 4, defines</p>
<p>$$\Gamma_q(z)=(1-q)^{1-z}\frac{(q;q)<em>\infty}{(q^z;q)</em>\infty}.$$</p>
<p>Its reciprocal is extended by zero at the poles $z\in-\mathbb N_0$.
