<p>Translation: For $x&gt;0$, define $g_n(x)=R_{n-1}(x)/R_n(x)$.
Investigate for which $k$ the differences of this function, at every
fixed $x$, are nonnegative, $\Delta^k g_n(x)&gt;0$.
Conjecture: for all $k$. For $k=1$ the conjecture reduces to (42),
and for $k=2$ to the inequality of Problem 1 on page 20.</p>
<p>Equation (1) defines</p>
<p>$$R_n(x)=e^x-\sum_{j=0}^{n}\frac{x^j}{j!},\qquad x\ge0.$$</p>
<p>The difference is forward in the natural index:</p>
<p>$$\Delta^k g_n(x)=\sum_{j=0}^{k}(-1)^{k-j}\binom{k}{j}g_{n+j}(x).$$</p>
<p>The Lean assertion quantifies over every natural $k\ge1$, every natural
$n\ge1$ and every positive real $x$, and asserts $\Delta^k g_n(x)\ge0$.
This weakens the source's displayed strict inequality. A negative
instance refutes both the nonnegative and the strict readings.</p>
