3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Jeffrey Uhlmann, <em>Partial Proof of a Conjecture with Implications for Spectral Majorization</em>, arXiv:2309.01302v1, Section 4, asks about removing the dimensional restriction from the diagonal-spectrum majorization relationship:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, the fact that such a construction does not generally exist for n &gt; 6 does not necessarily imply that the diagonal-spectrum majorization relationship does not hold in general.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Empirical investigation may be able to provide sufficient evidence to justify a conjecture or, alternatively, empirical and/or analytical efforts may identify counterexamples that constrain such generalizations</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The assertion is that for every natural dimension $n$, every real symmetric positive definite $P$, and every real diagonal $D$, the diagonal of $PDP^{-1}$ majorizes its spectrum. The spectrum is the vector of diagonal entries of $D$. Section 3 defines $x\prec y$ by $x=Sy$ for a doubly stochastic matrix $S$. Preregistration: <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18572">#18572</a>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Lean counterpart | Fidelity |
