column-stochastic matrix with six-cycle support cannot be obtained from the generators of Eq. (10).</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="vom-ende-shahbeigi-2024-stochastic-generator-set-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>The Eq. (10) set does not generate the 3 × 3 stochastic matrices</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>F. vom Ende and F. Shahbeigi, <em>Generating Sets of Stochastic Matrices</em>, arXiv:2411.18946, study sets G of column-stochastic matrices whose finite products give every stochastic matrix. N_G(s(n)) is the number of factors needed in the worst case (Definition 3). These are the classical counterparts of generating sets of quantum channels: a generating set models the operations an experiment can implement, and N_G bounds the circuit length needed to implement every classical channel.</p>
<p>Theorem 3 states that G = {P} ∪ conv{1, V1, V2, V3} of Eq. (10) generates s(3) with G^20 = s(3). The Outlook asks &quot;is the upper bound N_G(S) ≤ 20 in Theorem 3 optimal? We conjecture that this is true&quot;.</p>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumChannels/vomendeshahbeigi2024stochasticgenerators.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
