-party pure state has an admissible zero-volume fill but is not biseparable, refuting the qudit genuine-entanglement claim.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The witness does not settle the source&#x27;s four-qubit claims, additivity argument or numerical LOCC study.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="xie-younis-mei-eberly-2024-qudit-tetrahedron-genuine-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The entropic tetrahedron is not a genuine four-qudit entanglement measure</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Xie, D. Younis, Y. Mei and J. H. Eberly, <em>Multipartite Entanglement: A Journey Through Geometry</em>, arXiv:2304.03281v2; Entropy 26(3), 217 (2024), define the genuine requirement in <code>tetrahedron.tex</code>, line 57:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The ``genuine'' requirement requires that a GME measure should only, and always, vanish for biseparable states \cite{ma2011,xie2021}.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Their Scalability paragraph, line 220, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Nonetheless, our tetrahedron measure presented in this work maintains its scalability. Specifically, we can consistently partition a large system into four groups and explore genuine quadripartite entanglement among these groups. Essentially, the tetrahedron measure is a genuine four-qudit entanglement measure, where each party possesses more than two dimensions. The extension of entropic fill to qudit systems has been made possible by a recent study \cite{yang2022}. Additionally, we have conducted numerical verifications, demonstrating the existence of unique nonnegative solutions for Eq.~\eqref{eqn1} and \eqref{eqn2} in qudit systems up to $d=50$. Consequently, we conjecture that our tetrahedron measure is also applicable to qudit systems.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These quotations were checked against the complete v2 TeX source, <code>tetrahedron.tex</code> (233 lines, md5 <code>da0b24bf21c330f90979f63e7679749c</code>), fetched from https://arxiv.org/src/2304.03281v2. The definitions and further quotations are collected in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/xieyounismeieberly2024journey.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20861">#20861</a> fixes the following reading. Each of four parties has Hilbert space $\mathbb C^d$, with $d\ge2$, and $\psi$ is a coefficient function with $\sum_x|\psi(x)|^2=1$. Write $S_X$ for the base-two von Neumann entropy of its reduced density matrix on $X$. Biseparability means factorization across some nonempty proper subset of the four parties.</p>
<p>An admissible fill consists of six nonnegative real coordinates $\sigma=(\sigma_{AB},\sigma_{AC},\sigma_{AD},\sigma_{BC},\sigma_{BD},\sigma_{CD})$ and a real parameter $\lambda$. The face equations are $\sigma_{ij}+\sigma_{ik}+\sigma_{il}=S_i$ for each party $i$. Put $p=\sqrt{\sigma_{AB}\sigma_{CD}}$, $q=\sqrt{\sigma_{AC}\sigma_{BD}}$ and $r=\sqrt{\sigma_{AD}\sigma_{BC}}$. The radical equations are $-p+q+r=\lambda S_{AB}$, $p-q+r=\lambda S_{AC}$ and $p+q-r=\lambda S_{AD}$. These are the source equations labelled <code>eqn1</code> and <code>eqn2</code>, TeX lines 103–121.</p>
<p>The source volume, TeX lines 92–101, is $V=(\sqrt2/3)\sqrt{2\sum_{ij}\sigma_{ij}}\big((p+q+r)(-p+q+r)(p-q+r)(p+q-r)\big)^{1/4}$. The formal claim is: for every $d\ge2$ and every unit $\psi$, if an admissible fill exists and every admissible fill has $V=0$, then $\psi$ is biseparable. This implication is refuted.</p>
