lity is one minus f(p minus one) divided by two to the power p. the proof uses a bijective argument. the argument gives a bound, not an exact optimum for arbitrary nonuniform input laws; that optimum remains open here. - the large-player limit. released-dossier 07da64133eb64cb5937933fd2a4fdf859befe125a7b456855be929d923cdd8b1 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2022</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The classical value of the odd polygon game is its Fibonacci bound</strong><span class="result-excerpt">For every odd P at least three, the polygon game&#x27;s optimal classical winning probability is one minus F(P minus one) divided by two to the power P.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Sondhi, Playing nonlocal games with phases of quantum matter, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper compares classical performance with a cluster-state quantum strategy and with strategies using states in a symmetry-protected topological phase.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The proof uses a bijective argument.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For every odd P at least three, the polygon game&#x27;s optimal classical winning probability is one minus F(P minus one) divided by two to the power P.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The argument gives a bound, not an exact optimum for arbitrary nonuniform input laws; that optimum remains open here. - The large-player limit.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="bulchandani-burnell-sondhi-2023-polygon-game-classical-value"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The classical value of the odd polygon game is its Fibonacci bound</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>V. B. Bulchandani, F. J. Burnell and S. L. Sondhi, <em>Playing nonlocal games with phases of quantum matter</em>, Phys. Rev. B 107, 045412 (2023), arXiv:2206.11252v2, Section 4.1, &quot;Classical winning probabilities&quot;, state in <code>ArxivResub.tex</code>, lines 714–717, the equation labelled <code>eq:classwinbound</code> and its conjecture:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>$p_{\mathrm{cl}}^*(P) \geq \frac{2^P - F_{P-1}}{2^P} = 1 - \frac{F_{P-1}}{2^P}, \quad P ,\mathrm{odd}.$</p>
<p>We conjecture that this bound is tight.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $P\ge3$ is the number of players and $F_1=F_2=1$. The game's rules are in the same section, <code>ArxivResub.tex</code>, lines 629–655; see also <a href="../Library/Resource/bulchandaniburnellsondhi2023polygon.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Index players and oriented edges by $\mathbb Z/P$, with edge $i$ joining $i$ to $i+1$. A deterministic strategy assigns triples $(a_i,b_i^0,c_i)$ on input $0$ and $(d_i,b_i^1,e_i)$ on input $1$. The module allows the middle bit to depend on the input. For a matching $M$, let $x_M$ be the input whose ones are exactly its covered vertices. On input $x$, the strategy wins when every returned triple has even parity, the returned middle bits have even total parity, and, for every matching with $x_M=x$,</p>
<p>$$\sum_{i\in M}(d_i+e_{i+1})+\sum_{j\text{ uncovered by }M}a_j\equiv |M|\pmod2.$$</p>
<p>The empty matching gives Condition 3; matchings with one, two and more edges give Conditions 4–6. Inputs are uniform over all $2^P$ Boolean strings, including those arising from no matching. Write $w_P(s)$ for the fraction of these inputs won by a deterministic strategy $s$, and $W_P(\mu)=\sum_s\mu(s)w_P(s)$ for a nonnegative weight function of total mass one on all deterministic strategies.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26289">#26289</a> fixes the reading: for every odd $P\ge3$, every such $\mu$ satisfies $W_P(\mu)\le1-F_{P-1}/2^P$, and some deterministic $s$ attains equality. This is proved.</p>
