<p>The checked v1 TeX has md5 <code>2e3f9a1a91cb695794df49b0123dfc37</code>. Its evolution convention is $U^kz$ (lines 135–142), and its bipartite-walk definition is $U=(2P-I)(2Q-I)$ (lines 154–163). The projections for the seven-edge example are printed at lines 320–338, and its displayed transition matrix at lines 340–351 and 1278–1286. These definitions are documented in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/chenetal2024bipartitewalkhamiltonians.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Use the source's one-based coordinate labels throughout this dossier. Let $U_{\rm def}=(2P-I)(2Q-I)$ for those explicit projections and let $U_{\rm disp}$ be the printed matrix. Exact rational comparison gives one discrepancy: their entries at row 3, column 5 are respectively $-1/3$ and $+1/3$; all other entries agree.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21532">#21532</a> fixes a reading covering both matrices. In Lean, <code>PerfectStateTransfer U a b</code> means that there exist $k\in\mathbb N$ and $\gamma\in\mathbb C$ with $|\gamma|=1$ and $U^ke_a=\gamma e_b$. The predicate imposes no unitarity assumption and includes step zero. The declaration <code>claim</code> says that neither $U_{\rm def}$ nor $U_{\rm disp}$ has such transfer from $e_6$ to $e_1$. This claim is proved by <code>result</code>.</p>
