n Klausen&#x27;s thm:nogap that is additional to the three structural assumptions.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The commutator terms cancel and the jump gain cancels the two half losses, with all relevant sums finite.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Finite-range translation-invariant lattice Lindblad matrices define a bounded operator with spectral zero.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Removing rank one from that alternative remains open in this settlement, as do the source&#x27;s separate finite-volume gap-closing and spectral-convergence questions.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="klausen-2025-lattice-lindbladian-zero-spectrum"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Zero belongs to the spectrum of a finite-range translation-invariant lattice Lindbladian</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Frederik Ravn Klausen, <em>Spectra of generators of Markovian evolution in the thermodynamic limit: From non-Hermitian to full evolution via tridiagonal Laurent matrices</em>, arXiv:2206.09879v3 (2025), asks in “Further questions” (<code>section04.tex</code>, line 574; verbatim TeX):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Prove that if $\Li$ is \eqref{eq:finite-range}, \eqref{eq:translation-invariance}, and  \eqref{eq:Rank1} then $0 \in \sigma(\Li)$, thereby strengthening \Cref{thm:no_gap}.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here the source macro <code>\Li</code> denotes $\mathcal L$, the three assumption labels mean (Finite Range), (TransInv), and (Rank 1), and the theorem label is <code>thm:no_gap</code>. The source assumes bounded self-adjoint $H$ and $G&gt;0$; (Rank 1) means $\operatorname{rank}(L_k)=1$ for every $k\in\mathbb Z$ (<code>section02.tex</code>, lines 12 and 50). It takes $\mathcal L$ on $\mathrm{HS}(\ell^2(\mathbb Z))$ (<code>section02.tex</code>, lines 6–13 and 54–57). Its theorem (<code>section03.tex</code>, lines 506–509) reads:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If $\Li$ is \eqref{eq:finite-range}, \eqref{eq:translation-invariance},  \eqref{eq:Rank1} and $0 \in \sigma(\Li)$.
Then $\Li$ is gapless or $\dim(\ker(\Li))=\infty.$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23482">#23482</a> fixes the matrix-kernel reading. Put $E=\ell^2(\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z;\mathbb C)$, the module's <code>ObserverHilbertSpace (ℤ × ℤ)</code>. For every $r\in\mathbb N$, every $g\in\mathbb R$, every $h:\mathbb Z^2\to\mathbb C$, and every $\ell:\mathbb Z^3\to\mathbb C$, assume:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>IsFiniteRange r h ℓ</code>: $h(x,y)=0$ whenever $|x-y|&gt;r$, and $\ell(k,x,y)=0$ whenever $\max{|x-k|,|y-k|}&gt;r$;</li>
<li><code>IsTranslationInvariant h ℓ</code>: $h(x+1,y+1)=h(x,y)$ and $\ell(k+1,x+1,y+1)=\ell(k,x,y)$ for all integer indices.</li>
</ul>
<p>The conclusion of <code>result</code> is that there exists a continuous complex linear operator $T:E\to E$ such that, for every $\rho\in E$ and every $x,y\in\mathbb Z$, $(T\rho)(x,y)=\operatorname{lindbladEntry}(g,h,\ell,\rho,x,y)$, and $0\in\sigma_{\mathbb C}(T)$. Explicitly, the entry is</p>
<p>$$
\begin{aligned}
&amp;-i\left(\sum_z h(x,z)\rho(z,y)-\sum_z\rho(x,z)h(z,y)\right)\
&amp;\quad+g\sum_k\left[
\sum_u\sum_v\ell(k,x,u)\rho(u,v)\overline{\ell(k,y,v)}
-\frac12\sum_z\left(\sum_w\overline{\ell(k,w,x)}\ell(k,w,z)\right)\rho(z,y)
-\frac12\sum_z\rho(x,z)\left(\sum_w\overline{\ell(k,w,z)}\ell(k,w,y)\right)
\right].
\end{aligned}
$$</p>
<p>All indices in these sums range over $\mathbb Z$; the Lean definition uses unconditional sums, whose nonzero terms are finite under <code>IsFiniteRange</code>. The theorem imposes no rank condition on the jumps, no self-adjointness condition on $h$, and no sign condition on $g$. Its specialization to $g&gt;0$ and self-adjoint $h$ answers the registered question, including its rank-one case.</p>
