hi–Eghbali Amlashi: the hyperbolic ratio below one</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Furuichi and M. Eghbali Amlashi, <em>On bounds of logarithmic mean and
mean inequality chain</em>, arXiv:2203.01134v2, Remark 3.2(i), proposes the
positive definite function conjecture. The supplied source identifies
this with Remark 2(i) in Mathematical Inequalities &amp; Applications 27(2)
(2024), 379–399.</p>
<p>The ratio is</p>
<p>$$
\frac{K_r(e^{2t},1)}{L(e^{2t},1)}
=\frac{t(\cosh t)^{1-r}}{\sinh t}=: \phi_r(t),\qquad \phi_r(0)=1,
$$</p>
<p>where $K_r=G^r A^{1-r}$ and $L$ is the logarithmic mean. The source states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Thus these numerical computations support the conjecture that the function ϕr (t) for r &lt; 1, is not positive definite.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The exact target is: for every real $r&lt;1$, the complex cast of $\phi_r$
is not positive definite. Positive definiteness means that for every
natural $m$ and every $x:\operatorname{Fin}(m)\to\mathbb R$, the matrix
with entries $f(x_j-x_i)$ is positive semidefinite. The existing
<code>FiniteInversePoissonCriterion.PositiveDefinite</code> supplies this definition.</p>
