<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Lemma 5.13. The constant C = √2 is such that for all a, b ∈ ℝ, one has |coth((a − b)/2) (a coth(a/2) − b coth(b/2))| ≤ C √(ab(a − b) coth(a/2) coth(b/2) coth((a − b)/2)).&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Remark 5.14. We conjecture, based on numerical evidence, that the optimal constant in the above inequality is C = 1/√2.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source proves the coefficient $\sqrt2$ and conjectures the optimal
coefficient $1/\sqrt2$. The repository resolves that conjecture: the optimal
constant in</p>
<p>$$
\left|\coth\frac{a-b}{2}
\left(a\coth\frac a2-b\coth\frac b2\right)\right|
\le C\sqrt{ab(a-b)\coth\frac a2\coth\frac b2\coth\frac{a-b}{2}}
$$</p>
<p>is $C=1/\sqrt2$. The quantifiers range over all real $a,b$ with
$a\ne0$, $b\ne0$, and $a\ne b$. Optimality means that for every real
$C&lt;1/\sqrt2$, some such pair violates the estimate strictly.
Here $\coth x=\cosh x/\sinh x$ and square roots are nonnegative real roots.</p>
