<p>The quotation preserves the source's text and formula, with its displayed equation rendered as a separate mathematical paragraph. The v2 TeX was read directly; its MD5 is <code>4452c28a26c7a76c87642af46fe0c8a9</code>. The source defines central moments and standardized moments at lines 228–236, the original coefficient of variation at lines 290–295, and the shifted one-sided ratios at lines 397–409. See <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/meerweeks2024boundedmoments.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Write $\nu$ for the probability law, distinguishing it from the paper's mean $\mu$, take $X(x)=x$, and put</p>
<p>$m=\int x,d\nu(x)$, $v=\int(x-m)^2,d\nu(x)$, $\sigma=\sqrt v$, $D_5=\int(x-m)^5,d\nu(x)/\sigma^5$, and $F(z)=z^3-z+z^{-1}-z^{-3}$.</p>
<p>In Lean, these are <code>Defs.mean</code>, <code>centralMoment id 2</code>, <code>Real.sqrt</code>, the ratio involving <code>centralMoment id 5</code>, and <code>fifthBound</code>, respectively. Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21300">#21300</a> fixes the statement: for every Borel probability law on $\mathbb R$ with $\int|x|^5,d\nu(x)&lt;\infty$ and $v&gt;0$, both of the following implications hold:</p>
<ul>
<li>for every real $L$, if $X\ge L$ almost surely, then $F(\sigma/(m-L))\le D_5$;</li>
<li>for every real $U$, if $X\le U$ almost surely, then $D_5\le-F(\sigma/(U-m))$.</li>
