uantum coherence with general measurement processes, quantum information processing 21, 48 (2022), arxiv:2004.10875v2, section 3.2, coh-measure.tex, line 298: the paper asks how much coherence a measurement can create when its outcome is discarded. every qubit density matrix has an attained maximum of output coherence under finite nonselective measurements, with exact zero and maximal gain criteria. the lemma names below belong to luderscoherencecreationoptimum unless qualified otherwise.  released-dossier 0705137bd24ae1481f54e55ce13d2e5b18ca6bc3807b05f855565a8d8d89a6b9 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2020</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The attained coherence optimum for arbitrary finite qubit Lüders measurements</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Every qubit density matrix has an attained maximum of output coherence under finite nonselective measurements, with exact zero and maximal gain criteria.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Pati, Creation of quantum coherence with general measurement processes, Quantum Information Processing 21, 48 (2022), arXiv:2004.10875v2, Section 3.2, coh-measure.tex, line 298:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper asks how much coherence a measurement can create when its outcome is discarded.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The lemma names below belong to LudersCoherenceCreationOptimum unless qualified otherwise.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Every qubit density matrix has an attained maximum of output coherence under finite nonselective measurements, with exact zero and maximal gain criteria.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="mohanty-sharma-sazim-pradhan-pati-2022-luders-coherence-optimum"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The attained coherence optimum for arbitrary finite qubit Lüders measurements</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. D. Mohanty, G. Sharma, Sk Sazim, B. Pradhan and A. K. Pati, <em>Creation of quantum coherence with general measurement processes</em>, Quantum Information Processing 21, 48 (2022), arXiv:2004.10875v2, Section 3.2, <code>coh-measure.tex</code>, line 298:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We couldn't get the expression for $\max_{{\vec{a},a_{\pm}}}C_{l_1}(\rho'<em>{\pm})$ analytically, so we have plotted it against the initial state coherence $C</em>{l_1}(\rho_{\pm})$.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>However in this case, we don't know yet for which states there can not be any coherence gain and for which states the coherence gain will be maximum.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Conclusion, line 505, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As a future line of work, it would be interesting to obtain the analytical results for two outcome and n-outcome POVMs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source defines $C_{l_1}(\rho)=\sum_{i\ne j}|\rho_{ij}|$ at line 109 and the nonselective Lüders action $\Phi_E(\rho)=\sum_i\sqrt{E_i}\rho\sqrt{E_i}$ at lines 127–130. A qubit density matrix is a positive semidefinite complex $2\times2$ matrix of trace one; an $n$-outcome POVM is a family of positive semidefinite effects indexed by $\mathrm{Fin},n$ whose sum is $I$.</p>
<p>Put $s=C_{l_1}(\rho)$, $R=\sqrt{(\operatorname{Re}(\rho_{00}-\rho_{11}))^2+4|\rho_{01}|^2}$ and $\lambda=(s+R)/2$. Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22649">#22649</a> fixes the statement: for every qubit density matrix and every integer $n\ge2$, $\lambda$ is the attained greatest output coherence; the optimal gain $\lambda-s$ vanishes exactly when $\rho_{00}=\rho_{11}$, is at most $1/2$, and equals $1/2$ exactly for $\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$ and $\operatorname{diag}(0,1)$. This is proved.</p>
<p>The definitions and source locators are also in <a href="../Library/QuadraticForms/mohantysharmasazimpradhanpati2022coherence.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
