source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The sharp lower bound on skewness times kurtosis for unidirectional unicyclic currents</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>K. Ptaszyński, <em>Bounds on skewness and kurtosis of steady-state currents</em>, Phys. Rev. E 106, 024119 (2022), arXiv:2204.08421v4, gives the following numerical conjecture in the Appendix (<code>main.tex</code>, line 792):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it was numerically conjectured that the minimum and maximum values of $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{K}$, $\mathcal{S}+\mathcal{K}$, $\mathcal{S}-\mathcal{K}$ and $\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{K}$ are always obtained for a specific type of network topology, with all rates $k_{ij}$ equal to $k$ apart from one (here $k_{21}$) equal to $ak$, with the parameter $a$ depending on the optimized quantity and the number of states $N$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For the product's lower endpoint, the subsection “Unidirectional networks” gives (<code>main.tex</code>, line 445):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>$\min(\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{K})=\min_A \frac{1-10A+22A^2-12A^3}{(1+A)^8}$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Line 424 specifies the limit $N\to\infty$ with $a=A/N$; line 439 gives the approximate minimum $-0.054$ at $A\approx0.23$. The summary's Eq. <code>produni</code> states $\mathcal{K}\times\mathcal{S}\in[-0.054,1]$ (lines 129–137), explicitly treating decimals as approximate. These quotations were checked against the v4 source member <code>main.tex</code>, MD5 <code>5b72a7d5de1e5c34c76db21badb56492</code>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25992">#25992</a> fixes the reading to the sharp lower endpoint for unidirectional cycles. Write $R_j=\sum_{i=1}^N k_i^{-j}$ for a strictly positive real rate vector $k:\mathrm{Fin},N\to\mathbb R$, and use the existing definitions</p>
<p>$\mathcal S(k)=3R_2^2/R_1^4-2R_3/R_1^3$,</p>
<p>$\mathcal K(k)=15R_2^2/R_1^4-20R_3/R_1^3+6R_4/(R_1^2R_2)$.</p>
<p>These are <code>UnicyclicSkewnessMinimum.skewness</code> and <code>UnicyclicKurtosisLowerBound.kurtosis</code>, respectively. They match the source's current-cumulant ratios (lines 53–54) upon substitution of its waiting-time formulas (Appendix, lines 772–790); they are not the usual standardized third and fourth moments of a probability distribution.</p>
<p>Define $E(A)=(1-10A+22A^2-12A^3)/(1+A)^8$ and $C=\inf{E(A):A&gt;0}$, the module's <code>lowerBound</code>. The statement is that $\mathcal S(k)\mathcal K(k)&gt;C$ for every $N\ge2$ and every strictly positive real rate vector, and that $C$ is the greatest lower bound of all these products, allowing $N$ to vary. Both factors use the same vector; repeated rates are allowed. This is proved.</p>
