<blockquote>
<p>Let $M$ be a $n \times n$ real matrix with $p(M)= \lambda &lt; 1$ partitioned into an $\ell \times \ell$ array of blocks.  Is it the case that for all elements $\delta$ of $[k]^{\ell \times \ell}$ with zero diagonals, $\rho(M^{(\delta)}) \leq \lambda^{\nicefrac{1}{k+1}}?$  If not, is there some function $f \colon (0,1) \times \N \rightarrow (0,1)$ such that $\rho(M^{(\delta)}) \leq f(\lambda,k)$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This quotation is from the downloaded v1 TeX, <code>Sections/ratfish.tex</code>, lines 75–77, md5 <code>08f1f56ce6f899219ee74094fe3c0e53</code>. The surrounding model, lines 48–49, specifies:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>$\tilde{x}<em>i^{(k+1)} = \sum_j M</em>{ij}\tilde{x}<em>j^{(k - \delta</em>{ji})}$</p>
</blockquote>
<p>and says “we assume that $\delta_{ii}=0$”. Here $p(M)$ in the question is read as the spectral radius $\rho(M)$ of that model. The operator $M^{(\delta,k)}$ acts on the newest iterate and its $k$ preceding iterates, updating the newest slot by this recurrence and shifting the remaining slots. The source uses $M^{(\delta)}$ for a history operator with enough slots (lines 51–62).</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21351">#21351</a> fixes the formal reading: <code>claim</code> is <code>Q1 ∨ Q2</code>, where <code>Q1</code> is the universal power bound and <code>Q2</code> is the existence of the uniform bound strictly below one. Both alternatives are refuted, even with two scalar blocks, positive remote delays and $0&lt;\lambda&lt;1$. The source and its locator are also recorded in <a href="../Library/Eigenstructure/aksoyetal2023asynchrony.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
