<p>The following matrix is Eq. (A11), lines 460–473. Put $N=2^{n-1}$ and order computational basis words with qubit 1 most significant. Its diagonal entries are $a/2$ at the all-zero and all-one words, $b/(N-1)$ at every other first-bit-zero word, and $(1-a-b)/(N-1)$ at every other first-bit-one word. Its opposite corners are $d,d^*$ and all other entries vanish. Line 474 restricts $a,b\in[0,1]$, $a+b\le1$, and $|d|\le a/2$.</p>
<p>The source defines the CSS as a minimizer over fully separable states of $D_{HS}(\rho,\sigma)=\operatorname{Tr}((\rho-\sigma)^2)$ (Section II, lines 70–93). The definitions and source locator are in <a href="../Library/Divergence/consiglioapollarowiesniak2022separability.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25134">#25134</a> fixes the low-branch reading: for every integer $n\ge2$ and every $\gamma\in\mathbb C$ with $|\gamma|\le1/(N+1)$, there exists a fully separable $\sigma$ of Eq. (A11) such that</p>
<p>$\operatorname{hsSq}(\rho,\sigma)\le\operatorname{hsSq}(\rho,\tau)$ for every fully separable $\tau$.</p>
<p>Here $\rho=\operatorname{xmems}(n,\gamma)$ has diagonal $1/(N+1)$ at all first-bit-zero words and the all-one word, zero at the remaining words, and opposite corners $\gamma,\gamma^*$; this is Eq. (A1) on the branch where $f=g=1/(N+1)$ (lines 341–373). The module defines $\operatorname{hsSq}(\rho,\sigma)=\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{Tr}((\rho-\sigma)^\dagger(\rho-\sigma))$. The statement asserts attainment as well as comparison with every fully separable competitor. It is refuted.</p>
