d allow norming functionals at different unit vectors to contribute a common convex range.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The algebra instances use the associativity, distributivity, scalar compatibility and submultiplicative norm lemmas.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A three-dimensional complex normed algebra has two spatial numerical range points whose midpoint is absent.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The stronger proposed conclusion that every nonconvex spatial range of an idempotent transfers to a nonconvex algebra spatial range remains open: inclusion alone is insufficient, because other norming pairs can add points.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="dedania-patel-2024-spatial-numerical-range-convexity"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>Convexity of the spatial numerical range in non-unital normed algebras</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>H. V. Dedania and A. B. Patel, <em>Spatial numerical range in non-unital, normed algebras and their unitizations</em>, arXiv:2306.16172v1, <em>Sarajevo Journal of Mathematics</em> 20(2) (2024), §1, p. 2, write:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let $S(A)={x\in A:|x|=1}$ be the unit sphere in $A$. Let $A^*$ be the Banach space dual of $A$. Let $D_A(x)={\varphi\in A^*:|\varphi|=1=\varphi(x)}$ for each $x\in S(A)$. Further, let $V_A(a;x)={\varphi(ax):\varphi\in D_A(x)}$ for $a\in A$ and $x\in S(A)$. Then $V_A(a)=\cup{V_A(a;x):x\in S(A)}$ is the spatial numerical range (SNR) … It is still open problem wether $V_A(a)$ is always convex? This problem is discussed in [5].</p>
