<ul>
<li><strong>The measures.</strong> With the X-matrix X^A = Σ_i ‖A_i‖A_i, they are E(A) = ‖I − X^A‖ and E′(A) = Tr[(I/d)(I − X^A)] (Eqs. (44) and (48)).</li>
<li><strong>Monotonicity criteria.</strong> Theorems 3 and 4 show that E and E′ are non-decreasing under the white noise A^λ_i = λA_i + (1 − λ)I/n exactly when Σ^A_1 ≥ Σ^A_2 (Eq. (50)) and Σ′^A_1 ≥ Σ′^A_2 (Eq. (60)) hold. Here Σ^A_1 = x^A_min − 1/n and Σ^A_2 = Σ_i ‖A_i‖/n − x^A_min, with x^A_min the lowest eigenvalue of X^A.</li>
<li><strong>The conjecture.</strong> Propositions 1–2 prove both inequalities for dichotomic qubit observables. Then: &quot;Conjecture 1. For any qubit observable A, inequality Σ^A_1 ≥ Σ^A_2 and inequality Σ′^A_1 ≥ Σ′^A_2 hold and therefore, E(A^λ) ≥ E(A) and E′(A^λ) ≥ E′(A) for all 1 ≥ λ ≥ 0.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/mitra2022unsharpness.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/17118">#17118</a> fixes the reading: every qubit POVM with any number n ≥ 1 of outcomes, the operator norm, and all four clauses. The conjecture is refuted.</p>
