d protocol for approximating permutations by sequential two-level thermalisations.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The conjecture would make the protocol without intermediate memory resets, which lets memory correlations pass between successive transpositions, uniformly at least as accurate as the protocol that resets the memory after each transposition.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The module defines the infinite-temperature elementary operation average, the nested-round operation block, the uniform-memory coupling joint, the memory reset, and the two folded outputs.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Removing intermediate memory resets increases the permutation approximation error in a finite reduced protocol.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="czartowski-deoliveirajunior-korzekwa-2023-thermal-recall-truncation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Removing intermediate memory resets can increase permutation approximation error</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. Czartowski, A. de Oliveira Junior and K. Korzekwa, <em>Thermal Recall: Memory-Assisted Markovian Thermal Processes</em>, PRX Quantum 4, 040304 (2023), arXiv:2303.12840v2, introduce a memory-assisted protocol for approximating permutations by sequential two-level thermalisations. Conjecture 1, “Improved convergence,” is stated in the source TeX <code>TR.tex</code>, lines 417–427:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the infinite temperature limit, $\beta=0$, and for an $N$-dimensional memory, $\widetilde{\mathcal{P}}^{{\Pi}}$ gives a better approximation of a permutation $\Pi$ than $\mathcal{P}^{\Pi}$:
$$\delta\left( \Pi \mathbf p,\tilde{\mathbf q}\right) \leq  \delta\left( \Pi \mathbf p, \mathbf{q}\right),$$
where $\tilde{\mathbf{q}}$ and $\mathbf{q}$ are defined via $\widetilde{\mathcal{P}}^{{\Pi}}(\mathbf{p}\otimes \boldsymbol\eta_M) = \tilde{\mathbf{q}}\otimes \boldsymbol\eta_M$ and $\mathcal{P}^{{\Pi}}(\mathbf{p}\otimes \boldsymbol\eta_M) = \mathbf{q}\otimes \boldsymbol\eta_M$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The repository proposition <code>claim</code> is the following exact finite reading. For every $d,n\in\mathbb N$, $p:\mathrm{Fin},d\to\mathbb R$, and word $w:\mathrm{List}(\mathrm{Fin},d\times\mathrm{Fin},d)$, if $0&lt;n$, $(\forall i,\ 0\le p(i))$, $\sum_i p(i)=1$, <code>StrictAnti p</code>, and <code>IsReducedNeighbourWord p w</code>, then
[
\operatorname{totalVariation}(\operatorname{applySwaps}(p,w),\operatorname{truncatedOutput}(n,p,w))
\le
\operatorname{totalVariation}(\operatorname{applySwaps}(p,w),\operatorname{resetOutput}(n,p,w)).
]
Here <code>applySwaps</code> folds the word by precomposing with each <code>Equiv.swap</code>; <code>totalVariation</code> is one half of the finite sum of absolute coordinate differences. <code>average</code> replaces the values at two selected joint states by their arithmetic mean. <code>block</code> applies these averages for every first memory index and, inside each round, every second memory index, both in increasing <code>List.finRange</code> order. <code>joint</code> couples $p$ to a uniform memory, <code>reset</code> replaces a joint state by the product of its system marginal and that uniform memory, <code>truncatedOutput</code> folds the blocks without intermediate resets, and <code>resetOutput</code> folds a reset after each block. <code>IsReducedNeighbourWord</code> requires, at every position, an increasing index pair whose two current population values are ordered with no current value strictly between them.</p>
<p>The Lean declaration is <code>result : \lnot claim</code>.</p>
