ons</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>R. Grünwald and Zs. Páles, <em>Comparison of Gini means with fixed number of variables</em>, arXiv:2408.07658v1, §4, state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 2. Assume that 0 = inf I · I⁻¹ and let n ≥ 3. Then ((r, s), (p, q)) ∈ Γ_n(I) if and only if, for all u₁, …, u_n ∈ ℝ₊ such that G^{[n]}<em>{r,s}(u₁, …, u_n) = 1, ∃ρ ∈ ℝ such that χ′</em>{p,q}(u_k) + ρχ′<em>{r,s}(u_k) = 0 (k ∈ {1, …, n}) the inequality 1 ≤ G^{[n]}</em>{p,q}(u₁, …, u_n) holds.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $I$ is a nonempty interval in $(0,\infty)$ and $\Gamma_n(I)$ consists of the parameter pairs satisfying $G_{r,s}(x)\le G_{p,q}(x)$ for every $x\in I^n$. The stationary test universally quantifies over positive normalized tuples satisfying the common-multiplier equations. It does not assert that every normalized tuple admits such a multiplier.</p>
<p>The formal reading is:</p>
<p>| Source object or condition | Lean encoding and meaning |
|---|---|
| $n\ge3$, $x\in I^n$ | <code>n : ℕ</code>, <code>3 ≤ n</code>, <code>x : Fin n → ℝ</code>, and <code>∀ i, x i ∈ I</code> |
| Nonempty positive interval $I$ | <code>I ⊆ Set.Ioi 0</code>, <code>I.OrdConnected</code>, and <code>I.Nonempty</code> |
| $\inf(I\cdot I^{-1})=0$ | <code>sInf ((fun z : ℝ × ℝ =&gt; z.1 / z.2) '' (I ×ˢ I)) = 0</code>; the set is precisely ${a/b:a,b\in I}$, rather than a product of two scalar infima |
| $G_{p,q}$, $p\ne q$ | <code>Defs.giniMean</code> delegates to <code>GiniMeanEndpointComparison.giniMean</code>, namely $(\sum x_i^p/\sum x_i^q)^{1/(p-q)}$ |
| $G_{p,p}$ | $\exp(\sum x_i^p\log x_i/\sum x_i^p)$, the equal-parameter branch of <code>Defs.giniMean</code> |
| $\chi_{p,q}$ | <code>Defs.chi</code>: $(t^p-t^q)/(p-q)$ for $p\ne q$, and $t^p\log t$ for $p=q$ |
| $\chi'<em>{p,q}$ | <code>Defs.chiDeriv p q t := deriv (Defs.chi p q) t</code> |
| $\Gamma_n(I)$ | <code>Defs.Gamma n I</code>: comparison for every tuple from $I$ |
| Stationary criterion | <code>Defs.stationaryTest n r s p q</code>: positivity, $G</em>{r,s}(u)=1$, and existence of a common real multiplier imply $1\le G_{p,q}(u)$ |
| Claimed equivalence | <code>Defs.claim</code>: all the displayed interval conditions, all $n\ge3$, and all real $r,s,p,q$ |</p>
