ion in main.tex, line 1365:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper presents testers as a way to turn mixed-state entanglement detection into a projective norm calculation on Euclidean output spaces.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The module follows a finite fourth-root design and a swap witness.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Entangled Werner states with dimension at least three and parameter between one over the dimension and one half escape every pair of complex testers.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>It does not settle completeness after an index permutation, the source&#x27;s separate statements about realignment or SIC POVM testers, or restricted variants of the tester class. - Effect on the source.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="jivulescu-lancien-nechita-2022-werner-tester-incompleteness"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Complex testers do not detect every entangled Werner state</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M. A. Jivulescu, C. Lancien and I. Nechita, <em>Multipartite Entanglement Detection Via Projective Tensor Norms</em>, arXiv:2010.06365v1 (Annales Henri Poincaré 23 (2022), 3791–3838), Section <code>sec:conclusions</code>, posed the following question in <code>main.tex</code>, line 1365:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The main question that remains unanswered at this point is whether our family of entanglement criteria is complete, without allowing for permutation of indices before applying the testers. Concretely, we would like to know if, for any entangled state $\rho$ on $\mathbb C^{d_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbb C^{d_m}$, there exist testers $\mathcal E_i:S_1^{d_i}\to\ell_2^{n_i}$, $1\leq i\leq m$, such that $|\mathcal E_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathcal E_m(\rho)|<em>{\ell_2^{n_1}\otimes</em>\pi\cdots\otimes_\pi\ell_2^{n_m}}&gt;1$. Even for bipartite states we are only able to show a weaker version of this statement (see Section \ref{sec:completeness}).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source defines the projective tensor norm by the infimum of the sums of products of factor norms (Definition, <code>main.tex</code>, lines 234–238), the trace norm by $|X|<em>1=\operatorname{Tr}\sqrt{X^*X}$ (<code>main.tex</code>, lines 288–290), and a complex tester as a complex-linear map of norm exactly one from the Schatten-1 space to the Euclidean space (<code>main.tex</code>, lines 340–352). Its Werner state is
$\sigma</em>\mu=\mu\frac{I+F}{d(d+1)}+(1-\mu)\frac{I-F}{d(d-1)},\qquad 0\leq\mu\leq1$
(<code>main.tex</code>, line 946).</p>
<p>The Lean declaration <code>claim : Prop</code> is the bipartite instance. For every $d_1,d_2:\mathbb N$ and every $\rho:\operatorname{Matrix}(\operatorname{Fin}d_1\times\operatorname{Fin}d_2)(\operatorname{Fin}d_1\times\operatorname{Fin}d_2),\mathbb C$, its hypotheses are <code>ρ.PosSemidef</code>, <code>ρ.trace = 1</code>, and <code>¬ Separable ρ</code>; its conclusion requires $n_1,n_2:\mathbb N$ and complex-linear maps $e_i:\operatorname{Matrix}(\operatorname{Fin}d_i)(\operatorname{Fin}d_i),\mathbb C\to\operatorname{EuclideanSpace},\mathbb C,(\operatorname{Fin}n_i)$ satisfying <code>IsTester e₁ ∧ IsTester e₂ ∧ 1 &lt; ‖testerImage e₁ e₂ ρ‖</code>.</p>
<p>Here <code>IsTester e</code> is exactly the conjunction $\forall x,\ |e x|\leq\operatorname{traceNorm}(x)$ and $\exists x,\ x\neq0\land|e x|=\operatorname{traceNorm}(x)$. The formal <code>Separable</code> predicate is membership in the real convex hull of matrices <code>Matrix.kroneckerMap (· * ·) ρ₁ ρ₂</code>, where each factor is positive semidefinite with trace $1$.</p>
<p>The output of <code>testerImage</code> has type <code>⨂[ℂ] s : Bool, EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin (cond s n₂ n₁))</code>: the <code>false</code> factor is the first Euclidean output space and the <code>true</code> factor is the second. Its norm $|\cdot|$ is Mathlib's bundled <code>PiTensorProduct.projectiveSeminorm</code> on this tensor product. This represents the source's $\ell_2^{n_1}\otimes_\pi\ell_2^{n_2}$ norm from <code>main.tex</code>, lines 234–238: the two factors carry their Euclidean norms, and each finite pure-tensor expansion has cost equal to the sum of the products of those factor norms. Mathlib permits explicit complex scalar coefficients in these expansions; absorbing each coefficient into either factor gives the source's convention, with the same cost. The tensor norm notation and application of <code>PiTensorProduct.projectiveSeminorm</code> are definitionally equal.</p>
<p>The settlement is the negation of this bipartite claim. The source's universal multipartite question is therefore already false at two parties.</p>
