<p>The module formalizes the second question through <code>claim</code>. Its <code>weightClass n m</code> is the finite set of Boolean memories $f : \mathrm{Fin}(2^n) \to \mathrm{Bool}$ having exactly $m$ true addresses. Its <code>copyState n t m</code> averages the $t$-fold product of one <code>addressState n f</code> over that class, with the same $f$ in every copy. For every $n \ge 1$, $t \ge 1$, $m_0,m_1 \le 2^n$, every $e : \operatorname{Matrix}(\mathrm{Fin}(t) \to \mathrm{Fin}(2^n))(\mathrm{Fin}(t) \to \mathrm{Fin}(2^n)),\mathbb{C}$ with <code>e.PosSemidef</code> and <code>(1 - e).PosSemidef</code>, and <code>successProb</code> as defined in the module, <code>claim</code> requires a Boolean rule $g : \mathrm{Fin}(t+1) \to \mathrm{Bool}$ such that
$\operatorname{successProb}(n,t,m_0,m_1,e) \le \operatorname{successProb}(n,t,m_0,m_1,\operatorname{countTest}(n,t,g))$.
This is the equal-prior special case: if the count were sufficient for every prior, it would in particular satisfy this equal-prior assertion.</p>
