<p>The source defines $\mathcal A_d^M$ by the existence of $M$ perfectly distinguishable quantum states with the same classical version (<code>Distinguishability.tex</code>, lines 147–149), defines that version as the diagonal in the preferred basis (lines 236–241), and identifies perfect distinguishability with pairwise orthogonal supports (lines 297–300). The $\boldsymbol f^i$ are the four face centres of the probability simplex, each a permutation of $(1,1,1,0)/3$ (line 462). The definitions and quotation are also recorded in <a href="../Library/HilbertOperators/korzekwaetal2019distinguishing.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>For the formal statement, index the four coordinates and face centres by $0,1,2,3$, and put $f^j_k=(1-\delta_{jk})/3$. <code>InRegion p</code> means that $p$ is nonnegative with coordinate sum one and that there exist three complex $4\times4$ positive semidefinite matrices $\rho_i$ of trace one satisfying $\rho_i\rho_j=0$ for $i\ne j$ and $\operatorname{Re}(\rho_i)_{kk}=p_k$ for every $i,k$. <code>ProductForm f p</code> is precisely the displayed combination with $a,b\in[0,1]$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25867">#25867</a> fixes <code>claim</code>: there exists a permutation $\sigma$ of the four face centres such that every $p$ satisfying <code>InRegion p</code> has <code>ProductForm</code> with centres $f^{\sigma(0)},\ldots,f^{\sigma(3)}$. The existential labelling accommodates the different conventions in the source's text and figure. The module proves <code>result : ¬ claim</code>.</p>
