ttice dismantling of the priority lattice</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The priority lattice is not always sublattice dismantlable.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Adrián Lillo and Mercedes Rosas, The Priority Lattice, arXiv:2603.28905v1, Section 6, item 4, ask:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Thus one positive parameter refutes the whole universal question.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The parents of vertices 3 and 6 distinguish all sixteen forests; top is distinct from every forest.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The priority lattice is not always sublattice dismantlable.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="lillo-rosas-2026-priority-lattice-sublattice-dismantling"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Sublattice dismantling of the priority lattice</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Adrián Lillo and Mercedes Rosas, <em>The Priority Lattice</em>, arXiv:2603.28905v1,
<a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2603.28905v1#S6.I1.i4">Section 6, item 4</a>, ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A sublattice dismantling is a subdivision of a lattice into two non-empty sublattices. A lattice is sublattice dismantlable if it can be decomposed into 1-element lattices by consecutive sublattice dismantlings. We have checked with the help of SageMath that $\Pi(n)$ is sublattice dismantlable for $n=$ 1 to 5. Is this always the case?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The question quantifies over every positive integer $n$. The source's priority
forests are increasing rooted forests on $0,\ldots,n$ with interval components.
Their order is edge containment, with an additional greatest element.
The existing <code>PrincipalFilters.Pi</code> is this literal carrier, and <code>coreOrderIso</code>
identifies it with <code>WithTop (IntervalForest n)</code>.</p>
<p>A subdivision is read as a disjoint partition into nonempty sublattices, closed
under the ambient binary meet and join, without requiring the ambient bounds.
The identical definition and the interval/complement split in
<a href="https://github.com/sagemath/sage/blob/133eb21d995e6aa5b6de2cd8db798a442e5cc033/src/sage/combinat/posets/lattices.py#L4032">Sage's immutable implementation</a>
support this standard meaning. The source does not separately say “disjoint”.
