<blockquote>
<p>This shows that while $\vartheta(K)=0$ may hold for all compacts $K$ with $\mathrm{cap}(K)=1$, the decay rate of $A_n(K)$ is not universal. It will be interesting to determine whether $A_n(K)$ can only decay like $\frac1n$ or $\frac1{\log n}$, or whether other rates are possible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For compact $K\subseteq\mathbb C$, let $P_n(K)$ consist of the monic
degree-$n$ polynomials whose zeros all lie in $K$. Write
$\Lambda_p={z:|p(z)|&lt;1}$ and
$A_n(K)=\inf_{p\in P_n(K)}m(\Lambda_p)$, where $m$ is planar Lebesgue
measure. The paper defines capacity as logarithmic capacity,
equivalently the transfinite diameter.</p>
<p>The Lean <code>claim</code> says that for every nonempty compact $K$ of transfinite
diameter one, the real-valued minimal-area sequence is either
$\Theta(1/n)$ or $\Theta(1/\log n)$ as $n\to\infty$.
Thus <code>result : ¬ claim</code> refutes the proposed two-rate restriction.</p>
