iv class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Hanna&#x27;s OEIS A075885, revision 28, gives the formula:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>Every theorem is classified bind-only; no escape witness or priority for the proof ingredients is claimed.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Hanna&#x27;s floor-prefix sum has the positive root limit specified by its logarithmic series.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="hanna-a075885"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Hanna&#x27;s A075885 root limit</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Paul D. Hanna's OEIS A075885, revision 28, gives the formula:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>a(n) = 1 + Sum_{m=1..n} Product_{k=1..m} floor(n/k).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Its conjecture reads:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture: limit a(n)^(1/n) = L where L = 2.200161058099... is the geometric mean of Luroth expansions, where log(L) = Sum_{n&gt;=1} log(n)/(n*(n+1)) = 0.7885305659115... (cf. A085361).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Define $p(n,m)=\prod_{k=1}^{m}\lfloor n/k\rfloor$ for $0\le m\le n$,
with empty product $1$, and $P(n)=p(n,n)$. Then
$a(n)=1+\sum_{m=1}^{n}p(n,m)$, including $a(0)=1$.
The proved assertion is the existence of $L&gt;0$ such that the real series
$\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\log(j)/(j(j+1))$ converges to $\log L$ and
$a(n)^{1/n}\to L$ over positive integers. The decimals and the description
by Lüroth expansions identify the source constant; no numerical accuracy or
ergodic theorem is included in this assertion. Preregistration: #23630.</p>
