lass="news-result" id="jiang-klco-di-matteo-2025-cartesian-control-angle-matrix-invertibility"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Cartesian-control angle matrices are invertible for mixed qudit dimensions</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. Jiang, N. Klco and O. Di Matteo, <em>Non-Abelian dynamics on a cube: Improving quantum compilation through qudit-based simulations</em>, arXiv:2506.10945v1 (Phys. Rev. D 112, 074512, 2025), state the following conjecture in <code>subsec_3c_restricted-rotations.tex</code>, lines 38–46 (the display labelled <code>eq:conjecture</code>). The quotation expands the source macro <code>\ctrlseq</code> as $\boldsymbol C$ and renders its displayed equation inline:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By keeping $\boldsymbol C$ general, we sacrifice the guarantee that the matrix $M$ is invertible, although we conjecture our particular implementation yields an invertible $M$ when ${\boldsymbol C} = \mathbb{Z}<em>{d_1} \times \mathbb{Z}</em>{d_2} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}_{d_k}$, i.e., uniform control but non-uniform qudit dimension.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Equation (5), <code>subsec_3a_uniform-rotations.tex</code>, lines 38–49, defines $\boldsymbol\theta=M\boldsymbol\beta$ and $M_{ij}=\prod_l(-1)^{f(b_l^{(i)},g_l^{(j)})}$, where $f(b,g)=1$ when $0&lt;b\le g$ and $f(b,g)=0$ otherwise. Here $b$ and $g$ are control-word sequences. The definitions and source locators are collected in <a href="../Library/Eigenstructure/jiangklcodimatteo2025cube.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23818">#23818</a> fixes the reading as invertibility for every pair of enumerations of the full Cartesian control set. Precisely, for every $k\in\mathbb N$, every $d:\mathrm{Fin},k\to\mathbb N$, and every pair of bijections $b,g:\mathrm{Fin},N\simeq C$, where $N=\prod_l d_l$ and $C=\prod_{l:\mathrm{Fin},k}\mathrm{Fin}(d_l)$, <code>angleMatrix d b g</code> is the real matrix with entries
$M_{ij}=\prod_l\bigl(\text{if }0&lt;b(i)_l\le g(j)_l\text{ then }-1\text{ else }1\bigr)$.
The proposition <code>claim</code> says that this matrix is a unit in the real square-matrix ring, so it has a two-sided inverse; <code>result : claim</code> proves it. There is no lower-bound hypothesis on the dimensions.</p>
