on-Local Nonstabilizerness</em>, arXiv:2502.06393v4, Phys. Rev. A <strong>111</strong>, 052443 (2025), define the non-local nonstabilizerness (NN) of a state as the stabilizer Rényi entropy minimized over local unitaries. For two-qubit pure states it depends only on the Schmidt weight x = cos²θ (Eq. (C1)): M(x) = ln(1/(1 + 4x(x − 1)(1 − 2x)²)). For Haar-random states x has density 3(1 − 2x)², and Appendix C derives the density (C5) of y = M(x) on 0 &lt; y &lt; ln(4/3).</p>
<p>§III states that &quot;a closed-form expression for the average value of M … remains unclear&quot; and reports approximately 0.192. Appendix C writes: &quot;we have not been able to find a simple closed-form expression for the average amount of NN, ȳ ≡ ∫ yP(y)dy, for the Haar random state&quot;, and gives ȳ ≈ 0.1917 by numerical integration. The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumStates/qian2025nonlocalnonstabilizerness.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16544">#16544</a> fixes the reading. With P the literal (C5) on (0, ln(4/3)) and 0 elsewhere, y·P(y) is integrable and ȳ = ∫ y P(y) dy = 10/3 − π. It is proved.</p>
