:2601.00916v4, Section 5, page 19, ask:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In addition to the above, it is natural to ask whether analogous structural properties hold for I_n^m when m is arbitrary. Can one prove or disprove that I_n^m (i) stays uniformly away from 1, (ii) is bounded, or (iii) forms an increasing family in n?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source concludes: “Thus this question, together with question (iii), remains open.” This dossier settles question (ii).</p>
<p>Write $f_n(x)=(x^n-1)/(x^{n+1}+1)$, $H_m={x\in(0,\infty)^m:\prod_jx_j=1}$ and $I_n^m={x\in H_m:\sum_j f_n(x_j)&gt;0}$. For all natural numbers $m,n\ge1$, the result is</p>
<p>$$I_n^m\text{ is bounded}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad m\le3\ \lor\ (m=4\land n\le4)\ \lor\ (m\in{5,6}\land n=1).$$</p>
<p>The classification is the repository's answer, rather than a proposition quoted from the paper.</p>
