esult-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>The quantum value of balanced hypercube rendezvous is strictly below its no-signalling value</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>C. Wang, <em>Quantum-assisted rendezvous on hypercubes: a quantum Bernoulli noise approach</em>, arXiv:2610.12949v1 (9 October 2026), studies one-step rendezvous on the hypercube {0,1}^d.</p>
<ul>
<li>Inputs: Alice and Bob receive vertices σ, τ with |σ △ τ| = 2t, uniformly.</li>
<li>Outputs: each moves t coordinates, Alice A and Bob B, both t-subsets.</li>
<li>Winning: they meet, σ △ A = τ △ B, that is A ∩ B = ∅ and A ∪ B = σ △ τ.</li>
</ul>
<p>Lemma 5.1 gives the no-signalling value ω_NS(d,t) = C(2t,t)/C(d,t). Theorems 5.5 and 6.4 show that for t ≥ 2 and d &gt; 2t no finite-dimensional quantum strategy attains it. §7 asks: &quot;It remains open whether ω_Q(d,t) is then strictly below ω_NS(d,t)&quot;, where ω_Q is the supremum over finite-dimensional quantum strategies (Eq. (2.8)). The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/wang2026rendezvous.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/15814">#15814</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<ul>
<li>arbitrary finite-dimensional systems, density matrices and POVMs;</li>
<li>no-signalling correlations with input-independent marginals;</li>
<li>both values as suprema;</li>
<li>the claim ω_Q(d,t) &lt; ω_NS(d,t) for all t ≥ 2 and d &gt; 2t.</li>
</ul>
