<blockquote>
<p>“Due to the limitations of our research method, we cannot solve the problem that Theorem 2.3 holds for $1/2 \le a \le 1$.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Their Theorem 2.3 states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>“Let $A \in M_n$. Then $[[|A|^{2a}, A^*], [A, |A|^{2(1-a)}]] \ge 0$, for $0 \le a \le 1/2$ if and only if $A$ is normal.”</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The notation is “$|A| = (A^<em>A)^{1/2}$ and $A^</em>$ is the conjugate transpose of $A$” (p. 595). The delivered claim extends the displayed equivalence to every real $a$ with $1/2 \le a \le 1$, every natural dimension, and every complex square matrix, using the existing block construction with $|A|=\operatorname{CFC.sqrt}(A^*A)$ and real powers.</p>
