<blockquote>
<p>a(n+1) = round(sqrt(a(n)^2 + a(n-1)^2 + a(n-2)^2 - a(n-3)^2)).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The complete conjectural COMMENTS paragraph states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Numerical evidence suggests that the ratio of consecutive terms (a(n+1)/a(n)) tends to 1.3122819079... (sqrt(A289917)) which is the largest real root of the asymptotic equation of this sequence: x^8-x^6-x^4-x^2+1. This is related to A116732 where the asymptotic equation is y^4-y^3-y^2-y+1 via y=x^2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Mathematica initialization is $a_1=a_2=a_3=0$, $a_4=1$. Subtraction in the radicand is signed integer subtraction. The recurrence applies for $n\ge4$; index zero is unused. Set</p>
<p>$$r=\frac{1+\sqrt{13}+\sqrt{2\sqrt{13}-2}}4,\qquad \alpha=\sqrt r.$$</p>
<p>The assertion is existence of a natural-valued source sequence and, for every such sequence, eventual positivity and $a_{n+1}/a_n\to\alpha$, together with $\alpha^8-\alpha^6-\alpha^4-\alpha^2+1=0$ and maximality among all real roots.</p>
