e 10-245 has density 1/4 + λ/2 at every time from one onwards, starting from the fair bernoulli cylinder law. 2. two moments. let dt be the density and qt the probability of a 0 followed by a 1 at distance two.  released-dossier 16644b5a300421845f57aa420f14c401b2e80a63a267b7faf133171bdcae8611 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Analysis &amp; dynamics</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The diploid cellular automaton 10-245 has exact density 1/4 + λ/2</strong><span class="result-excerpt">For every mixing parameter in [0,1], diploid rule 10-245 has density 1/4 + λ/2 at every time from one onwards, starting from the fair Bernoulli cylinder law.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Starting from the symmetric Bernoulli measure, dt = Pr(si(t) = 1) is the density and d∞ = lim dt (§2).</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper sorts the 168 endogamous diploid rules into six classes by the shape of d∞(λ) and tests how far local structure theory predicts them.</p><p><span>The idea</span>2. Two moments. Let dt be the density and qt the probability of a 0 followed by a 1 at distance two.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>For every mixing parameter in [0,1], diploid rule 10-245 has density 1/4 + λ/2 at every time from one onwards, starting from the fair Bernoulli cylinder law.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="fuks-fates-2026-diploid-10-245-density"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The diploid cellular automaton 10-245 has exact density 1/4 + λ/2</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>H. Fukś and N. Fatès, <em>Analysis of the asymptotic density of endogamous diploid cellular automata</em>, arXiv:2607.01917v1 (EPJ ST 2026), study probabilistic cellular automata on ℤ in which every cell independently applies elementary rule f with probability 1 − λ and rule g with probability λ: w(1|x₁x₂x₃) = (1−λ) f(x₁,x₂,x₃) + λ g(x₁,x₂,x₃) (Eq. (2)). Starting from the symmetric Bernoulli measure, d_t = Pr(s_i(t) = 1) is the density and d∞ = lim d_t (§2). For rule 10-245, third-order local structure approximation predicts d∞ = 1/4 + λ/2 (§3.2), while mean-field and second-order approximation give a nonlinear curve. §5 asks: &quot;for rule 10-245, the expression for the asymptotic density, d = 1/4 + λ/2, appears to be exact. Could this be formally demonstrated?&quot; The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/fuksfates2026diploid.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16027">#16027</a> fixes the reading: for every λ ∈ [0, 1], with the lattice process encoded by its cylinder probabilities (independent simultaneous updates of each interval from its n + 2 parents, fair Bernoulli start), d_t → 1/4 + λ/2. The settlement proves the stronger d_t = 1/4 + λ/2 for every t ≥ 1.</p>
