<blockquote>
<p>However, up to the moment the problem whether or not the CHSH inequality is violated under Alice and Bob high spin $s\geq1$ measurements is still open -- though specifically this Bell inequality has been used for proving \cite{1, Lou.Kuz.Han:24}  nonlocality of every pure nonseparable two-qudit state.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The same source's Conjecture section, lines 913–914, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Under local Alice and Bob spin-$1$ measurements in an arbitrary nonseparable pure two-qutrit state, the
CHSH inequality is not violated.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For spin $s$, the measurements are the linear projections $S_u=u\cdot S$ for unit $u\in\mathbb R^3$. The companion paper, E. R. Loubenets and L. Hanotel, <em>High-spin measurements in an arbitrary two-qudit state</em>, J. Phys. A: Math. Theor. 58, 265304 (2025), arXiv:2412.03470v2, <code>Revision_Loubenets_Hanotel.tex</code>, lines 210–237 and 253–261, specifies the spin matrices and the CHSH operator
$\mathcal B=S_a\otimes S_b+S_a\otimes S_d+S_c\otimes S_b-S_c\otimes S_d$.
Its lines 348–363 give the local-hidden-variable bound $2s^2$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24190">#24190</a> fixes the reading: for every integer $n=2s\ge2$, every positive semidefinite trace-one complex matrix $\rho$ on $\mathbb C^{n+1}\otimes\mathbb C^{n+1}$, and every four unit real directions $a,c,b,d$,
$|\operatorname{tr}(\rho\mathcal B)|\le n^2/2=2s^2$.
This is proved, including integer and half-integer spins $s\ge1$ and both pure and mixed states.</p>
<p>The source definitions and bibliographic locators are also recorded in <a href="../Library/Observer/hanotelloubenets2025chshspinone.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
