Section 7, Question 20(iii), define the collision density of an arbitrary bracket
expression and separately ask: “Is it always rational?” Section 1 specifies
integer coefficients and positive rational moduli.</p>
<p>For every finite $t$, coefficients $c_i\in\mathbb Z$ and positive
$\alpha_i\in\mathbb Q$, set
$w_n=\sum_{i=1}^t c_i\lfloor n\alpha_i\rfloor$ for $n\geq0$ and
$W={w_n:n\geq0}$. For every positive integer $k$, set
$C(k)={x\in W:x+k\in W}$. The result proves that</p>
<p>$$\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{|C(k)\cap{0,\ldots,N}|}{N}$$</p>
<p>exists and is rational. This is the complete rationality subquestion, including
existence of the limit, rather than the other requests in Question 20.</p>
