eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Identity and transposition classes have the two largest tensor high-temperature degrees</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. O'Connor and S. Ramgoolam, <em>Gauged permutation invariant tensor quantum mechanics, least common multiples and the inclusion-exclusion principle</em>, arXiv:2506.18813v2 (2025), Section 7.2, state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Computations with Mathematica and the analysis of special families give strong evidence that the leading contributions in the high temperature limit $ x \rightarrow 1 $ come from $ p = [ 1^N ] $ followed by $ p = [ 2,1^{ N-2} ] $.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Section 9 asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It would be very interesting to find the proof of the general $s$ conjecture for the leading two terms.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>These quotations are from lines 1068 and 1322 of the v2 e-print member <code>Perm-Invt-Tensor-Counting-Functions-Journal.tex</code>, md5 <code>202b33a8e55dec1592ee90b7e5fbd8d9</code>.</p>
<p>For a partition $p$ of $N$, let $A(p)$ be its set of distinct part values and $m_a$ the multiplicity of $a$. The module's rational-valued <code>degree</code> is the first sum in the source's equation (7.21), Section 7.2, TeX lines 1079–1087:</p>
<p>$$D_s(p)=\sum_{t:\mathrm{Fin},s\to A(p)}
\frac{\prod_{j\in\mathrm{Fin},s}t_jm_{t_j}}
{\operatorname{lcm}{t_j:j\in\mathrm{Fin},s}}.$$</p>
<p>Tuple entries may repeat. The part values, multiplicities and natural-number least common multiple are cast to the rationals for this expression.</p>
<p>Write $I_N=[1^N]$ and $T_N=[2,1^{N-2}]$. Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24869">#24869</a> fixes the reading: for every natural $N\ge2$ and $s\ge1$,</p>
<p>$$D_s(I_N)=N^s,\qquad D_s(T_N)=\frac{N^s+(N-2)^s}{2}&lt;D_s(I_N),$$</p>
<p>and every partition $p$ of $N$ different from both $I_N$ and $T_N$ satisfies $D_s(p)&lt;D_s(T_N)$. This assertion is proved. The subtraction $N-2$ is natural subtraction; it agrees with integer subtraction in the stated range.</p>
