esolved</span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Forberg&#x27;s exponential formula for the quarter-squares</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Forberg&#x27;s exponential quotient has the shifted quarter-square floor at every positive integer.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Empirical: a(n-1) = floor(n/(e^(4/n)-1)). - Richard R.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The result explains the agreement through uniform analytic bounds and the spacing of integer floor intervals.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Forberg&#x27;s exponential quotient has the shifted quarter-square floor at every positive integer.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="oeis-a002620-forberg-exponential-floor"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Integer sequences (OEIS)</p><h3>Forberg&#x27;s exponential formula for the quarter-squares</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>OEIS A002620, revision 880, contains the formula attributed to Richard R. Forberg:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Empirical: a(n-1) = floor(n/(e^(4/n)-1)). - <em>Richard R. Forberg</em>, Jul 24 2013</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The entry defines the quarter-squares by
$a(m)=\lfloor m/2\rfloor\lceil m/2\rceil=\lfloor m^2/4\rfloor$, with offset zero.
The complete assertion is</p>
<p>$$
\forall n\in\mathbb N,\quad 1\le n\ \Longrightarrow
\left\lfloor\frac{n}{\exp(4/n)-1}\right\rfloor
=\left\lfloor\frac{(n-1)^2}{4}\right\rfloor.
$$</p>
<p>All operations inside the floors are in $\mathbb R$, with $n$ coerced before
division and subtraction. The exponential is <code>Real.exp</code>; both floors are
<code>Int.floor</code> and the equality is in $\mathbb Z$. The index shift is $m=n-1$.
The assertion includes $n=1$, both parities, and arbitrarily large indices,
with no additional hypothesis.</p>
