<p>Section 1 says: “All graphs considered are finite, simple and undirected.”
Section 2, page 4, defines a general position set by the absence of three
distinct selected vertices $x,y,z$ with $d(x,z)=d(x,y)+d(y,z)$, and says:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For each k ≥ 0, let α_k(G) denote the number of general position sets of size k.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The general position polynomial of G is ψ(G) = ∑_{k≥0} α_k(G)x^k.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The settlement is <strong>Proved</strong>: for every nonempty finite connected simple
graph, $\psi(G)$ is real-rooted if and only if $G$ is complete.</p>
