<blockquote>
<p>Several other properties of the SDE have not yet been established, e.g. whether the SDE is submodular or if the SDE can be used successfully in applications to enhance or reduce assortativity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the tier-2 external question preregistered in
https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/19958.
The settlement is <strong>Refuted</strong> for edge sets on a fixed vertex set.</p>
<p>Definition 2.1 and equation (2.1), p. 1, define the exponent by
$\lambda_1=((1/N)\sum_i d_i^q)^{1/q}$ for a simple undirected nonregular graph.
Theorem 2.2 places it in $[2,\infty]$. Here every exponent used is a finite real
number and $\lambda_1$ is the supremum of the real adjacency spectrum.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause or explicit interpretation | Lean counterpart | Fidelity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Simple undirected graph on $N$ vertices | <code>SimpleGraph (Fin n)</code> | Unweighted finite graphs form a subclass of the source's graphs. |
| Degree mean $((1/N)\sum_i d_i^q)^{1/q}$ | <code>degreePowerMean</code> | Natural degrees and $n$ are cast to real numbers; both powers are real powers. |
| Nonregular graph | <code>IsRegular</code>, negated in <code>IsSpectralDegreeExponent</code> | Regularity is exactly existence of a common natural degree, using <code>SimpleGraph.IsRegularOfDegree</code>. |
| Largest adjacency eigenvalue | Real-spectrum supremum in <code>IsSpectralDegreeExponent</code> | The literal preregistered expression is inlined. The private correspondence <code>spectral_sup_eq_edgeMax</code> connects it to the reused <code>SpikeEdgeEstimate.edgeMax</code> in the proofs. |
| Finite solution with $q\ge2$ | <code>IsSpectralDegreeExponent G q</code> | Includes nonregularity, the lower bound and the defining equation. Infinite exponents are outside this restricted claim. |
| Submodular on the possible edges of one vertex set | <code>claim</code> | All $n,G,H,q_G,q_H,q_U,q_I$ are quantified; all four defining equations precede $q_U+q_I\le q_G+q_H$. |
| Answer to the question | <code>result</code> | Negation of that whole universal proposition. |</p>
<p>The source does not specify the ground set. The fixed-vertex edge-set
interpretation is fixed by #19958. Refuting its finite-exponent restriction
also refutes a submodularity claim whose domain includes these instances.
The induced-vertex interpretation has separate numerical evidence below.
Uniqueness of the exponent and monotonicity of all degree means are not
hypotheses of the Lean refutation.</p>
