ry><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Discrete structures</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2026</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Three-central edge extremality against all metrics</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A four-point ultrametric has more central edges than a three-central metric.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>The comparator is unrestricted: it need not be ultrametric.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>It refutes the published assertion under its unrestricted comparator class.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A four-point ultrametric has more central edges than a three-central metric.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="central-edges"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Three-central edge extremality against all metrics</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Dovgoshey and Rovenska, <em>On the Graph Corresponding to Center of Distances of
Metric Space</em>, arXiv:2610.11596v1, Conjecture 4.2, p. 13, assert an equivalence
for every finite ultrametric space $X$ with $|X|\ge4$ and three central distances.
The center is
$C(X)={t\ge0:\forall p\in X;\exists y\in X,\ d(y,p)=t}$.
The central graph joins distinct vertices whose distance belongs to $C(X)$;
the diametrical graph joins vertices at the diameter.</p>
<p>Clause (i) says that the diametrical graph is complete bipartite on a partition
$X=A\sqcup B$ with $\min(|A|,|B|)=2$, and that the central graph is its union
with one star on each part. Clause (ii) says that its central graph has at most
as many edges as every metric space $Y$ with $|Y|=|X|$ and $|C(Y)|=3$.
The comparator is unrestricted: it need not be ultrametric.</p>
