:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>n=6 is the largest n≥3 such that the set of non-standard n-sided dice admits a non-transitive cycle of maximum length n with uniform and minimal winning advantage.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The exact target is the conjunction $S(6)$ and $\forall n\in\mathbb N,\ n\ge7\Rightarrow\neg S(n)$.
For $n\ge3$, a die is a nondecreasing list of exactly $n$ labels in $[1,n]$, with repetitions permitted. The standard tuple is included, as in the source's Example 1 and its full graph enumeration. Each of the $n^2$ independent face-index pairs has equal weight. A strict win excludes ties from the numerator and retains them in the denominator.
Let $m_n$ be the least attainable strict-win probability greater than one half. An edge $d\to e$ means that the actual unconditional probability that $d$ beats $e$ equals $m_n$. A cycle is a nonempty list of pairwise distinct dice, with every successive edge and its closing edge. The proposition $S(n)$ says that a cycle of length $n$ exists and that no cycle of any length $k&gt;n$ exists. The theorem quantifies over all such $k$ and all $n\ge7$; it introduces no finite cutoff.</p>
