curves</em>, arXiv:2505.03967v1, Section 2, Problem 2.2:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Suppose the compact set $K$ is such that for some $n\in\mathbb N$, $T_{n,K}=\hat F_n$, where $\hat F_n$ is the $n$th monic Faber polynomial associated to the map $\phi$ that satisfies (1.2). Is it true then that $T_{n,L_r}=\hat F_n$ for all $r&gt;1$?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $\Omega$ is the unbounded component of $\mathbb C\setminus K$,
$g_\Omega(z,\infty)=\log|\phi(z)|$, and
$\phi(z)=cz+c_0+c_{-1}/z+\cdots$, with
$c=\operatorname{cap}(K)^{-1}$. When $\Omega$ is simply connected,
$\psi=\phi^{-1}$. The equipotential curve is
$L_r={z\in\Omega:|\phi(z)|=r}=\psi({|w|=r})$.
The polynomial $F_n$ is the polynomial part of $\phi^n$ at infinity,
$\hat F_n=c^{-n}F_n$, and $T_{n,E}$ is the unique monic degree-$n$
sup-norm minimizer on $E$.</p>
<p>The Lean <code>claim</code> quantifies over exterior maps $\psi$ differentiable
and injective on $|w|&gt;1$, a positive capacity $\kappa=1/c$, compact
complement $K$, and a bounded correction $\psi(w)-\kappa w$ for
$|w|&gt;2$. Its <code>IsMonicFaber</code> requires a monic degree-$n$ polynomial
$F$ with $F(\psi(w))-(\kappa w)^n\to0$ at infinity. Its
<code>IsChebyshev</code> compares every uniform real bound for every monic
degree-$n$ competitor. For compact nonempty sets this expresses
sup-norm minimization. The implication asks whether such an $F$
Chebyshev on $K$ remains Chebyshev on every $L_r$, $r&gt;1$.</p>
