printed page 12, states verbatim (line breaks joined):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 1. Suppose b ≥ 0, ν1 ≥ ν2 &gt; −1 and 0 &lt; a1 ≤ a2. Then the ratio x → Iν1(a1 x)/Iν2(a2 x) is unimodal on (0, ∞). Moreover, if ν1 ≥ ν2 &gt; 0 it is log-concave.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The modified Bessel function of the first kind has the standard series</p>
<p>$$
I_\nu(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{(z/2)^{2k+\nu}}{k!\Gamma(k+\nu+1)}.
$$</p>
<p>The encoded claim is</p>
<p>$$
\forall\nu_1\ge\nu_2&gt;0\ \forall,0&lt;a_1\le a_2,\qquad
x\longmapsto\log\frac{I_{\nu_1}(a_1x)}{I_{\nu_2}(a_2x)}
\quad\hbox{is concave on }(0,\infty).
$$</p>
<p>The source's $b\ge0$ is immaterial because $b$ does not occur in the ratio.
The unimodality assertion is outside this claim.</p>
