<p>Conjecture 18 (Unicode transcription, preserving the printed superscripts):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 18. There are only countably many infinite sets M ⊂ N with η(M) ≤ η⁰(M). For all other sets we have ηⁿ(M) → ∞.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Kreh writes <code>N</code> for the positive integers, writes <code>x ⊳ y</code> when the decimal
string of <code>x</code> is obtained from that of <code>y</code> by deleting zero or more digits,
and writes <code>S(M)</code> for the elements of <code>M</code> minimal under that order. The Lean
definition <code>digitSubseq a b</code> is <code>a ⊳ b</code>; <code>minimal</code>, <code>peel</code>, and <code>eta</code> are
Kreh's <code>S</code>, <code>δ</code>, and <code>η</code> constructions.</p>
<p>The literal refuted statement is the second sentence: for every infinite set
<code>M</code> of positive natural numbers with <code>eta M 0 &lt; eta M 1</code>, every natural bound
<code>B</code> eventually satisfies <code>B ≤ eta M k</code> at every later layer. This is exactly
<code>claim</code>, including the explicit positivity premise. Lean's <code>Set.ncard</code> is zero
on an infinite set, but that convention is never used in the refutation:
every displayed minimal layer is proved to be a singleton or a pair. That
theorem does not address the first sentence, concerning countably many
infinite sets with <code>η(M) ≤ η⁰(M)</code>. A companion result for that sentence is
described below; this dossier's resolution binding remains the divergence
theorem.</p>
