rtain success in a strict majority of repetitions of the qubit game is equivalent to certain single-game success.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="arunachalam-molina-russo-2013-hedging-majority-threshold"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Bob cannot perfectly hedge more than n/2 of n parallel games</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Arunachalam, A. Molina and V. Russo, <em>Quantum hedging in two-round prover-verifier interactions</em>, arXiv:1310.7954v5, TQC 2017, LIPIcs 73 (2018) 5:1–5:30, Section 5:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, it still remains open to determine the conditions under which Bob can always win at least $k$ out of $n$ repetitions for some $k &gt; 1$.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Based on our current numerical evidence, it is possible that Bob cannot perfectly hedge more than $k = n/2$ games.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The game, the shared state u_α = α|00⟩ + √(1 − α²)|11⟩, Alice's measurement {P₀,θ, P₁,θ} with P₁,θ = v_θ v_θ* and v_θ = cos θ|00⟩ + sin θ|11⟩, and the parallel repetition with one joint channel Φₙ are quoted in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/arunachalam2017quantumhedging.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/19590">#19590</a> fixes the reading. Bob perfectly hedges k games if some channel on his n registers makes Alice record at least k wins with certainty. For all n and k with n &lt; 2k ≤ 2n, all α ∈ (0, 1] and all θ, this is possible exactly when α² = cos²θ, that is, when a single game can already be won with certainty. The conjecture is proved.</p>
