 of the average mixing matrix and explains that it provides no useful bound for the instantaneous diagonal entries in the conference-graph case.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The lemma requires no square-order hypothesis; in the square case it attains the lower bound.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Conference walks have sharp square-order floors and nonsquare vanishing infima.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="godsil-2017-conference-graph-sedentariness"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Sharp return-probability bounds for conference graphs</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Chris Godsil, <em>Sedentary quantum walks</em>, arXiv:1710.11192v1 (2017; Linear Algebra and its Applications 614 (2021), 356–375), Section “Questions?”, TeX source <code>conemix.tex</code>, lines 1037–1038:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We do not know whether, for conference graphs, the diagonal entries of $M(t)$ can be
bounded away from zero.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In the Introduction, <code>conemix.tex</code>, lines 190–207, Godsil defines $U(t)=\exp(itA)$ for the adjacency matrix $A$ and $M(t)=U(t)\circ\overline{U(t)}$, where $\circ$ is the Schur product. Thus $M(t)<em>{u,u}=|U(t)</em>{u,u}|^2$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26302">#26302</a> fixes the reading as a sharp classification, distinguishing a positive bound for each fixed graph from a bound uniform across orders. Write $n=4m+1$, with $m\in\mathbb N$ and $m\ge1$. The module takes a finite vertex type $V$ with decidable equality and a simple graph $G$ with decidable adjacency satisfying <code>G.IsSRGWith (4 * m + 1) (2 * m) (m - 1) m</code>. This means that $|V|=n$, every vertex has degree $2m$, adjacent vertices have $m-1$ common neighbours, and distinct nonadjacent vertices have $m$ common neighbours. For $u\in V$, put $p_u(t)=|\exp(itA)_{u,u}|^2$.</p>
<p>The classification gives a sharp positive bound $1/n^2$ exactly at square orders; at nonsquare orders, return probabilities become arbitrarily small at positive times. Its precise quantified statement is given under “Proved by <code>result</code>”.</p>
