l randomness and value complementarity in its study of mutually unbiased observables. conditional randomness implies uniform attenuation for all finite quantum observables.  such stability bounds, and extensions to infinitely many outcomes or infinite-dimensional spaces, remain open here. released-dossier 3a5e538e2a953c3f4117e662de19aec6f668e7509e29f9dd6261dc1c04a43068 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2021</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Conditional randomness implies uniform attenuation without sharpness</strong><span class="result-excerpt">Conditional randomness implies uniform attenuation for all finite quantum observables.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Stan Gudder, Mutually Unbiased Quantum Observables (2021), arXiv:2110.03099v1, Section 2, asks:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source compares sequential measurement, conditional randomness and value complementarity in its study of mutually unbiased observables.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Conditional randomness implies uniform attenuation for all finite quantum observables.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Such stability bounds, and extensions to infinitely many outcomes or infinite-dimensional spaces, remain open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="gudder-2021-conditionally-random-uniform-attenuation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Conditional randomness implies uniform attenuation without sharpness</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Stan Gudder, <em>Mutually Unbiased Quantum Observables</em> (2021), arXiv:2110.03099v1, Section 2, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We do not know whether the converse of Theorem~\ref{thm21}(i) or (ii) holds.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the first sentence of line 223 of the downloaded v1 TeX, <code>MutualUnbiasedQO.tex</code> (576 lines, MD5 <code>ea23a0dd97764fa2b7444408b910866f</code>); the reference resolves to Theorem 2.1. Its first two parts, lines 186–187, read, with typographical commands expanded:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(i) Condition (1) implies Condition (2).</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>(ii) If $A$ and $B$ are sharp, then Condition (1) and Condition (2) are equivalent.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For finite observables $A=(A_x)<em>{x\in\Omega_A}$ and $B=(B_y)</em>{y\in\Omega_B}$, with $|\Omega_A|=m\ge1$ and $|\Omega_B|=n\ge1$, the source specifies at line 182:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Condition~(1) is shorthand for $A_x\circ B_y=\tfrac{1}{n},A_x$ and $B_y\circ A_x=\tfrac{1}{m}B_y$ for all $x\in\Omega _A$, $y\in\Omega _B$ and Condition~(2) is shorthand for $(B\mid A)_y=\tfrac{1}{n},I$ and $(A\mid B)_x=\tfrac{1}{m},I$ for all $x\in\Omega _A$, $y\in\Omega _B$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here $E\circ F=E^{1/2}FE^{1/2}$, using the positive square root, and $(B\mid A)_y=\sum_x A_x\circ B_y$ (Section 1, lines 97 and 117–120). Each observable consists of positive semidefinite effects summing to $I$. The source calls Condition (1) uniform attenuation and Condition (2) conditional randomness. The definitions and source locators are also in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/gudder2021mutuallyunbiasedobservables.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21653">#21653</a> fixes the statement: for every finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space and all finite observables with positive outcome counts, Condition (2) implies Condition (1), without assuming sharpness. This converse of Theorem 2.1(i) is proved. The separately worded, ambiguous question about a converse of part (ii) is outside the registered statement.</p>
