particle numbers question resolved quantum information eighteen-mode bosonic occupation assignments exist exactly for even particle numbers kurzyński, contextuality of identical particles, phys. the source distinguishes an obstruction to assigning occupations from contextuality witnessed only after imposing constraints from a particular quantum state. the eighteen-mode bosonic occupation model admits an assignment exactly for even particle numbers.  the separate possibility of other state-independent contextuality inequalities for two particles (line 113), and the questions for arbitrary dimensions or other mode sets (line 242), remain open in this settlement. released-dossier 71de12d79e5b6afff2fe3e66e31f8f276f5a21336b4a88381797906753e5a620 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Question resolved</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2016</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Eighteen-mode bosonic occupation assignments exist exactly for even particle numbers</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The eighteen-mode bosonic occupation model admits an assignment exactly for even particle numbers.</span></span><span class="result-outcome proved">Proved</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Kurzyński, Contextuality of identical particles, Phys.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source distinguishes an obstruction to assigning occupations from contextuality witnessed only after imposing constraints from a particular quantum state.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The eighteen-mode bosonic occupation model admits an assignment exactly for even particle numbers.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The separate possibility of other state-independent contextuality inequalities for two particles (line 113), and the questions for arbitrary dimensions or other mode sets (line 242), remain open in this settlement.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="kurzynski-2017-eighteen-mode-bosonic-assignment-parity"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Eighteen-mode bosonic occupation assignments exist exactly for even particle numbers</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>P. Kurzyński, <em>Contextuality of identical particles</em>, Phys. Rev. A 95(1), 012133 (2017), arXiv:1609.01250v1, asks in “Contextuality of $N&gt;2$ bosons” (<code>contextuality_fermions.tex</code>, line 233):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Hardy-like proof shows that there is the state-dependent contextuality in the 18-mode scenario for any $N&gt;0$. However, the lack of the state-independent contextuality was only shown for $N=2$. It is possible that for some $N&gt;2$ the bosonic occupation numbers of 18 modes cannot be assigned in a non-contextual way. This would require further research, since for large numbers of particles there are many ways in which the particles can be distributed between the modes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source's bosonic model assigns a nonnegative integer to each mode, independently of the context, with the occupations in every context summing to the particle number $N$ (lines 70–76, Eq. (1)). A mode $v_{ij}$ belongs to contexts $i$ and $j$ (line 83); the nine contexts are displayed in lines 132–221. The definitions and source locator are also recorded in <a href="../Library/Observer/kurzynski2017contextualityidentical.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>The formal definition <code>modes</code> is the set</p>
<p>$$
E={(1,2),(1,6),(1,7),(1,8),(2,3),(2,8),(2,9),(3,4),(3,7),
(3,9),(4,5),(4,7),(4,8),(5,6),(5,8),(5,9),(6,7),(6,9)}.
$$</p>
<p>For $i\in{1,\ldots,9}$, put $C_i={(a,b)\in E:a=i\text{ or }b=i}$. The definition <code>IsNoncontextualAssignment</code> requires one function $v:\mathbb N\times\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ satisfying $\sum_{p\in C_i}v(p)=N$ for every such $i$. Values outside $E$ are unrestricted and do not enter any context sum.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24135">#24135</a> fixes the statement <code>claim</code>: for every $N\in\mathbb N$, such an assignment exists if and only if $N$ is even. This classification is proved.</p>
