n symmetries.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Any two continuous invariant parity-odd real variables vanish together on a chiral four-particle event in each of the two event classes.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Restricting to a fixed-mass, equal-mass, massless or momentum-conserving subdomain can remove the displayed sphere; the minimum number on such a subdomain remains open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="lester-haddadin-gripaios-2022-chiral-event-parity-minimality"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Three continuous invariant parity-odd variables are necessary for two interchangeable pairs</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>C. G. Lester, W. Haddadin and B. Gripaios, <em>Lorentz and permutation invariants of particles III: constraining non-standard sources of parity violation</em>, Int. J. Mod. Phys. A 37(16) (2022) 2250093, arXiv:2008.05206v6, Introduction, <code>introduction.tex</code>, lines 103–104:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No claim is made that either set $S$  or $\hat S$ is globally \textbf{minimal} for the class of events with which it is concerned.  That is to say:  it has not been proved that there does not exist some other set $S_?$ containing only one or two event variables but which retains all the  stated properties.
Nonetheless, we \textit{conjecture} that there is indeed  no smaller set $S_?$ and that $S$ and $\hat S$ are therefore examples of \textbf{minimal} sets.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The quotation is from the downloaded v6 TeX member <code>introduction.tex</code> (md5 <code>2b21a1456fcb7bb5df295f300f8b61ae</code>). The definitions and source locators are collected in <a href="../Library/Geometry/lesterhaddadingripaios2022parity.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>An event is an ordered quadruple $(p,q,a,b)$ of real four-vectors. Write $u\cdot v=u_0v_0-u_1v_1-u_2v_2-u_3v_3$ and $u^2=u\cdot u$. The forward cone consists of vectors with $u_0\ge0$ and $u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2\le u_0^2$, including zero. The two domains are:</p>
<ul>
<li>$\Omega_{(pq)(ab)X}$: all quadruples of forward-cone vectors (<code>allEvents</code>);</li>
<li>$\Omega_{(pq)\to(ab)X}$: those quadruples additionally satisfying $p^2q^2&lt;(p\cdot q)^2$ (<code>collisionEvents</code>).</li>
</ul>
<p>The symmetry uses a common matrix $\Lambda$ satisfying $\Lambda^{\mathsf T}\eta\Lambda=\eta$, $\det\Lambda=1$ and $\Lambda_{00}&gt;0$, where $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$, together with independent permutations of $(p,q)$ and $(a,b)$. Parity $\mathscr P$ fixes each energy and negates each spatial momentum. An event is chiral when no such symmetry carries it to its parity image. These match <code>prelims.tex</code>, lines 25–27, 190–193, 228–243, 265–268, 433–443 and 656–666; neither domain adds momentum conservation or fixed masses.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25338">#25338</a> fixes the assertion: for either domain and every pair $f_1,f_2:\mathrm{event}\to\mathbb R$ continuous on that domain, invariant under these symmetries there, and parity odd there, some chiral event in the domain satisfies $f_1=f_2=0$. This assertion is proved.</p>
