<em>Genuinely quantum SudoQ and its cardinality</em>, arXiv:2106.02967v2,
Section III.A, page 4, state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Conjecture.</strong> Similarly to the classical Sudoku there are no $4\times4$ SudoQ with 3 clues that are uniquely solvable.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>G. Rajchel-Mieldzioć, <em>Quantum mappings and designs</em>, arXiv:2204.13008,
Chapter 7, restates it:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Uniquely solvable grids of $4 \times 4$ SudoQ admit at least 4 clues.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause | Formal counterpart | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A quantum Latin square has orthonormal rows and columns. | <code>SudoQClueGeometry.IsSudoQ</code> | Four vectors in $\mathbb C^4$ in each row and column. |
| Every disjoint block is an orthonormal basis. | <code>IsSudoQ</code> and <code>blockCell</code> | Four $2\times2$ blocks; zero-based coordinates $(2b_1+k_1,2b_2+k_2)$. |
| A quantum grid has blank entries; its clues are the nonblank entries. | <code>C</code> and <code>v</code> in <code>IsSolution</code> | A finite set of cells with literal prescribed vectors; values outside <code>C</code> impose no constraint. |
| Solvability includes existence of a completion. | <code>UniquelySolvable</code> | Its existential quantifier supplies an <code>IsSolution</code>. |
| Solutions agree up to phase. | <code>SameRays</code> | A separate complex scalar of norm one for each entry. Clues remain literal vector equalities. |
| No uniquely solvable grid with three clues. | <code>SudoQMinimumClues.claim</code> and <code>result</code> | At most three clues, hence also exactly three; arbitrary clue values, including unsolvable grids. |</p>
<p><code>RayEq</code> and <code>HasRepeatedRay</code> are auxiliary predicates for a pair of vectors
and for one ray occurring once in every unit. They add no hypothesis to the
settled claim. The four-dimensional specialization is the source's $N=2$
SudoQ, not the $N=4$ Sudoku parameter.</p>
