ion of this particular inequality can have a pure-preparation realization, even when finite dimension and the two POVMs are unrestricted.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Positivity and purity are not hypotheses of this lemma.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Pure preparations satisfying both trit parity equivalences have HTMB success probability at most two thirds in every finite complex dimension.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>The tight mixed-state maximum, explicitly left open at line 509, remains unresolved by this theorem. - Neighbouring questions.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="tavakoli-zambrini-cruzeiro-uola-abbott-2021-pure-state-htmb"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status proved">Proved in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Pure preparations cannot violate the HTMB inequality in finite dimension</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Tavakoli, E. Zambrini Cruzeiro, R. Uola and A. A. Abbott, <em>Bounding and simulating contextual correlations in quantum theory</em>, PRX Quantum 2, 020334 (2021), arXiv:2010.04751v3, subsection “Mixed states as resources for quantum contextuality”, state:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This gives us strong evidence (i.e., up to numerical precision) that pure states cannot violate the HTMB inequality~\eqref{eq:HTMB}, and we conjecture this to indeed be the case exactly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The quotation is from line 506 of the e-print member <code>Manuscript_infocontext_reresubmission.tex</code>, md5 <code>f767464510150f9e02bc504393cf40e8</code>. The scenario and Eq. (33) are at lines 492–500; the numerical pure-state bound is at lines 502–507. See also <a href="../Library/Observer/tavakolizambrinicruzeirouolaabbott2021contextual.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Alice receives two trits $x=(x_1,x_2)\in\mathbb Z_3^2$, and Bob guesses either coordinate using one of two three-outcome measurements. Write $P_x=\operatorname{projector}(\psi_x)=|\psi_x\rangle\langle\psi_x|$. Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25326">#25326</a> fixes the finite-dimensional reading: for every $d\in\mathbb N$, every family of unit vectors $\psi_x\in\mathbb C^d$ and every pair of POVMs $(M_b)<em>{b\in\mathbb Z_3}$ and $(N_b)</em>{b\in\mathbb Z_3}$, assume</p>
<p>$$
\sum_{x:,x_1+a x_2=k}P_x=\sum_{x:,x_1+a x_2=0}P_x
\qquad(a\in{1,2},\ k\in\mathbb Z_3).
$$</p>
<p>Each POVM has positive semidefinite effects summing to the identity. Then the module's <code>htmbValue</code> satisfies</p>
<p>$$
\operatorname{htmbValue}(\psi,M,N)
=\frac1{18}\sum_{x\in\mathbb Z_3^2}
\left(\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}(P_xM_{x_1})+
\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}(P_xN_{x_2})\right)
\le\frac23.
$$</p>
<p>This is <code>claim</code>, and <code>result : claim</code> proves it.</p>
