<blockquote>
<p>It is still unclear whether or not the walk has a limit probability distribution for a general localized initial state.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Thus we conjecture that, for a general localized initial state, the QBN-based walk might still have the same limit probability distribution as the classical random walk.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source's coin space $h$ has orthonormal basis ${Z_\sigma:\sigma\in\Gamma}$, where $\Gamma$ consists of the finite subsets of $\mathbb N$ (Section 2, Eq. (7), p. 2). Its annihilation and creation operators remove or add a coordinate when permitted, and otherwise give zero (Lemmas 1–2, Eqs. (8)–(9)). Lemma 5 and Definition 6, p. 3, specify</p>
<p>$$
R_k=\tfrac12(\partial_k^<em>+\partial_k+I),\qquad
L_k=\tfrac12(\partial_k^</em>+\partial_k-I),\qquad
\Phi_{n+1}(x)=R_n\Phi_n(x-1)+L_n\Phi_n(x+1).
$$</p>
<p>A localized initial state is $\Phi_0(0)=\varphi$, $\Phi_0(x)=0$ for $x\ne0$, with $|\varphi|=1$ (Eq. (53), p. 6); the position probabilities are $\Pr(X_n=x)=|\Phi_n(x)|^2$ (Section 3, p. 3).</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22700">#22700</a> fixes the reading: for every unit vector $\varphi\in h$, the law of $X_n/\sqrt n$ converges weakly to $N(0,1)$. This is proved in the coefficient-space model below. The source definitions and their locators are also described in <a href="../Library/Asymptotics/wangwangtangren2018bernoulliwalk.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
