surement in the bases of the compound.</p><p><span>The idea</span>For x, z ∈ {0, …, 7}, let (D(x, z)u)(b) = (−1)^{z·(b ⊕ x)} u(b ⊕ x) on ℂ⁸, with z·y the parity of the bitwise AND.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Eight orthonormal fiducials generate a SIC-compound in complex dimension eight under the three-qubit Pauli action.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="tavakoli-bengtsson-gisin-renes-2020-hoggar-sic-compound-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A SIC-compound in dimension 8 from Hoggar SICs</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Tavakoli, I. Bengtsson, N. Gisin and J. M. Renes, <em>Compounds of symmetric informationally complete measurements and their application in quantum key distribution</em> (Phys. Rev. Research 2, 043122 (2020); arXiv:2007.01007), define a SIC-compound: d³ unit vectors ψ_{jk} in ℂ^d, j ∈ [d²], k ∈ [d], such that</p>
<ul>
<li>for every k the vectors ψ_{jk} form a SIC, |⟨ψ_{jk}, ψ_{lk}⟩|² = (dδ_{jl} + 1)/(d + 1);</li>
<li>for every j the vectors ψ_{jk} form an orthonormal basis.</li>
</ul>
<p>They construct compounds for d = 2 and d = 4 and prove that none exists for d = 3.</p>
<ul>
<li>For d = 5, 6, 7, 8 they enumerate the Weyl–Heisenberg fiducials and find no compound. The introduction states: &quot;for d = 5, 6, 7, 8 we give evidence in support of the same conclusion.&quot;</li>
<li>For d = 8 they add: &quot;dimension 8 also houses SICs that are not based on the WH group … Whether a SIC-compound can be formed from these exceptional SICs is left as an open question.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/tavakolibengtssongisinrenes2020siccompounds.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18207">#18207</a> reads the exceptional SICs as the Hoggar SICs: the orbits of a fiducial vector under the three-qubit Pauli group. Its claim is the negative answer to the question. The claim is refuted, which answers the question in the positive: a SIC-compound in dimension 8 is formed from eight Hoggar SICs.</p>
