<p>The procedure is specified at lines 151–156: use the three-state bounds through each common neighbour of an unmeasured pair, intersect the inferred intervals, add the edge, and continue. Line 165 also allows revisiting inferred edges. The source's definitions and bounds are at lines 97–131; see <a href="../Library/Observer/galvaobrod2020overlaps.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23699">#23699</a> fixes the reading to graphs with exactly one unknown pair. Write
$U(x,y)=(\sqrt{xy}+\sqrt{(1-x)(1-y)})^2$, and let $L(x,y)=(\sqrt{xy}-\sqrt{(1-x)(1-y)})^2$ when $x+y&gt;1$, and $L(x,y)=0$ otherwise. These are the module's <code>upper</code> and <code>lower</code>.</p>
<p>For $m$ labelled states, distinct indices $i_0,j_0$, and an array $r$, a realization is a family of unit vectors $\psi_i\in\mathbb C^n$ satisfying $|\langle\psi_i,\psi_j\rangle|^2=r_{ij}$ for every ordered pair of distinct indices except the unordered pair ${i_0,j_0}$. Diagonal entries impose no conditions. The set $F$ of feasible unknown overlaps ranges over every finite dimension $n$. The procedure's endpoints are
$\ell=\sup{L(r_{ki_0},r_{kj_0}):k\ne i_0,j_0}$ and $u=\inf{U(r_{ki_0},r_{kj_0}):k\ne i_0,j_0}$, encoded by <code>procLower</code> and <code>procUpper</code>.</p>
<p>The proposition <code>claim</code> asserts that, for every $m\ge3$, every distinct $i_0,j_0$, and every $r$ with $F\ne\varnothing$, both $\ell=\inf F$ and $u=\sup F$. This universal tightness statement is refuted.</p>
