h r∗ ≺ q for every q in the ball (Eq. (3.1)).</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>A majorization-minimizer of a ball maximizes every Schur-concave function over the ball at once, which gives universal local continuity bounds for entropies.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Let J(p, q) = (1/2) Σᵢ [pᵢ ln(2pᵢ/(pᵢ + qᵢ)) + qᵢ ln(2qᵢ/(pᵢ + qᵢ))].</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A three-point Jensen-Shannon ball has no majorization-minimizer.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="hanson-2020-jensen-shannon-ball-majorization-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>Square-root Jensen–Shannon balls need not admit majorization-minimizers</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>E. P. Hanson, <em>Entropic Continuity Bounds &amp; Eventually Entanglement-Breaking Channels</em> (PhD thesis, Cambridge, 2020; arXiv:2010.02408), derives continuity bounds for Schur-concave functions from the majorization structure of total-variation balls.</p>
<ul>
<li>Every total-variation ball in the probability simplex contains a majorization-minimizer r∗, with r∗ ≺ q for every q in the ball (Eq. (3.1)). Every Schur-concave function, such as an entropy, attains its maximum over the ball at r∗.</li>
<li>For p-norm balls with 1 &lt; p &lt; ∞ no such element exists (Section 3.7).</li>
<li>Chapter 8, Question 1 asks: &quot;Do balls in Hellinger distance, Bures distance, the (square-root) Shannon-Jensen divergence, Wasserstein distances, or other metrics on P admit majorization-minimizers?&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/Divergence/hanson2020entropiccontinuity.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18189">#18189</a> fixes the reading of the Jensen–Shannon clause: for every probability vector p and every positive radius, the square-root Jensen–Shannon ball around p has a majorization-minimizer. This clause is answered in the negative.</p>
