rresponding product norm bound. a unital 2-positive complex-linear map violates lin&#x27;s operator wielandt product norm bound.   released-dossier 6f36cf0b8dd9b0b33e9e863545ad325527110d28a8f473a52468226c583d04da "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Conjecture refuted</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2012</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Lin&#x27;s operator Wielandt norm conjecture fails</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A unital 2-positive complex-linear map violates Lin&#x27;s operator Wielandt product norm bound.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Minghua Lin, On an operator Kantorovich inequality for positive linear maps, J.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The order inequality in Theorem 3.1 suggests a corresponding product norm bound.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A unital 2-positive complex-linear map violates Lin&#x27;s operator Wielandt product norm bound.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="lin-2013-operator-wielandt-norm-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Lin&#x27;s operator Wielandt norm conjecture fails</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Minghua Lin, <em>On an operator Kantorovich inequality for positive linear maps</em>, J. Math. Anal. Appl. 402 (2013), 127–132; arXiv:1212.5690v1, §3, pp. 6–7.</p>
<p>§3, p. 6, Theorem 3.1 states verbatim:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Theorem 3.1. [3] Let 0 &lt; m ≤ A ≤ M and let X, Y be two partial isometries on H whose final spaces are orthogonal to each other. Then for every 2-positive linear map Φ, Φ(X<em>AY)Φ(Y</em>AY)⁻¹Φ(Y<em>AX) ≤ ((M−m)/(M+m))² Φ(X</em>AX).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>§3, pp. 6–7, immediately before Conjecture 3.4:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Despite the invalidity of (3.6), there is some evidence that the following assertion could be true. We have been unable to prove (or disprove) it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>§3, p. 7, Conjecture 3.4 states verbatim:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Conjecture 3.4. Under the same condition as in Theorem 3.1. Then for every 2-positive linear map Φ, ‖Φ(X<em>AY)Φ(Y</em>AY)⁻¹Φ(Y<em>AX)Φ(X</em>AX)⁻¹‖ ≤ ((M−m)/(M+m))².</p>
</blockquote>
<p>§3, p. 6, defines 2-positivity verbatim:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let Φ be a unital positive linear map between C*-algebras. We say that Φ is 2-positive if whenever the 2 × 2 operator matrix [[A, B],[B*, C]] is positive, then so is [[Φ(A), Φ(B)],[Φ(B*), Φ(C)]].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>P. Zhang, <em>Remarks on an operator Wielandt inequality</em>, arXiv:1506.00737, describes Conjecture 3.4 as still open.</p>
