easurements</em>, Phys. Rev. A 104, 022206 (2021), arXiv:2101.05752v4, Section III.A asks whether the broadcasting channels of an observable have a highest element and whether they have a lowest element under precomposition by channels that are nondisturbing for that observable. The source repeats both questions in its Conclusions.</p>
<p>The formal convention uses an observable with outcomes <code>Fin m</code>, input matrices on <code>Fin d</code>, output matrices on <code>Fin d × Fin d</code>, finite Kraus channels, the broadcasting predicate, the nondisturbing predicate, and the precomposition relation. The settling declaration <code>D5/S3/Quantum/Measurements/BroadcastingChannelExtremaRefutation.result</code> has type <code>¬ claim</code> and carries <code>OpenProblemResolutionClaim(Refuted)</code>.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>The source's broadcasting condition is the pair of marginal trace equalities in <code>Broadcasts</code>; its nondisturbance condition is equality of each observable effect's trace after channel application in <code>Nondisturbing</code>; <code>ConstructedFrom</code> is precomposition by such a channel. <code>HasHighest</code> and <code>HasLowest</code> quantify over broadcasting channels and this relation. <code>claim</code> combines observability, existence of a broadcasting channel, and the disjunction of the two extrema. The fixed witness uses <code>d = 3</code>, <code>m = 2</code>, and the observable <code>A</code>; the remaining binders and hypotheses are those displayed by the corresponding Scribe nodes.</p>
