re" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Guha, Lower bounds on bipartite entanglement in noisy graph states, arXiv:2404.09014v2; Quantum 10, 2111 (2026), §5.4, “The subsystem with more qubits has higher coherent information?” (main.tex, line 951):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source uses coherent information as a lower bound on distillable entanglement.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Here the graph is exactly bipartiteGraph N, with no edges within either part.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A uniformly dephased graph state on five plus four qubits has greater coherent information on its smaller subsystem.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>It does not settle a universal rank-two ordering, the separate higher-rank robustness conjecture in §5.3, or the extensions to other noise models raised in the conclusion.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="sajjad-kaur-goodenough-towsley-guha-2026-coherent-information-ordering-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Subsystem size does not order coherent information in noisy graph states</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Sajjad, E. Kaur, K. Goodenough, D. Towsley and S. Guha, <em>Lower bounds on bipartite entanglement in noisy graph states</em>, arXiv:2404.09014v2; Quantum 10, 2111 (2026), §5.4, “The subsystem with more qubits has higher coherent information?” (<code>main.tex</code>, line 951):</p>
