disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Hillery, Optimizing Initial State of Detector Sensors in Quantum Sensor Networks, ACM Transactions on Quantum Computing 5(2), Article 10 (2024), DOI 10.1145/3655028, arXiv:2306.17401v6, Section 5, Conjecture 2 (optimal.tex, lines 131–149, label conj:avg):</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The source uses Conjecture 2 as a route to Conjecture 1, its proposed optimal Dicke probe.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Two optimal three-sensor probes refute strict improvement by coefficient-square averaging.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>A strict statement restricted to nonoptimal probes, or requiring different fired-state Gram matrices, remains open here. - A weak replacement.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="zhan-gupta-hillery-2024-detector-sensor-strict-averaging-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Strict averaging need not improve detector-sensor probes</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>C. Zhan, H. Gupta and M. Hillery, <em>Optimizing Initial State of Detector Sensors in Quantum Sensor Networks</em>, ACM Transactions on Quantum Computing 5(2), Article 10 (2024), DOI 10.1145/3655028, arXiv:2306.17401v6, Section 5, Conjecture 2 (<code>optimal.tex</code>, lines 131–149, label <code>conj:avg</code>):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>For a given $U$, consider two initial states $\ket{\psi} = \sum\limits_j \psi_j \ket{j}$ and $\ket{\psi'} = \sum\limits_j \psi'_j \ket{j}$ such that (i) they are two unequal coefficient-squares, i.e., for some $j$, $|\psi_j|^2 \neq |\psi'<em>j|^2$, and (ii) they have the same objective function value, i.e., $P(\ket{\psi}, U) = P(\ket{\psi'}, U)$. We claim that the ``average'' state given by $\ket{\psi</em>{avg}} = \sum_j \sqrt{\frac{|\psi_j|^2 + |\psi'<em>j|^2}{2}}  \ket{j}$ has a lower objective function value, i.e., $P(\ket{\psi</em>{avg}}, U) &lt; P(\ket{\psi'}, U)$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The quotation is from the v6 source TeX. The <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/zhanguptahillery2024detectorsensor.md">Library note</a> records the paper and its encoding. In the source's setting (<code>problem-related.tex</code>, lines 17–47), exactly one of $n$ qubit sensors fires, with equal priors $1/n$; its unitary has eigenvalues $e^{\pm i\theta}$. The objective is the minimum error of a global POVM discriminating the $n$ possible fired states.</p>
<p>Write $X_n={0,1}^{\mathrm{Fin},n}$, with $1$ representing the plus eigenvector. A probe is an amplitude function $\psi:X_n\to\mathbb C$, normalized by $\sum_x|\psi(x)|^2=1$. For $n\ge1$, the reused <code>fire</code> and <code>isoSuccess</code> give $\phi_i(x)=e^{i\theta(2x_i-1)}\psi(x)$ and $S_\theta(\psi,M)=n^{-1}\sum_i\operatorname{Re}\langle\phi_i,M_i\phi_i\rangle$. The module defines <code>optSuccess</code> as the real indexed supremum of $S_\theta(\psi,M)$ over <code>LocalPOVM (Fin n → Bool) (Fin n)</code>, <code>errorProb</code> as $P_\theta(\psi)=1-\operatorname{optSuccess}_\theta(\psi)$, and <code>average</code> as $a(\psi,\psi')(x)=\sqrt{(|\psi(x)|^2+|\psi'(x)|^2)/2}$, included in $\mathbb C$ with zero imaginary part.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/26032">#26032</a> fixes <code>claim</code>: for every natural $n$, every $0&lt;\theta&lt;\pi$, and every pair of normalized complex probes, unequal coefficient-squares somewhere and equal $P_\theta$ imply $P_\theta(a(\psi,\psi'))&lt;P_\theta(\psi')$. Radians replace the source's degrees. This universal strict statement is refuted at $n=3$.</p>
