 nonzero coefficient at one, giving the contradiction.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The literal exact-degree requirement in Cigler&#x27;s v2 Conjecture 16 fails at k=1 and m=0.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="cigler-sixteen-literal-numerator-degree"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The literal numerator degree in Cigler&#x27;s Conjecture 16</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Johann Cigler, <em>Some experimental observations about Hankel determinants of
convolution powers of Catalan numbers</em>, arXiv:2308.07642v2, Section 3,
Conjecture 16, equation (30), printed page 10, asserts the following whole
conjunction. For every integer $k\ge1$ and every integer $m\ge1-k$, there is
an actual polynomial $Q=Q_{2k-1,m}$ such that</p>
<p>$$
\left(1+(-1)^k x^{2k-1}\right)^{\binom{k+m}{2}+1}
\sum_{N\ge0}D_{2k-1,m}(N)x^N=Q(x),
\qquad \deg Q=\pi_{2k+1}(m+k-1)+k.
$$</p>
<p>Here $C(x)=\sum_{n\ge0}C_nx^n$, where $C_n=\binom{2n}{n}/(n+1)$ is the
Catalan number; $C_{r,t}=[x^t]C(x)^r$ for $t\ge0$, and $C_{r,t}=0$ for
$t&lt;0$. The actual determinant is</p>
<p>$$
D_{r,m}(N)=\det(C_{r,i+j+m})<em>{0\le i,j&lt;N},\qquad D</em>{r,m}(0)=1,
\qquad \pi_j(t)=\frac{t((j-2)t+(j-4))}{2}.
$$</p>
<p>Every $Q$ is either palindromic or skew palindromic: for its actual degree
$d$, $x^dQ(1/x)=Q(x)$ or $x^dQ(1/x)=-Q(x)$. Palindromicity is required
if $k\equiv1\pmod4$ and $m$ is even, if $k\equiv3\pmod4$ and $m$ is odd,
and if $k\equiv0,2\pmod4$ and $m\equiv0,1\pmod4$. These implications
include no converses. The generating identity is coefficientwise.</p>
<p>The source does not specify a coefficient ring. The formal claim uses complex
polynomials and series. Its exact-degree clause uses an actual natural degree
$d$ with $Q.\mathrm{degree}=d$, then equates $d$ to the source's expression
in exact rational arithmetic. It therefore excludes the zero polynomial and
does not assign a nominal or padded degree. The reciprocity equations are
stated directly at every nonzero complex argument with that actual degree.</p>
