<h3>Formal reading</h3>
<p>All local Lean names have prefix
<code>D5.S3.Entropy.GohGlibichukKonyagin.Result</code>.
Here $p$ is prime, $\mathbf F_p$ is <code>ZMod p</code>, $\delta_0$ is the point mass at zero,
and $U_p$ assigns mass $1/p$ to each field element. Outside the source quotation,
$H$ denotes Shannon entropy in nats and $H_{\mathrm{bits}}=H/\log 2$;
<code>shannonEntropy</code> is reused from <code>D5.S3.Entropy.MaxEntropy</code>.</p>
<p>A law $\mu:\mathbf F_p\to\mathbb R$ satisfies
$\mu(a)\ge0$ and $\sum_a\mu(a)=1$.
<code>sumLaw k μ</code> is the pushforward of the product weights
$\prod_{i\in\operatorname{Fin}k}\mu(x_i)\mu(x'_i)$,
indexed by $(x,x'):(\operatorname{Fin}k\to\mathbf F_p)^2$,
under $(x,x')\mapsto\sum_i x_i x'_i$.
Here $\operatorname{Fin}k={0,\ldots,k-1}$.
For the convolution computation, <code>iter_product</code> uses tuples of pairs
$v:\operatorname{Fin}k\to\mathbf F_p\times\mathbf F_p$.
The standard equivalence <code>Equiv.arrowProdEquivProdArrow</code> sends
$v$ to its two projection functions; its inverse sends $(x,x')$ to
$i\mapsto(x_i,x'_i)$. In <code>sum_spike</code>, <code>Equiv.sum_comp</code> proves
that this change of indexing preserves the entire pushforward sum.
Thus $Z=\sum_iX_iX'_i$ has exactly this law for the $2k$ independent copies.
<code>claim</code> retains the existential $k\ge3$, all primes $p$, all such laws,
and the constant $1$ in bits. <code>result</code> states exactly <code>¬ claim</code>.</p>
