<p>Though $D_2(\Lambda)=D_2'(\Lambda)$ in this case, we conjecture this equality may fail in certain cases. But we have not found the counterexample satisfying $D_2(\Lambda)&gt;D_2'(\Lambda)$ yet.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For a two-state qubit ensemble $\mathcal E={(p,\rho),(1-p,\sigma)}$, let $p\in[0,1]$ and let $\rho,\sigma$ be positive semidefinite complex two-by-two matrices of trace one. Equation (1), on page 2, specializes to</p>
<p>$$Q(\mathcal E)=-\sum_{i,j=1}^2p_ip_j\operatorname{tr}[\rho_i,\rho_j]^2.$$</p>
<p>In the module, <code>quantumness</code> takes the real part of each trace. With all suprema below over these two-state ensembles, its definitions are</p>
<p>$$Q_{\max,2}=\sup_{\mathcal E}Q(\mathcal E),\qquad D_2(\Lambda)=\sup_{\mathcal E}\bigl(Q(\mathcal E)-Q(\Lambda(\mathcal E))\bigr),$$</p>
<p>$$D_2'(\Lambda)=\sup_{\mathcal E:,Q(\mathcal E)=Q_{\max,2}}\bigl(Q(\mathcal E)-Q(\Lambda(\mathcal E))\bigr).$$</p>
<p>This is the two-ingredient reading used in the source's page-5 calculation, where the maximal initial quantumness is $1/4$; it does not optimize over ensembles of arbitrary size. The definitions and source locations are also given in <a href="../Library/Entropy/songli2022dequantumness.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22778">#22778</a> fixes <code>claim</code> as universal equality: every completely positive trace-preserving complex-linear qubit map satisfies $D_2=D_2'$. The resolution <code>refuted</code> negates that equality claim and supplies the strict separation sought by the authors; it confirms their quoted conjecture.</p>
