<blockquote>
<p>Let $A,B,C\in S_p$. Then for $2\le p&lt;\infty$,</p>
<p>$|A+B+C|_p^p+|A|_p^p+|B|_p^p+|C|_p^p\le\frac{3^{p-1}+1}{2^p}\left(|A+B|_p^p+|B+C|_p^p+|C+A|_p^p\right).$</p>
<p>For $0&lt;p\le2$, the inequality is reversed. Moreover, the constant $\frac{3^{p-1}+1}{2^p}$ is optimal: equality holds for all $p&gt;0$ by taking $A=B=C\neq0$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This quotation is from lines 574–580 of the downloaded TeX member <code>v1_Clarkson_McCarthy_type_inequalities_part_I.tex</code>, MD5 <code>3ba34aa0913b51484593b3594a9f69d6</code>; only mathematical display layout and whitespace are normalized. The conjecture is introduced by “numerical evidence suggests the following sharp inequality” (line 571). Zhang restates it in <em>On five questions of Bourin and Lee</em> (2026), arXiv:2602.01463v2, TeX member <code>On_five_questions_of_Bourin_and_Lee.tex</code>, lines 411–419, MD5 <code>b3e91436b76494a53687b26bf5b2d06e</code>.</p>
<p>For a finite complex matrix and $p&gt;0$, write $|X|_p^p=\sum_j s_j(X)^p$. Abbreviate the four-term expression on the left by $L_p(A,B,C)$, the sum of the three pairwise terms by $P_p(A,B,C)$, and the proposed coefficient by $c(p)=(3^{p-1}+1)/2^p$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22304">#22304</a> fixes the reading settled by <code>claim</code>: for every real $p\ge2$, every natural dimension $n$, and every $A,B,C\in M_n(\mathbb C)$, $L_p(A,B,C)\le c(p)P_p(A,B,C)$. This universal statement is refuted. The reverse clause for $0&lt;p\le2$ is outside <code>claim</code>.</p>
<p>The source and norm definition are documented in <a href="../Library/QuadraticForms/zhang2024clarksonmccarthy.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
