| “Remove a color from Ip(S) and assign it to vj.” | <code>AdmissiblePick</code> and <code>assign</code>. | Every choice from a nonempty integer pool is allowed; insertion and load increment are explicit. |
| “let j ∈ S be the index for which w(j) is minimum (if more than one j with minimum w, then take the smallest index)” | <code>best</code>. | The minimum-load vertices are sorted by index and the first is selected. |
| “If wi ≥ b for each i or i = n + 1, then Stop.” | <code>stopped</code>, <code>runPool</code> and the terminal case of <code>runSupports</code>. | Global stopping is rechecked at each color assignment; exhausting the finite support order implements the terminal step. |
| “Goto Step i until Ip(S) is empty.” | <code>runPool</code>. | The pool loses one chosen color per assignment; initial fuel is its cardinality. |
| “Color every proper intersections of sets of size 1: vi gets |Ip({i})| colors. Thus wi = |Ip({i})|.” | <code>initial</code>. | Singleton atoms determine both the initial outputs and loads. |
| “produces an (L, b)-coloring if K_n is (L, b)-colorable” | <code>colorSym</code>, <code>claim</code> and <code>result</code>. | The claim asks for some admissible order and pick whose output contains a valid $b$-coloring; <code>result</code> negates it. |</p>
<p>The existential choice of order and pick and the allowance to trim an output
to $b$ colors give the algorithm the weakest favorable reading. Every
admissible execution fails on the witness, so this allowance does not rescue
the conjecture. The fuel parameter, option for an empty support, product
state and shifted indices implement the algorithm; they impose no additional
hypothesis on the public claim. There is no separation bound in the conjecture.</p>
