asurement has time-dependent two-classical dephasing statistics without mutual unbiasedness.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="lonigro-chruscinski-2022-dephasing-two-classicality-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A block measurement refutes mutual-unbiasedness necessity for two-classical dephasing</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. Lonigro and D. Chruściński, <em>On the classicality of quantum dephasing processes</em>, arXiv:2211.02014v2; Frontiers in Quantum Science and Technology 1, 1090022 (2022), Section 3.2, immediately following Proposition <code>prop:2classicality_mubs</code>. The v2 TeX source (<code>dephasing-v2.tex</code>, md5 <code>e2904085775dc9d0af3b2dba4d50c065</code>), line 347, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It may be conjectured that this is the only measurement choice yielding a genuinely time-dependent $2$-classical process---excepting the particular case in which $\rho_{t_0}$ is maximally mixed, cf.~the discussion in the next subsection.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The antecedent is the phase-modified Fourier measurement basis of Eq. <code>eq:mubs</code>, whose squared overlaps with every dephasing basis vector are $1/d$ (TeX lines 284–289). Proposition <code>prop:2classicality_mubs</code> assumes an initial state diagonal in the dephasing basis and gives two-classicality for that measurement family, for arbitrary bath Hamiltonians and bath state (lines 326–347). The definitions and source locators are also in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/lonigrochruscinski2022dephasing.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21441">#21441</a> fixes the following necessity statement, encoded by <code>claim</code>. For every $d,m\in\mathbb N$, every family of Hermitian bath matrices $H_j$ on $\mathbb C^m$, positive semidefinite unit-trace matrices $\sigma$ and $\rho$ on the bath and system respectively, unitary measurement matrix $V$, and initial time $t_0\in\mathbb R$, assume:</p>
<ul>
<li>$\rho$ is diagonal in the dephasing basis and $\rho\ne I_d/d$;</li>
<li>some rank-one measurement projector $P_x=|m_x\rangle\langle m_x|$, with $m_x$ the $x$th column of $V$, does not commute with some dephasing projector $E_j=|e_j\rangle\langle e_j|$;</li>
<li>for every $t_0\le t_1\le t_2$, the one-time law is normalized and both marginals of the two-time law equal the corresponding one-time law;</li>
<li>for some fixed outcomes $x,y$, two ordered time pairs at or after $t_0$ give different two-time values.</li>
</ul>
<p>The conclusion is $|V_{jx}|^2=1/d$ for every $j,x$. The laws here are the literal joint-system trace formula of the paper's Eq. (2), with Hamiltonian $\mathbf H=\sum_jE_j\otimes H_j$, propagator $e^{-i(t-s)\mathbf H}$, initial product $\rho\otimes\sigma$, and system Lüders operations $X\mapsto(P_x\otimes I_m)X(P_x\otimes I_m)$. There is no bath reset. The definitions <code>prob1</code> and <code>prob2</code> take values in $\mathbb C$; <code>twoClassical</code> imposes the stated normalization and marginal equalities. No positive lower bound on $d$ or $m$ is added to <code>claim</code>.</p>
<p>This is a consequence of the conjectured necessity: it restricts to finite baths and time-independent Hamiltonians, asks only for unbiasedness rather than membership in the named Fourier family, and additionally requires actual nonconstancy of the two-time law. It is refuted.</p>
