emory implementation exists whose Gram matrix is not classical.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="salazar-shahbeigi-2025-passive-memory-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A passive incoherent memory realizes a non-classical dephasing superchannel in dimension 3</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>R. Salazar and F. Shahbeigi, <em>Dephasing noise simulation for coherence-generating devices</em>, Phys. Scr. <strong>100</strong> (2025) 085110, arXiv:2403.19247v1, study dephasing superchannels, which multiply the Choi matrix J(E) of every channel E entrywise by a Gram matrix C. Such a superchannel is implemented with a memory state τ and encoding and decoding channels on system ⊗ memory: Ξ(E)(ρ) = Tr_M[N_de((E ⊗ id)(N_en(ρ ⊗ τ)))] (Eq. (9)).</p>
<ul>
<li>The memory is <em>passive</em> if its marginal after encoding does not depend on the input (Definition 2).</li>
<li>C is <em>classical</em> if C = Σ_i q_i C₁^{(i)} ⊗ C₂^{(i)}, the Gram matrix of dephasing channels applied before and after E under classical randomness.</li>
</ul>
<p>Proposition 1 shows that for qubit channels a passive memory realizes exactly the classical Gram matrices. §V.C asks &quot;whether a passive memory in any dimension is equivalent to a classical memory&quot;. §V.D argues that it is, for incoherent implementations. The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumChannels/salazar2025dephasing.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/15829">#15829</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<ul>
<li>every system dimension d and finite memory dimension r;</li>
<li>every memory state and every Kraus encoder and decoder;</li>
<li>the source's output-first normalised Choi matrix and Eq. (13)'s equal diagonal blocks;</li>
<li>passivity for every pair of input density matrices;</li>
<li>classical Gram matrices as finite convex combinations of Kronecker products of unit-diagonal positive semidefinite matrices.</li>
</ul>
