<blockquote>
<p>We were not able to find a simple analytic solution of the minimization procedure for the most general case of BD states. However, numerical minimization provided us with empirical evidence that the procedure (22) also leads for these states to the squared concurrence, as shown in figure \ref{figBD_ED}, which represent a face of the tetrahedral domain of BD states.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a reported numerical belief, rather than a proved identity. The source read is the arXiv v2 TeX, MD5 <code>232776c9cef202cc624a80958cb4c818</code>. Its figure caption at line 372 plots $E[\rho_{BD}]/2$; the Werner-state formula at lines 396–400 is described as “twice the square of the concurrence”. These fix the factor-two reading registered in issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20089">#20089</a>.</p>
<p>Let $B_0,B_1,B_2,B_3$ be the projectors onto $(|00\rangle+|11\rangle)/\sqrt2$, $(|00\rangle-|11\rangle)/\sqrt2$, $(|01\rangle+|10\rangle)/\sqrt2$ and $(|01\rangle-|10\rangle)/\sqrt2$, respectively. For $p_\alpha\ge0$ and $\sum_{\alpha=0}^3p_\alpha=1$, put $\rho_{BD}(p)=\sum_\alpha p_\alpha B_\alpha$ and $c(p)=\max(0,2\max_\alpha p_\alpha-1)$. Here $c$ denotes Bell-diagonal concurrence; $C_\mu$ below denotes site quantum correlation distance (QCD).</p>
<p>The registered claim is that $E(\rho_{BD}(p))=2c(p)^2$ for every such probability vector, where $E$ minimizes the site QCD of locally rotated mixtures over all finite density-matrix decompositions and component-dependent local rotations, summing both sites. This claim is refuted.</p>
