result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2025</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Blecher&#x27;s reversible four-by-four algebra is not unique up to unitary equivalence</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A weighted anticommuting algebra of four-by-four complex matrices disproves unitary uniqueness of reversible algebras.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>Why it matters</span>Blecher&#x27;s reversible operator algebras retain their matrix norms when multiplication is reversed.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Applying claim to witness, witnessalgebra, witnessnoncommutative, and witnessreversible yields result : ¬ claim.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A weighted anticommuting algebra of four-by-four complex matrices disproves unitary uniqueness of reversible algebras.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="blecher-2025-reversible-four-by-four-nonuniqueness"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Blecher&#x27;s reversible four-by-four algebra is not unique up to unitary equivalence</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. P. Blecher, <em>Commutativity of operator algebras</em>, arXiv:2509.17673v2, Example 5.2, ends its construction of the CAR algebra $A={\rm Span}{U,V,UV}\subseteq M_4$ with the sentence in <code>3commfarx.tex</code>, line 684:</p>
