ivity rank</em>, arXiv:2610.10820v1, derive the maximum purity of a qutrit-qutrit APPT state from Hildebrand's spectral criterion.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The criterion.</strong> For the ordered eigenvalues λ1 ≥ … ≥ λ9 ≥ 0, ρ is APPT iff the two matrices L1, L2 of Eq. (5) are positive semidefinite (Theorem II.1, citing Hildebrand).</li>
<li><strong>The rank bound.</strong> &quot;If λ⃗ is an ordered vector of qutrit-qutrit density matrix eigenvalues, then L1 and L2 of Eq. (5) have at most one negative eigenvalue&quot; (Theorem II.2, p. 5).</li>
<li><strong>The question.</strong> &quot;it may be prudent to explore whether components of the proof, e.g., the limited rank available for inequality violation established in Theorem II.2, persists for the increasing number of corresponding inequalities in qudit-qudit systems with d &gt; 3&quot; (§V, p. 16).</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumBounds/chenklco2026negativityrank.md">the literature note</a>. The corresponding inequalities are Hildebrand's (<a href="../Library/QuantumStates/hildebrand2007pptspectra.md">note</a>): for a pair of orderings (σ₊, σ₋) in Σ±, Λ(σ₊, σ₋) + Λ(σ₊, σ₋)ᵀ ⪰ 0.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/17687">#17687</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<ul>
<li>S₊ = {(k, l) : k ≤ l} and S₋ = {(k, l) : k &lt; l}, with orderings as bijections onto ranks.</li>
<li>Σ± is the set of pairs compatible, in the sense of Hildebrand's Definition 1, with some x₁ &gt; … &gt; x_d &gt; 0.</li>
<li>L = Λ + Λᵀ has diagonal entries 2λ_{d²+1−σ₊(k,k)} and off-diagonal entries λ_{d²+1−σ₊(k,l)} − λ_{σ₋(k,l)}. For d = 3 the two pairs of Σ± give L1 and L2.</li>
<li>The property: for every d &gt; 3, every ordered spectrum (λ_k ≥ 0, Σλ_k = 1, no APPT condition, as in Theorem II.2) and every pair in Σ±, L has at most one negative eigenvalue counted with multiplicity.</li>
</ul>
<p>The property is refuted in this reading.</p>
