class="news-result" id="ghosh-konar-francica-pal-sen-de-2025-stabilizer-battery-degree-difference-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">arXiv</p><h3>The work stored in a stabilizer battery is not a function of the degree difference</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>D. Ghosh, T. K. Konar, G. Francica, A. K. Pal and A. Sen(De), <em>Quantum advantage unlocked: Charging quantum batteries with K-regular graph stabilizers</em>, arXiv:2512.22908v2, Section III, Remark:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While our initial investigation with $N\leq 10$ indicates that $W_{(N,K^\prime,K)}$ remains unchanged as long $\Delta = |K-K^{\prime}|$ is constant for $N\neq 4N^\prime$ with $N^\prime=1,2,\cdots$, no such trend is observed in the case of $N\neq 4N^\prime$. However, concrete conclusion requires extensive numerical analysis with large $N$, which is challenging.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hamiltonians:</strong> $H_{(N,K)}=\sum_iH_{(i,K)}$ is the stabilizer Hamiltonian of the circulant $K$-regular graph on $N$ sites, $H_{(i,K)}=[\bigotimes_{j\le K/2}Z_{i-j}]X_i[\bigotimes_{j\le K/2}Z_{i+j}]$.</li>
<li><strong>Battery and charger:</strong> the battery $H_B=H_{(N,K')}$ starts in a ground state $\rho(0)$, and the charger $H_C=H_{(N,K)}$ drives $\rho(t)=e^{-iH_Ct}\rho(0)e^{iH_Ct}$.</li>
<li><strong>Work:</strong> $W_{(N,K',K)}(t)=\mathrm{Tr}[(\rho(t)-\rho(0))H_B]$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The definitions are quoted in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/ghoshkonarfrancicapalsende2025stabilizer.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20091">#20091</a> fixes the reading: for every $N&gt;2$ with $4\nmid N$ and all even interior degrees with $K'\ne K$, $L'\ne L$ and $|K-K'|=|L-L'|$, every pair of ground states gives $W_{(N,K',K)}(t)=W_{(N,L',L)}(t)$ for all $t\ge0$. Excluding the endpoint degrees $0$ and $K_{\max}$ makes the claim weaker than the literal one. It is refuted.</p>
