most $d+1$</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. Das, L. O. Conlon, J. Suzuki, S. K. Yung, P. K. Lam and S. M. Assad, <em>Holevo Cramér-Rao bound: How close can we get without entangling measurements?</em>, Quantum 9, 1867 (2025), DOI 10.22331/q-2025-09-30-1867; arXiv:2405.09622v2, Section 3.2.1, after Theorem 3, <code>DraftQuantumSubmission.tex</code>, lines 1147–1155:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Although we prove an upper bound of~$d+2$, based on numerical evidence shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:estimateallarbitstate}, we expect the attainable bound to be~$d+1$ and expect~$\rat[{\rho_\theta}]$ to be maximised at~$\theta=0$ over~$\Theta$. Thus for the linear GMM model, we propose~$\rat[{\rho_\theta}]\leq d+1$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The quotation preserves the TeX macros: <code>\rat</code> denotes $\mathcal R^{\mathrm{NH}}$. The source defines $\mathcal R^{\mathrm{NH}}[{\rho_\theta}]=\max_{\theta\in\Theta}\mathrm C_{\mathrm{NHCRB}}[\rho_\theta]/\mathrm C_{\mathrm{HCRB}}[\rho_\theta]$ in Eq. <code>ratiodef</code>, lines 798–801.</p>
<p>For $d\ge2$, put $n=d^2-1$. Let $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ be traceless Hermitian $d\times d$ complex matrices with $\operatorname{tr}(b_jb_k)=\delta_{jk}$, and let $\theta\in\mathbb R^n$. The model is $\rho_\theta=I_d/d+\sum_j\theta_jb_j$, with $\Theta$ consisting of the parameters making $\rho_\theta$ positive semidefinite. Its trace is one. This includes the source's generalized Gell-Mann model, whose normalization is specified in Section 5.2, lines 2071–2074.</p>
<p>The bounds use identity weight and the source's uncentered locally unbiased operators. Precisely:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>IsLUB</code> requires every $X_k$ to be Hermitian, $\operatorname{tr}(\rho_\theta X_k)=\theta_k$, and $\operatorname{tr}(b_jX_k)=\delta_{jk}$.</li>
<li><code>zMatrix</code> has entries $Z_{jk}=\operatorname{tr}(\rho_\theta X_jX_k)$; <code>imPart</code> takes the entrywise imaginary part and embeds it back into complex matrices. The trace norm is $\lVert A\rVert_1=\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}\sqrt{A^\dagger A}$.</li>
<li><code>IsAdmissible</code> requires Hermitian blocks $L_{jk}=L_{kj}$ and positive semidefiniteness of the matrix with blocks $L_{jk}-X_jX_k$, indexed by parameter–quantum pairs.</li>
<li><code>hcrb</code> is $\inf_X{\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}Z+\lVert\operatorname{Im}Z\rVert_1}-\sum_j\theta_j^2$, over <code>IsLUB</code> families; <code>nhcrb</code> is $\inf_{X,L}\operatorname{Re}\sum_j\operatorname{tr}(\rho_\theta L_{jj})-\sum_j\theta_j^2$, over <code>IsLUB</code> families and <code>IsAdmissible</code> blocks.</li>
</ul>
<p>These are the infimum forms of the source's Eqs. <code>HNCRBdefn</code>, <code>origunbiaseq</code> and <code>Holevodefn1</code>, lines 588–602 and 658–666. The definitions are also described in <a href="../Library/Estimation/dasetal2025holevo.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/25805">#25805</a> fixes the settlement as the following multiplicative inequality: for every such $d,b,\theta$ with $\rho_\theta$ positive semidefinite, $\mathrm C_{\mathrm{NHCRB}}[\rho_\theta]\le(d+1)\mathrm C_{\mathrm{HCRB}}[\rho_\theta]$. This is <code>claim</code>, proved by <code>result</code>.</p>
