<p>The preceding sentence, line 652, identifies the initial coin by $\theta=\phi=\pi/2$. The walk, measure and initial states are specified in Sections II A–C and the localized initial-state equation at TeX lines 423–425, respectively; see <a href="../Library/Entropy/donkerslootsondenheimersperling2026walks.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>For $P\ge1$, the modes are $(b,x)\in{0,1}\times\mathbb Z/P\mathbb Z$. The evolution is $T=S(H\otimes I)$, where $H=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\1&amp;-1\end{pmatrix}$ and $S$ shifts coin component $0$ left and component $1$ right, periodically. A normalized coin $c=(\alpha,\beta)$ starts at position zero. Write $\lambda_{P,n,c}=T^n(c\otimes|0\rangle)$ and $c_*=(1,i)/\sqrt2$.</p>
<p>For a finite amplitude family $\lambda$, the encoded single-photon vector is $|\psi_\lambda\rangle=\sum_k\lambda_k|1_k\rangle$, with one tensor factor per mode. The module defines $g_{\max}(\lambda)=\sup_{|z_k|=1}|\langle\bigotimes_k z_k|\psi_\lambda\rangle|^2$, with $z_k\in\mathbb C^2$, and $E_g(\lambda)=1-g_{\max}(\lambda)$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22527">#22527</a> fixes the reading: for every natural $P\ge1$, every $n\in\mathbb N$ and every $c\in\mathbb C^2$ with $|\alpha|^2+|\beta|^2=1$, $E_g(\lambda_{P,n,c})\le E_g(\lambda_{P,n,c_*})$. This is proved, including $P=1$ and $n=0$.</p>
