he other element. trianglecompatible establishes compatibility for precisely this complex and these two actions.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A doubled triangle with simultaneous exchange of edge copies has no local factorization.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="delascuevas-klingler-netzer-2024-nonfactorizable-doubled-triangle"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>A nonfactorizable doubled triangle</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>G. De las Cuevas, A. Klingler and T. Netzer, <em>Polynomial decompositions with invariance and positivity inspired by tensors</em>, arXiv:2109.06680v2; Linear Algebra and its Applications 698 (2024), 537–588, DOI 10.1016/j.laa.2024.05.025, ask in Question 38:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Are there non-factorizable $(\Omega,G)$ structures?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the verbatim question at lines 1546–1549 of the v2 TeX source <code>Manuscript_final.tex</code> (md5 <code>e601e0a3202f352cba4bf6d4d13e7db4</code>). Question 38 is also on PDF page 28; the conclusion repeats the existence question at TeX line 2035. Definitions and further quotations are in <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/delascuevasetal2024invariantpolynomialdecompositions.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>The source's definitions at lines 318–332, 425–444 and 1515–1522 give the following reading. A weighted simplicial complex on $[n]={0,\ldots,n}$ assigns natural weights $\Omega(S)$ to vertex subsets, with $\Omega(S)\mid\Omega(T)$ whenever $S\subseteq T$, and nonzero singleton weights. Each maximal nonzero-weight subset $F$ is a facet with $\Omega(F)$ distinct copies. Write $\widetilde{\mathcal F}$ for these multifacets and $\widetilde{\mathcal F}_i$ for those containing $i$. A finite group $G$ acts on vertices preserving weights and on multifacets compatibly with collapse to their underlying facets. Its action on labelings is inverse pullback, ${}^g\gamma(x)=\gamma(g^{-1}x)$.</p>
<p>For each finite index set $\mathcal I$ and labeling $\alpha:\widetilde{\mathcal F}\to\mathcal I$, define
$K_\alpha=\left|\left{\gamma:\widetilde{\mathcal F}\to\mathcal I:\ \forall i\in[n],\ \exists g_i\in G,\ g_i i=i\ \text{and}\ ({}^{g_i}\gamma)|<em>i=\alpha|<em>i\right}\right|$.
Factorizability requires, for every finite $\mathcal I$, strictly positive real factors $C^{[i]}</em>\beta$, equivariant under $C^{[gi]}</em>{{}^g\beta}=C^{[i]}<em>\beta$, satisfying $\prod</em>{i\in[n]}C^{[i]}_{\alpha|<em>i}=K</em>\alpha^{-1}$ for every global labeling $\alpha$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21655">#21655</a> fixes the formal <code>claim</code> as universal factorizability: for every natural $n$, every such weight function, every finite group and every compatible pair of actions, the structure is factorizable. <code>result : ¬ claim</code> refutes that universal assertion and answers the quoted existence question affirmatively.</p>
