und quantum information two-qubit conditional domains need not be c*-subalgebras parzygnat, conditional distributions for quantum systems (qpl 2021, eptcs 343, 1–13; arxiv:2102.01529), constructs quantum conditionals by categorical bayesian inversion. on a conditional domain the conditional is a positive map, so inference by conditioning is valid there. a two-qubit density matrix with invertible marginals has a conditional domain that is not closed under multiplication. in the basis (00, 01, 10, 11), let ν = (1/32)·[[11, 1, 1, 0], [1, 7, 0, −1], [1, 0, 9, −1], [0, −1, −1, 5]].  released-dossier ef1776329a07e39748c7afd1688f80a2034408487c495193083447c6ca30c4a4 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2021</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>Two-qubit conditional domains need not be C*-subalgebras</strong><span class="result-excerpt">A two-qubit density matrix with invertible marginals has a conditional domain that is not closed under multiplication.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Parzygnat, Conditional Distributions for Quantum Systems (QPL 2021, EPTCS 343, 1–13; arXiv:2102.01529), constructs quantum conditionals by categorical Bayesian inversion.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>On a conditional domain the conditional is a positive map, so inference by conditioning is valid there.</p><p><span>The idea</span>In the basis (00, 01, 10, 11), let ν = (1/32)·[[11, 1, 1, 0], [1, 7, 0, −1], [1, 0, 9, −1], [0, −1, −1, 5]].</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A two-qubit density matrix with invertible marginals has a conditional domain that is not closed under multiplication.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="parzygnat-2021-two-qubit-conditional-domain-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Two-qubit conditional domains need not be C*-subalgebras</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. J. Parzygnat, <em>Conditional Distributions for Quantum Systems</em> (QPL 2021, EPTCS 343, 1–13; arXiv:2102.01529), constructs quantum conditionals by categorical Bayesian inversion. For a density matrix ν on M_m ⊗ M_n with invertible marginals ρ = tr_B ν and σ = tr_A ν (Corollary 4.5), the conditional domains are:</p>
<ul>
<li>B_ν = {B : tr_B(ν(1 ⊗ B)) commutes with ρ};</li>
<li>A_ν = {A : tr_A((A ⊗ 1)ν) commutes with σ} (Definition 5.5).</li>
</ul>
<p>They are the largest operator systems on which the conditionals are positive (Lemma 5.8, Corollary 5.13).</p>
<ul>
<li>After Example 5.10 the author asks whether the domains are always C*-subalgebras, and answers no with an unrecorded 9 × 9 density matrix of rank 2.</li>
<li>Footnote 13 adds: &quot;Also, I could not find a 4 × 4 density matrix ν for which the conditional domains are not C∗-subalgebras, and I suspect that this may always be the case. I hope to resolve this in future work.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumChannels/parzygnat2021conditionaldistributions.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18167">#18167</a> fixes the reading: for every two-qubit density matrix with invertible marginals, both conditional domains are C*-subalgebras of M₂(ℂ). This suspicion is refuted.</p>
