<p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The quantum coherence fraction can increase under incoherent operations</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>Y. Yao, D. Li and C. P. Sun, <em>Quantum coherence fraction</em>, Phys. Rev. A 100, 032324 (2019), arXiv:1906.09789v1, Section V, <code>CF.tex</code>, lines 422–423:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>First, similar to the behavior of fully entangled fraction \cite{Badziag2000}, we conjecture that $C_{\mathcal{F}}(\rho)$ may not satisfy the monotonicity under general incoherent operations.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The source fixes the computational basis as the incoherent basis. An incoherent operation has a Kraus representation whose individual branches map diagonal density matrices to diagonal matrices; the normalized nonzero branches are again incoherent states (Section II, lines 122–131). Its quantum coherence fraction is $C_{\mathcal F}(\rho)=\max_{|\phi\rangle\in\mathcal{MCS}}\langle\phi|\rho|\phi\rangle$, where the maximally coherent unit vectors have coordinates of modulus $1/\sqrt d$ (lines 141–150). The definitions and source locator are in <a href="../Library/QuadraticForms/yaolisun2019coherencefraction.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/22563">#22563</a> fixes <code>claim</code> as the universal monotonicity assertion: for every $d,m\in\mathbb N$, every complex $d\times d$ matrix $\rho$ that is positive semidefinite with trace one, and every family $K:\mathrm{Fin},m\to M_d(\mathbb C)$ satisfying $\sum_\alpha K_\alpha^\dagger K_\alpha=I$ and preserving diagonality under each sandwich $X\mapsto K_\alpha X K_\alpha^\dagger$ for every complex diagonal matrix $X$, one has $C_{\mathcal F}(\sum_\alpha K_\alpha\rho K_\alpha^\dagger)\le C_{\mathcal F}(\rho)$. Here the module defines $C_{\mathcal F}(A)=\sup{\operatorname{Re}(z^\dagger Az)/d: z\in\mathbb C^d,\ |z_j|=1\text{ for every }j}$ using the real <code>sSup</code>.</p>
<p>The formal resolution is <code>refuted</code>, because <code>result : ¬ claim</code> negates universal monotonicity. This confirms the source's conjecture of non-monotonicity.</p>
