<p>However, we would like to conjecture that it could hold for the bipartite mixed states with the dimension $N \geq 6$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Appendix D, “The conjecture,” lines 1396–1397, repeats:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In this sense, we would like to conjecture that this relation should be satisfied in $(N\geq 6)$-dimensional systems.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The quotations are checked against the v1 TeX member <code>coherenceLy.tex</code>, md5 <code>026b6fb07e6d276a7bd743cc480af58c</code>. Lemma 1, lines 170–178, defines the reference-basis coherence by $C(\rho)=1-\sum_k\langle k|\sqrt\rho|k\rangle^2$, with the positive square root. Write $Q(\rho)=1-C(\rho)$. Theorem 2, lines 543–550, states $Q(|\Psi\rangle_{AB})\le Q(\rho_A)Q(\rho_B)$ for every bipartite pure state and its two reduced density matrices. The conjecture asks for the same inequality for arbitrary bipartite mixed states of total dimension $N\ge6$; the $2\otimes3$, $3\otimes3$, $3\otimes4$ and $4\otimes4$ figure captions identify $N$ as the total dimension.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/23835">#23835</a> fixes the reading: for all natural $m,n$ with $mn\ge6$ and every positive semidefinite complex matrix $\rho$ of trace one on $\mathbb C^m\otimes\mathbb C^n$, in the product reference basis,</p>
