>Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Blumenfeld, Steady states of two-dimensional granular systems are unique, stable, and sometimes satisfy detailed balance, J.</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The paper connects uniqueness to experiments in which sheared granular packings always settle into detailed-balance steady states.</p><p><span>The idea</span>Take C = 10, with its sixteen processes ordered (3,3), (3,4), (3,5), (3,6), (3,7), (3,8), (3,9), (4,4), (4,5), (4,6), (4,7), (4,8), (5,5), (5,6), (5,7), (6,6).</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>Strictly positive merging and splitting rates admit two distinct positive normalized stationary cell-order distributions at C = 10.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="myhill-blumenfeld-2023-granular-steady-state-uniqueness-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The cell-order dynamics of two-dimensional granular systems can have two physical steady states</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>A. D. C. Myhill and R. Blumenfeld, <em>Steady states of two-dimensional granular systems are unique, stable, and sometimes satisfy detailed balance</em>, J. Phys. A 56, 345001 (2023), arXiv:2306.10526v2, study the evolution of the cell-order distribution Q_3, …, Q_C of a two-dimensional granular packing.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The dynamics.</strong> Cells of orders i and j merge into a cell of order i + j − 2 at rate p_{i,j}, and split back at rate q_{i,j}; the net flux of the process is η_{i,j} = p_{i,j}Q_iQ_j − q_{i,j}Q_{i+j−2} (§I.A). Eq. (1) gives Q̇_k as the gain from processes producing k-cells, minus the loss from processes consuming them, plus Q_k Σ η, the renormalization for the changing number of cells.</li>
<li><strong>What is proved there.</strong> A detailed-balance steady state (every η_{i,j} = 0) is unique when it exists; for C = 6 and C = 7 no other steady state exists when it does.</li>
<li><strong>The conjecture.</strong> &quot;Based on these investigations, we conjecture that, for any choice of constant rates, there is only one physical solution regardless of the upper order, C&quot; (§I.D), where a physical solution has every Q_k real and positive (§III).</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/myhill2023granularsteadystates.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/16957">#16957</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<ul>
<li>every C ≥ 3, every assignment of strictly positive constant rates to the processes 3 ≤ i ≤ j with i + j − 2 ≤ C, and normalized positive states;</li>
<li>stationarity is the vanishing of the full right-hand side of Eq. (1), read with the flux of §I.A;</li>
<li>the printed loss term of Eq. (1) is read as η_{k,i−k+2}, whose splitting factor is the product cell Q_i, as in Eq. (2).</li>
</ul>
<p>The conjecture is refuted at C = 10.</p>
