<p>The formal <code>claim</code> quantifies over every $n\in\mathbb N$, charge map $c:\mathrm{Fin},n\to\mathbb Z$ and real vectors $r,s:\mathrm{Fin},n\to\mathbb R$ with nonnegative entries and $\sum_i r_i=\sum_i s_i=1$. Put $p_r(q)=\sum_{i:c(i)=q}r_i$, the module's reused <code>pushforward</code>, and let $\operatorname{sec}_q(r)$ be the descending list of $r_i/p_r(q)$ for all indices with $c(i)=q$, the module's <code>sectorSpectrum</code>. Zero eigenvalues retain their multiplicities. The claim says that $\operatorname{desc}(r)\succ\operatorname{desc}(s)$ implies $\operatorname{sec}_q(r)\succ\operatorname{sec}_q(s)$ for every integer $q$ with $p_r(q)&gt;0$ and $p_s(q)&gt;0$.</p>
<p>For equal-length descending lists $x,y$, the reused <code>Majorizes</code> requires every prefix sum of $x$ to be at least the corresponding prefix sum of $y$ for $1\le k\le |x|$, and equal total sums. This is the direction used in the source's definition of majorization (TeX line 830).</p>
