tion theory 71(4) (2025) 2726–2740, doi 10.1109/tit.2025.3543276; arxiv:2401.14385v3, remark 45, equation (58). the quantum inverse sumset problem asks how the entropy increase under self-convolution controls distance to the mean state. the equal mixture of two levels in dimension seven violates the proposed mixed-state quantum inverse sumset bound.  this refutation does not settle that separate conjecture, the qubit bounds or the rényi versions. released-dossier 5591dd8c0d8cc460e2deb1e1ecfcf74afb221d811672e5372cab19d1203d7976 "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2024</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The Bu–Gu–Jaffe mixed-state quantum inverse sumset bound is false</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The equal mixture of two levels in dimension seven violates the proposed mixed-state quantum inverse sumset bound.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Jaffe, Quantum Ruzsa Divergence to Quantify Magic, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 71(4) (2025) 2726–2740, DOI 10.1109/TIT.2025.3543276; arXiv:2401.14385v3, Remark 45, equation (58).</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The quantum inverse sumset problem asks how the entropy increase under self-convolution controls distance to the mean state.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The equal mixture of two levels in dimension seven violates the proposed mixed-state quantum inverse sumset bound.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>This refutation does not settle that separate conjecture, the qubit bounds or the Rényi versions.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="bu-gu-jaffe-2025-mixed-state-inverse-sumset"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The Bu–Gu–Jaffe mixed-state quantum inverse sumset bound is false</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>K. Bu, W. Gu and A. Jaffe, <em>Quantum Ruzsa Divergence to Quantify Magic</em>, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 71(4) (2025) 2726–2740, DOI 10.1109/TIT.2025.3543276; arXiv:2401.14385v3, Remark 45, equation (58). The v3 source <code>IEEE_Final_BGJ.tex</code>, line 2106, states verbatim:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In addition, we also expect that the property (2) in Theorem \ref{thm:QIST} holds, which  has the following form</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The reference is to Theorem 44. Its proposed mixed-state inequality, at lines 2107–2109, is</p>
<p>$D(\rho|\mathcal M(\rho))\leq\dfrac{\log R(\rho)}{\log R(\rho)-\log[1+\lambda(R(\rho)\operatorname{Tr}[\rho^2]-1)]}\log C.\qquad(58)$</p>
<p>Here $R(\rho)=\operatorname{rank}\mathcal M(\rho)$ and $\lambda=(1-MG(\rho))^2$. The characteristic function is $\Xi_\rho(p,q)=\operatorname{Tr}[\rho w(-p,-q)]$; the mean state retains exactly its coefficients of modulus one. The magic gap $MG$ is one minus the largest modulus among the nonzero coefficients whose modulus is not one, with $MG=0$ if that finite set is empty. The source definitions and their locations are in <a href="../Library/Divergence/bugujaffe2025ruzsa.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24984">#24984</a> fixes the single-qudit reading encoded by <code>claim</code>: for every nonzero prime dimension $d$, every nonzero $s,t\in\mathbb Z_d$ with $s^2+t^2=1$, every three density matrices $\rho,\sigma,\tau$ with $\sigma=\mathcal M(\rho)$ and $\tau=\rho\boxtimes_{s,t}\rho$, and every real $C$, require (58) whenever</p>
<p>$1&lt;\delta_q[\rho]=\exp(S(\tau)-S(\rho))\leq C,\qquad A=1+\lambda(R(\rho)\operatorname{Tr}[\rho^2]-1)&gt;0,\qquad\log R(\rho)-\log A&gt;0.$</p>
<p>Density matrices are positive semidefinite complex matrices of trace one. Entropy and relative entropy are the natural-logarithm trace expressions $S(\rho)=-\operatorname{Tr}(\rho\log\rho)$ and $D(\rho|\sigma)=\operatorname{Tr}[\rho(\log\rho-\log\sigma)]$. The two positivity assumptions restrict to a positive logarithm argument and denominator. This restriction of the expected universal bound is refuted.</p>
