<ul>
<li>A map is semi-nonnegative (SN) if it sends some density matrix to a density matrix (Definition 2), equivalently if Ψ ∘ Φ = Ξ for some CPTP Φ and Ξ (Theorem 2).</li>
<li>It is semi-positive (SP) if it sends some invertible density matrix to an invertible one (Definition 1). SP maps are the intermediate maps of CP-indivisible, non-Markovian evolutions.</li>
<li>For qubits, the non-SP SN maps send only one pure state to a state, so they &quot;signal no information backflow&quot;.</li>
<li>The Outlook conjectures: &quot;We conjecture that non-SP SNTP maps still signal no information backflow and are not uniquely defined even for higher dimensional H.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/QuantumChannels/caofitzsimmonsmannpereiralaflamme2023cptphptp.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/18048">#18048</a> fixes the reading of the first conjunct: in every context Ψ ∘ Φ = Ξ of a non-SP SNTP map, the intermediate map does not increase the trace distance of two states. This conjunct, and with it the conjecture, is refuted.</p>
