atedness implies componentwise relatedness, so the same obstruction excludes four-way support comparisons.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A locality structure on real five-space refutes both conjectures for componentwise and four-way locality.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="clavier-foissy-lopez-paycha-2022-locality-tensor-product-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Locality tensor products need not be locality vector spaces</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>P. J. Clavier, L. Foissy, D. A. López and S. Paycha, <em>Tensor products and the Milnor-Moore theorem in the locality setup</em>, arXiv:2205.14616v1 (European Journal of Mathematics 11, 41, 2025), Conjectural statement 6.16, states:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Given a locality vector space $( {E},\top)$ and $V_1$, $V_2$ two of its subspaces, the subspace $I_{\rm bil} {(V_1, V_2)} {\cap\mathbb{K}(V_1\times_\top V_2)}\subset \mathbb{K}(V_1\times_\top V_2)$ is locality compatible with $\top_{V_1\times_\top V_2}$.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The locator is the downloaded TeX <code>Clavier_Foissy_Lopez_Paycha_arxiv.tex</code>, lines 1984–1987, label <code>conj:main1</code>, md5 <code>1f6041a82cd80114573aa776694ccf20</code>. Quotations expand typesetting macros without changing the wording.</p>
<p>The paper's Notations, <code>Clavier_Foissy_Lopez_Paycha_arxiv.tex</code>, line 321, specifies that $\mathbb K$ is a commutative field of characteristic zero. Both conjectures below, under both generator-relation readings, quantify over fields of characteristic zero. The thesis states no characteristic restriction; refuting its assertion even on characteristic-zero fields also refutes the unrestricted assertion.</p>
<p>The tensor-product conjecture is stated explicitly by P. J. Clavier, <em>TRAPs, Generalisations of MZVs, Locality and Resurgence for Quantum Field Theories</em>, arXiv:2506.09493v1 (2025), chapter <code>LOC.tex</code>, lines 1797–1799, label <code>conj:loc_tensor</code>, md5 <code>41f5e772e40d5eaf8583e8903293dbbb</code>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Let $(E,\top)$ be any locality vector space, $V$ and $W$ any two locality vector subspaces of $E$. Then $(V\otimes_\top W,\overline{\top})$ is a locality vector space.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>For a field $\mathbb K$, a locality vector space is a $\mathbb K$-vector space $E$ with a symmetric relation $r$, with $r(0,0)$, such that the polar $U^r={x:\forall u\in U,\ r(u,x)}$ of every subset $U\subseteq E$ is a linear subspace. For subspaces $V,W\subseteq E$, put $F=\mathbb K(V\times_\top W)$, the free span of pairs $(v,w)\in V\times W$ satisfying $r(v,w)$. In the ambient free span on all pairs, let $I_{\rm bil}(V,W)$ be the span of the two additivity and two scalar-multiplication relations, and put $I=F\cap I_{\rm bil}(V,W)$.</p>
<p>The module uses two generator relations on pairs $(v,w)$ and $(v',w')$:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Prod.RProd r r</code>: $r(v,v')\wedge r(w,w')$, as in the 2022 paper, Eq. <code>eq:locreltoptimes</code>, TeX lines 447–449;</li>
<li><code>fourWay</code>: $r(v,w)\wedge r(v,v')\wedge r(v,w')\wedge r(w,v')\wedge r(w,w')\wedge r(v',w')$, all six pairs among the four entries, as in the thesis's locality cartesian power, Eq. <code>eq:lprod</code>, <code>LOC.tex</code> lines 219–221, and its description of tensor locality through $E^{\times_\top4}$, lines 743–747.</li>
</ul>
<p>The sources disagree: the paper's Remark at lines 1972–1975 identifies its componentwise relation, when $V=W$, with $V^{\times_\top4}$, whereas the thesis's cartesian-power definition requires all six pairs to be related. The two generator relations differ in general, even on local pairs; the computed example in Triage distinguishes them.</p>
<p>For either generator relation $g$, write $s_g(a,b)$ when every pair in the support of $a\in F$ is $g$-related to every pair in the support of $b\in F$. The relation on $F/I=V\otimes_\top W$ is existential: two classes are related precisely when they have $s_g$-related representatives. Locality compatibility of $I$ means that for all $x,y,z\in F$ and $w\in I$, if $s_g(x,y)$ and $s_g(x+w,z)$, there is $w'\in I$ with both $s_g(x+w',y)$ and $s_g(x+w',z)$.</p>
<p>The problem is tracked in issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21552">#21552</a>, including <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21552#issuecomment-6396883196">comment 6396883196</a>. The module defines <code>TensorConjecture g</code> as the tensor-product assertion and <code>PairConjecture g</code> as the compatibility assertion for the chosen generator relation, each universally quantified over every field $\mathbb K$ of characteristic zero, every $\mathbb K$-vector space $E$ in <code>Type</code>, every locality relation and every pair of subspaces, without a finite-dimensionality hypothesis. The module defines <code>claim := TensorConjecture (fun _ r =&gt; Prod.RProd r r) ∨ TensorConjecture fourWay ∨ PairConjecture (fun _ r =&gt; Prod.RProd r r) ∨ PairConjecture fourWay</code> and proves <code>result : ¬ claim</code>, refuting both universal assertions for each generator relation.</p>
<p>The definitions and source locators are recorded in <a href="../Library/HomologicalAlgebra/clavieretal2022localitytensor.md">the paper note</a> and <a href="../Library/HomologicalAlgebra/clavier2025localityhdr.md">the thesis note</a>.</p>
