dea</span>- the complete graph on {0, 6, …, 15} with all rates 1.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>A sixteen-state network contradicts the proposed general-network activity bound on cross-correlation asymmetry.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="gu-2023-cross-correlation-activity-bound-refutation"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The activity bound on cross-correlation asymmetry fails for general networks</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>J. Gu, <em>Thermodynamic bounds on the asymmetry of cross-correlations with dynamical activity and entropy production</em>, Phys. Rev. E 109, L042101 (2024), arXiv:2312.00354, bounds the normalized asymmetry χ_ab = α_ab/√(D_a D_b) of the cross-correlation of two observables in a Markov jump process on N states.</p>
<ul>
<li>Eq. (8) bounds it by the dynamical activity A, the entropy production σ and the flux scale ratio γ: |χ_ab| ≤ γ/tan(π/N) · √(σ/(2A)).</li>
<li>The author proves Eq. (8) for unicyclic networks and the weaker Eq. (9) in general, and writes: &quot;We conjecture that Eq. (8) is valid for arbitrary networks&quot;. The evidence is 10⁶ random samples on three four-state topologies.</li>
