<p>The quotation is checked against the v2 TeX source, md5 <code>4452c28a26c7a76c87642af46fe0c8a9</code>. The referenced inequalities are at lines 461–464; their definitions are at lines 437–460. The moment conventions are at lines 228–236 and 290–293. See <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/meerweeks2024boundedmoments.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21539">#21539</a> fixes the following universal reading. For every real interval $L&lt;U$ and every probability law $P$ on $\mathbb R$ with $L\le X\le U$ almost surely, mean $\mu=\mathbb E X&gt;0$ and variance $v=\mathbb E[(X-\mu)^2]&gt;0$, put</p>
<p>$\sigma=\sqrt v$, $\delta=\sigma/\mu$, $\delta_{\min}=|1-L/\mu|$, $\delta_{\max}=|1-U/\mu|$, $\delta_1=\min(\delta_{\min},\delta_{\max})$, $\delta_2=\max(\delta_{\min},\delta_{\max})$, $\delta'=\sqrt{\delta_1\delta_2}$,</p>
<p>and $D_4=\mathbb E[(X-\mu)^4]/v^2$, $D_{4,m}(\delta)=(\delta/\delta_m-\delta_m/\delta)^2+1$ for $m=1,2$. The assertion requires both:</p>
<ul>
<li>if $\delta&lt;\delta_1$, then $1\le D_4\le D_{4,2}(\delta)$;</li>
<li>if $\delta_1\le\delta&lt;\delta'$, then $D_{4,1}(\delta)\le D_4\le D_{4,2}(\delta)$.</li>
