ares do not maximize the mean exit time of lattice clusters</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>S. Rognone and V. Nicosia, <em>Clusters in randomly-coloured spatial networks</em>, arXiv:2308.07880v1 (J. Phys. Complexity, 2026), section on the mean exit time from a cluster:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Characterising clusters with maximal exit time at each fixed $N$ seems to be a harder quest. Here we conjecture that the maximal exit time is obtained for squares with $N$ nodes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cluster:</strong> a set of nodes of the square lattice, connected through nearest neighbours; the walk moves to each of the four neighbours with probability 1/4.</li>
<li><strong>Mean exit time</strong> $\tau(S)$: the average over the starting nodes of $S$ of the expected first time the walk is outside $S$.</li>
</ul>
<p>The definitions are quoted in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/rognonenicosia2023clusters.md">the literature note</a>. The source's displayed formula averages node-to-node hitting times, while its prose and every worked first-step system use the first-exit time; the settlement uses the first-exit time.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/20034">#20034</a> fixes the reading: for every $m\ge1$ and every connected cluster $S$ of $m^2$ nodes, $\tau(S)\le\tau({0,\dots,m-1}^2)$. This is refuted.</p>
