Section 4.1, p. 18, immediately after Theorem 4, asks:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does DiversityMaximizingMeasure correctly compute the maximizing-diversity measure of all finite subsets of ℝⁿ? Of all finite metric spaces of negative type?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This dossier settles only the second question, <strong>Refuted</strong>. The source's negative
type means that the square-root distance embeds isometrically into a Hilbert
space. The input is any nonempty finite metric space, with similarity
$Z_{ij}=e^{-d(i,j)}$. Algorithm 1 repeatedly solves on its retained support,
extends the weighting by zero, discards nonpositive weights if another solve
is necessary, and normalizes at the first nonnegative solve.</p>
<p>The printed stopping test is reversed relative to Theorem 4's proof. The
convention here is the proof's sentence: “At the end of the repeat loop, the
vector w is non-zero and non-negative.” The counterexample uses that intended
stop and does not exploit the printed test.</p>
<h3>Statement echo</h3>
<p>| Source clause or domain condition | Lean counterpart | Fidelity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Every nonempty finite metric space | <code>claim</code>, positive <code>n</code> and arbitrary <code>MetricSpace (Fin n)</code> | Finite labeling; the empty space has no probability measure. |
