<p>The versioned TeX source reads verbatim:</p>
<pre><code class="language-tex">\item[OQ4.] \textbf{Values of $\pi(q)/z(2q+1)$.} Is $\{\pi(q)/z(2q+1) : q &gt; 5 \text{ with } z(2q+1) \mid \pi(q)\} = \{\text{odd integers}\}$?
</code></pre>
<p>The Introduction defines $z(p)$ at primes as the least positive index $k$ with
$p\mid F_k$, and $\pi(n)$ as the period of the Fibonacci residues modulo $n$.
The standing domain in Sections 5–7 is Sophie Germain primes: $q$ and $2q+1$
are both prime. The assertion here uses $q&gt;5$ and $z(2q+1)\mid\pi(q)$.
The right-hand side is the positive odd integers; reading it as all odd integers
also fails because the ratios are positive.</p>
