 class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>Non-local partial entanglement annihilation is not closed under aligned tensor products</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>B. Mallick, N. Ganguly and A. S. Majumdar, <em>On the characterization of partially entanglement breaking and annihilating channels</em>, arXiv:2411.19315v2; J. Math. Phys. 67, 082201 (2026), ask in Section VI, Conclusions, PDF page 16:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We have also shown that $r$-partially entanglement breaking channels are not closed under tensor products, motivating the investigation of whether a similar property holds for $r$-partially entanglement annihilating channels.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the verbatim sentence in <code>main.tex</code>, line 1167, from the v2 source archive (member MD5 <code>02f5d47ee4475c5ba30a52b1e5931b9b</code>). The definitions are in Section IV.A, PDF pages 9–10, <code>main.tex</code> lines 630–632; see also <a href="../Library/QuantumContext/mallickgangulymajumdar2026partialannihilating.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Write $\operatorname{PEA}_{d,r}(E)$ for the module's <code>IsPEA d r E</code>: $E$ is a completely positive, trace-preserving complex-linear map on the matrices of $\mathbb C^d\otimes\mathbb C^d$, cannot be written as a tensor product of two individual CPTP maps, satisfies $r&lt;d$, and sends every positive semidefinite trace-one input to a state of Schmidt number at most $r$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/21802">#21802</a> fixes the closure assertion: for every $d,r\in\mathbb N$ and every pair of complex-linear bipartite matrix maps $E,F$, $\operatorname{PEA}<em>{d,r}(E)$ and $\operatorname{PEA}</em>{d,r}(F)$ imply $\operatorname{PEA}<em>{d^2,r}(E\otimes</em>{\mathrm{aligned}}F)$. The aligned split groups $B_1B'_1,|,B_2B'_2$ when the two copies are $B_1B_2$ and $B'_1B'_2$. The threshold remains $r$.</p>
<p>This closure assertion is refuted. The source asks whether non-closure occurs; it does not assert closure. Thus refuting the registered assertion answers the source's non-closure question affirmatively.</p>
