ment prose"><p>I. Sakai and T. Akimoto, <em>Symmetry breaking of current response in disordered exclusion processes</em>, arXiv:2512.03316v2, Phys. Rev. E 113, 044123 (2026), study the stationary current J(ε) of an exclusion process on a ring of L sites with N particles.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Model.</strong> A particle at site i hops to i + 1 at rate p_i(ε) = (1 + ε) w_{i,i+1}/2 and to i − 1 at rate q_i(ε) = (1 − ε) w_{i,i−1}/2, with ∏ w_{i,i−1}/w_{i,i+1} = 1 (Eq. (1)).</li>
<li><strong>Condition (i).</strong> The bond-bias ratio r_i(ε) = p_i(ε)/q_{i+1}(ε) does not depend on i (Eq. (5)).</li>
<li><strong>Condition (ii).</strong> There is a k such that, for every i, p_{k+i}(ε) = q_{k−i}(−ε) or p_{k+i}(ε) = q_{k−i+1}(−ε) (Appendix C).</li>
<li><strong>The conjecture.</strong> Either condition makes J odd in ε. The authors write (§III.D, p. 3): &quot;We conjecture—and later confirm numerically— that an ε² contribution appears whenever neither condition (i) nor condition (ii) is satisfied.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The verbatim statements are in <a href="../Library/StatisticalMechanics/sakaiakimoto2026currentresponse.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/17823">#17823</a> fixes the reading:</p>
<ul>
<li>the ε² contribution is the coefficient c₂ of J(ε) = c₁ε + c₂ε² + o(ε²) under the unique stationary law;</li>
<li>condition (ii) is read in its weakest per-site form;</li>
<li>the claim is restricted to at least two particles and at least two holes, because the source (§III.F) already reports an exactly odd current for a single particle.</li>
</ul>
<p>This restricted conjecture is refuted.</p>
