601; arxiv:2312.07738v3, section 4, main.tex, lines 736–740: the conjectured equality would turn a minimization over hidden-variable assignments into a geometric intersection problem. the iiz perp hyperplane refutes the classical-hexagon contextuality generalization. the lower bound on the natural infimum uses lecsinf with set.rangenonempty; the range contains the violation count of the zero assignment. which further geometric hypotheses guarantee equality remains open here. released-dossier cb770663e18d04c835b1db6b56fb984005a31a784798efa4ebc7ddf37770f88b "><summary><span class="result-impact-list"><span class="result-impact-field">Quantum information</span><span class="result-impact-tag">Counterexample found</span><span class="result-impact-history"><span>First preprint</span> <span class="result-impact-year">2023</span></span></span><span class="result-summary"><strong>The perp hyperplane refutes the three-qubit hexagon contextuality generalization</strong><span class="result-excerpt">The IIZ perp hyperplane refutes the classical-hexagon contextuality generalization.</span></span><span class="result-outcome refuted">Refuted</span><span class="result-disclosure" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading-item-body"><div class="result-story-expanded"><p><span>The question</span>Giorgetti and H. de Boutray, Hexagons govern three-qubit contextuality, Quantum 9 (2025) 1601, DOI 10.22331/q-2025-01-20-1601; arXiv:2312.07738v3, Section 4, main.tex, lines 736–740:</p><p><span>Why it matters</span>The conjectured equality would turn a minimization over hidden-variable assignments into a geometric intersection problem.</p><p><span>The idea</span>The lower bound on the natural infimum uses lecsInf with Set.rangenonempty; the range contains the violation count of the zero assignment.</p><p><span>Our contribution</span>The IIZ perp hyperplane refutes the classical-hexagon contextuality generalization.</p><p><span>Scope &amp; next question</span>Which further geometric hypotheses guarantee equality remains open here.</p></div><details class="verification-details"><summary>Verification details</summary><article class="news-result" id="saniga-et-al-2025-hexagon-contextuality-generalization"><div class="news-result-meta"><span>Release source record</span><span class="news-status refuted">Refuted in Lean</span></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Open problem</p><h3>The perp hyperplane refutes the three-qubit hexagon contextuality generalization</h3><div class="result-statement prose"><p>M. Saniga, F. Holweck, C. Kelleher, A. Muller, A. Giorgetti and H. de Boutray, <em>Hexagons govern three-qubit contextuality</em>, Quantum 9 (2025) 1601, DOI 10.22331/q-2025-01-20-1601; arXiv:2312.07738v3, Section 4, <code>main.tex</code>, lines 736–740:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The message from the above-given observations is more than obvious: the <em>degree of contextuality</em> of a contextual quantum geometry of the three-qubit $\mathcal{W}(5,2)$ whose contexts are i) all the 315 lines of the whole space $\mathcal{W}(5,2)$, ii) all the 45 lines of an elliptic quadric, iii) all the 105 lines of a hyperbolic quadric or iv) all the 15 lines of a doily is equal to the <em>number of lines</em> each of these geometries shares with a classically-embedded hexagon, with the understanding that each shared line corresponds to an unsatisfiable context. A natural generalization of these findings is that this should hold for <em>any</em> contextual sub-geometry of $\mathcal{W}(5,2)$ whose contexts are <em>lines</em>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The next paragraph begins, “As a particular stance of this conjecture, let us consider the complement of a skew-embedded hexagon” (<code>main.tex</code>, lines 742–744), so the proposed generalization extends beyond the four named families. Definitions and source locators are also in <a href="../Library/FiniteGeometry/saniga2025hexagons.md">the literature note</a>.</p>
<p>For the formal reading, put $V=(\mathbb F_2^2)^3$. Its nonzero vectors label the canonical three-qubit Pauli observables $P_v$. Write $B$ for the binary symplectic form. Lines are the triples ${u,v,u+v}$ with $u,v$ nonzero, distinct and $B(u,v)=0$. Set $s(\ell)=1$ when $\prod_{q\in\ell}P_q=-I$, and $s(\ell)=0$ otherwise. For a finite family $C$ of these lines, define $d(C)=\min_{f:V\to\mathbb F_2}|{\ell\in C:\sum_{q\in\ell}f(q)\ne s(\ell)}|$.</p>
<p>Issue <a href="https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/issues/24997">#24997</a> fixes <code>claim</code>: for every such $C$ with $d(C)&gt;0$, there exists $H$ satisfying <code>IsClassicalHexagon</code> with $d(C)=|C\cap H|$. The predicate, specified below, includes all the source's classical embeddings. This claim is refuted.</p>
